---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import Documents from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Documents.astro";
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";

/* This page is thirty-odd documents long and a reader arriving at it has no way
   in. The contents below are read out of THIS FILE at build time, so a section
   cannot be added without appearing here — the same reason the gallery is built
   from the pages rather than written by hand. */
/* read by path, not import.meta.url: after bundling that points at the chunk,
   not at this file. The build runs from site/, so this path is stable. */
const src = readFileSync("src/pages/documents.astro", "utf-8");
const toc = [...src.matchAll(/<h2 id="([a-z0-9-]+)"[^>]*>([\s\S]*?)<\/h2>/g)]
  .filter(m => m[1] !== "contents")
  .map(m => {
  const text = m[2]
    .replace(/<[^>]+>/g, "")
    .replace(/&middot;/g, "·").replace(/&ndash;/g, "–").replace(/&mdash;/g, "—")
    .replace(/&rsquo;/g, "’").replace(/&ldquo;|&rdquo;/g, "“").replace(/&quot;/g, "“")
    .replace(/&amp;/g, "&").replace(/&pound;/g, "£")
    .replace(/\s+/g, " ").trim();
  const yr = (text.match(/\b(1[6-9]\d\d|20[0-2]\d)\b/) || [])[1];
  const sep = text.indexOf("·");
  return { id: m[1], year: yr ? +yr : 9999,
           label: sep > -1 ? text.slice(sep + 1).trim() : text };
}).sort((a, b) => a.year - b.year);
---
<Base title="The documents"
      eyebrow="Four civil records, read for the first time"
      dek="The family tree carried these scans for years with nobody transcribing them. They are not snapshots — they are death registrations, a death notice and a marriage register. Reading them corrected three dates, killed one family legend, and caught the surname changing on a single sheet of paper.">

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="contents">What is on this page</h2>
  <p class="dek">{toc.length} documents, in the order the events happened rather than the order they were
    read. Every one of them has been read from the image unless the entry says otherwise.</p>
  <Documents u={u}
    rows={toc.map((t) => ({ year: t.year === 9999 ? null : t.year,
                            title: t.label, href: "#" + t.id }))} />
</section>

<Rule name="thorn" label="The documents, oldest first" />

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1905 · The death registration that settles the biggest question</h2>
  <p class="dek">Cape Colony, <em>Form of Information of a Death, Act No. 7 of 1894</em>. Entry 299,
    sub-district Indwe, district Wodehouse. Registered 14 September 1905.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/death-1905-willem-hermanus.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Cape Colony death registration form for Willem Hermanus Booysen, 1905, handwritten in ink." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Death registration of <strong>Willem Hermanus Booysen</strong>, Indwe, 1905.
      MyHeritage tree <em>Defranceski Family Site (23andMe)</em>, extracted 9 September 2026.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:14rem">Usual place of residence</td><td><strong>Doorn Street, Indwe</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Age</td><td>Fifty Five years</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Occupation</strong></td><td><strong>Mason</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Date of death</strong></td><td><strong>September Thirteenth 1905</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Intended place of burial</td><td>Public Cemetery, Indwe</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Cause of death</strong></td><td><strong>Ulcer of Stomach</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Duration of last illness</td><td><strong>Fourteen months</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Medical man</td><td>W. C. Macdonald, M.B.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Informant</td><td><strong>J. B. Booyzen</strong> — qualification: <strong>Present at Death</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Total children</td><td>Twelve</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What it proves</h3>
      <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> The death date the tree flagged
        <em>"To be confirmed, not sure"</em> is <strong>correct</strong>. He died at Indwe on
        <strong>13 September 1905</strong>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px"><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>He was not shot by the
        British.</strong> He died of a <strong>stomach ulcer after fourteen months of illness</strong>,
        under a doctor's care, in his own house, three years after the war ended.
        <a href={u("/rebels/")}>What that does to the testimony &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">A farmer who became a mason</h3>
      <p>At his marriage in 1879 his occupation is <strong>Boer</strong> — farmer. At his death in 1905 it
        is <strong>Mason</strong>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">A man who works land and then works stone has, in between, very often
        <strong>lost the land</strong>. The war sits in that interval.
        <span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> Suggestive, not probative — but it is exactly the
        trajectory the burned-farm testimony describes.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The name changing, on one sheet of paper</h2>
  <p class="dek">The registering clerk writes the deceased's surname. Four lines below, his son signs his
    own. They are not the same word.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1905-clerk-booysen.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Handwritten name on the 1905 form reading Willem Hermanus Booysen, with an s." />
      <figcaption class="cap">The clerk, September 1905: <strong>Willem Hermanus Boo<em>y</em>sen</strong></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1905-signature-booyzen.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Informant signature on the 1905 form reading J B Booyzen, with a clear z." />
      <figcaption class="cap">His son, the same day: <strong>J. B. Boo<em>y</em><em>z</em>en</strong></figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:24px">
    The father is registered under the old spelling. The son signs the new one. <strong>Both are on the
    same page, in September 1905 — three years after the Anglo-Boer War ended.</strong>
  </blockquote>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">The informant is <strong>John Barry Booyzen</strong> (b. 1884), the eldest
    son, then twenty-one, recorded as <em>present at the death</em>. This is as close to a photograph of
    the surname changing as the archive is ever likely to find.
    <a href={u("/name/")}>The name &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1879 · The marriage register — and one ambiguous letter</h2>
  <p class="dek"><em>Duplicaat Origineel Register</em>, in Dutch. Entry 15, 22 December 1879. Married in
    the <strong>Nederduits Gereformeerde Kerk at Barkly</strong>, after banns, by <strong>David Ross</strong>,
    <em>consulent</em>.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/marriage-1879-booysen-barry.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Dutch-language duplicate marriage register page for 1879, Barkly, Wodehouse district." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Marriage of Willem Hermanus Booysen and Catharine Mary Barry, Barkly, 1879.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Groom</th><th>Bride</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Name as entered</td><td><strong>Willem Hermanus Booysen</strong></td><td><strong>Catharine Mary Barry</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Age</td><td class="m">22</td><td class="m">21</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Status</td><td>Jongman</td><td>Jongedochter</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Rank or occupation</strong></td><td><strong>Boer</strong> (farmer)</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Residence</td><td><strong>Lady Grey</strong></td><td><strong>Barkly</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:18px"><strong>Witnesses: J. Sutherland and P. Schoeman.</strong> Set that beside
    Anna Johanna Maria <strong>Schoemann</strong> marrying Hendrik Nicholaas Barry in 1877, the 1901
    Pretorius–Barry wedding held <em>at the home of D. Schoeman, "Caerlaverock", Barkly East</em>, and a
    <strong>Commandant D. Schoeman</strong> leading Cape Rebels at Labuschagne's Nek in 1900. The Schoemans
    are braided through this family across three generations.
    <a href={u("/rebels/")}>The rebels &rarr;</a></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:32px">The most consequential letter in the archive</h3>
  <figure style="margin:18px 0 0;max-width:520px">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1879-groom-signature.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Extreme magnification of the groom's 1879 signature surname, reading Booij-something-en." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The groom's own signature, 1879, at maximum available magnification.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p>The clerk wrote <b>Booysen</b>. The groom signed what reads as <b>"Booij_en"</b> — using the older
      Dutch <b>ij</b> digraph, with a medial letter whose descending loop is more consistent with
      <b>z</b> than <b>s</b>.</p>
    <blockquote>
      If that reading is right, <strong>he was already signing with a z in 1879</strong> — twenty years
      before the Anglo-Boer War — and the testimony's explanation of <em>why</em> the spelling changed is
      a later rationalisation of something that had already happened.
    </blockquote>
    <p><span class="chip inf">Not established</span> <b>The reading is not certain.</b> Cursive Dutch
      <em>s</em> and <em>z</em> are genuinely confusable, and this is a photograph of a duplicate register,
      not the original. The archive states both readings and prefers neither.</p>
    <p><b>This single letter is now the most consequential unresolved question here.</b> It wants an eye
      trained on nineteenth-century Cape Dutch hands, and the original register at the Cape Archives.
      <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1915 · John Barry's death notice — typewritten, and therefore unarguable</h2>
  <p class="dek">Prepared by solicitors at Barkly East, stamped 18 April 1915, No. 925. Signed by
    <strong>James H. Barry, "Son (second)"</strong>. <b>This is the very document the family's earlier
    research note describes</b> — it can now be quoted rather than paraphrased.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1915-john-barry.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Typewritten 1915 death notice for John Barry, Barkly East." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Death Notice of John Barry, died 1903, notice prepared 1915.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:15rem"><strong>Birthplace and nationality</strong></td>
            <td><strong>"At sea (exact whereabouts unknown), British."</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Father</td><td>John Barry (address unknown)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Mother</strong></td><td><strong>Barry, born Neagle</strong> (Christian names and address unknown)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Age</td><td>83 years (about)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Occupation</td><td><strong>Carpenter and builder</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Residence</td><td><strong>Manorowen</strong>, Barkly East District</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Spouse</td><td>Margaret Wilhelmina Elizabeth Barry, born <strong>Kolbe</strong> (died 2 August 1912). In community.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Place of last marriage</td><td><strong>"Burghersdorp (probably)"</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Day of decease</strong></td><td><strong>3rd August 1903</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Where died</td><td>on farm <strong>Manorowen</strong>, district Barkly East</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Estate</td><td>movable <strong>Yes</strong> · immovable <strong>No</strong> · exceeds £300 <strong>Yes</strong> · will <strong>No</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">"At sea" is official, not folklore</h3>
      <p>The Irish/High-Seas ambiguity is <strong>contemporaneous and legal</strong>, sworn by his own son.
        The mother's maiden name <strong>Neagle</strong> — almost certainly <strong>Nagle</strong>, of
        County Cork — is on a state document, not a family memory.
        <a href={u("/barry/")}>The Barrys &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Manorowen is Welsh</h3>
      <p>The tree renders the farm "Manor Owen", which hides it. The notice types it as one word:
        <strong>Manorowen</strong> — <strong>a village in Pembrokeshire, Wales</strong>. He also owned
        <strong>no immovable property</strong>: he lived on Manorowen but did not own it. Both are new and
        entirely unexplored.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>Two corrections fall out of this page.</b><br />
    <b>1. The death date is 3 August 1903</b>, not the 25 August the tree carries — a 22-day discrepancy.<br />
    <b>2. A duplicate person is resolved.</b> The tree lists both <em>Catherine Maria Sophia "Kathleen"
    Barry (1859–1940), married Booyzen</em> and a separate sister <em>Kathleen Mary Barry (Booysen)</em>
    with no dates. This notice lists <b>only one</b> Barry daughter married to a Boyson. The arithmetic
    closes exactly: the tree's <b>11</b> children = the notice's <b>8</b> surviving + <b>2</b> who
    predeceased him + <b>1 duplicate</b>. They are the same woman, and the archive's earlier claim of
    "two Barry sisters marrying Booysens" is <b>withdrawn</b>.
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1947 · George's death notice corrects his own death date</h2>
  <p class="dek">Signed by <strong>G. Booyzen</strong> at Johannesburg on 21 February 1948 under the
    Administration of Estates Act 1913.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1947-george.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Handwritten 1947 death notice for George Downing Mountjoy Booyzen, Johannesburg." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Death Notice of <strong>George Downing Mountjoy Booyzen</strong>, died
      11 December 1947 at Forest Hill, Johannesburg.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:24px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1947-death-date.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Detail of the 1947 death notice showing the day of decease as 11th December 1947." />
    <figcaption class="cap">"The day of the decease: On <strong>11th December 1947</strong>" — died at
      <strong>85 Rheeden St, Forest Hill</strong>, Johannesburg.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:22px">
    <strong>He died on 11 December 1947 — not 21 February 1947.</strong><br />
    21 February is the date the notice was <em>signed</em>, and it was signed in <strong>1948</strong>.
    A transcriber conflated the two and lost ten months.
  </blockquote>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">This also dissolves an anomaly this archive had itself flagged: a burial at
    West Park recorded on <b>14 December 1947</b> looked like a ten-month delay. It was not.
    <b>He died on the 11th and was buried on the 14th — three days later</b>, exactly as one would expect.</p>
  <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1947-occupation.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Detail showing age 46 years 6 months and occupation S.A.R. and H." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Age <strong>46 years 6 months</strong>; occupation
      <strong>S.A.R. &amp; H.</strong> — the South African Railways and Harbours; residence
      <strong>85 Rheeden Street, Forest Hill</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1947-spouses.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Detail showing surviving spouse Gertruida Alexandra born Kinnear and pre-deceased spouse Anna Johanna Maria born Pretorius." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Surviving spouse <strong>Gertruida Alexandra Booyzen, born Kinnear</strong>, in
      community of property; pre-deceased spouse <strong>Anna Johanna Maria Booyzen (Pretorius), died 26
      February 1944</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1947-children.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Detail listing the children of the prior marriage with birth dates for the minors." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Children of the prior marriage. The two eldest are entered as majors; the four
      minors carry birth dates — <strong>25/3/29, 12/10/30, 22/9/32, 10/1/34</strong> — which
      <strong>match the tree exactly</strong>, independently confirming Catherine Mary Sophia's birth on
      25 March 1929. The second daughter is given a married name the tree does not have:
      <strong>Kelly</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>He worked for the railways, not the mines.</b> The testimony's "the boys all got jobs on the mines"
    belongs to an earlier generation. By the 1940s this branch was <b>S.A.R. &amp; H.</b>
    <a href={u("/johannesburg/")}>Johannesburg &rarr;</a><br /><br />
    The notice also records <b>"no children of last marriage"</b> — consistent with both infant Gertrude
    Elizabeths having died before him.
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="koppie" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>The photographs</h2>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0;max-width:560px">
    <img src={u("/docs/photo-george-isaak-transkei.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Two men shaking hands in open grassland, one young in a 1930s suit, one elderly with a white moustache and walking stick." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>"George Booyzen and Isaak Pretorius In Transkei"</strong> — the tree's
      own caption. Left: <strong>George Downing Mountjoy Booyzen</strong>, in his thirties, in the wide
      trousers of the 1930s. Right: <strong>Isaak Pretorius</strong>, a relative of George's wife, walking
      stick planted in the grass behind him. <strong>This appears to be the only photograph of George in
      the tree</strong> — his profile still carries MyHeritage's prompt to add a personal photo.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Three uncaptioned photographs</h3>
  <p class="dek">Attached to George's profile with no caption, no date and no tagging. The identifications
    below are <span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> — reasoning is given so it can be overturned.</p>
  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:20px;align-items:start">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/photo-untitled-43.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="A young woman in a patterned dress, an elderly woman seated in black, and a young man outside a house." />
      <figcaption class="cap">An <strong>elderly woman seated in full black</strong> between two young
        people, outside a plastered house. <em>Hypothesis:</em> the seated woman is
        <strong>Kathleen Barry (1859–1940)</strong> in widow's black — the dress and print date the image
        to the 1930s, when she was in her seventies and living in Johannesburg. <strong>Unverified.</strong></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/photo-untitled-45.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="A young man in a dark uniform tunic and peaked cap standing in a yard by a bare tree." />
      <figcaption class="cap">A young man in a <strong>dark uniform tunic and peaked cap</strong>.
        <em>Hypothesis:</em> <strong>George Downing Mountjoy Booyzen in South African Railways and
        Harbours uniform</strong> — the employer his 1947 death notice names.
        <strong>Unverified.</strong></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/photo-untitled-46.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="A man in a light suit and fedora leaning on a late-1920s sedan with a small boy beside him." />
      <figcaption class="cap">A man in a light suit and fedora on a late-1920s sedan, registration
        <strong class="m">TAS 486</strong>, a small boy beside him. The registration is a hard,
        researchable detail. <strong>Unidentified.</strong></figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1819 · A sworn falsehood in Shoreditch</h2>
  <p class="dek">A marriage allegation of the <strong>London Diocese</strong>, 20 January 1819 — the sworn
    statement required before a marriage licence could be issued. Fee, 2/6.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-marriage-allegation-1819.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Handwritten 1819 marriage allegation from the London Diocese for George Kolbe and Margaret Downing." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Marriage allegation of George Kolbe and Margaret Downing, London Diocese,
      20 January 1819, signed <strong>G. Kolbe</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:22px">
    "Appeared personally <strong>George Kolbe</strong> and made Oath, that he is of the Parish of
    <strong>Saint Leonard Shoreditch</strong> in the County of Middlesex, <strong>a Bachelor of the age of
    Twenty-one Years and upwards</strong>, and intendeth to marry with <strong>Margaret Downing</strong> of
    the same Parish, <strong>a Spinster of the age of Twenty-one Years and upwards</strong>…"
  </blockquote>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-allegation-age-detail.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Magnified detail of the oath reading a Bachelor of the age of Twenty-one Years and upwards." />
    <figcaption class="cap">"…a Bachelor of the age of <strong>Twenty-one Years and upwards</strong>."</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>Both ages are false, and the document is an oath.</b><br />
    George Augustus Kolbe was born <b>7 December 1802</b>. On 20 January 1819 he was
    <b>sixteen years and six weeks old</b>. Margaret Downing was born <b>23 February 1803</b>; she was
    <b>fifteen years and eleven months</b>.<br /><br />
    Under <b>Hardwicke's Marriage Act of 1753</b> a minor under twenty-one could not marry by licence
    without a parent's consent. Swearing to be "of the age of twenty-one years and upwards" was the
    ordinary way round it — and it was perjury. The four weeks' residence in the parish was very often
    nominal too.
  </div>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:22px">
    This archive's 1820 Settler <strong>married at sixteen, by lying under oath about his age</strong>,
    and sailed for Africa eleven months later. <a href={u("/kolbe/")}>The Kolbes &rarr;</a>
  </blockquote>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1844 · The estate the archive had a reference for but had never read</h2>
  <p class="dek">The Cape Archives reference <span class="m">KAB MOOC 6/9/35: 7698 (1845)</span> was
    already noted here as "not yet ordered". It was in the family tree all along — the estate number
    <strong>7698</strong> is written at the head of the page.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0;max-width:820px">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-death-notice-1844-zoom.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1845 death notice for George Augustus Kolbe under Ordinance 104, estate number 7698." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Death Notice of <strong>George Augustus Kolbe</strong>, made under Section 9,
      Ordinance 104, at Colesberg in January 1845 and signed <strong>M. Kolbe</strong> — Margaret, his
      widow.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-death-notice-1844.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="The complete 1845 death notice page for George Augustus Kolbe." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The whole sheet, for anyone who wants to check the reading against it.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:14rem">Birth-place</td><td><strong>London</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Names of the parents</td><td><em>left blank</em></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Age</td><td>42 years</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Condition in life</strong></td><td><strong>Agriculturist</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Day of the decease</td><td>1 December 1844</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Where died</td><td><strong>At Knapdaar, in the field cornetcy of Rhenosterberg, District of Colesberg</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Property</td><td><strong>"about £1000 Sterling"</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">He died a farmer</h3>
      <p>The tree gives his occupations as <b>Reverend Missionary of the London Missionary Society</b>,
        <b>Chemist</b> and <b>Tailor</b>. The official record at the end of his life calls him simply an
        <b>agriculturist</b>, and he died on his own farm. Those are not contradictions — they are a life
        in sequence — but the archive should stop leading with "missionary".</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Ten children, and one who is missing</h3>
      <p>The notice names ten. <b>Keetor Peter Benjamin Kolbe</b>, whom the tree gives a birth in 1840, is
        <b>not among them</b> — nor among the ten in his mother's 1867 notice. He died young, or he does
        not belong in this family. <b>Julia Kolbe</b>, whom MyHeritage flags as born two years after her
        father's death, is likewise absent, as she must be.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1867 · Margaret's death notice solves a problem this archive had raised</h2>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/downing-death-notice-1867.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1867 death notice for Margaret Downing, widow of George Augustus Kolbe, Burghersdorp." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Death Notice of <strong>Margaret Downing, widow of the late George Augustus
      Kolbe</strong>, estate 4094, filed 30 January 1868 and signed by her son-in-law
      <strong>G. D. O. Werdmuller</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:14rem">Birthplace</td><td>London</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Names of the parents</strong></td><td><strong>"Both dead"</strong> — no names given</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Age</strong></td><td><strong>64 years 9 months &amp; 2 days</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Day of the decease</td><td>25 November 1867</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Where died</td><td>at her own residence in <strong>Burghersdorp</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Property</td><td><strong>a dwelling house in Burghersdorp and a farm in the division of Albert</strong>; household furniture, live stock, sheep and cattle</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:18px"><b>"64 years 9 months &amp; 2 days"</b> on 25 November 1867 computes to a
    birth of <b>23 February 1803</b> — exactly what the tree says and exactly what the gravestone says.
    A three-way agreement, which in this archive is rare enough to be worth stating.</p>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <b>And it settles the Enslin problem.</b> This archive flagged an impossibility: Margaretha Kolbe
    appearing to marry <b>George Frederick Enslin</b> in 1850 while married to John Augustus Barry, and
    bearing Enslin and Barry children <b>three and a half months apart</b> in 1851. The archive's own
    guess was that the Enslin children belonged to her <b>sister Mary Elizabeth</b>.<br /><br />
    The notice lists, among Margaret Downing's children:
    <b>"Mary Elizabeth (dead) who was married to George F. Enslin — left issue of 6 minor children."</b>
    <span class="chip doc">Resolved</span> The Enslin marriage and its children are
    <b>Mary Elizabeth's, not Margaretha's</b>. The tree has attached one sister's family to the other.
    <a href={u("/disputed/")}>Disputed &rarr;</a>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px">One thing it does <em>not</em> give: her parents, entered only as
    <strong>"Both dead"</strong>. The tree's <strong>John Paul Downing</strong> and
    <strong>Mary P King</strong> are therefore not corroborated here.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The grave at Burgersdorp</h2>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px;align-items:start">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-grave-monument.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="A tall urn-topped granite monument inside a cast-iron railed enclosure in a grassy cemetery." />
      <figcaption class="cap">The monument, inside its cast-iron enclosure, Burgersdorp Old Cemetery.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-grave-inscription.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Close view of the cut inscription on the Kolbe monument." />
      <figcaption class="cap">The inscription.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:24px">
    SACRED / TO THE MEMORY OF / <strong>GEORGE AUGUSTUS KOLBE</strong>, / BORN IN LONDON 7 DECEMBER 1802, /
    AND DIED AT HIS FARM <strong>WURTHEMBURG</strong>, / DIVISION OF ALBERT, 1 DECEMBER 1844. /
    ALSO HIS RELICT / <strong>MARGARET DOWNING</strong>, / BORN IN LONDON 23 FEBRUARY 1803, /
    AND DIED AT BURGHERSDORP / 25 NOVEMBER 1867.
  </blockquote>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p>The farm is spelled <b>WURTHEMBURG</b> in cut stone, with a <em>th</em> — closer to
      <em>Württemberg</em> than the tree's "Wurtemburg". And the stone says <b>"HIS FARM"</b>: he owned it,
      which Margaret's 1867 notice confirms with "a farm in the division of Albert".</p>
    <p>She is commemorated under her <b>maiden name</b>, as <em>his relict</em> — the old word for widow.
      She outlived him by twenty-three years and was buried in his grave, as the Dutch note on the
      MyHeritage record says: <em>"Zijn vrouw is later bijgezet in het graf."</em></p>
    <p class="cap">The 1845 notice says <em>district of Colesberg</em>; the stone says
      <em>division of Albert</em>. Both are right — Albert, with its seat at Burgersdorp, was carved out
      of the older district afterwards. The stone is the later text.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="awl" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>The portraits — and one that cannot be who it says</h2>
  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:20px;align-items:start">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-couple-misattributed.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Carte-de-visite of a seated bearded man and a standing woman in a bustle dress." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Captioned "Margaret Elizabeth Georgina Downing and George Kolbe"
        — and it is not them.</strong> See below.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/downing-margaret-portrait.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Heavily retouched head-and-shoulders portrait of a woman with centre-parted hair and a white collar." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Margaret Downing.</strong> Heavily hand-retouched, but the centre
        parting and plain white collar fit the <strong>1850s–60s</strong>, which fits a woman who died in
        1867. <span class="chip inf">Plausible</span></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-george-portrait.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Vignetted portrait of a young man with dark curling hair and a high collar." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>George Augustus Kolbe.</strong> He died in <strong>1844</strong>,
        when photography was five years old and vanishingly rare in the Cape interior. If it is him, it is
        a copy of a <strong>painted miniature</strong>. <span class="chip inf">Unverified</span></figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>The couple photograph is misattributed.</b> It is a <b>carte-de-visite</b> — albumen print on a card
    mount with rounded corners. The woman wears a cuirass bodice with a long buttoned front, a ruched
    overskirt and a <b>bustle</b>; the man has a full beard and a watch chain. That costume is
    <b>c. 1875–1885</b>.<br /><br />
    <b>George Augustus Kolbe died in 1844. Margaret Downing died in 1867.</b> Neither can appear in a
    photograph taken in the late 1870s. A pencilled inscription on the mount does not appear to read
    "Kolbe".<br /><br />
    <span class="chip inf">Hypothesis</span> It is their son <b>George Augustus Kolbe (b. 1836)</b> — who
    would be about forty in 1876, matching the sitter — with his wife. The repeated forename is the
    obvious route by which a caption goes astray. <b>Unproved.</b>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:26px;align-items:start">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/st-annes-soho.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="St Anne's church, Soho, Westminster." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>St Anne's, Soho</strong> — where he was christened, 2 February 1803.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/st-leonards-shoreditch.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="St Leonard's church, Shoreditch." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>St Leonard's, Shoreditch</strong> — where he swore the oath and
        married, 20 January 1819.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1842 · The will of a Bond Street tailor</h2>
  <p class="dek">A <strong>Prerogative Court of Canterbury</strong> register copy, from the Public Record
    Office. Signed <strong>25 May 1842</strong>; proved at London <strong>23 February 1846</strong>.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-will-1.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Blackletter heading reading This is the last Will and Testament, with a marginal note naming John Gottlob Kolbe." />
    <figcaption class="cap">"<strong>This is the last Will and Testament</strong> of me
      <strong>John Gottlob Kolbe of Conduit Street, Bond Street</strong>, in the County…"</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-will-2.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Dense register-hand page of the will naming trustees and the partnership." />
    <figcaption class="cap">"…of Middlesex <strong>Tailor</strong> I give devise and bequeath all my
      Estate both Real and Personal … unto <strong>Timothy Duke</strong> of Lombard Street, Gentleman,
      <strong>Christian William Kent</strong> of Bentinck Street, Poland Street, Tailor, and
      <strong>my daughter Marian Kolbe</strong> … <strong>upon Trust</strong>."</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-will-4.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Closing page of the will carrying the probate act clause." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The closing page and the probate act — proved at London,
      <strong>23 February 1846</strong>, three months after his burial at the Savoy.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:26px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">He had a partner and a going concern</h3>
      <p>"…an agreement with my present partner <b>Mr Richard Davies</b> that the Executors … should
        permit the capital to be invested by me in our <b>partnership business of Tailors</b> at the time
        of my death <b>to remain in such business</b>…" — for a term of <b>fourteen years</b> after he
        died.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">This is not a jobbing tailor. It is a firm with capital, a partner, and
        a fourteen-year plan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">His daughter was an executrix</h3>
      <p>He appointed <b>Timothy Duke, Christian William Kent and Marian Kolbe</b> — his own daughter —
        <b>"Executors and Executrix"</b>. At probate she is sworn as
        <b>"Marian Kolbe Spinster the daughter."</b></p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">A woman administering a substantial estate in her own right in 1846 is
        worth pausing on.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>And a provision with a sting in it.</b> For those fourteen years the profits were to be divided
    among such of his daughters <b>"as shall continue sole and unmarried"</b> — and a daughter's share
    <b>ceased entirely if she married</b>, and was added to her unmarried sisters'. A maintenance fund for
    spinster daughters, and an incentive not to leave.
  </div>

  <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-will-3.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Page of the will setting out the residue clause and division among children." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The residue: "…<strong>unto for and amongst all and every my child and
      children who may be living at the time of my decease in equal shares</strong> … and
      <strong>the issue of such of my children respectively as may be dead</strong>, such issue however to
      take only the share to which their parent would have been entitled."</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <blockquote style="margin-top:24px">
    <strong>George Augustus Kolbe died on 1 December 1844. His father died on 18 November 1845 — a year
    later.</strong> Under that clause, George's share passed <strong>to his own children</strong> — to
    Margaret Downing's family at Burgersdorp, and so to <strong>Margaretha Kolbe, who married John
    Augustus Barry</strong>.
  </blockquote>
  <p style="margin-top:14px"><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> <b>There is, on paper, a line of money
    from a Bond Street tailor's estate to the Barkly East family.</b> A later clause names
    <b>"my said children or grandchildren"</b> explicitly. Whether a shilling of it ever reached the Cape
    is unknown and untested — it would show in the PCC estate papers and in the Cape estate files.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a></p>

  <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-will-5.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Final page of the will with signature, witnesses and the probate act." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Signed <strong>"J. G. Kolbe"</strong>, 25 May 1842, "written in one sheet of
      paper". Witnessed by <strong>Wm Gribble Jr</strong> and another, described as
      <strong>"Clerks to Mr Duke, Lombard Street"</strong> — the executor's own clerks.
      <strong>PROVED at London the 23rd February 1846</strong> before Herbert Thomas Pratt, Doctor of Laws
      and Surrogate.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1845 · Two documents correct the tree twice over</h2>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-death-certificate-1845.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1845 General Register Office death certificate for John Gottlob Kolbe, May Fair district." />
    <figcaption class="cap">General Register Office, entry <strong>206</strong>, district of
      <strong>May Fair</strong> within <strong>St George Hanover Square</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:13rem">When died</td><td><strong>Eighteenth of November 1845, at 34 Conduit Street</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Age</td><td>80 years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rank or profession</td><td><strong>Tailor</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Cause of death</strong></td><td><strong>"Decay of Nature"</strong> (certified) — the Victorian phrase for old age</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Informant</td><td><strong>S. Leather, in attendance, 34 Conduit Street</strong> — an attendant, not family</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Correction 1 — he did not die in Lamb's Conduit Street</h3>
  <p class="dek">The tree records <em>"34 Lamb's Conduit Street"</em>. Three independent documents say
    otherwise.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:16rem">Document</th><th>Address as given</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Death certificate, 1845</td><td><strong>34 Conduit Street</strong>, registered at <strong>May Fair</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Burial register, 1845</td><td><strong>"Conduit Street, Regent Street"</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>His own will, 1842</td><td><strong>"of Conduit Street, Bond Street, in the County of Middlesex, Tailor"</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:18px"><b>Conduit Street</b> runs between Regent Street and New Bond Street, in
    <b>Mayfair</b>. <b>Lamb's Conduit Street</b> is in <b>Bloomsbury</b>, over a mile away and in the
    <b>Holborn</b> registration district — a death there could not have been registered at May Fair.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px;align-items:start">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The consequence</h3>
      <p>The photograph in the tree captioned <b>"34 Lamb's Conduit Street"</b> is
        <b>a picture of the wrong building, in the wrong part of London</b>. Someone went and
        photographed a real 34 — just not his.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px"><b>He lived and died above his own shop, in Mayfair.</b></p>
    </div>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-wrong-building.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Modern photograph of a shopfront reading Langham Gallery, with doors numbered 34 and 34A." />
      <figcaption class="cap">34 Lamb's Conduit Street, Bloomsbury — <strong>not</strong> the Kolbe
        address.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:36px">Correction 2 — he was not buried in Warwickshire</h3>
  <p class="dek">The tree records <em>"St John the Baptist, Leamington, Warwickshire."</em></p>
  <figure style="margin:18px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-burial-heading.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Register heading reading Burials in the Parish of St John Baptist in the German Savoy Church in the County of Middlesex." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The register's own heading: <strong>"BURIALS in the Parish of St John Baptist,
      in the GERMAN SAVOY CHURCH, in the County of Middlesex."</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-burial-detail.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Burial register entry number 55 for Mr John Gottlob Kolbe of Conduit Street, Regent Street." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Entry <strong>No. 55</strong>: <strong>Mr John Gottlob Kolbe</strong>, abode
      <strong>Conduit Street, Regent Street</strong>; died 18 Nov, <strong>buried 26 November 1845</strong>,
      aged <strong>81</strong>; ceremony by <strong>Revd Dr Steinkopff</strong>, <strong>Grave 7,
      Rows 1–2</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-burial-register-1845.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="The full 1845 burial register page of the German Savoy Church showing entry 55." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The full page. Entry 55 sits in a column of German names — which is rather the
      point of the correction.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    Somebody matched the dedication <em>St John the Baptist</em> to the parish church at
    <b>Leamington Spa</b>. It is the <b>German Lutheran Chapel Royal of the Savoy</b>, in the
    <b>Strand</b> — a hundred miles away, and the <b>same congregation whose children's burial ground
    already holds five of his children and his wife Mary Larman</b>.<br /><br />
    <b>The correction makes the family more coherent, not less.</b> They are German Lutherans of the Savoy
    from first to last. The officiant, <b>Revd Dr Steinkopff</b>, is Karl Friedrich Adolf Steinkopf
    (1773–1859), minister of the German Savoy Church and a founder of the British and Foreign Bible
    Society. <a href={u("/kolbe/")}>The Kolbes &rarr;</a>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px">The neighbouring entries in the register are almost all German —
    Wüst, Nitsch, Wuleko, Stürcke, Kollmann, Schnitzlein, Grubusch, Wettig. A German congregation in
    London, burying its own.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1901 · A wedding in a rebel's house, under martial law</h2>
  <p class="dek">The archive knew from a note in the tree that this marriage was held "at the home of
    D Schoeman". The original register gives the man a <strong>signature</strong>.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/marriage-1901-caerlaverock.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Dutch-language duplicate marriage register for 1901, Caerlaverock, Barkly East district." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Marriage of <strong>Petrus Jacobus Pretorius</strong> and
      <strong>Magrita Wilhelmina Elizabeth Barry</strong>, 8 July 1901. Book 4584, entry 5.
      The card in the frame is the South African Department of Home Affairs' damage notice.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Groom</th><th>Bride</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:12rem">Age</td><td class="m">22</td><td class="m">21</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Occupation</strong></td><td><strong>Veeboer</strong> — stock farmer</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Residence</strong></td><td><strong>“Arundale”</strong> (reading uncertain), Barkly Oost</td><td><strong>Caerlaverock</strong>, Barkly Oost</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:22px">
    "Getrouwd in <strong>woonhuis D. Schoeman</strong> te <strong>Caerlaverock</strong> voormeld, na
    huwelijksgeboden, door mij: <strong>J. G. van der Walt, Pred. Geref. Kerk</strong>."
  </blockquote>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0;max-width:620px">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1901-witnesses.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Two witness signatures reading J. J. Parsons and D. W. Schoeman." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The witnesses: <strong>J. J. Parsons</strong> and
      <strong>D. W. Schoeman</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>The initials are new, and they matter.</b> In <b>March 1900</b>, about <b>400 Cape Rebels under
    Commandant D. Schoeman</b> held <b>Labuschagne's Nek</b>, north of Dordrecht — the next district over.
    Sixteen months later a <b>D. W. Schoeman of Caerlaverock, Barkly East</b> signs this register as a
    witness, in his own house, under martial law.<br /><br />
    <span class="chip inf">Hypothesis</span> That is now a <b>specific, searchable identity</b> to set
    against the rebel commandant lists — not the bare initial the archive had before.
    <b>It is still not proof.</b> <a href={u("/rebels/")}>The rebels &rarr;</a>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Married in a house, not a church</h3>
      <p>In the middle of the war, in a private dwelling. And in the <b>Gereformeerde Kerk</b> — the
        "Dopper" church — not the <em>Nederduitse</em> Gereformeerde Kerk in which Willem Hermanus Booysen
        had married in 1879. A different, more conservative denomination.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And two more British farm names</h3>
      <p>He was of a farm whose name reads as <b>“Arundale”</b> — the word is genuinely ambiguous at full magnification — and she of <b>Caerlaverock</b>. Set beside John Barry's
        <b>Manorowen</b>, the farms of this district carry <b>Scottish and Welsh</b> place-names —
        Dumfriesshire, Scotland, Pembrokeshire.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px"><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> These are British settlers' farms,
        and the families on them were siding with the republics. That complicates the picture in a way
        worth keeping.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>c. 1909 · Anna Pretorius, aged about four</h2>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px;align-items:start">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/photo-pretorius-family-c1909.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Studio portrait of a man, a woman, a boy of about seven and a small girl in a white dress." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Captioned in the tree
        <strong>"anna johanna maria pretorius with brother and parents"</strong>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p><b>Left:</b> a boy of about seven in a pleated tunic and wide collar — <b>Dirk Jacobus
        Pretorius</b> (b. 8 June 1902).<br />
        <b>Centre:</b> <b>Petrus Jacobus Pretorius</b> (1878–1941), the stock farmer.<br />
        <b>Right:</b> <b>Margarietha Wilhelmina Elizabeth Barry</b> (1880–1961), in a high-necked blouse
        with her hair dressed in a pompadour.<br />
        <b>Front:</b> a small girl of about four — <b>Anna Johanna Maria Pretorius</b> (b. 3 Oct 1905).</p>
      <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> The hairstyle and blouse date the picture to about
        <b>1908–1912</b>; the children's ages narrow it to roughly <b>1909</b>.</p>
      <p>This is <b>David Defranceski's great-grandmother as a small child</b> — the woman who would die at
        thirty-eight of an ectopic pregnancy at Forest Hill, leaving Catherine orphaned at eighteen. It
        appears to be the only photograph of her parents together, eight years after they married in
        D. W. Schoeman's house.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="gable" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>1940 · Kathleen Barry's death certificate, and one unexpected word</h2>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0;max-width:860px">
    <img src={u("/docs/kathleen-barry-death-1940.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1940 South African death form for Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen, born Barry." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><em>Form of Information of a Death / Aangifte van 'n Sterfgeval</em>,
      Act No. 17 of 1923. Entry <strong>5970</strong>, district Johannesburg.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:15rem">Name</td><td><strong>Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen</strong> — "(Born Barry.)"</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Age · birthplace · condition</td><td>82 · <strong>Cape Province</strong> · <strong>Widow</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Occupation</td><td><strong>Home Duties</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Pensioner</strong></td><td><strong>MILITARY PENSIONER</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Place of death</strong></td><td><strong>No. 1, Fifteenth Avenue, Mayfair, Johannesburg</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Intended place of burial</strong></td><td><strong>Brixton Cemetery, Johannesburg</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Causes of death</td><td><strong>Carcinoma of the Uterus</strong>; Senility — <strong>one year one month</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Informant</td><td><strong>M. v. Rensburg</strong>, "Causing Burial", of 20 Commercial Road, <strong>Fordsburg</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  <figure style="margin:24px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1940-address-burial.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Detail of the 1940 death form showing the Mayfair address and Brixton Cemetery." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The two lines that place her at the end: <strong>No. 1, Fifteenth Avenue,
      Mayfair</strong>, and <strong>Brixton Cemetery</strong> — a mile and a half apart, and both a long
      way from Barkly East.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  </div>
  <figure style="margin:24px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1940-military-pensioner.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Detail of the death form reading Occupation Home Duties, Pensioner or dependent of pensioner: Military Pensioner." />
    <figcaption class="cap">"8. Occupation — <strong>Home Duties</strong>. 9. Pensioner or dependent of
      pensioner — <strong>Military Pensioner</strong>."</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>An eighty-one-year-old widow in Mayfair, drawing a military pension in 1940.</b> Her husband died in
    1905 of a stomach ulcer, so it is not his service.<br /><br />
    <span class="chip inf">Untested</span> The likeliest route is a <b>son's First World War service</b> —
    several of her sons were of age — with a dependant's pension attaching to her. It is a hard,
    searchable fact and <b>an entirely new thread in this archive</b>.<br /><br />
    It also carries an irony worth leaving in rather than smoothing away: <b>a family that remembers
    fighting the British ends with its matriarch on a military pension in a British dominion.</b>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:20px">Two smaller gains. The archive had only "Mayfair" for her death; it now has
    <b>No. 1, Fifteenth Avenue</b>. And the tree records <b>no burial place for her at all</b> — she is at
    <b>Brixton Cemetery</b>. <a href={u("/johannesburg/")}>Johannesburg &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1912&ndash;1915 · "Father: Kolbe, thinks James was christian name"</h2>
  <p class="dek">Of everything read in these four days, this is the line that most justifies the archive
    existing.</p>
  <p><span class="cap">She died on <b>2 August 1912</b>; her son completed the notice on
    <b>13 May 1915</b>, nearly three years later. That gap matters — and it is the same gap as the
    <b>1840 Montjoy notice</b>, written thirteen years after that death, which answers the same question
    with <em>"Not Known."</em> In both cases the delay is part of the reason the answer is empty.</span></p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0;max-width:900px">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-barry-death-notice-1912.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1915 death notice for Margaret Elizabeth Barry, born Kolbe, of Manorowen, Barkly East." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Death Notice for <strong>Margaret Elizabeth Barry, born Kolbe</strong>,
      who died 2 August 1912 in the house of her son at <strong>Manorowen</strong>. Signed
      <strong>R. P. Barry</strong>, <em>"Son in whose house deceased died"</em>, Barkly East, 13 May 1915.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:24px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1912-father-unknown.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Detail of the form: Father — Kolbe, thinks James was christian name. Mother — Unknown." />
    <figcaption class="cap">"3. Names and addresses of the Parents of the Deceased —
      <strong>Father: Kolbe, thinks James was christian name. Mother: Unknown.</strong>"</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:24px">
    <p>Her son, filling in his own mother's death notice, <b>did not know his grandfather's name</b>.
      He guessed <em>James</em>. Of his grandmother he knew nothing at all.</p>
    <p>His grandfather was <b>George Augustus Kolbe</b> — who married at sixteen on a sworn lie in
      Shoreditch, sailed on the <em>Nautilus</em> in 1819, preached across the Orange River, named a Karoo
      farm after his father's Württemberg, and lies under a cut-stone monument at <b>Burgersdorp</b> with
      his name, his London birth and his farm carved into it. His grandmother was <b>Margaret Downing</b>
      of London, buried in the same grave.</p>
    <blockquote>
      <strong>The family forgot them inside seventy years, while the headstone stood forty miles up the
      road.</strong>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Two generations after the <em>Nautilus</em>, the notice describes her as
      <b>"Afrikander of European extraction"</b> and guesses her birthplace: <em>"Believe Colesberg
      District."</em> <a href={u("/kolbe/")}>The Kolbes &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>And she died with nothing.</b> Movable property: <em>No</em>. Immovable: <em>No</em>. Estate:
    <em>Nil</em>. No will. She died in her son's house at Manorowen.<br /><br />
    Set that beside her mother <b>Margaret Downing</b>, who in 1867 left a house in Burgersdorp and a farm
    in the division of Albert; beside her grandfather <b>Johan Gottlieb Kolbe</b>, whose 1842 will
    disposed of a partnership in a Bond Street tailoring firm; and beside her husband <b>John Barry</b>,
    whose estate in 1903 exceeded £300. <b>The Kolbe money did not last three generations.</b>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px">This notice also supplies a <strong>third</strong> date for John
    Barry's death — <strong>"August 1904"</strong> — against the <strong>3 August 1903</strong> of his own
    1915 death notice and the <strong>25 August 1903</strong> in the tree. Both notices were made in 1915,
    by two different sons, and they contradict each other.
    <a href={u("/disputed/")}>Disputed &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1923 · The Schoemans get a name</h2>
  <p class="dek">The archive has been chasing a "D. Schoeman" of Caerlaverock since the beginning. This
    document does not name him — but it plants his family.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0;max-width:760px">
    <img src={u("/docs/schoeman-barry-sterfkennis-1923.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1923 Sterfkennis for Anna Johanna Maria Barry, born Schoeman, of Evaton." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><em>Unie van Zuid-Afrika — Sterfkennis</em> for
      <strong>Anna Johanna Maria Barry, née Schoeman</strong>, died 24 February 1923 at
      <strong>Evaton, district Potchefstroom</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:13rem">Geboorteplaats</td><td><strong>Queenstown</strong>, Cape Province</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Vader</strong></td><td><strong>Ruben Schoeman</strong>, <strong>Barkly Oost</strong>, K. Prov.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Moeder</strong></td><td><strong>Magdalena Johanna Botha</strong>, Barkly Oost, K. Prov.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Echtgenoot</td><td><strong>Hendrik Nicolaas Barry</strong>, in community of property</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Plaats van laatste huwelik</td><td><strong>Barkly Oost</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Signed</td><td><strong>D. Booyzen</strong>, <em>"dochter van overledene"</em>, present at the death</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Ruben Schoeman of Barkly Oost</h3>
      <p>Anna Johanna Maria Schoemann — who married Hendrik Nicholaas Barry in 1877, and whose grandchild
        Anna married George Booyzen in 1924 — was the daughter of <b>Ruben Schoeman</b> and
        <b>Magdalena Johanna Botha</b>, <b>both of Barkly Oost</b>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">The naming confirms it: her fourth son is
        <b>Ruben Jacobus Josuah Barry</b>, after his maternal grandfather.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px"><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> This does not identify
        <b>D. W. Schoeman</b> of Caerlaverock — but it places a named Schoeman family in exactly the right
        district and generation to hold him. <a href={u("/rebels/")}>The rebels &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And a Booyzen signs it</h3>
      <p>The informant is <b>"D. Booyzen"</b> — <b>Dorothea Regina Barry</b>, who married
        <b>Henry James Booyzen</b> at Elliot in 1912. She signs as a Booyzen, daughter of the deceased,
        present at the death.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">A third document, independent of the other two, confirming the third
        Barry–Booyzen marriage. <a href={u("/cousins/")}>The cousin marriage &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1803 · The London end of the line, closed</h2>
  <p class="dek">A baptism register index from <strong>St Anne's, Soho</strong> — birth date at the left,
    day of baptism at the right. A note on the same page records that the register holds
    <em>"410 Baptisms for the Year 1802."</em></p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1803-baptism-entry.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Register entry under the heading February 1803 reading 1802 Dec 7 George Augustus Kolbe of John Gottlob and Mary, 2." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Under <strong>FEBRUARY 1803</strong>, the second entry:
      <strong>"1802 Dec: 7 — George Augustus Kolbe of John Gottlob &amp; Mary — 2"</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:24px">
    <p>Born <b>7 December 1802</b>. Baptised <b>2 February 1803</b>. Son of <b>John Gottlob and Mary</b>.</p>
    <blockquote>
      An English parish register and a Karoo gravestone, written ninety years and six thousand miles
      apart, give the same birth date to the day: <strong>7 December 1802</strong>.
    </blockquote>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> And the parentage is no longer taken on the tree's word.
      The link from <b>George Augustus Kolbe</b>, the 1820 Settler, to <b>John Gottlob Kolbe</b>, tailor
      of Conduit Street, and <b>Mary Larman</b> is <b>on the parish register</b>.
      <b>The London end of this line is closed.</b></p>
  </div>
  <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-baptism-register-1803.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Full two-page baptism register index for December 1802 to May 1803." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The full opening, December 1802 to May 1803. Westminster parish records,
      FindMyPast reference <span class="m">GBPRS_WSMTN_005109304_00342</span>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>And a small correction of emphasis.</b> The register writes the father <b>"John Gottlob"</b>. So
    does his <b>will</b> — <em>"of me John Gottlob Kolbe"</em>. So does his <b>death certificate</b>. So
    does his <b>burial register</b> — <em>"Mr John Gottlob Kolbe"</em>.<br /><br />
    <b>Four independent documents, and not one of them says "Johan Gottlieb."</b> That is the German
    original the family tree leads with. <b>"John Gottlob" is the name he actually used, and was buried
    under, in England</b> — and this archive now follows the documents.
    <a href={u("/kolbe/")}>The Kolbes &rarr;</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="anna-1944-civil">1906 · A one-digit slip, caught by two documents</h2>
  <p class="dek">A <strong>Gereformeerde Kerk</strong> baptism register. Entry 29, under
    <em>Gedoopt 11 Nov. 1906</em>:</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1906-anna-baptism.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Baptism register entry reading Anna Johanna Maria, geb. 3 Oct. 1906, Petrus Jacobus Pretorius, Margaretha Wilhelmina Elizabeth Berry, ouders." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>"29. Anna Johanna Maria, geb. 3 Oct. 1906 — Petrus Jacobus Pretorius,
      Margaretha Wilhelmina Elizabeth Berry } ouders."</strong> The birth year is written identically to
      the <em>1906</em> in the line above it.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p>The tree gives her birth as <b>3 October 1905</b> and her christening as <b>11 November 1906</b> —
      a thirteen-month gap. The register shows the two are <b>five weeks apart</b>, both in 1906. It is
      the same register the christening date came from; the birth year was mistranscribed by one digit.</p>
    <p>And her own death form settles it independently: <b>"Age — 37 years"</b> at 26 February 1944.
      A birth in October 1905 would make her 38.</p>
    <blockquote>
      <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>Anna Johanna Maria Pretorius was born
      3 October 1906.</strong> A contemporary baptism register and a death registration agree, against
      the tree.
    </blockquote>
  </div>
  <figure style="margin:26px 0 0;max-width:760px">
    <img src={u("/docs/anna-death-form-1944.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1944 death form for Anna Johanna Maria Booysen, born Pretorius." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Entry <strong>2045</strong>, Johannesburg. Note the surname typed
      <strong>Booysen</strong>, with an <em>s</em>, in 1944.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:14rem">Age</td><td><strong>37 years</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Place of death</td><td><strong>85, Rheeders Street, Forrest Hill, Johannesburg</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Intended place of burial</strong></td><td><strong>West Park Cemetery</strong> — the same ground as her husband; the tree gives only "Johannesburg"</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Causes of death</strong></td><td><strong>"Haemorrhage due to Ruptured Tubal Pregnancy (extra-uterine). Not 1st child."</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Informant</td><td>"Causing Burial", of <strong>9–11 Jack Street, Germiston</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="wagon" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>1961 · Margarietha Barry, and a pension that is only ordinary</h2>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0;max-width:700px">
    <img src={u("/docs/margarietha-death-form-1961.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Typewritten 1961 death form for Margerietha Wilhelmina Elizabeth Pretorius, born Barry." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Entry <strong>180/61</strong>, district Germiston.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:15rem"><strong>Age and date of birth</strong></td><td><strong>81 years, 16.3.1880</strong> — confirming the tree exactly</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Place of birth</td><td><strong>Barclay East, Cape Province</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Died</td><td><strong>First April 1961, General Hospital, Germiston</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Causes of death</td><td><strong>Uraemia — Hypertensive Congestive Cardiac Failure</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Residence</td><td>5 Plaza Court, Rietfontein Road, <strong>Primrose, Germiston</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Burial</td><td><strong>Primrose Cemetery</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Allowance</td><td><strong>Old Age</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Identity number</td><td><strong>"Unobtainable"</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <b>The contrast is the point.</b> Margarietha Barry, dying in 1961, drew an <b>Old Age</b> allowance —
    the ordinary thing. Her husband's mother-in-law <b>Kathleen Barry</b>, dying in 1940, was a
    <b>Military Pensioner</b>. That really is exceptional, and it still has no explanation.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px">One small human detail. The informant here, <strong>P. Gouws of
    9 Jack Street, Germiston</strong>, gives the same address as the informant on Anna's 1944 form
    seventeen years earlier. Almost certainly an <strong>undertaker's premises</strong> rather than a
    relative — a reminder that "informant" on these forms often means the funeral director, not family.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Two corrections to this archive's own work</h2>
  <p class="dek">Both were found by reading the last four documents. Both were mine, not the tree's.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The farm was not "Armadale"</h3>
      <p>This archive read the groom's 1901 residence as <b>Armadale</b> and built a small argument on it —
        that the Barkly East farms carried <b>Scottish</b> names. At full magnification on the
        <em>original</em> register the word is <b>genuinely ambiguous</b>: it reads as <b>"Arundale"</b>,
        possibly <em>Armsdale</em> or <em>Ormsdale</em>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px"><b>"Armadale" was an over-reading and is withdrawn.</b> The wider point
        survives on the two names that <em>are</em> certain — <b>Caerlaverock</b> (Dumfriesshire) and
        <b>Manorowen</b> (Pembrokeshire) — but the archive should not have leant on a word it could not
        read.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Catherine was orphaned at eighteen</h3>
      <p>This archive said in four places that Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen was
        <b>"seventeen years old and had no parents."</b> That was calculated from the <em>wrong</em> death
        date for her father — the tree's 21 February 1947, which this archive itself later corrected to
        <b>11 December 1947</b> without carrying the correction through to her age.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">Born 25 March 1929, she was <b>eighteen</b> when her father died, and
        <b>fourteen</b> when her mother did. <b>Corrected throughout.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1851 · The baptism that settles an age, and names a godfather</h2>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/baptism-register-1851.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="The full Graaff-Reinet baptism register opening for 1851, entries 2808 to 2815." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The register opening, entries <strong>2808&ndash;2815</strong>. Entry
      <strong>2809</strong> is second down on the left.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1851-baptism-entry.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Dutch Reformed baptism register entry 2809 for Willem Hermanus, born 4 May 1851." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Entry <strong>No. 2809</strong>: <em>Het Kind</em>
      <strong>Willem Hermanus</strong>, <em>Geboren</em> <strong>4 Mei 1851</strong>, <em>Vader</em>
      <strong>Petrus Jacobus Booyzen</strong>, <em>Moeder</em>
      <strong>Johanna Catharina Mountjoy</strong>, <em>Gedoopt</em>
      <strong>31 October 1851</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The age question is settled</h3>
      <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <b>Born 4 May 1851</b>, from a contemporary register,
        matching the tree exactly.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">So of the three figures the archive had, <b>the marriage register is the
        outlier</b>: it gives his age as <b>22</b> in December 1879, when he was <b>28</b>. His 1905 death
        registration says <b>55</b>, which is right within a year. <b>The 1879 entry is wrong by six
        years.</b></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And he was named for his godfather</h3>
      <p>The witnesses are <b>Willem Hermanus Pieterzen</b>, <b>Maria Catharina Magdalena Potgieter</b> and
        <b>Xaverius Hermanus Pieterzen</b>. <span class="cap">(The archive scan corrects this archive's
        earlier reading of the surname as <em>Pietersen</em> — see below; the first given name, earlier read
        as <em>Iwerus</em>, is still not certain.)</span></p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">Johanna Catharina Mountjoy's <b>half-brothers were Pieterzens</b> — the
        sons of <b>Anna Catharina Sleer</b> by her first marriage. The archive had them only as names
        in a list. Here one of them is standing at the font, <b>and the child takes his name.</b>
        <a href={u("/women/")}>The women &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:20px">The register's place column reads <em>achter Sneeuwberg</em> — the family
    was farming <b>behind the Sneeuberg</b>, not living in Cradock town.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Resolved — by a better scan, and not in the direction expected</h3>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
    <p>This section previously read the father's surname here as <b>"Booij_en"</b>, with the same
      ambiguous medial letter as the 1879 signature, and concluded: <em>"I cannot separate them from an
      image&hellip; The change is real. Its date is not established, and <b>no scan will establish
      it.</b>"</em></p>
    <p>That was read from a <b>photograph of the register on the family tree</b>. The archive has since
      read the <b>Cape Town Archives scan of the register itself</b>, at nine times magnification. It is
      not ambiguous at all:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1851-father-booyzen.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1851 register, father's name reading Petrus Jacobus Booyzen with a clear z." />
      <figcaption class="cap">The father: <strong>Petrus Jacobus Boo<em>y</em><em>z</em>en</strong></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1851-pieterzen-control.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1851 register witnesses, both reading Pieterzen with the same z letterform." />
      <figcaption class="cap">The witnesses, same hand, same ink:
        <strong>Pieter<em>z</em>en</strong>, twice</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <p><b>The letter is a z.</b> And it is a z for the Pieterzens too — a family with no name-change story
      attached to it. In Dutch orthography <b>-zen</b> is simply how the patronymic ending is written, and
      this clerk wrote it that way for everyone.</p>
    <blockquote>The scan settled the letter. Settling the letter is what took the argument away.</blockquote>
    <p><b>What this costs.</b> The z cannot have been <em>invented</em> by anyone in this family — it was
      ordinary Cape-Dutch spelling, sitting in the registers in 1851. The <b>1879 signature question is
      not merely unsettled but largely emptied</b>: a z-shaped letter in a Dutch hand is not evidence of
      intent.</p>
    <p><b>What survives, unchanged.</b> The <b>1905 death registration</b>, which turns on
      <em>two spellings of one man's name on one sheet</em> — clerk <b>Booysen</b>, son
      <b>Booyzen</b> — and not on the shape of any single letter. The tree's recorded
      <b>"Former name: Booysen"</b>. And the <b>distribution data</b>: 646 against 53,764, Gauteng against
      the Cape, gathered a century later by people who had never heard of this family.</p>
    <p><b>The change is real. The spelling it changed to was not new.</b>
      <a href={u("/name/")}>The name &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1946 and 1947 · The two Gertrudes — a contradiction resolved</h2>
  <p class="dek">This archive flagged as <em>Serious</em>: two daughters recorded born on
    <strong>11 May</strong> a year apart, both named Gertrude Elizabeth. Either two children given the
    same name and the same birthday — or one record duplicated a year out.</p>
  <p><b>Both certificates exist, and they are irreconcilably different documents.</b></p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px;align-items:start">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/gertrude-1-death-1946.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1946 death form for Gertrude Elizabeth Booyzen, aged 4 days." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Entry <strong>4256</strong>, registered 16 May 1946.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/gertrude-2-death-1947.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1947 typewritten death form for Gertrude Booyzen, aged 6 hours." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Entry <strong>3920</strong>, registered 13 Mei 1947.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Gertrude Elizabeth Booyzen</th><th>Gertrude Booyzen</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:11rem">Date of death</td><td><strong>15 May 1946</strong></td><td><strong>11 May 1947</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Age</strong></td><td><strong>4 days</strong></td><td><strong>6 hours</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Place of death</strong></td><td><strong>Queen Victoria Hospital</strong></td><td><strong>Turf Club Nursing Home</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Cause</strong></td><td>Prematurity <strong>± 30 weeks</strong></td><td>Atelectasis; prematurity <strong>7½ months</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Doctor</td><td>Dr Dennis W. P. Lavery</td><td>J. S. Porter</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Burial</strong></td><td><strong>West Park Cemetery</strong></td><td><strong>Primrose Cemetery, Germiston</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:24px">
    Two hospitals. Two cemeteries. Two doctors. Two registration entries a year apart. Four days, and six
    hours. <strong>There were two children.</strong> <span class="chip doc">Resolved</span>
  </blockquote>
  <p style="margin-top:16px"><b>And the coincidence the archive doubted is real.</b> Both were born on
    <b>11 May</b>, exactly a year apart, and both were badly premature — the first at about thirty weeks,
    the second at seven and a half months. The first lived four days. The second lived six hours. They are
    not buried together.</p>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    Set the whole sequence out, and it happened inside <b>three years and ten months</b>:<br /><br />
    <b>February 1944</b> — Anna dies at 85 Rheeder Street, aged 37. &nbsp;·&nbsp;
    <b>May 1946</b> — Gertrude Elizabeth, four days old. &nbsp;·&nbsp;
    <b>May 1947</b> — Gertrude, six hours old. &nbsp;·&nbsp;
    <b>December 1947</b> — George, aged 46.<br /><br />
    <b>Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen was eighteen.</b> Two years later she married at St Joseph's,
    Mayfair. <a href={u("/johannesburg/")}>Johannesburg &rarr;</a>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px">Three smaller corrections. The first Gertrude <strong>did not die
    at 85 Rheeden Street</strong> — the certificate gives Queen Victoria Hospital and names the street only
    as the mother's residence. The second child is registered simply <strong>"Gertrude"</strong>, not
    Gertrude Elizabeth. And the street appears as <strong>Rheeder</strong>, <strong>Rheeders</strong> and
    <strong>Rheeden</strong> across three documents.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1819 · The marriage entry — and a bride who could not sign her name</h2>
  <p class="dek">The allegation was sworn that morning. This is the register entry itself,
    <strong>St Leonard's Shoreditch, No. 858</strong>.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-marriage-entry-1819.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1819 marriage register entry for George Kolbe and Margaret Downing at St Leonard's Shoreditch." />
    <figcaption class="cap">"George Kolbe of this Parish, Bachelor, and Margaret Downing of this Parish,
      Spinster, were married in this Church <strong>by Licence</strong> … this <strong>Twentieth</strong>
      Day of <strong>January</strong> … <strong>Nineteen</strong>. By me <strong>Tho: Baxter,
      Min<sup>r</sup></strong>."</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:24px">
    <p>He signed <b>George Kolbe</b> — after striking through a first attempt. She did not sign.</p>
    <blockquote>
      <strong>Margaret Downing † her mark.</strong>
    </blockquote>
    <p><b>She could not write her own name.</b> She was fifteen.</p>
    <p>And in 1867 she died at Burgersdorp owning <b>a dwelling house and a farm in the division of
      Albert</b>, having buried her husband at forty-one and raised ten children across a mission circuit
      from Uitenhage to Bethulie. <b>It is the steepest arc in this archive</b>, and it starts with a
      cross in a Shoreditch register.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The blank that proves the lie</h3>
      <p>The line reading <b>"with Consent of ————"</b> is <b>left empty</b>. Under Hardwicke's Act a
        minor marrying by licence needed a named consenting parent. The blank is the visible consequence
        of the oath they had sworn that morning: that both were <em>"of the age of Twenty-one Years and
        upwards."</em> He was sixteen; she was fifteen.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And a witness called "John Paul"</h3>
      <p>The witnesses are <b>John Paul</b> and <b>Mary Downing</b>. The tree records the groom's father,
        Johan Gottlieb Kolbe, as also known as <b>"John Paul" Kolbe</b>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px"><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> If that is him, <b>the father
        witnessed the marriage</b> — which makes this something more complicated than an elopement: a
        parent standing by while his sixteen-year-old son swore he was of age. <b>Unproved</b>; "John
        Paul" could be an unrelated man.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="thorn" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>The portrait is a painting — as it had to be</h2>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px;align-items:start">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-george-painted-portrait.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Painted portrait of a young man with reddish-brown curling hair, high white stock and tan coat." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>George Augustus Kolbe</strong> — a painting, photographed under
        glass. The flare across the chest is the glazing.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>This archive had cautioned that any likeness of a man who died in <b>1844</b> — when photography
        was five years old and almost unknown in the Cape interior — would have to be
        <b>a painted portrait</b>, not a photograph from life.</p>
      <p><span class="chip doc">Resolved</span> <b>It is.</b> A young man with reddish-brown curling hair,
        clean-shaven, in a <b>high white stock</b> and a <b>tan coat with wide lapels</b> — dress of the
        <b>1820s and 30s</b>, which fits a man born in 1802.</p>
      <p class="cap">A third file in the same set is a left-facing profile so heavily processed —
        posterised, speckled, colour-fringed — that it is either a separate image or a modern
        "restoration" that has invented a great deal. <b>Not relied on here for likeness.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1924 · A tree nurseryman marries at Vereeniging</h2>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/marriage-1924-vereeniging.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1924 duplicate original marriage register for George Downing Montjoy Booyzen and Anna Johanna Maria Pretorius." />
    <figcaption class="cap">No. <strong>15/24</strong>, solemnized at <strong>Vereeniging</strong>,
      district Heidelberg, by <strong>H. F. Steyn</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Husband</th><th>Wife</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:11rem"><strong>Age</strong></td><td class="m">24</td><td class="m"><strong>18</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Occupation</strong></td><td><strong>Boomkweker</strong> — tree nurseryman</td><td>Geen</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Residence</strong></td><td><strong>Evaton</strong></td><td><strong>Evaton</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consent</td><td><em>Eigen</em> — his own</td><td><strong><em>Ouders</em> — her parents'</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">A third document for 1906</h3>
      <p>She is <b>18</b> at 30 December 1924 — which requires a birth in <b>late 1906</b>, not 1905. And
        her consent is recorded as <b>"Ouders"</b>, her parents' — required because she was a
        <b>minor</b>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">With the baptism register and the death form, <b>three independent
        documents now give 1906.</b> The tree is wrong three times over.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Boomkweker</h3>
      <p>Not a miner, and not yet a railwayman. In 1924 <b>George grew trees.</b> A wholly new occupation
        for him — and by 1947 he was a <b>clerk on the South African Railways</b>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">Both were living at <b>Evaton</b> — where Anna's grandmother
        <b>Anna Johanna Maria Schoeman</b> had died the year before. The whole connection had moved north
        together.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1947 · The death registration, and a garbled memory vindicated</h2>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0;max-width:680px">
    <img src={u("/docs/george-death-registration-1947.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1947 death registration for George Downing Mountjay Booyzen, entry 10,899." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Entry <strong>10,899</strong>, Johannesburg, registered 12 December 1947 —
      a <em>second</em> document, distinct from the death notice.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:14rem"><strong>Occupation</strong></td><td><strong>Clerk, South African Railways, Johannesburg</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Date of death</strong></td><td><strong>Eleventh December 1947</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Place</td><td>85 Rheeders Street, Forest Hill · buried <strong>West Park</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Causes</strong></td><td><strong>Coronary Thrombosis; Chronic Myocarditis</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Duration</td><td><strong>2 days / 11 months</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>The 11 December date is now confirmed twice</b>, by two separate documents. The tree's 21 February
    1947 is wrong twice over.<br /><br />
    <b>And the tree's strangest note turns out to be substantially right.</b> It carried
    <em>"Cause: HEART ATTACK 1946"</em>, which read like nonsense against a February 1947 death. The
    registration gives <b>chronic myocarditis of eleven months' duration</b> ending in a
    <b>coronary thrombosis of two days</b>. A year-long heart illness, ending in a heart attack.
    <b>A garbled memory, but a true one.</b>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1889 · Two grandparents at a font, and a name on the facing page</h2>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/doopregister-1889-barkly.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="The full Doop Register page for Barkly Oost, 1889." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The <strong>Doop Register, Barkly Oost</strong> page itself — the Booyzen and
      Mountjoy baptisms sit three weeks and one page apart.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1889-godparents.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1889 baptism register entry 40 for Johanna Catharina, with godparents John Barry and Margaret Wilhelmina Elizabeth Kolby." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><em>Doop Register, Barkly Oost</em>, entry <strong>40</strong>, 15 September
      1889: <strong>Johanna Catharina</strong>, geb. 20 Juni 1889; <em>Ouders</em>
      <strong>Willem Hermanus Booijsen</strong> and <strong>Catharina Maria Sophia Barry</strong>;
      <em>Getuigen</em> <strong>John Barry</strong> and <strong>Margaret Wilhelmina Elizabeth
      Kolby</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:22px">
    The godparents are the child's own <strong>maternal grandparents</strong> — <strong>John Augustus
    Barry I</strong>, the Irishman born at sea, and <strong>Margaretha Kolbe</strong>, the 1820 Settler's
    daughter, standing together at a font in Barkly East in September 1889.
  </blockquote>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">Her surname is written <b>"Kolby"</b> — a phonetic variant, twenty-six years
    before her own son would tell a magistrate he thought his grandfather's name was <em>James</em>.
    <a href={u("/kolbe/")}>The Kolbes &rarr;</a></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">And on the facing page, entirely by accident</h3>
  <figure style="margin:18px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1889-mountjoy.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1889 baptism entry for George, son of Hercules Johannes Mountjoy and Maria Sophia van Niekerk." />
    <figcaption class="cap">6 October 1889: <strong>George</strong>, geb. 21 Juli 1889; <em>Ouders</em>
      <strong>Hercules Johannes Mountjoy</strong> and Maria Sophia van Niekerk; among the witnesses
      <strong>Jakobus Jonathan Mountjoy</strong> and <strong>Maria Sophia Mountjoy</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <b>Mountjoys, alive and baptising at Barkly Oost in 1889.</b> This line descends from
    <b>Johanna Catharina Mountjoy</b> (1819–1863), and the archive had her family as little more than a
    surname and a father who died in 1827.<br /><br />
    <b>Hercules Johannes</b>, <b>Jakobus Jonathan</b> and <b>Maria Sophia Mountjoy</b> are a named,
    searchable cluster in the right district, twenty-six years after her death — and they turned up on the
    page opposite a Booyzen baptism, found by accident.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1863 &middot; The death notice that adds a generation</h2>
  <p class="dek">Found by searching a spelling nobody had tried: <strong>Montjoy</strong>.</p>
  <p>
    The National Archives index had been searched for <b>MOUNTJOY</b> — forty-two documents, none of them
    the one wanted. Searched for <b>MONTJOY</b> it returns four, and two of them are the junction between
    the two families:
  </p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin:18px 0">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>File</th><th class="m">Reference</th><th class="m">Year</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>MONTJOY, JAMES. DEATH NOTICE.</strong></td><td class="m">KAB MOOC 6/9/21, 4393</td><td class="m">1840</td></tr>
        <tr><td>MONTJOY, JOHANNA CATHARINA. WIFE OF PETRUS JACOBUS BOOYSEN. WILL.</td><td class="m">KAB MOOC 7/1/268, 87</td><td class="m">1845</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>BOOYSEN, JOHANNA CATHARINA. (NEE MONTJOY). DEATH NOTICE.</strong></td><td class="m">KAB MOOC 6/9/104, 499</td><td class="m">1863</td></tr>
        <tr><td>MONTJOY, JOHANNA CATHARINA. LIQUIDATION AND DISTRIBUTION ACCOUNT.</td><td class="m">KAB MOOC 13/1/221, 6</td><td class="m">1866</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:24px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1863-johanna-catharina.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Printed Cape death notice form of 1863 for Johanna Catharina Montjoy, completed in ink." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Filed <strong>25 July 1863</strong>, reference <strong>499</strong>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1. Name of the Deceased</td><td><b>Johanna Catharina Montjoy</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>2. Birth-place</td><td><b>Graaff Reinet</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>3. <b>Names of the Parents</b></td><td><b>James Montjoy and Anna Catharina Sleer</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>4. Age</td><td><b>44 years &amp; 19 days</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>5. Condition in life</td><td><b>farmer's wife</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>6. Married or unmarried</td><td><b>Married to Petrus Jacobus Booysen</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>7. Day of decease</td><td><b>19th May 1863</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>8. At what House</td><td><b>Zevenfontein, Brak River</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>10. Property</td><td><b>Moveable property</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1863-parents.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line three of the 1863 death notice, reading James Montjoy and Anna Catharina Sleer." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Line 3 — the generation the archive did not have from any document:
      <strong>James Montjoy and Anna Catharina Sleer</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">An age given to the day</h3>
      <p>The form asks for years. The informant wrote <b>"44 years &amp; 19 days"</b>. Counted back from
        19 May 1863, that puts her birth at <strong>30 April 1819</strong>, at Graaff-Reinet.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">The tree had "1819". The document has the date, and the informant knew
        it without being asked.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The surname, settled by convergence</h3>
      <p>The hand is genuinely ambiguous between <b>Sleer</b> and <b>Steer</b>, and this archive has
        over-read a single word before.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">It is settled from outside: the family tree carries
        <b>Schleher</b>, FamilySearch's index carries <b>Slier</b>. Three renderings from three
        independent sources, <b>all with an l and none with a t</b>. The letter is an uncrossed
        <em>l</em>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Eight children, and one of them is the direct line</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1863-children.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line nine of the 1863 death notice listing eight children, one major and seven minors." />
    <figcaption class="cap">One <em>Major</em>, seven <em>Minors</em>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Jacobus Nicholas &middot; Johanna Catharina &middot; Petrus Jacobus &middot; David Schalk &middot;
    <strong>Willem Hermanus</strong> &middot; Magdalena Johanna &middot; Jan Gerhardus Lodewicus &middot;
    Jacoba Catharina.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>The fifth child is the man the 1905 death registration is about.</strong>
      <b>Willem Hermanus Booyzen</b>, baptised at Achter Sneeuwberg on 31 October 1851, twelve years old
      and a minor when his mother died. His parentage — <b>Petrus Jacobus Booyzen and Johanna Catharina
      Montjoy</b> — is now carried by two original registers that agree, and the Mountjoy descent enters
      this family through <em>his mother</em>.</p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:20px">
    Seven of the eight are matched to baptisms in the Graaff-Reinet registers. An eighth child,
    <b>Anna Catharina</b>, baptised 21 September 1842, is <em>not</em> on the list — she died before her
    mother, and the notice is silent about her, as these forms are.
  </p>
  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">The signature</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0;max-width:560px">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1863-signature.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Signature at the foot of the 1863 death notice reading P. J. Booy, an angular letter, then en." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The widower signs.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <span class="chip inf">Not established</span> The surname ends in an angular letter with a descender —
    the form is more consistent with <b>z</b> than <b>s</b>, and the clerk who filled in the body of the
    form wrote <b>Booysen</b> at line 6. But the archive has just finished demonstrating, on the 1851
    register above, that <b>this letterform proves nothing on its own</b>. It is recorded here and
    claimed for nothing.
  </p>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>Still to be read: MOOC 6/9/21, reference 4393 — the 1840 death notice of
      James Montjoy himself.</strong> It would name <em>his</em> parents, and with them the point at
      which the name entered the Cape. It is not name-indexed; the probate collection's 846 films are
      catalogued by film number alone, so reaching it means finding the film that covers the 1840 volume.
      <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="windpump" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>1840 &middot; The oldest document in this archive — and it answers by refusing</h2>
  <p class="dek">KAB MOOC 6/9/21, reference 4393. Named on the previous page as the most valuable unread
    document here. It has now been read.</p>
  <div class="note" style="margin:20px 0">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>How it was found.</strong> It is not name-indexed, and the probate
      collection's 846 films are catalogued by film number alone. The route was arithmetic: find any
      <em>indexed</em> 1840 death in the collection, take its film — <span class="m">007844936</span>,
      "Cape Province. Probate Records 1840&ndash;1843", 2,223 images — and read the reference numbers
      pencilled in the top corner of a handful of pages. They ascend by roughly one per image. Image 700
      is reference 4726; image 330 is 4386. Interpolating and then narrowing put reference
      <strong>4393 at image 338</strong>. It was there.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:24px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1840-james-montjoy.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Cape death notice of 1840 for James Montjoy, completed in ink on a printed form." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Filed <strong>9 July 1840</strong>, reference <strong>4393</strong>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1. Name of the Deceased</td><td><b>James Montjoy</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>2. Birth-place</td><td><b>Europe</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>3. <b>Names of the Parents</b></td><td><b>Not Known</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>4. Age</td><td><b>About 45 years</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>5. Condition in life</td><td><b>Saddler</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>6. Married or unmarried</td><td><b>Married to Catharina Sl[i/e]er, widow of William
            Pietersen</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>7. Day of decease</td><td><b>September 1827</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>8. At what House</td><td><b>In the village of Cradock</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>9. Children</td><td>James, George, Johanna, Jacoba — <b>all minors</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>10. Property</td><td><b>A house and erf at Cradock, of the value of &pound;90</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Signed</td><td>Colesberg, <b>25 June 1840</b>, by a Montjoy</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Line 3</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1840-parents-not-known.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line three of the 1840 death notice: Names of the Parents of the Deceased — Not Known." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Names of the Parents of the Deceased. <strong>Not Known.</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>This archive went looking for this document because it was supposed to give the generation above —
      the point at which the Mountjoy name entered the Cape. Instead it says, in a firm hand, that in 1840
      <b>the family did not know who James Montjoy's parents were</b>.</p>
    <p>And they had every chance to find out. He died in <b>September 1827</b>. The notice was completed
      <b>thirteen years later</b> by a relative who knew his trade, his property, the value of his house,
      the village he died in and the name of his wife's first husband — and could not name his mother or
      his father.</p>
    <blockquote>The wall at the top of this line is not a gap in the archive. It is a gap in the family,
      and it was already there in 1840.</blockquote>
    <p>It is the same sentence the archive found on the <b>1915 death notice</b> at Barkly East, where a
      grandson wrote <em>"Father: Kolbe, thinks James was christian name. Mother: Unknown"</em> with the
      man's carved headstone standing forty miles away. Two families, two centuries, the same box on the
      same printed form, the same answer. <a href={u("/")}>Why this archive exists &rarr;</a></p>
    <p><b>"Birth-place: Europe."</b> Not a country. The most that was remembered of where he came from was
      that it was not here.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:30px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">A trade, passed down</h3>
      <figure style="margin:0 0 12px">
        <img src={u("/docs/detail-1840-saddler.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
             alt="Line five of the 1840 death notice, reading Saddler." />
        <figcaption class="cap">1840, line 5 — <strong>Saddler</strong></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> James Montjoy was a <b>saddler</b> at Cradock. His son
        <b>George Mountjoy</b> — four years old when his father died — is recorded at his own 1848
        marriage as a <b>saddle maker</b>, in the same town.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">The archive had George's trade already and treated it as the first
        recorded for a Mountjoy. It was the second. <b>The boy learned his dead father's trade.</b></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The Pietersen marriage, confirmed</h3>
      <figure style="margin:0 0 12px">
        <img src={u("/docs/detail-1840-married-pietersen.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
             alt="Line six of the 1840 death notice: Married to Catharina Slier, widow of William Pietersen." />
        <figcaption class="cap">1840, line 6 — <strong>"Married to Catharina Sl[i/e]er, widow of William
          Pietersen"</strong></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <b>"Married to Catharina Sl[i/e]er, widow of William
        Pietersen."</b> The archive had inferred, from a godparent at an 1851 font, that Johanna
        Catharina's <b>half-brothers were Pieterzens</b> by her mother's first marriage.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">Here is the first marriage, named, in a document eleven years older than
        the baptism. <b>The inference was right.</b> <a href={u("/women/")}>The women &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Four children, and a date this archive had flagged as a contradiction</h3>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:16px">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1840-death-1827.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Line seven of the 1840 death notice, reading September 1827." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Line 7: <strong>September 1827</strong>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1840-children.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Line nine of the 1840 death notice listing James, George, Johanna and Jacoba, all minors." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Line 9: <strong>James · George · Johanna · Jacoba</strong>, all minors.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:18px">
    The archive had raised this as an open contradiction: our tree has Johanna Catharina's father dying in
    <b>1827</b>, but the archival file is dated <b>1840</b>. <span class="chip doc">Resolved</span>
    <b>Both are right.</b> He died in September 1827; the notice was filed thirteen years afterwards, and
    the file carries the filing year. <strong>The tree was correct and the contradiction was mine.</strong>
  </p>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">The four children fix the family at September 1827 exactly.
    <b>Johanna Catharina</b> was eight — her own 1863 death notice puts her birth at 30 April 1819.
    <b>Jacoba</b> was seven, <b>George</b> four, <b>James</b> one. And <b>Thomas Jacobus</b>, born 1818,
    is <em>not on the list</em> — which independently supports the correction that he died in 1827 rather
    than in infancy in 1818.</p>
  <p style="margin-top:14px"><span class="chip inf">A correction the tree needs</span> Our tree records
    <b>James Mountjoy as born and died in 1826</b>. He is listed here <b>alive, as a minor</b>, at his
    father's death in September 1827. The infant-death date is wrong.</p>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:26px">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>Where the line now ends, and why it is a real end.</strong> James Montjoy,
      a saddler born in "Europe" about 1782, who owned a house and erf at Cradock worth &pound;90 and died
      there in September 1827 leaving four children under nine. Above him: nothing, and nothing recoverable
      from this family — because the people who buried him could not name his parents thirteen years later.
      Anything further will have to come from <b>outside</b> the Cape: English, Irish or Continental records
      for a saddler named Montjoy born around 1782 who reached the Cape before 1818.
      <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1884 &middot; A farmer who died owning his clothes — and a third "not known"</h2>
  <p class="dek">The tree had carried this image for years under its own citation. Nobody had opened it.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:22px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1884-petrus-jacobus.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1884 Cape death notice for Petrus Jacobus Booysen, estate number 8907." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Filed <strong>1 August 1884</strong>, estate <strong>8907</strong>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1. Name</td><td><b>Petrus Jacobus Booysen, Senior</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>2. Birth-place</td><td><b>Graaff Reinet District</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>3. <b>Parents</b></td><td><b>Petrus Jacobus Booÿsen — mother's name, not known</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>4. Age</td><td><b>72 years</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>5. Condition in life</td><td><b>Farmer</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>6. Married or unmarried</td><td><b>Married</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>7. Day of decease</td><td><b>30 March 1884</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>8. At what house</td><td><b>Zevenfontein, District Middelburg</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>9. Children</td><td><b>Gerrit Jacobus Booÿsen, geboren 19 Januarij 1870</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>10. Property</td><td><b>Moveables only, such as wearing apparel &amp;c</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">Age 72 in 1884 puts his birth at about 1812, which matches the
        register exactly. Zevenfontein is the farm where his first wife had died twenty-one years before.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:32px">It happens a third time</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1884-mother-not-known.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line three of the 1884 death notice: Petrus Jacobus Booysen, mother's name not known, underlined." />
    <figcaption class="cap">"<strong>Petrus Jacobus Booÿsen</strong> — <strong>mother's name, not
      known</strong>" — and the informant underlined it.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>This archive has now read three death notices that ask for the parents of the deceased. All three
      fail, and <b>they fail in the same direction</b>:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin:18px 0">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m" style="width:7rem">Written</th><th style="width:16rem">For</th><th>Line 3 says</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">1840</td><td><b>James Montjoy</b> (d. 1827)</td><td><b>"Not Known."</b> — both parents</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">1884</td><td><b>Petrus Jacobus Booysen</b> (d. 1884)</td><td>Father named. <b>"Mother's name, not known."</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">1915</td><td><b>Margaret Elizabeth Barry née Kolbe</b> (d. 1912)</td><td>"Father: Kolbe, <b>thinks</b> James was christian name. Mother: <b>Unknown</b>."</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>Three families, three generations, one printed box — and it is the mother
      who goes first.</strong> In 1884 the father's full name is written out and the mother has no name at
      all. In 1915 the father survives as a surname and a guess at a christian name; the mother is
      <em>Unknown</em>. Only in 1840, the oldest of the three, are both gone together.</p>
    <p style="margin:10px 0 0">His mother was <b>Anna Maria van der Merwe</b>. She had died before March
      1825, when her son was twelve. By the time he died at seventy-two, nobody in the house could name
      her. <a href={u("/women/")}>The women &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">The second wife, confirmed but still not named</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1884-children.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line nine of the 1884 death notice naming Gerrit Jacobus Booysen born 19 January 1870." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The only child entered: <strong>Gerrit Jacobus Booÿsen, geboren 19 Januarij
      1870</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> He is recorded as <b>Married</b> at his death in 1884 —
    <b>twenty-one years after Johanna Catharina died</b>. That settles the open question: there was a
    second wife. And the single child entered here, <b>Gerrit Jacobus, born 19 January 1870</b>, is
    <em>new to this archive</em> and can only be hers.
  </p>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">
    With <b>Izak (1864)</b> and <b>Stephanus Francois (1875)</b>, that is now three children born to this
    man after 1863 — the ones the tree had wrongly attached to his first wife.
    <span class="chip inf">Still open</span> <b>The form never asks her name, and so it does not give it.</b>
    The marriage itself, at Cradock or Middelburg between 1863 and 1870, is the document that would.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a>
  </p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">"Moveables only"</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1884-moveables.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line ten of the 1884 death notice: moveables only, such as wearing apparel etc." />
    <figcaption class="cap">"<strong>Moveables only. Such as wearing apparel &amp;c.</strong>"</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>He is entered on line 5 as a <b>farmer</b>. On line 10 he leaves <b>no fixed property at all</b> —
      his clothes and whatever else would fit in a wagon. He was living at Zevenfontein; he did not own it.</p>
    <p>Set that beside the oldest document here. <b>James Montjoy, a saddler, died in 1827 owning a house
      and erf at Cradock worth £90.</b> Fifty-seven years later his son-in-law's household has movables
      and wearing apparel.</p>
    <blockquote>The family was already landless in 1884 — <b>fifteen years before the war</b>.</blockquote>
    <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred, and it cuts both ways</span> This does <em>not</em> dispose of the
      burned-farm claim: <b>Willem Hermanus is entered as a <em>boer</em>, farmer, of Lady Grey at his
      marriage in 1879</b> — five years <em>after</em> his father's estate showed nothing. A son can farm
      land his father never owned, by lease, by marriage or by his wife's people. But it does mean the
      family's landlessness cannot simply be dated to the war, and any account that starts with a
      prosperous farm being burned has to explain 1884 first.
      <a href={u("/rebels/")}>The testimony &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1817 &middot; The marriage register — where the blank actually begins</h2>
  <p class="dek">Two index entries existed for this marriage and neither carried an image. A third
    indexing, under the spelling <em>Montyoy</em>, did.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/marriage-register-1817-graaff-reinet.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Graaff-Reinet Dutch Reformed marriage register opening for March 1817." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Graaff-Reinet marriage register, folios 60&ndash;61.
      <strong>"Den 9 Maart 1817 getrouwd"</strong> — married on 9 March 1817.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1817-montjoy-entry.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Register entry reading James Montjoy, Met Anna Catharina Slier weduwe van Harmanus Pieterzen." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The entry, with a pencil cross beside it:
      <strong>"James Montjoy — Met · Anna Catharina Slier, weduwe van Harmanus Pieterze[n]"</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:26px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What it does not say, and that is the point</h3>
      <p>This archive went to this register expecting <b>an origin</b>. Cape marriage entries of this date
        routinely record where a party came from, and <b>this very page does it repeatedly</b> — three
        couples in the right-hand column are entered <em>"beide van Uitenhage"</em>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">Against James Montjoy's name there is <b>a long ruled dash and nothing
        else</b>.</p>
      <blockquote style="margin-top:12px">He arrived in the record already without a past.</blockquote>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">So the silence is not a failure of the 1840 death notice, written
        thirteen years after his death. <b>It was there at his wedding, twenty-three years earlier, while
        he was standing in the room.</b></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Her surname, a sixth time</h3>
      <p>The register reads <b>Slier</b>. The tally across independent sources is now:</p>
      <table style="width:100%;margin-top:10px">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="m">1817</td><td>marriage register</td><td><b>Slier</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">1863</td><td>daughter's death notice</td><td><b>Sleer</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">—</td><td>FamilySearch index</td><td><b>Slier</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">—</td><td>family tree</td><td><b>Schleher</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">—</td><td>indexer, entry 1</td><td><b>Sher</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">—</td><td>indexer, entry 2</td><td><b>Hier</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">The contemporary register agrees with the index against the tree:
        <b>Slier</b>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px"><span class="chip doc">And now a seventh test</span>
        FamilySearch's whole South African corpus has been counted for each spelling.
        <b>SLIER returns 851</b> people. <b>SLEER returns 7.</b>
        <b>SCHLEHER returns nothing at all &mdash; not one record in the country.</b></p>
      <p style="margin-top:10px">That is not proof of what her name was. It is proof that
        <b>the tree's spelling is the one form of it that no South African record uses</b>, which is
        exactly what you would expect of a form arrived at by a modern hand.
        <a href={u("/register/")}>The register &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:36px">And a name in this family turns out not to be a family name</h3>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:16px">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1840-first-husband.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1840 death notice line reading widow of William Pietersen." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>1840:</strong> "widow of <strong>William Pietersen</strong>"</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1851-pieterzen-control.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1851 baptism witnesses reading Willem Hermanus Pieterzen and Xaverius Hermanus Pieterzen." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>1851:</strong> the godfather,
        <strong>Willem Hermanus Pieterzen</strong></figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p>Three documents name Anna Catharina's first husband, and they look like three different men:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>1817</b>, the marriage register, contemporary — <b>Harmanus Pieterze[n]</b></li>
      <li><b>1840</b>, the death notice, written by a relative — <b>William Pietersen</b></li>
      <li><b>1851</b>, the baptism, the godfather who is his son — <b>Willem Hermanus Pieterzen</b></li>
    </ul>
    <p>The third contains both of the first two, and this archive read that as one man with a long
      name &mdash; &ldquo;the 1817 clerk wrote the second given name, the 1840 informant the first&rdquo;.
      <b>That was wrong, and it was corrected on 15 September 2026.</b>
      <span class="chip doc">Corrected from the printed genealogy</span> De Villiers gives
      <b>&ldquo;Hermanus Pietersen, namen van ouders onbekend, gehuwd 5 April 1807 met Anna Catharina
      Slier&rdquo;</b> and their sons as <b>Willem Hermanus (1809)</b> and <b>Johan Christiaan (1810)</b>.
      The husband is <b>Hermanus</b>; the 1851 godfather is his <b>son</b>, which this page already said
      in the line above without following it. The 1840 notice is the odd one out.
      <a href={u("/documents/#slier-pietersen")}>The entry, and a second half-brother &rarr;</a></p>
    <blockquote>Which means the name <strong>Willem Hermanus</strong> did not come down the Booyzen line
      at all.</blockquote>
    <p>It enters through <b>Anna Catharina Slier's first marriage</b> — a Pieterzen household with
      <b>no blood relation to the Booyzens whatever</b>. Her son by that marriage stood godfather at the
      Booyzen font in 1851, and <b>the child was given his name</b>.</p>
    <p>That child is <b>Willem Hermanus Booyzen</b>, who farmed at Lady Grey, laid stone at Indwe, died of
      an ulcer of the stomach in September 1905, and whose son signed the death form <em>Booyzen</em> under
      a clerk's <em>Booysen</em>. His grandson was <b>Willem Hermanus "Willy" Booyzen</b>, born 1925 in
      Johannesburg. <b>Four generations carried a step-relation's name</b>, and the archive can now say
      exactly where it came from.
      <a href={u("/women/")}>The women &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1891 &middot; The same font, two years on — and the name that runs three generations</h2>
  <p class="dek">One of the last unread images on the tree, and it turns out to be the same register as
    the 1889 page, at pages 262&ndash;263.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/doopregister-1891-barkly.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="The Doop Register Barkly Oost opening for 1891, pages 262 and 263." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Pages <strong>262&ndash;263</strong> — the same register as the 1889
      baptisms, fifty pages further on.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1891-baptism-entry.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Doop Register Barkly Oost 1891 entry 120 for Catharina Maria Sophia, parents Willem Hermanus Booysen and Catharina Maria Sophia Barry." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Entry <strong>120</strong>, <em>gedoopt</em> <strong>24 Mei 1891</strong>:
      <strong>Catharina Maria Sophia</strong>, <em>geb.</em> <strong>22 April 1891</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The entry</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td style="width:8rem">Kind</td><td><b>Catharina Maria Sophia</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Geboren</td><td><b>22 April 1891</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gedoopt</td><td><b>24 Mei 1891</b>, Barkly Oost</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Ouders</b></td><td><b>Willem Hermanus Booysen</b><br /><b>Catharina Maria Sophia Barry</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Getuigen</td><td>Philippus Rudolph Olivier · Elizabeth Susanna Germishuizen ·
            Gert Francois Botha · Catharina Anna Susanna Botha</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">The father is entered <b>Booysen</b>, with an s — as he is
        on every register entry the archive has read for him.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">A daughter named for her mother</h3>
      <p>The child takes her mother's name entire: <b>Catharina Maria Sophia</b>. And it goes on:</p>
      <table style="width:100%;margin-top:10px">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="m">1859</td><td><b>Catherine Maria Sophia "Kathleen" Barry</b> — the mother</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">1891</td><td><b>Catharina Maria Sophia Booysen</b> — this entry</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">1929</td><td><b>Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen</b> — the granddaughter, and the
            starting point of this archive</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">Three women, seventy years, one name — and the spelling of the
        <em>surname</em> shifts underneath it while the given names hold. <a href={u("/women/")}>The women
        &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:20px">
    <span class="chip doc">New to this archive</span> <b>Catharina Maria Sophia Booysen, born 22 April
    1891</b>, is a daughter of Willem Hermanus and Kathleen that the archive did not have. She was
    <b>fourteen</b> when her father died at Indwe in 1905.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1940 &middot; A fourth time the form asks for the parents, and a fourth time it fails</h2>
  <p class="dek">Found only after this archive noticed it had spent its whole life searching one province
    out of nine. <span class="m">TAB MHG 2428/40</span></p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:22px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/sterfkennis-1940-stephanus-francois.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1940 Sterfkennis for Stephanus Francois Booyzen, estate 2428/40, Pretoria." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Sterfkennis</strong> under the Estates Act 1913, filed at Pretoria
        <strong>29 July 1940</strong>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1. Naam</td><td><b>Stephanus Francois Booyzen</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>2. Geboorteplek</td><td><b>Kaap Provinsie</b> — nasionaliteit <b>Afrikaner</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>3. <b>Ouers</b></td><td><b>Vader: oorlede. Moeder: oorlede.</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>4. Ouderdom</td><td><b>65 jaar 7 maande</b> — so born about October 1874</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5. <b>Beroep</b></td><td><b>Messelaar</b> — mason</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6. Woonplek</td><td>East Lynne, Pk. Silverton, dist. Pretoria</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7. Getroud</td><td><b>Beatrix Magdalena Catharina, geb. Boshoff</b>, in gemeenskap;
            last married at <b>Mosselbaai, K.P.</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>8. Sterfdag</td><td><b>14 Mei 1940</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>10. Kinders</td><td><b>eleven</b>, the youngest born 18 August 1930</td></tr>
          <tr><td>11–14</td><td>moveable <b>and</b> immovable property; estate over £300; a will</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:30px">Line 3</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1940-ouers-oorlede.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line three of the 1940 Sterfkennis: Name en adresse van ouers van oorledene — Vader oorlede, Moeder oorlede." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><em>"Name en adresse van ouers van oorledene"</em> — <strong>Vader: oorlede ·
      Moeder: oorlede</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The form asks for the <b>names and addresses</b> of the parents. His widow answered, for both,
      <b>"deceased"</b>.</p>
    <p>This archive went to this document for one reason: <b>Stephanus Francois was a son of the second
      marriage</b>, and a death notice names its subject's parents, so his mother should have been on
      this line. She is the woman the archive has proved to exist and cannot name.
      <b>She is not on it.</b></p>
    <blockquote>Four death notices in this family now ask who the parents were. Four fail.</blockquote>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin:16px 0">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m" style="width:7rem">Written</th><th style="width:16rem">For</th><th>Line 3 says</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="m">1840</td><td><b>James Montjoy</b></td><td><b>"Not Known."</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">1884</td><td><b>Petrus Jacobus Booysen</b></td><td>Father in full. <b>"Mother's name, not known."</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">1915</td><td><b>Margaret Elizabeth Barry née Kolbe</b></td><td>"Father: Kolbe, <b>thinks</b> James was christian name. Mother: <b>Unknown</b>."</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">1940</td><td><b>Stephanus Francois Booyzen</b></td><td><b>"Vader: oorlede. Moeder: oorlede."</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The 1940 answer is different in kind from the other three, and worse. The earlier informants at
      least <em>said</em> they did not know. His widow answered a question about identity with a question
      about mortality — <b>and nobody at the Master's office sent it back</b>.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">And a second mason</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1940-messelaar.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line five of the 1940 Sterfkennis, occupation: Messelaar." />
    <figcaption class="cap">5. Beroep van oorledene — <strong>Messelaar</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <b>Messelaar</b> is a mason. His half-brother <b>Willem Hermanus Booyzen</b> is entered as a
    <b>mason</b> on his own death registration at Indwe in 1905.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>Two brothers, both in stone.</strong> The testimony ends with the boys
      going onto the mines. Of the occupations this archive has now actually read for the men of this
      family — <b>saddler, farmer, mason, tree nurseryman, railway clerk, mason</b> — <b>not one is a
      miner</b>. They went into trades, and two of them into the same one.
      <a href={u("/searched/")}>What was searched &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:18px" class="cap">He also left <b>immovable property and a will</b>, and an estate
    over £300 — which is more than his father managed in 1884, and more than his half-brother, who left
    no estate at all.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1870 &middot; The second wife, named at last</h2>
  <p class="dek">The oldest open question in this archive, and it was answered by a christening rather
    than a death.</p>
  <div class="note" style="margin:20px 0">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>What had been established.</strong> Petrus Jacobus Booysen's first wife
      died in <b>1863</b>. His own 1884 death notice enters him as <b>married</b> twenty-one years later
      and names one child — <b>Gerrit Jacobus, born 19 January 1870</b> — who can only be a second wife's.
      Three children in all were born after 1863 and the tree had wrongly attached them to a woman already
      dead. <b>But no document would give her name.</b> Not his death notice, which does not ask. Not her
      son Stephanus Francois's, read this morning, where the widow wrote only <em>"oorlede"</em>.</p>
    <p style="margin:10px 0 0"><strong>The mistake was looking at deaths.</strong> A baptism names both
      parents, and the child whose birth date the 1884 notice gives to the day was baptised
      <b>19 June 1870</b>.</p>
  </div>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/doopregister-1870-gert-jacobus.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Dutch Reformed baptism register opening for 1870, pages 1296 and 1297." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The register opening, pages <strong>1296&ndash;1297</strong>. The entry is
      low on the right-hand page.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1870-second-wife.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1870 baptism register entry naming the parents as Petrus Jacobus Booyzen and Johanna Margaritha Pretorius." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><em>Oud:</em> <strong>Petrus Jacobus Booyzen</strong> en
      <strong>Johanna Margaritha Pretorius</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The entry</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td style="width:7rem">Kind</td><td><b>Gert Jacobus</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Geboren</td><td><b>19 January 1870</b> — the exact date on the 1884 death notice</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gedoopt</td><td><b>19 June 1870</b>, Cape Colony</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Ouders</b></td><td><b>Petrus Jacobus Booyzen</b><br /><b>Johanna Margaritha Pretorius</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Getuigen</td><td>Petrus Jacobus Booijzen jr · Magdalena Johanna Francina Booijzen ·
            Andries Gottlieb Pretorius</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Look at who stood at the font</h3>
      <p><b>Petrus Jacobus Booijzen junior</b>, born 10 September 1839. <b>Magdalena Johanna Francina
        Booijzen</b>, born 16 July 1853.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">Both are on the <a href={u("/documents/")}>1863 death notice</a> — the
        third and sixth of Johanna Catharina Mountjoy's eight children.</p>
      <blockquote style="margin-top:12px">Two children of the first marriage stood godparent to the
        first child of the second.</blockquote>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">Whatever the household was after 1863, it was not divided. The third
        witness, <b>Andries Gottlieb Pretorius</b>, is presumably the bride's people.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:22px">
    <span class="chip doc">Open question 7, answered</span> <b>Johanna Margaritha Pretorius</b> is the
    second wife of Petrus Jacobus Booysen, mother of <b>Gert Jacobus (1870)</b>, and — with the tree's
    other misattributions now corrected — of <b>Izak (1864)</b> and <b>Stephanus Francois (1874)</b>,
    the mason who died at Pretoria in 1940.
  </p>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">
    <span class="chip inf">One thing not to jump at</span> <b>Pretorius</b> is also the surname of
    <b>Anna Johanna Maria Pretorius</b>, who married George Downing Mountjoy Booyzen in 1924 — and this
    archive has already shown that marriage to be between cousins, through her <em>Barry</em> mother.
    A second Pretorius connection fifty-four years earlier is <b>not evidence of anything</b>: Pretorius
    is among the commonest surnames in the Cape. It is noted here so that nobody later mistakes a
    coincidence for a link. <a href={u("/cousins/")}>The cousin marriage &rarr;</a>
  </p>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px">Read from the register image, not the index — this archive has
    been caught once already trusting a volunteer transcription, on the Pieterzen witnesses of 1851. The
    father is written <b>Booyzen</b> and the two witnesses <b>Booijzen</b>, in the same hand, in 1870.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1944 &middot; The parents line, filled in</h2>
  <p class="dek">Anna Pretorius's Sterfkennis. <span class="m">TAB MHG 2945/44</span> — the second of the
    nine Transvaal estates, and the first in this archive where question three gets a real answer.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:22px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/sterfkennis-1944-anna-pretorius.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1944 Sterfkennis for Anna Johanna Maria Booyzen born Pretorius, estate 2945/44." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Estate <strong>2945/44</strong>. Signed by her husband,
        <strong>G. D. M. Booyzen</strong>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td style="width:7rem">1. Naam</td><td><b>Anna Johanna Maria Booyzen (geb. Pretorius)</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>2. Geboorteplek</td><td><b>Barkly Oos, Kaap Provinsie</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>3. <b>Ouers</b></td><td><b>Vader: Petrus Jacobus Pretorius</b> — 96 James Street, Germiston<br />
            <b>Moeder: Margareth Wilhelmina Elizabeth Pretorius</b> — 96 James Street, Germiston</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4. Ouderdom</td><td><b>37</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>6. Woonplek</td><td><b>Rheeder Straat 85, Forest Hill, J'hburg</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>8. Sterfdag</td><td><b>26 Februarie 1944</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>10. Kinders</td><td><b>six, alle minderjarig</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:30px">Line 3, filled in</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1944-parents-named.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line three of the 1944 Sterfkennis, naming both parents with their address at 96 James Street, Germiston." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Both parents, <strong>and their address</strong> — 96 James Street,
      Germiston.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>This archive has now read <b>five</b> death notices that ask who the parents were. Four failed:
      <em>"Not Known"</em> in 1840, <em>"mother's name, not known"</em> in 1884, <em>"Mother: Unknown"</em>
      in 1915, <em>"oorlede"</em> in 1940.</p>
    <blockquote><b>This one is filled in completely</b> — both names, and the street number they were
      living at.</blockquote>
    <p>The difference is not the form. It is the same printed question. <b>The difference is who was
      holding the pen and what they knew.</b> Her husband filled this in, and her parents were alive at
      96 James Street, Germiston — her mother <b>Margaretha Wilhelmina Elizabeth Barry</b>, who outlived
      her by seventeen years. <a href={u("/cousins/")}>The cousin marriage &rarr;</a></p>
    <p>Set beside the 1940 sheet, where a widow could not name her own husband's parents, it is a
      reminder that these blanks are not archival accidents. <b>They record what a particular person, on
      a particular afternoon, actually knew about their own family.</b></p>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><b>This section first said "the one time the form worked."</b>
      The next document opened after it &mdash; the 1942 Daschner notice, <a href="#daschner-1942">below</a>
      &mdash; names both parents too. So the line is not unique; it is the earlier of two, and the pattern
      is a different one. <a href={u("/research-log/")}>The log &rarr;</a></p>
    <p><b>Two documents were filed for this one death, and they are not the same document.</b> The civil
      registration &mdash; entry 2045, Johannesburg, read
      <a href="#anna-1944-civil">further up this page</a>
      &mdash; gives the cause of death, the cemetery and the age, but it never asks who her parents were.
      The Master's Sterfkennis does, because the Master needs to know who might inherit. <b>The archive
      that seems less clinical is the one that carries the genealogy.</b></p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Six children, all minors</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1944-six-children.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line ten of the 1944 Sterfkennis listing six children with their birth dates, all minors." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>(Alle Minderjarig)</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Child</th><th class="m">Born, per this document</th><th class="m">Age in Feb 1944</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Willem Hermanus</td><td class="m">15 Oct 1925</td><td class="m">18</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Margrett Wilhelmina Elizabeth</td><td class="m"><b>14 Oct 1926</b></td><td class="m">17</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Catherine Mary Sophia</b></td><td class="m">25 Mar 1929</td><td class="m"><b>14</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Anna Johanna Maria</td><td class="m">12 Oct 1930</td><td class="m">13</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Georgina Jeanette</td><td class="m">22 Sep 1932</td><td class="m">11</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Petrus Jacobus</td><td class="m">10 Jan 1934</td><td class="m">10</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">
    <span class="chip doc">A correction</span> This archive has carried <b>Maggie's birthday as 1 October
    1926</b>. Her mother's death notice, written by her father, gives <b>14 October</b>. The document
    wins.
  </p>
  <p style="margin-top:12px">Every other date matches what the archive already held. And the bracket at
    the foot of the list — <b>"Alle Minderjarig"</b> — is the whole of it: six children under nineteen,
    the youngest ten, and three years and ten months later their father would be dead too.
    <a href={u("/direct-line/")}>The line &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<Rule name="thorn" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="daschner-1942">1942 &middot; A daughter married at Indwe, and eleven cousins</h2>
  <p class="dek">Joseph Heinrich Daschner's death notice. <span class="m">TAB MHG 1928/42</span> &mdash;
    opened to check a marriage, and it returned a branch.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:22px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1942-daschner.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Typewritten 1942 death notice for Joseph Heinrich Daschner, estate 1928/42, filed at Pretoria." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Estate <strong>1928/42</strong>, stamped <span class="m">13-5-1942,
        Pretoria</span>. Typewritten throughout &mdash; the first document in this archive that is.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td style="width:7rem">1. Deceased</td><td><b>Joseph Heinrich Daschner</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>2. Birthplace</td><td><b>Stutterheim, Cape Province</b> &middot; South African</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3. Parents</td><td><b>Joseph Daschner</b>, deceased<br /><b>Sophia Daschner (Stoter)</b>, deceased</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4. Age</td><td><b>58 years 5 months</b> &mdash; born about November 1883</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5. Occupation</td><td><b>Pensioner (Electrician)</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>6. Residence</td><td><b>132 Sheffield Street, Kenilworth, Johannesburg</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>7a. Spouse</td><td><b>Catherine Mary Sophia Daschner (born Booyzen)</b> &mdash; married in community of property</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7b. Earlier wife</td><td><b>"Not previously married."</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>7c. Married at</td><td><b>Indwe, Cape Colony</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>8. Died</td><td><b>19 April 1942</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>11&ndash;14</td><td>Movables <b>yes</b> &middot; immovables <b>yes</b> &middot; over &pound;300 <b>yes</b> &middot; a will <b>yes</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="cap" style="margin:14px 0 0">Signed <b>C. M. S. Daschner</b>, "Surviving Spouse &mdash;
        Saw body after death."</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:30px">Two words that close a marriage</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1942-born-booyzen.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question seven of the 1942 death notice: Catherine Mary Sophia Daschner, born Booyzen, married in community of property; place of last marriage, Indwe, Cape Colony." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>"CATHERINE MARY SOPHIA DASCHNER (born Booyzen)"</strong> &mdash; and
      below it, <strong>"Indwe, Cape Colony."</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>This archive already held the marriage, from the family tree: <b>Catharina Maria Sophia
      Booysen</b>, baptised at Barkly Oost on 24 May 1891, married Joseph Heinrich Daschner. What it did
      not have was a document. Here are two lines of one, and they do more than confirm a husband's
      name.</p>
    <blockquote><b>They married at Indwe.</b> Indwe is where her father, <b>Willem Hermanus
      Booyzen</b>, died in September 1905, when she was fourteen. It is a colliery village in the
      north-eastern Cape that appears in this archive for exactly one reason &mdash; a death form. Now it
      appears for a wedding as well.</blockquote>
    <p>And note the spelling. The clerk who filled in her father's 1905 death form wrote
      <b>Booysen</b>, with an <em>s</em>, and this archive has spent some care on how little that proves.
      Thirty-seven years later, a Johannesburg typist taking dictation from his daughter typed
      <b>Booyzen</b>, with a <em>z</em>. <a href={u("/name/")}>The name &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Eleven children</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1942-eleven-children.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question ten of the 1942 death notice, listing eleven Daschner children, six majors and five minors with dates of birth." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Six majors, then five minors <em>with dates</em> &mdash; because the form
      demands a date only where the child is still a minor.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Child</th><th class="m">Status, 1942</th><th class="m">Born</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>Catherine Mary Sophia</b> Daschner</td><td class="m">Major</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Joseph Charles Daschner</td><td class="m">Major</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Margaret Wilhelmina Elizabeth Jeanette</b> van Jaarsveld, born Daschner</td><td class="m">Major</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>William Herman</b> Daschner</td><td class="m">Major</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Martha Magdeline van Deventer, born Daschner</td><td class="m">Major</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td>John Frederick Daschner</td><td class="m">Major</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Richard Peter</b> Daschner</td><td class="m">Minor</td><td class="m">8 May 1922</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Albert Alfred Daschner</td><td class="m">Minor</td><td class="m">3 Mar 1927</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Edith Dorothy Daschner</td><td class="m">Minor</td><td class="m">20 Jan 1930</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Henry James</b> Daschner</td><td class="m">Minor</td><td class="m">25 Feb 1932</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Yvonne Muriel Daschner</td><td class="m">Minor</td><td class="m">15 Jul 1935</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p><b>Read the bolded names again.</b> <em>Catherine Mary Sophia</em> is her own name, and her
      mother's before her &mdash; <a href={u("/who/catherine-maria-sophia-kathleen-barry/")}>Kathleen
      Barry</a>, born 1859. <em>Margaret Wilhelmina Elizabeth</em> is the Barry name that runs down the
      other side of this family too. <em>William Herman</em> is <a href={u("/who/willem-hermanus-william-henry-booyzen/")}>Willem
      Hermanus</a>, her father. <em>Richard Peter</em> is her uncle,
      <a href={u("/who/richard-peter-barry/")}>Richard Peter Barry</a>. <em>Henry James</em> is her
      brother.</p>
    <blockquote><b>Five of eleven Daschner children carry Booyzen and Barry names.</b> The surname
      leaves the family in one generation. The names do not.</blockquote>
    <p>This is the same evidence the archive uses on the <a href={u("/name/")}>name</a> and in the
      <a href={u("/cousins/")}>cousin marriage</a>: in this family, forenames are inherited property, and
      they travel through daughters as reliably as surnames travel through sons. It is why
      <em>Willem Hermanus</em> &mdash; a name that entered by
      <a href={u("/who/willem-hermanus-pieterzen/")}>step-relation, with no blood tie at all</a> &mdash;
      is still being given to children a century and a half later.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">A small correction to the index, not to this archive</span>
      FamilySearch's volunteer index gives Richard Peter Daschner's birth as <b>1923</b>. The document
      reads <b>1922</b>, and reads it clearly at full resolution. The table above follows the
      document.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">And what question three actually depends on</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1942-daschner-parents.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question three of the 1942 death notice: Father, Joseph Daschner, Deceased; Mother, Sophia Daschner born Stoter, Deceased." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Joseph Daschner</strong> &mdash; deceased.
      <strong>Sophia Daschner (Stoter)</strong> &mdash; deceased.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Both parents dead, and both parents named. Put that beside the 1940 sheet two years earlier, where
      a widow answered the same printed question with the single word <b>"oorlede"</b> and nothing else,
      and the pattern in this archive stops being about the form at all.</p>
    <blockquote><b>Question three fails when the informant is remembering the nineteenth-century
      Cape interior. It works when the informant married into the family in the twentieth century and
      the names were still in the house.</b> 1840, 1884, 1915 and 1940 fail. 1942 and 1944 work. All six
      are the same question.</blockquote>
    <p>It also matters which way the marriage ran. In 1940 a Boshoff widow was asked for her Booyzen
      husband's parents and could not do it. In 1942 and 1944 the informant was the spouse of the
      deceased, writing down <em>their own</em> in-laws &mdash; people they had met, at addresses they
      could quote. <a href={u("/method/")}>The method &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:26px">
    <b>One more thing this file taught, the hard way.</b> A second Daschner death notice surfaced in the
    same index &mdash; Charles Bruce Rennie, a miner of <em>Booysens, Johannesburg</em>, whose
    pre-deceased wife was a Minnie Bertha Sophia Daschner. Nothing to do with this family. But it is a
    warning worth keeping: <b>Booysens is a suburb of Johannesburg</b>, a mile from where this family
    actually lived, and every name-index search for <em>Booysen</em> in that city will drag in the
    people who merely had an address there. <a href={u("/searched/")}>What was searched &rarr;</a>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="koppie" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="sterfkennis-1876">1876 &middot; The oldest man in this archive, and the two names above him</h2>
  <p class="dek">Sterfkennis No. 678, written at the farm <strong>Brakfontein on 14 September 1876</strong>
    under the Transvaal Republic's <em>Wet op de Weeskamer</em>. It is the document this archive has been
    looking for since the first page was written.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:22px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/sterfkennis-1876-petrus-jacobus.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1876 Sterfkennis No. 678 for Petrus Jacobus Booijsen, born Graaff-Reinet, died district Rustenburg." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Notice <strong>678</strong>. Countersigned by the
        <strong>veldkornet</strong> and by <strong>H. S. Booijsen</strong> &mdash; Hendrik Stefanus, one
        of the sons listed four lines above his own signature.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td style="width:7rem">1. Naam</td><td><b>Petrus Jacobus Booijsen</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>2. Geboorteplaats</td><td><b>Graaffreinet</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>3. <b>Ouders</b></td><td><b>Gerthardus L&ouml;dewikus Booijsen</b><br /><b>Magdalena de Beer</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>4. Ouderdom</td><td><b>88 jaar</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>5. Beroep</td><td><b>Landbouwer</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>6. Staat</td><td>Gehuwd &mdash; <b>weduwenaar</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>7. Overleden</td><td><b>1 September 1876</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>8. Waar</td><td><b>Destrikt Rustenburg</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>9. Testament</td><td><b>Neen</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>10. Goederen</td><td><b>Niets nagelaten</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>11. Boven &pound;25</td><td><b>Niets bekend</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>12. Kinderen</td><td><b>eight, all mondig</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:30px">The top of the line, in someone's handwriting</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1876-parents-named.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line three of the 1876 Sterfkennis reading Gerthardus Lodewikus Booijsen and Magdalena de Beer." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>"Gerthardus L&ouml;dewikus Booijsen"</strong> &mdash;
      <strong>"Magdalena de Beer"</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Generation seven of this family has stood at the head of
      <a href={u("/direct-line/")}>the direct line</a> since this archive began with the flattest entry on
      the site: <em>no birth date, no death date, no father, no mother.</em> He was there because the
      family tree said so, and for no other reason. Everything below him had documents. He had a name.</p>
    <blockquote><b>He now has a document too.</b> Not his own &mdash; his son's. In September 1876, at a
      farm in the Transvaal, somebody was asked who the dead man's parents were and wrote down
      <b>Gerthardus L&ouml;dewikus Booijsen and Magdalena de Beer</b>.</blockquote>
    <p>It does not date him, and it does not name <em>his</em> parents, so
      <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>the oldest open question on this site stays open</a>. What it does
      is move the two of them out of the tree and into the record, and that matters more than it sounds,
      because this archive had already found a reason to distrust the pairing. Searching the Cape depot
      for Magdalena de Beer's estate turned up <span class="m">KAB NCD 2/1: 49</span>, a notarial will of
      1793 for <em>"Jan Booysen, married to the late Magdalena de Beer"</em> &mdash; a different husband,
      and a wife fifty years too early. It was recorded as a warning that
      <b>Booysen&ndash;de Beer was not a unique pairing.</b></p>
    <p>It still is not. But the pairing in <em>this</em> line is no longer an assertion, and the 1793
      will can now be set aside for good rather than merely resisted.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Eight children, and three names that come back</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1876-eight-children.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line twelve of the 1876 Sterfkennis listing eight Booijsen children, all majors." />
    <figcaption class="cap">All eight entered <em>mondig</em> &mdash; the eldest was in his sixties.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Child, as written</th><th>What this archive already knew</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>David Schalk Booijsen</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Magdalena Johanna Booijsen</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Petrus Jacobus Booijsen</b></td><td><b>generation five</b>, b. 10 May 1812, the farmer at Zevenfontein who signed the 1863 death notice</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Anna Eliezabet Booijsen</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Jan Gerthardus Lodewikus Booijsen</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Eliezabet Cornelia Booijsen</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Hendrik Stefanus Booijsen</b></td><td>signed this notice</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Anna Maria Booijsen</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p>Seven of these eight were unknown to this archive an hour ago. And three of the names &mdash;
      <b>David Schalk</b>, <b>Magdalena Johanna</b>, <b>Jan Gerthardus Lodewikus</b> &mdash; are the same
      names that appear on the <a href={u("/documents/")}>1863 death notice</a> as children of
      <em>Petrus Jacobus the younger</em>, born 1848, 1853 and 1855.</p>
    <blockquote>They are uncles and aunts and their namesake nephews and nieces, one generation apart,
      and nothing but a birth year tells them apart. <b>This archive has now met that trap four times.</b>
      It is why the people index says out loud when two people share a page.</blockquote>

  </div>

  <h3 id="the-name-twice" style="margin-top:34px">The name is on this page twice &mdash; and a stranger saw it first</h3>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="note"><b>A man who had not seen this document predicted what is on it, in thirteen minutes.</b> On 14 September 2026 <b>Pieter van Zyl</b> answered the
      G2G question about this document. He had not seen this page. He wrote: <i>"His real dad, Jan, was
      already dead for more than 40 years when Petrus Jacobus died&hellip; Quite possibly, the informant
      'thought' the dad's name was G L. I searched for a marriage between a Gerhardus Lodewicus and
      Magdalena de Beer but could not find one. <b>If I remember correctly, there was a child of Petrus
      called Gerhardus Lodewicus on his Sterfkennis.</b>"</i></p>

    <p><b>There is. He is in the table above.</b> The fifth child in line 12 is
      <b>JAN GERTHARDUS LODEWIKUS BOOIJSEN</b> &mdash; and the name this archive takes from line 3, for
      the dead man's <em>father</em>, is the back half of his <em>son's</em> name. Both are on the same
      sheet of paper, eight lines apart, in one clerk's hand.</p>

    <p>De Villiers puts Petrus Jacobus under <b>JAN</b> (b3c4d7). The son on this page is
      <b>Jan</b> Gerthardus Lodewikus. <b>The informant had a compound name in the house and wrote half
      of it into the wrong line, dropping the half that agreed with the printed genealogy.</b> That is a
      mechanism, not a guess, and it is visible without leaving the document.</p>

    <p class="cap"><b>What this does and does not do.</b> It does <em>not</em> prove the father was Jan;
      this archive is not swapping one unproved father for another on the strength of a good reading.
      What it does is <b>remove this document as decisive evidence for Gerthardus Lodewikus</b> &mdash;
      which is a real loss, because line 3 is the only place that name has ever been found. The question
      stays open and gets harder. <a href={u("/disputed/")}>Disputed &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">"Niets nagelaten"</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1876-niets-nagelaten.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Lines eight to eleven: district Rustenburg; no will; nothing left; nothing known." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Destrikt Rustenburg &middot; Neen &middot; Niets nagelaten &middot;
      Niets bekend</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Eighty-eight years old, a lifetime of farming, and the estate is <b>nothing</b>. No will, no
      movables, no land, nothing known to be worth twenty-five pounds.</p>
    <p>Eight years later his son's notice would read
      <em>"moveables only, such as wearing apparel"</em>. Sixty-eight years after that, his
      great-great-grandson would be buried from a rented house in Forest Hill.
      <b>Three death notices across four generations, and not one of them has anything to leave.</b>
      <a href={u("/direct-line/")}>The line &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:26px">
    <b>And this changes the map.</b> This archive has spent its life treating the family as a Cape family
    that went north to Johannesburg in the twentieth century, and treating the Booyzens of the western
    Transvaal as strangers who happened to share a surname. <b>The direct ancestor died in district
    Rustenburg in 1876</b> &mdash; twenty-five years before the war, a generation before Johannesburg.
    Two other notices read the same afternoon sit in the same country: a
    <b>Magdalena Susanna Maria van den Berg, born Booyzen</b>, who died at Vaalbank in the district of
    Lichtenburg in 1920 and whose father is entered as <b>Jan Gerhardus Lodewicus Booyzen</b>; and an
    an <b>Anna Johanna Booijzen</b>, <b>born in the district of Cradock</b> about 1823, who died on a
    farm in the district of Rustenburg in 1891, leaving children called Petrus Jacobus, Magdalena Johanna
    and Hendrik Stephanus Nicolaas. <b>Neither was proved to be kin</b> when this was written. Both are
    the right names in the right district at the right time, and this archive could no longer say what it
    had been saying. <a href={u("/disputed/")}>What was withdrawn &rarr;</a>
    <br /><br />
    <b>Since then the second of those two has been settled.</b> The farm is <b>Brakfontein</b>, not
    Boekfontein, and the maiden name is <b>Oliefier</b>, not Alberts. She is
    <b>Anna Catharina Johanna Olivier</b>, the first wife of Hendrik Stefanus Booijsen, and she died on
    the family's own farm. <a href="#brakfontein-1891">How that was read &rarr;</a>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="gable" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="mountjoy-58379">1937&ndash;38 &middot; A whole estate, from the death to the last shilling</h2>
  <p class="dek">Estates <span class="m">58379</span> and <span class="m">58380</span>, KAB MOOC
    6/9/5047 &mdash; on this archive's Sources page as <em>"Located, not yet read"</em> for months. Read
    now, and not just the death notices: <b>the liquidation account and the distribution account too.</b>
    It is the only complete estate in this archive, and the only place where a life in this family is
    added up.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">The two notices, written the same afternoon</h3>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:18px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1937-james-john-mountjoy.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1938 death notice number 58379 for James John Mountjoy, retired builder of Barkly East." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>58379</strong> &mdash; <b>James John Mountjoy</b>, retired builder,
        Barkly East. Died 22 December 1937, aged <b>83 years 6 months</b>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1938-maria-kumm.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1938 death notice number 58380 for Maria Louisa Bertha Mountjoy, born Kumm." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>58380</strong> &mdash; <b>Maria Louisa Bertha Mountjoy, born
        Kumm</b>. Died 3 July 1934, aged <b>73 years 9 months</b>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <p>Both were dated at Barkly East on <b>19 February 1938</b>, in the same typewriter, and both were
    signed <b>G. Mountjoy &mdash; son, present at place of death</b>. She had been dead for three years
    and seven months.</p>

  <figure style="margin:22px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1937-kumm-not-reported.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question seven of the 1938 notice: Maria Louisa Bertha Mountjoy, born Kumm, died 3rd July 1934 — death not reported." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>"died 3rd July, 1934 &mdash; death not reported."</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Four words, typed on his form about her. <b>Her death was never reported to the Master</b>, and it
      was only when her husband died that anyone filed for either of them. The estates were then handled
      as one, because they had married in community of property and left
      <em>"a joint Will"</em>.</p>
    <p>This archive already predicted the file number. The Sources page has said for months that
      <em>"his wife's is the very next file, 58380"</em>, reasoning from the sequence. It was right &mdash;
      and the reason it was right turns out to be that <b>both were opened on the same day by the same
      son</b>, not that she died anywhere near him in time.</p>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> Her age of <b>73 years 9 months</b> at 3 July 1934 puts
      her birth at about <b>October 1860</b>. This archive had no birth date for her at all.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">And question three, twice more</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1937-parents-deceased.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question three of the 1938 death notice: Father, Deceased. Mother, Deceased." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Father &mdash; "Deceased." Mother &mdash; "Deceased."</strong> The
      same answer on both forms.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>Asked for the <b>names and addresses</b> of the parents of a man born in the Cape in 1854, his son
    typed the fact that they were dead and stopped. Her form says the same. That is
    <b>a fifth and a sixth failure</b> of the line this archive keeps returning to, and it fits the
    pattern exactly: the deceased were born in the nineteenth-century Cape, and the informant was a
    generation removed. <a href="#daschner-1942">Where it works &rarr;</a></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">The account</h3>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:18px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/liquidation-1938-mountjoy.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="First and final liquidation and distribution account in the estate of James John Mountjoy, Barkly East 58379." />
      <figcaption class="cap">The debit side. <strong>&pound;87. 19. 2.</strong> in costs and debts.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What he had</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Cash in the house</td><td class="m">&pound;6. 4. 2.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Post Office Savings Bank</b>, joint with his late wife</td><td class="m"><b>&pound;664. 11. 8.</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Promissory note, <b>E. W. Diesel</b>, at 6%</td><td class="m">&pound;400</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Rent, <b>Lot No. 380</b>, to W. Bendeman</td><td class="m">&pound;2. 10. 0. a month</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="cap" style="margin:12px 0 0">Brought into this account as
        <b>&pound;300. 3. 1.</b> capital and <b>&pound;13. 10. 6.</b> income &mdash; the savings-bank
        deposit belonged half to his wife's estate.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The debts are a street directory of Barkly East in 1938: <b>Die A.V.B.O.B. Bpk.</b> &mdash; the
      Afrikaans burial society &mdash; twelve pounds ten; <b>Dr A. R. A. Wilhelm</b>, eight pounds
      fifteen; <b>Premier Butchery</b> and <b>Star Butchery</b>; <b>Buys &amp; Sons</b> and
      <b>Buys Bros.</b>; <b>C. F. Hardie</b>; <b>Edelstein Bros. &amp; Co.</b>;
      <b>S. Seligmann &amp; Co., Ltd.</b>; <b>Mukheibir Bros.</b>; and <b>Greyvenstein &amp; Spence</b>,
      thirty pounds, the largest single creditor.</p>
    <blockquote>A doctor, a burial society, two butchers, and traders whose names are Afrikaans, Jewish
      and Lebanese. <b>It is a village, itemised.</b></blockquote>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">"The deceased died intestate"</h3>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:16px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1938-died-intestate.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Distribution account: the deceased died intestate and the balance is awarded to his heirs ab intestato, in tenth shares of twenty-one pounds four shillings and five pence." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>&pound;21. 4. 5.</strong> &mdash; one tenth share.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/distribution-1938-mountjoy.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="The full distribution account for estate 58379, listing eleven heirs and their shares." />
      <figcaption class="cap">The whole page. <strong>Eleven heirs</strong>, and the balance
        &pound;212. 3. 11. spent to the penny.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>Note the contradiction, because this archive is not going to resolve it.</b> Question 14 of the
      death notice, in the son's own typing, answers <em>"Joint Will with predeceased wife."</em> Four
      months later the executors' account opens with <em>"The deceased died intestate."</em> One estate
      file says both. Whatever happened between February and July 1938 &mdash; a will rejected, a will
      that dealt only with her half, a will that could not be found &mdash; the file as read does not
      say, and neither will this page.</p>
    <p>What it does say is who got what.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Heir</th><th>Relation</th><th class="m">Share</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>George Mountjoy</td><td>major son &mdash; <em>who signed both notices</em></td><td class="m">&pound;21. 4. 4.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Maria Sophia <b>Diesel</b>, born Mountjoy</td><td>daughter, m. i.c.p. Marthinus Frederick Diesel</td><td class="m">&pound;21. 4. 5.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Annie Louisa <b>Nepgen</b>, born Mountjoy</td><td>daughter, m. i.c.p. Arnoldus Fredeman Nepgen</td><td class="m">&pound;21. 4. 5.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ellen Sophia <b>Schmidt</b>, born Mountjoy</td><td>daughter, m. o.c.p. John William Schmidt</td><td class="m">&pound;21. 4. 5.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Charles Mountjoy</td><td>major son</td><td class="m">&pound;21. 4. 4.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>James John Mountjoy</td><td>major son &mdash; <em>the second of the name</em></td><td class="m">&pound;21. 4. 5.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Emma Jane Mountjoy</td><td>major daughter, <b>spinster</b></td><td class="m">&pound;21. 4. 5.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mathilda Elizabeth <b>Shooter</b>, born Mountjoy</td><td>daughter, m. o.c.p. Douglas Gilmore Shooter</td><td class="m">&pound;21. 4. 5.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lena Millie <b>Frachet</b>, born Mountjoy</td><td>daughter, m. o.c.p. Eric Bernard Frachet</td><td class="m">&pound;21. 4. 5.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gwendoline Diesel</td><td><b>granddaughter</b>, major &mdash; half of a tenth</td><td class="m">&pound;10. 12. 2.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Aubrey Diesel</td><td><b>grandson</b>, minor &mdash; half of a tenth</td><td class="m">&pound;10. 12. 2.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">The two Diesel grandchildren take their dead mother's tenth between them:
    <b>Louisa Elizabeth Diesel, born Mountjoy</b>, who had married Evelyn Wilfred Diesel and
    <b>died on 11 June 1923</b>. She is on her mother's notice and not on her father's, because his was
    typed first and ran out of page.</p>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>Set this beside the Booyzens and the point is not subtle.</b> In 1876 the direct ancestor's estate
    is <em>"Niets nagelaten. Niets bekend."</em> In 1884 his son leaves
    <em>"moveables only, such as wearing apparel."</em> In 1938 a Mountjoy of Barkly East leaves
    <b>six hundred and sixty-four pounds in the Post Office Savings Bank</b>, a house let at fifty
    shillings a month, and four hundred pounds out on note at six per cent &mdash; enough for eleven
    people to inherit from.
    <br /><br />
    These two families are joined by the marriage of 1838 and by the font at Barkly Oost in 1889, and
    they are three days' ride apart. <b>One of them was accumulating and one of them was not.</b>
    <a href={u("/direct-line/")}>The line &rarr;</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="van-niekerk-1902">1902 &middot; A wrong name in the index, and a right family behind it</h2>
  <p class="dek">Searching the Cape probate index for <b>Hercules Johannes Mountjoy</b> returns exactly one
    hit. It is not his.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:20px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1902-van-niekerk.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1902 Kennisgeving van Sterfgeval for Carolina Deybella Margrietta Alletta van Niekerk, born Botha, of Dordrecht." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><em>Kennisgeving van Sterfgeval</em>, filed 3 January 1903.
        <strong>Carolina Deybella Margrietta Alletta van Niekerk, geboren Botha</strong>, died at her own
        house at <strong>Dordrecht</strong>, 18 December 1902.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Why it comes up</h3>
      <p>Because his name is written on it. Fifth in her list of eleven children:</p>
      <blockquote style="margin:10px 0"><b>"5. Maria Sophia van Niekerk, getrouwd met Hercules Johannes
        Mountjoy."</b></blockquote>
      <p>The FamilySearch index keys <em>every name on the page</em>, so a search hit is not a statement
        about whose document it is. <b>Hercules Johannes still has no estate file</b>, and this archive's
        finding on that stands. What has changed is that the one false positive has been opened and
        turns out to be his mother-in-law.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>So the van Niekerks are now documented. <a href={u("/who/maria-sophia-van-niekerk/")}>Maria Sophia
      van Niekerk</a>, who married Hercules Johannes Mountjoy at Dordrecht on 8 October 1888, was a
      daughter of <b>Jan Dirk Abram Frans van Niekerk</b> and <b>Carolina Deybella Margrietta Alletta
      Botha</b>, who was herself born in the <b>district of Cradock</b> and whose parents were
      <b>Philip Rudolph Botha</b> and a mother of the same long name.</p>
    <p>Her ten brothers and sisters were Carolina Deybella Margrietta Alletta, Cornelius, Maria Francina
      Cecilia (widow of Gert Olivier), Jan Dirk Abram Frans, <b>Hercules Johannes</b>, Christiaan
      Jacobus, Cornelia Elizabeth Gertruida (married James Ross), Benjamin Jacobus, Pieter Jacobus and
      Philip Rudolph.</p>
    <blockquote><b>She had a brother called Hercules Johannes, and she married a man called Hercules
      Johannes.</b> This archive notes that and does nothing with it. It is the kind of coincidence that
      invites a story, and there is no document behind any story it invites.</blockquote>
    <p>The notice is signed by her widower, <b>J. D. A. F. van Niekerk</b>, and it is one more piece of
      evidence that <b>Dordrecht</b> is where this side of the family married.
      <a href={u("/places/dordrecht/")}>Dordrecht &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="awl" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="indwe-1907">1907&ndash;1910 &middot; An eighth child, and the word MIJNWERKER</h2>
  <p class="dek">Four documents from the Dutch Reformed congregation at Indwe, found the day the register
    was built by typing <em>Indwe</em> into it. Between them they add a son this archive did not have, and
    they answer the last line of the family testimony &mdash; not in the way it was told, and not in the
    way this archive said it could not be answered.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">First, the confirmation class of 1907</h3>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:18px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1907-confirmation-class.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Page of the Indwe lidmaten register listing thirty-two young people confirmed on 31 May 1907, including Booijzen, Willem Hermanus, aged 21." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><em>Lidmaten Register, Gemeente Indwe</em> &mdash; thirty-two young people
        <strong>aangenomen 31-5-1907</strong>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Five names off one page</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="m" style="width:2.5rem">8</td><td><b>Booijzen, Willem Hermanus</b></td><td class="m">21</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">25</td><td><b>Roodt, Catharina Maria Elizabeth</b></td><td class="m">17</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">26</td><td><b>Roodt, Susanna Adriana</b></td><td class="m">15</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">27</td><td>Roodt, Maria Cath<sup>a</sup> Cornelia</td><td class="m">16</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">28</td><td>Roodt, James Johannes</td><td class="m">17</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">29</td><td>Roodt, Cornelis de Klerk</td><td class="m">21</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="cap" style="margin:14px 0 0">Everyone who matters to the next three years is on this one
        page, on one afternoon, in one room.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0;max-width:620px">
    <img src={u("/docs/lidmaten-1907-indwe.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="The full right-hand page of the Indwe lidmaten register for 1907, page 456, signed by the kerkeraad." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The page entire, <span class="m">456</span>. At the foot,
      <strong>"Indwe, 31 Aug 1907 &mdash; Leden des Kerkeraads"</strong> and three signatures.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1907-booijzen-21.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line eight of the 1907 confirmation list: Booijzen, Willem Hermanus, aged 21." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>"8. Booijzen, Willem Hermanus &mdash; 21"</strong> &mdash; and note the
      spelling: <b>ij</b> <em>and</em> a <b>z</b>, a form this archive had not seen before.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>Twenty-one in May 1907 means born about 1886. This archive's list of the children of
    <a href={u("/who/willem-hermanus-william-henry-booyzen/")}>Willem Hermanus Booyzen</a> and
    <a href={u("/who/catherine-maria-sophia-kathleen-barry/")}>Kathleen Barry</a> ran John Barry
    (c. 1884), then Johanna Catharina (1889). <b>There was a gap and nobody had noticed it.</b></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Then a marriage, and one word in it</h3>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:18px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/marriage-1908-indwe.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Duplicaat Origineel Register: marriage number 13 at Indwe, 21 September 1908, William Hermanus Booyzen and Susanna Adriana Roodt." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><em>Duplicaat Origineel Register</em>, Indwe, parish of Indwe, district
        <strong>Wodehouse</strong>. Certificate <strong>13</strong>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">21 September 1908</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td style="width:7rem">Groom</td><td><b>William Hermanus Booyzen</b>, 22, <em>jonkman</em></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bride</td><td><b>Susanna Adriana Roodt</b>, 16, <em>jongedochter</em></td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Beroep</b></td><td><b>MIJNWERKER</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Woonplaats</td><td><b>Dugmore Mijn</b> &mdash; both of them</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Toestemming</td><td><b>Ouders</b> &mdash; she was sixteen, so her parents had to consent</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Married by</td><td>J. J. Krige, predikant der Ned. Geref. Kerk</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1908-mijnwerker.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="The occupation and residence columns of the 1908 marriage register: Jonkman, Mijnwerker, Dugmore Mijn." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Rang of Beroep: Mijnwerker. Woonplaats tijdens het Huwelijk:
      Dugmore Mijn.</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <blockquote><b>A Booyzen, on a mine, in a document.</b></blockquote>
    <p>The last line of the family testimony reads: <em>"after the war our great great grandmother put
      herself and her kids on a train to Johannesburg where the boys initially all got jobs on the mines
      to make ends meet."</em> This archive tested it, found six occupations across five generations
      &mdash; saddler, farmer, mason, tree nurseryman, railway clerk, mason &mdash; and wrote, on the
      <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>open questions page</a>:
      <b><em>"Not one miner among them."</em></b></p>
    <p>There is one. He is twenty-two, he lives at the mine, and the register writes his trade in the
      column that asks for it. <a href={u("/rebels/")}>The testimony &rarr;</a></p>
    <div class="note">
      <b>But read what it actually says, because the detail is wrong in a way that matters.</b>
      Indwe is a <b>colliery town in the north-eastern Cape</b> &mdash; coal, not gold &mdash; and this
      is <b>1908</b>. The testimony puts the mines <em>after</em> a train to Johannesburg. The family was
      still at Indwe. So the claim is now <b>supported and mislocated at the same time</b>: the boys did
      go onto the mines, and the first one to do it did it three hundred miles from Johannesburg and
      years before the family got there. <a href={u("/places/indwe/")}>Indwe &rarr;</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">And his mother was standing at the font</h3>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:18px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/doopregister-1908-indwe.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Doop Register, Gemeente Indwe, page 381, entry 15: Booyzen, Willem Hermanus, born 31 May 1908." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><em>Doop Register, Gemeente Indwe</em>, page 381. Entry <strong>15</strong>,
        baptised <strong>6 December 1908</strong>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Entry 15</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td style="width:6.5rem">Kind</td><td><b>Willem Hermanus Booyzen</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Geboren</td><td><b>31 May 1908</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gedoopt</td><td><b>6 December 1908</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Ouders</td><td><b>Willem Hermanus Booyzen</b><br /><b>Susanna Adriana Roodt</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Getuigen</b></td><td>Cornelis Philippus Roodt<br />Catharina Wilhelmina Levina Buitendag<br /><b>Catharina Maria Sophia Barry</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Aanmerkingen</td><td><b>onecht</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1908-barry-godmother.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="The parents and godparents columns of the 1908 baptism, ending with Catharina Maria Sophia Barry, and the remark onecht." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Catharina Maria Sophia Barry</strong> &mdash; third godparent. And in
      the last column, <strong>onecht</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The archive holds exactly one <b>Catharina Maria Sophia Barry</b>: Kathleen, born 1859, married to
      Willem Hermanus Booyzen in 1879, widowed at Indwe in September 1905. Dutch Reformed registers enter
      women under their maiden names, and there she is, in December 1908, standing godmother.</p>
    <blockquote><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> <b>The miner is her son.</b> No document says so
      in words. What the documents say is that a Willem Hermanus Booyzen born about 1886 was living at
      Indwe, and that Kathleen Barry stood at his child's font. This archive is satisfied and says which
      of those two things is the evidence.</blockquote>
    <p>The remark column is the harder reading. <b>Onecht</b> &mdash; born out of wedlock. The child was
      born on 31 May 1908; the parents married on 21 September 1908; the baptism was on 6 December. The
      register records the child's status at birth and does not revise it.</p>
    <p>It also fixes where the family was. <b>Kathleen Barry was still at Indwe in December 1908</b>,
      three years and three months after her husband died there. Whatever else the train to Johannesburg
      was, it was not immediate.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">And then a second child, by a second Roodt</h3>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:18px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/doopregister-1910-indwe.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Doop Register, Gemeente Indwe, July 1910 to July 1911, entry 12." />
      <figcaption class="cap">The next book &mdash; <em>1 Julie 1910 &ndash; 30 Julie 1911</em>, entry
        <strong>12</strong>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Entry 12</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td style="width:6.5rem">Kind</td><td><b>Catharina Willemina Levina Booyzen</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Geboren</td><td><b>8 February 1909</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gedoopt</td><td><b>22 September 1910</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Vader</td><td><b>Willem Hermanus Booyzen</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Moeder</b></td><td><b>Catharina Maria Elizabeth Roodt</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Getuigen</td><td>Cornelis Philippus Roodt<br />Catharina Willemina Levina Buitendag</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Aanmerkingen</td><td><b>onecht &middot; gratis</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1910-second-roodt.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="The 1910 entry naming the father as Willem Hermanus Booyzen and the mother as Catharina Maria Elizabeth Roodt, with the remark onecht, gratis." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Moeder: Catharina Maria Elizabeth Roodt.</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>A different woman.</b> Both were confirmed in the same class on 31 May 1907 &mdash; Catharina
      Maria Elizabeth at seventeen, number 25 on the page, and Susanna Adriana at fifteen, number 26. The
      two children were born <b>eight months and eight days apart</b>, which settles the question of
      whether the second name is a clerk's slip for the first: it cannot be.</p>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m" style="width:11rem">Date</th><th>What the register says</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="m">31 May 1907</td><td>Willem Hermanus Booijzen, 21, confirmed at Indwe alongside Catharina Maria Elizabeth Roodt, 17, and Susanna Adriana Roodt, 15</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">31 May 1908</td><td><b>Willem Hermanus</b> born to Susanna Adriana Roodt</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">21 Sep 1908</td><td>he marries <b>Susanna Adriana Roodt</b>, 16, at Indwe &mdash; both of Dugmore Mijn, her parents consenting</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">6 Dec 1908</td><td>the boy baptised, entered <b>onecht</b>; his grandmother Kathleen Barry stands godmother</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">8 Feb 1909</td><td><b>Catharina Willemina Levina</b> born to <b>Catharina Maria Elizabeth Roodt</b> &mdash; four and a half months after the wedding</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">22 Sep 1910</td><td>she is baptised, entered <b>onecht, gratis</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:18px">This archive sets that down because the register does, and stops there.
      The two Roodt women are not further identified here and the relation between them is not stated
      anywhere read so far. The daughter is named for her godmother, <b>Catharina Willemina Levina
      Buitendag</b>, who stood at both fonts.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="windpump" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="o2899">1885 &middot; The estate this archive gave to the wrong man</h2>
  <p class="dek">No image, no transcription, no new document read. Just an index line, set against two
    that have been read &mdash; and a correction that moves a whole branch of this family onto the map.</p>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>Since the Transvaal references were first pulled out of NAAIRS, this archive has carried a row
      saying <span class="m">TAB MHG O/2899 &mdash; Jan Gerhardus Lodewikus Booyzen &mdash; 1885</span>,
      and attached it to the seventh child on the 1863 death notice: <b>Jan Gerhardus Lodewicus Booysen,
      born 4 December 1855</b>. His entry on
      <a href={u("/people/")}>the people index</a> read <em>"he died in the Transvaal at about
      thirty."</em></p>
    <blockquote><b>He did not. That sentence had no source except the estate reference itself.</b> The
      archive read the year off the file number, wrote it into his life as a death date, and then quoted
      the death date back as if it were evidence.</blockquote>
    <p>FamilySearch's index of the same Transvaal probate series gives the estate's deceased in full:</p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin:20px 0">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0">What the index says</h3>
    <table style="width:100%">
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:9rem">Name</td><td><b>Jan Gerardus Lodewicus Booijzen</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Relationship</td><td><b>Deceased</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Age</td><td><b>67</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Birthplace</td><td><b>Distrik Graaf Reinet</b> &mdash; birth year about <b>1818</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Died</td><td><b>25 May 1885</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Death place</td><td><b>Vaalbank, distrik Lichtenburg</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="cap" style="margin:14px 0 0">
      <span class="m">ark 1:1:QPTJ-R6KB</span> and <span class="m">1:1:Q2DG-52P9</span>, two index passes
      over the same estate.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>A man born in 1818 is not a man born in 1855.</b> And the archive already holds a
      <b>Jan Gerthardus Lodewikus Booijsen</b> born in the right decade in the right place: he is
      <b>fifth on the 1876 Sterfkennis</b> of Petrus Jacobus Booysen of Brakfontein, born Graaff-Reinet
      about 1788. <a href="#sterfkennis-1876">The 1876 notice &rarr;</a></p>
    <p>So the estate is <b>the uncle's</b>, not the nephew's, and two things follow.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:20px 0">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">First, a date comes off a man</h3>
      <p><b>Jan Gerhardus Lodewicus Booysen, born 4 December 1855</b>, no longer dies in 1885. This
        archive does not know what became of him, and now says so. He was seven when his mother died and
        twenty-nine when his father's estate was wound up, and after that there is nothing.</p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">One more name back in the queue at
        <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>open questions</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Second, a farm joins the line</h3>
      <p>The uncle died at <b>Vaalbank, district Lichtenburg</b>. This archive has already read a
        Sterfkennis from that farm: <b>Magdalena Susanna Maria van den Berg, born Booyzen</b>, died there
        on 1 May 1920, aged 59 years 9 months, <b>father entered as Jan Gerhardus Lodewicus Booyzen</b>.</p>
      <p>She was recorded here as a lead that was <em>"NOT PROVED"</em>. She is his daughter, born about
        1860, on a farm where her father had died thirty-five years before.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <b>This is the third time the western Transvaal has moved closer.</b> First the 1876 Sterfkennis put
    the direct ancestor in district Rustenburg in 1876, which cost this archive its flat claim that the
    Marico Booyzens <em>"are not this family"</em>. Now his son is at Vaalbank in the district of
    Lichtenburg &mdash; the next district west &mdash; and his granddaughter is still on the same farm in
    1920. <b>Marico is the third district in that row.</b> It still is not proved, and the atlas still
    says unresolved. But the distance keeps shrinking, and it has only ever shrunk in one direction.
    <a href={u("/atlas/")}>The atlas &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px"><b>What this section is not.</b> The estate file itself has not
    been read. FamilySearch offers no image for it &mdash; the record page says <em>"Check Image
    Availability"</em> where the others say <em>"View original document"</em> &mdash; so what is above
    rests on an index entry, checked against two documents that have been read. The file is at the
    Transvaal Archives in Pretoria and this archive has not seen it.
    <a href={u("/sources/")}>Sources &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<Rule name="thorn" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="coetzee-1943">1930, filed 1943 &middot; A death notice thirteen years late</h2>
  <p class="dek">Death notice <span class="m">84992</span>, Cape Town. This archive went looking for it
    because the Master's own index files it under <b>BOOYZEN, with a z</b>. It found that, and a
    correction of exactly the kind it had just finished making about somebody else.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:20px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1943-isabella-coetzee.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1943 death notice number 84992 for Isabella Fredrika Booyzen, born Coetzee, of Middelburg, Cape." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Stamped <strong>No. 84992 &middot; 12 April 1943</strong>. Signed
        <strong>G. J. Booyzen</strong> &mdash; "Survivor, Present at death".</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td style="width:7rem">1. Name</td><td><b>Isabella Fredrika Booyzen (born Coetzee)</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>2. Birthplace</td><td><b>Middelburg</b> &middot; European</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3. Parents</td><td><b>Pieter Coetzee</b>, deceased<br /><b>Isabella Fredrika Coetzee</b>, deceased</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4. Age</td><td><b>55 years 11 months</b> &mdash; born about May 1874</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7a. Husband</td><td><b>Gert Jacobus Booyzen</b>, in community of property</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7c. Married at</td><td><b>Middelburg, Cape</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>8. Died</td><td><b>31 March 1930</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>9. Where</td><td>Church Street, Middelburg</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10. Children</td><td>Isabella Fredrika du Plessis &middot; <b>Pieter Jacobus Booyzen (deceased)</b> &middot; Gerhardus Jacobus Booyzen &mdash; all majors</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figure style="margin:18px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1930-married-and-died.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Questions seven and eight: married to Gert Jacobus Booyzen in community of property, at Middelburg Cape; died 31st March 1930." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>"31st March 1930."</strong> The form is dated <strong>1 April
      1943</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <blockquote><span class="chip inf">A correction</span> <b>This archive had her dying in 1943.</b>
      She died on <b>31 March 1930</b>. 1943 is when her husband finally filed.</blockquote>
    <p>The wrong date came from the same place as the last one: <b>the year in the estate reference,
      read as though it were the year of the death.</b> Three days ago the archive took a death date off
      a man on exactly that ground. It had made the same mistake here and had not noticed.
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>What was taken back &rarr;</a></p>
    <p>Thirteen years is a long gap, and the form does not explain it. What it does show is that
      <b>Gert Jacobus Booyzen was alive on 1 April 1943</b>, signing as survivor, eight years before his
      own estate was opened in the Transvaal.</p>
    <p>And their son <b>Pieter Jacobus</b> &mdash; the boy baptised with a z at Middelburg on
      12 February 1905, whom this archive has been carrying as a living name &mdash; is entered here as
      <b>deceased</b>. The Cape civil index gives a Petrus Jacobus Booyzen dying at Middelburg on
      <b>7 June 1937</b>, aged about thirty-two.</p>
  </div>
  <figure style="margin:18px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1943-coetzee-parents.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question three of the 1943 notice: Father Pieter Coetzee, deceased; Mother Isabella Fredrika Coetzee, deceased." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Question three, answered. <strong>Pieter Coetzee</strong> and
      <strong>Isabella Fredrika Coetzee</strong> &mdash; and the daughter carries her mother's whole
      name. <a href={u("/namesakes/")}>The namesakes &rarr;</a></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p class="note"><b>And the spelling.</b> Every occurrence on this form is
    <b>BOOYZEN</b> &mdash; in the Master's file number, in her name, in her husband's, in her sons', and
    in the signature. A Cape official document of 1943, thirty-eight years after the Indwe clerk wrote
    Booysen on her father-in-law's death form. <a href={u("/name/")}>The name &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<Rule name="koppie" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="johanna-1843">1843 &middot; The oldest Booyzen death notice in the Cape &mdash; and a warning</h2>
  <p class="dek">Death notice <span class="m">6167</span>, filed at Cape Town on 21 June 1843. It is not
    this family. It is put here because of what it nearly is.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:20px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1843-johanna-booyzen.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1843 death notice number 6167 for Johanna Booyzen, widow of Charles du Plessis, of 28 Roze Street, Cape Town." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Under <em>Section 9, Ordinance 104</em> &mdash; a generation older than
        every other form in this archive.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td style="width:7rem">1. Name</td><td><b>Johanna Booyzen</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>2. Birth-place</td><td><b>This Colony</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>3. Parents</td><td><b>"both dead"</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>4. Age</td><td><b>89 years, 2 months &amp; 26 days</b> &mdash; born about <b>20 March 1754</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>6. Condition</td><td>Widow of <b>Charles du Plessis, D<sup>ls</sup> son</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>7. Died</td><td><b>16 June 1843</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>8. Where</td><td><b>No. 28 Roze Street</b>, Cape Town</td></tr>
          <tr><td>9. Children</td><td>Johanna Petronella du Plessis, married to Benjamin Solomon</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10. Property</td><td><b>"no property whatever except a Bond amounting per balance to &pound;15"</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figure style="margin:18px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1843-age-89.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question four of the 1843 death notice: age of the deceased, 89 years 2 months and 26 days." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>&ldquo;89 years, 2 months &amp; 26 days.&rdquo;</strong> An age given
      to the day, which is what puts her birth at about <b>20 March 1754</b> &mdash; and which is exactly
      why this notice is dangerous. <b>1754 and 1843 are the only two dates this archive has for
      Magdelena Elisabeth de Beer</b>, and they came off a family tree with no source.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure style="margin:18px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1843-both-dead.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line three of the 1843 death notice: Names of the Parents of the Deceased — both dead." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>"both dead."</strong> 1843 &mdash; and the printed question is the
      same one that is still failing a century later.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Question three has now failed <b>seven times</b> in this archive, and this is the earliest and the
      bluntest. Asked for the <em>names</em> of the parents, the informant wrote down whether they were
      alive. <a href="#daschner-1942">Where it works &rarr;</a></p>

    <div class="note">
      <b>Now the part that needs care.</b> This archive's generation seven holds
      <b>Magdelena Elisabeth de Beer</b>, and the only dates it has for her are
      <b>born 1754, died 1843</b> &mdash; taken from the family tree, with no source stated anywhere.
      <br /><br />
      Here is a woman named <b>Booyzen</b>, <b>born about March 1754</b>, who <b>died in 1843</b>, whose
      estate sits in the Cape Master's papers &mdash; and whose name is not Magdelena, not Elisabeth, and
      not de Beer. She was the widow of a du Plessis and she died in a rented house in Cape Town.
      <br /><br />
      <b>This archive does not say they are the same person. It says the opposite.</b> But a birth year
      and a death year matching to the year, on the only two facts the tree offers, is exactly the
      material a records-matcher builds a wrong person out of &mdash; and it is worth knowing that such
      a record exists before somebody finds it and decides it is her.
      <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open question zero &rarr;</a>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:18px">One thing she does show, and it is not nothing.
      <b>Booyzen was a Cape surname before this family's paper trail starts.</b> She was born in the
      Colony in 1754, which is a year earlier than anything else in this archive, and she died owning
      fifteen pounds of somebody else's debt.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="wagon" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="barry-1903">1903 &middot; "Irishman born in Ireland"</h2>
  <p class="dek">Entry 74, district Barkly East. The registration of John Barry's death, made by the son
    who was standing there &mdash; and it settles a question this archive has carried since its first
    week.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:20px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/death-registration-1903-john-barry.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1903 Form of Information of a Death for John Barry of Manor Owen, Barkly East." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><em>Form of Information of a Death, Act No. 7 of 1894.</em> Registered
        <strong>28 October 1903</strong>, entry <strong>74</strong>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td style="width:7rem">1. Name</td><td><b>John Barry</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>3. Residence</td><td><b>Manor Owen, Ward IV, Barkly East</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>4. Age</td><td><b>eighty four years two months</b> &mdash; born about June 1819</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>5. Race</b></td><td><b>"Irishman born in Ireland"</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>7. Occupation</td><td><b>Carpenter</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>8. Died</td><td><b>25th August 1903</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>11. Causes</td><td><b>Inflammation and Old Age</b>, one month seven days</td></tr>
          <tr><td>12. Doctor</td><td>Dr Helm</td></tr>
          <tr><td>13&ndash;15. Informant</td><td><b>Richard Barry</b> &mdash; "Son present at death", of Manor Owen</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Footnote (b)</td><td><b>"eleven"</b> children</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figure style="margin:18px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1903-irishman.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question five of the 1903 registration: Race — Irishman born in Ireland." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>5. Race &mdash; "Irishman born in Ireland."</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <blockquote><span class="chip doc">Open question 5, answered</span> <b>He was born in Ireland.</b>
      Not at sea, not in Sunderland, not in an unknown place. His son wrote it down eight weeks after
      burying him, on a form that carries the penalties for perjury at the top of the page.</blockquote>
    <p>This archive has run that question three ways for weeks. The family tree guessed
      <em>Sunderland, Durham</em> with a question mark. His own <b>1915</b> death notice &mdash; sworn by
      a different son, twelve years after the fact &mdash; types
      <em>"At sea, exact whereabouts unknown. British."</em> And a research note on the same tree called
      him <em>"an Irishman &hellip; reputed to be anti English."</em></p>
    <p><b>The 1903 form beats the 1915 notice on every count that matters.</b> It is twelve years closer
      to the man; it was made by a son who was in the room; and it is a statutory registration rather
      than an estate paper. <b>Where the two disagree, this archive now follows 1903.</b>
      <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a></p>
    <p>Which also settles the date. The tree says 25 August 1903; the 1915 notice says the 3rd. The
      registration says <b>25th August</b>, and the man who signed it was present.
      <a href={u("/disputed/")}>Disputed &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:22px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1903-carpenter.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Questions six to eight: married, occupation Carpenter, date of death 25th August 1903." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Carpenter.</strong> Not a farmer &mdash; and the first trade this
        archive has for him.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1903-richard-son.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Informant: Richard Barry, son present at death." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Richard Barry &mdash; "Son present at death."</strong> He signs;
        he does not make a mark.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>And Manorowen was his own house.</b> This archive knew the farm only from 1915, when
      Margaretha Kolbe died there and <a href={u("/who/richard-peter-barry/")}>Richard Peter Barry</a>
      signed as "son in whose house deceased died". The 1903 form puts John Barry himself there, living
      and dying at <b>Manor Owen, Ward IV</b>, with Richard resident at the same address.</p>
    <blockquote><b>Eleven children.</b> The footnote asks for the total and the answer is eleven. This
      archive has named nine. <a href={u("/barry/")}>The Barrys &rarr;</a></blockquote>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="harriet-1912">1912 &middot; "Dutch, born in the District of Barkly East"</h2>
  <p class="dek">Nine years later, the same farm, the same registrar's form &mdash; and a grandchild of
    the Irishman is entered under a different nation.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:20px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/death-registration-1912-harriet-barry.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1912 death registration for Harriet Louisa Barry of Manor Owen, Barkly East." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Registered <strong>5 November 1912</strong>, district Barkly East.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td style="width:7rem">1. Name</td><td><b>Harriet Louisa Barry</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>3. Residence</td><td><b>Manor Owen, Barkly East District</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>4. Age</td><td><b>18 years 5 months and 3 days</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>5. Race</b></td><td><b>"Dutch, born in the District of Barkly East"</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>7. Occupation</td><td><b>Farm domestic work</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>8. Died</td><td><b>7 September 1912</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>9. Where</td><td><b>Cherry Ville</b>, Barkly East District</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10. Burial</td><td><b>Manor Owen</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>11. Cause</td><td><b>Fever (Typhoid)</b>, thirty days</td></tr>
          <tr><td>13&ndash;15. Informant</td><td><b>H. N. Barry</b> &mdash; "Father present when died", of Manorowen</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figure style="margin:18px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1912-dutch.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Questions four and five of the 1912 registration: age eighteen years five months and three days; race Dutch, born in the District of Barkly East." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>"Dutch, born in the District of Barkly East."</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>She is <b>Hendrik Nicholaas Barry's</b> daughter &mdash; he signs as father, present when she
      died &mdash; and her mother is <b>Anna Johanna Maria Schoeman</b>. Her baptism is on record at
      Barkly West in October 1894.</p>
    <blockquote><b>Two generations, two nations, one registrar's form.</b> In 1903 the grandfather is an
      <em>Irishman born in Ireland</em>. In 1912 the granddaughter, dead of typhoid at eighteen on the
      same farm, is <em>Dutch</em>.</blockquote>
    <p>That is what the family's own testimony says happened, in the one line of it nobody has
      questioned: <em>"as his son and grandson were married to Dutch girls their activity during the Boer
      War would be interesting."</em> Here it is on a government form, and it took nine years.
      <a href={u("/rebels/")}>The testimony &rarr;</a></p>
    <p>She also fixes a farm. She lived at <b>Manorowen</b>, died at <b>Cherryville</b> and was buried at
      Manorowen &mdash; so both are Barry places, and Cherryville is the third named Barry farm in this
      archive after Manorowen and Jerpoint. <a href={u("/atlas/")}>The atlas &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="koppie" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="hendrik-1908">1908 &middot; The son who signed the 1876 notice, found &mdash; and a mother nobody had</h2>
  <p class="dek">Estate <span class="m">14380</span>, Transvaal. <b>Hendrik Stephanus Booysen</b> put his
    name to his father's Sterfkennis at Brakfontein in September 1876. Thirty-two years later his own
    was filed, and it resolves a contradiction this archive has carried as <em>Serious</em> since the
    first week.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:20px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1908-hendrik-stephanus.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1908 Transvaal death notice for Hendrik Stephanus Booysen, estate 14380." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Under the <em>Administration of Estates Ordinance 1902</em>, stamped
        <strong>16 September 1909</strong>. Signed <strong>O. J. S. Booysen &mdash; son of the
        deceased</strong>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td style="width:6.5rem">Name</td><td><b>Hendrik Stephanus Booysen</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Birthplace</td><td><b>Cape Colony</b> &middot; nationality <b>Dutch</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Father</b></td><td><b>Petrus Jacobus Booysen</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Mother</b></td><td><b>Elizabetha Booysen, born van der Merwe</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Age</td><td><b>79 years 11 months</b> &mdash; born about June 1828</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Condition</td><td><b>Farmer</b> &middot; widower</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wives</td><td>1. Anna Catharina Johanna, born <b>Olivier</b><br />2. Charlotta Elizabetha, born <b>Dormehl</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Married at</b></td><td><b>ZEERUST, TRANSVAAL</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Died</td><td><b>11 May 1908</b>, at the house of Willem Grobelaar, <b>Koedoesfontein, district Rustenburg</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Children</td><td>Petrus Jacobus (deceased) &middot; Ockert Johannes Stephanus &middot; Anna Catharina Grobelaar &middot; Aletta Catharina Johanna Venter</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:30px">A mother this archive did not have</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1908-elizabetha-van-der-merwe.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line three of the 1908 notice: Father Petrus Jacobus Booysen; Mother Elizabetha Booysen, born van der Merwe." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>"Elizabetha Booysen, born van der Merwe."</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>This archive's <a href={u("/disputed/")}>disputed page</a> has carried a contradiction marked
      <span class="chip inf">Serious</span> since the beginning:</p>
    <blockquote><em>"Anna Maria van der Merwe (1785&ndash;1825) dies before 8 March 1825 &mdash; but
      Hendrik Stefanus is recorded born 23 August 1829. The same pattern one generation earlier: an
      unrecorded second marriage."</em></blockquote>
    <p><b>The second marriage is no longer unrecorded.</b> The mother is <b>Elizabetha van der Merwe</b>,
      and the son's own age on the form &mdash; seventy-nine years eleven months at 11 May 1908 &mdash;
      puts his birth at about June 1828, three years after the first wife died. Two van der Merwe wives,
      one after the other. The contradiction was never a contradiction; it was a missing row.</p>
    <p>And it repairs something this page has been apologising for. The
      <a href="#sterfkennis-1876">1876 Sterfkennis</a> lists eight children and names no mother for any
      of them, so this archive drew them all under Anna Maria van der Merwe with a warning printed
      beside them. <b>At least one of the eight is Elizabetha's.</b>
      <a href={u("/households/")}>The households &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">And then the place of the marriage</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1908-zeerust.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Place of last marriage: Zeerust, Transvaal; died eleventh day of May 1908." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>"Zeerust, Transvaal."</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <blockquote><b>Zeerust is the principal town of the district of MARICO.</b></blockquote>
    <p>This archive spent weeks saying of the Marico Booyzens that <em>"they are not this family"</em>,
      and has been walking that back for three days &mdash; Rustenburg in 1876, Lichtenburg in 1885 and
      1920, and Marico left standing as the one district still held at arm's length.</p>
    <p><b>Here is a son of the 1876 man marrying at Zeerust.</b> It is still not a link to any of the
      seven compensation claims, and the atlas still says unresolved. But the last district in the row
      has now had a documented Booysen marriage in it, and the sentence this archive withdrew was
      wronger than it knew. <a href={u("/atlas/")}>The atlas &rarr;</a> &middot;
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>What was taken back &rarr;</a></p>
    <p class="cap">He died in his son-in-law's house at Koedoesfontein, a farmer with no movables, some
      land, and mutual wills with both of his dead wives. His son Ockert Johannes Stephanus signed for
      him, as he had signed for his own father thirty-two years before.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="huwelijk-1892">1892 &middot; The marriage the 1908 notice named, found &mdash; and it takes four children off one mother</h2>
  <p class="dek">Entry <span class="m">13</span> of the <em>Duplicaat Origineel Register</em>,
    Huwelijks-Bevestiging te <b>Zeerust, Distrikt Marico</b>, <b>14 April 1892</b>. The 1908 death
    notice said only <em>Zeerust, Transvaal</em>. Here is the register page, and it answers three
    questions the notice left open and contradicts one thing this archive published about it.</p>

  <figure style="margin:20px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/huwelijk-1892-zeerust.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="The full page of the Zeerust duplicate marriage register for April 1892, one entry on the sheet." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>DUPLICAAT ORIGINEEL REGISTER &middot; Huwelijks-Bevestiging te
      Zeerust, Distrikt Marico.</strong> One marriage to the sheet, with the ten-shilling revenue stamp
      and the Landdrost's seal at the right, and the whole of the top two-thirds of the frame taken up by
      the black cloth the page was photographed on.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure style="margin:20px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1892-entry-13.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Entry thirteen of the Zeerust marriage register, 14 April 1892: Hendrik Stefanus Booijzen, 63, Graaff-Reinet, widower; Charlotta Elizabeth Roos born Dormehl, 51, Graaff-Reinet, widow." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Entry thirteen, entire. The columns are
      <em>Wanneer gehuwd &middot; Volle naam der gehuwden &middot; Ouderdom &middot; Waar geboren &middot;
      Conditie &middot; Met of zonder huwelijksvoorwaarden &middot; Woonplaats tijdens het huwelijk</em>.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:12rem">Column</th><th>Hendrik Stefanus Booijzen</th><th>Charlotta Elizabeth Roos</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Ouderdom</td><td class="m"><b>63</b> &mdash; born about 1829</td><td class="m"><b>51</b> &mdash; born about 1841</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Waar geboren</b></td><td><b>Graaff-Reinet, K.K.</b></td><td><b>Graaff-Reinet, K.K.</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Conditie</b></td><td><b>Weduwnaar</b></td><td><b>Weduwe</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Huwelijksvoorwaarden</td><td class="m">zonder</td><td class="m">zonder</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Woonplaats</td><td><b>Brakfontein, Rustenburg</b></td><td><b>Driefontein, Marico</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consent</td><td class="m">eigen</td><td class="m">eigen</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:26px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Graaff-Reinet, in his own marriage</h3>
      <p>The 1908 notice gave his birthplace as <b>Cape Colony</b> and nothing finer. This gives the town.
        It is the fourth man of this family documented as born at <b>Graaff-Reinet</b> &mdash;
        Petrus Jacobus baptised there in 1788, Petrus Jacobus born there in 1812,
        Jan Gerardus Lodewicus born there in 1818, and now Hendrik Stefanus about 1829.
        <a href={u("/atlas/")}>The atlas &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Brakfontein, sixteen years on</h3>
      <p>In September 1876 he signed his father's Sterfkennis at <b>Brakfontein</b> in the district of
        Rustenburg. In April 1892 he gives his home as <b>Brakfontein, Rustenburg</b>. He did not leave.
        The family that this archive spent weeks placing in the north-eastern Cape had a branch sitting
        on one farm in the Transvaal for at least sixteen years.
        <a href="#sterfkennis-1876">The 1876 notice &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">She was a widow, and her name is not what we published</h3>
      <p>This archive has called her <b>Charlotta Elizabetha Dormehl</b>. The register writes
        <b>Charlotta Elizabeth Roos, geboren Dormehl</b>, condition <b>weduwe</b>, and she signs
        <b>C. E. Roos geb. Dormehl</b>. <b>Dormehl was her maiden name and Roos was her first husband's.</b>
        She came to this marriage from Driefontein in Marico with a married name of her own.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And he signs with an <em>s</em></h3>
      <p>The clerk writes him into the register as <b>Booijzen</b>. Two inches below, in the box headed
        <em>Dit Huwelijk is voltrokken tusschen ons</em>, he signs his own name <b>H. S. Booysen</b>.
        The <b>z</b> and the <b>s</b> are not two families and they are not even two clerks. On this page
        they are one man and the official writing him down.
        <a href={u("/name/")}>The name &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figure style="margin:26px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1892-signatures.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="The foot of the page: witnesses J.H.J. Joubert and A.S. Engelbrecht, and the signatures H S Booysen and C E Roos geb. Dormehl." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Witnesses <strong>J. H. J. Joubert</strong> and
      <strong>A. S. Engelbrecht</strong>. Signed <strong>H. S. Booysen</strong> and
      <strong>C. E. Roos geb. Dormehl</strong>. Reference <span class="m">14413</span>.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <b>The correction this page forces.</b>
    <br /><br />
    The 1908 death notice names four children &mdash; <b>Petrus Jacobus</b> (deceased),
    <b>Ockert Johannes Stephanus</b>, <b>Anna Catharina Grobelaar</b> and
    <b>Aletta Catharina Johanna Venter</b> &mdash; and does not say which wife they belong to. This
    archive drew all four under the second wife, <b>Charlotta</b>, because she was the marriage it could
    name.
    <br /><br />
    <b>They cannot be hers.</b> She married him on 14 April 1892 at the age of <b>fifty-one</b>, a widow.
    By 1908 two of the four are married women carrying their husbands' surnames, one is dead, and the
    fourth signs his father's death notice as an adult son. Every one of them was born long before 1892.
    <b>The four children belong to the first wife, Anna Catharina Johanna Olivier</b>, and the household
    that has stood empty on this site since the 1908 notice was read is the one they go in.
    <a href={u("/households/")}>The households &rarr;</a> &middot;
    <a href={u("/corrections/")}>What was taken back &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p><b>What it does not give.</b> The register has no column for parents. Neither party's father or
      mother is named, so this page cannot be used to test Hendrik Stefanus against the 1876 list a
      second time &mdash; the 1908 notice is still the only document that puts
      <b>Elizabetha van der Merwe</b> in this family. What 1892 adds is the town, the farm, the
      condition of both parties, and a date the archive did not have.</p>
    <p class="cap">South Africa, Transvaal, Civil Marriages, 1870&ndash;1931 &middot; FamilySearch
      <span class="m">1:1:QPH1-9CX8</span>, film <span class="m">007747932</span> image
      <span class="m">01608</span>, read 13 September 2026.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="brakfontein-1891">1891 &middot; Two deaths at Brakfontein in five days, and a woman this archive had thrown away</h2>
  <p class="dek">This site has carried a woman called <b>Anna Johanna Booijzen, born Alberts</b> for a
    week, under the heading <em>NOT PROVED TO BE KIN</em>, on a farm this site called
    <em>Boekfontein</em>. Read at full resolution, her death notice says neither of those things. She is
    the <b>first wife of Hendrik Stefanus Booijsen</b>, she died on <b>Brakfontein</b> &mdash; the family
    farm &mdash; and the surname the archive read as <em>Alberts</em> has no <em>b</em>, no <em>r</em>,
    no <em>t</em> and no <em>s</em> in it.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:20px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/sterfkennis-1891-anna-olivier.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Sterfkennis 6332, 1891: Anna Johanna Booijzen, born Olivier, of Brakfontein, district Rustenburg." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Sterfkennis 6332</strong> &mdash; <em>Overeenkomstig de Wet op de
        Weeskamer, 10 November 1869</em>. Twelve questions, all of them answered.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <div class="scroll">
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1. Naam</td><td><b>Anna Johanna Booijzen, gebooren Oliefier</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>2. Geboorteplaats</td><td><b>District Cradock, Cap Kolonie</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>3. Ouders</b></td><td><b>Ocken Johannes Oliefier</b> en <b>Anna Johanna Botha</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>4. Ouderdom</td><td><b>67 jaar 8 maanden en 1 dag</b> &mdash; born about 8 September 1823</td></tr>
            <tr><td>5. Beroep</td><td>Landbouwster</td></tr>
            <tr><td>6. Staat</td><td><b>gehuwd</b> &mdash; not a widow. Her husband outlived her.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>7. Overleden</td><td><b>9 Mei 1891</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>8. Waar</b></td><td><b>op de plaas BRAKFONTIJN, district Rustenburg</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>9. Testament</td><td>Ja, bekend</td></tr>
            <tr><td>10&ndash;11. Boedel</td><td>roerend en onroerend, <b>boven £25</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>12. Kinderen</b></td><td><b>Allen mondig</b> &mdash; seven, all of age</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figure style="margin:20px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1891-anna-name-parents.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Questions one to three of Sterfkennis 6332: Anna Johanna Booijzen gebooren Oliefier; District Cradock, Cap Kolonie; Ocken Johannes Oliefier en Anna Johanna Botha." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The first three questions. <strong>"Anna Johanna Booijzen, gebooren
      Oliefier" &middot; "District Cradock, Cap Kolonie" &middot; "Ocken Johannes Oliefier en Anna
      Johanna Botha."</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">First, the farm</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1891-anna-brakfontein.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Questions six to eight: gehuwd; 9 May 1891; op de plaas Brakfontijn, district Rustenburg." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>"op de plaas Brakfontijn, Destrict Rustenburg."</strong> This archive
      read that as <em>Boekfontein</em> and filed her as a stranger.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>Brakfontein is not a strange farm. It is the farm.</b> Sterfkennis No. 678 for
      <b>Petrus Jacobus Booijsen</b>, the oldest document on this site, was written at Brakfontein on
      14 September 1876 and signed there by his son <b>Hendrik Stefanus</b>. Sixteen years after that,
      the <a href="#huwelijk-1892">Zeerust register</a> gives Hendrik Stefanus's home as
      <b>Brakfontein, Rustenburg</b>. A woman dying on that farm in 1891 was never a stranger. One letter
      &mdash; a <em>k</em> read for an <em>r</em> &mdash; kept her off this family for a week.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Then the name</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1891-the-maiden-name.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="The maiden name on line one of Sterfkennis 6332, enlarged: gebooren Oliefier." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Line one, enlarged three times. <strong>"Gebooren Oliefier."</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The letters are <b>o &middot; l &middot; i &middot; e &middot; f &middot; i &middot; e &middot; r</b>.
      There is no <em>b</em>, no <em>r-t-s</em>, and no second syllable that could carry
      <em>-berts</em>. <b>Oliefier is how a man who writes</b> <em>Booijzen</em>, <em>Stefanes</em> and
      <em>Brakfontijn</em> <b>spells OLIVIER.</b></p>
    <p>And the archive does not have to guess, because <b>her own son wrote her name down again</b>.
      Seventeen years later, filling in his father's death notice, <b>Ockert Johannes Stephanus Booysen</b>
      gave the first wife as <b>"Anna Catharina Johanna Booysen, born Olivier"</b>.
      <a href="#hendrik-1908">The 1908 notice &rarr;</a></p>
    <blockquote>Three documents, three spellings of one woman: <b>Anna Johanna</b> on her own notice,
      <b>Anna Catrena</b> on her son's, <b>Anna Catharina Johanna</b> on her husband's. Put together, she
      is <b>Anna Catharina Johanna Olivier</b>, and every one of them agrees on the surname.</blockquote>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Seven children, all of age</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1891-anna-children.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question twelve of Sterfkennis 6332, listing seven children, all of age." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Question twelve, entire. <strong>"Allen mondig."</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:22rem">As written, 1891</th><th>What became of them</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>Petrus Jacobus Booijsen</b></td><td>On the 1908 notice, and <b>already dead by then</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Ocken Johannes</b></td><td><b>Ockert Johannes Stephanus Booysen</b>, who signed his father's death notice in 1908 &mdash; and who is named for <b>his mother's father</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Anna Johanna Maria</td><td class="m">Not yet traced.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Elesabettha Maria Cornelya</td><td class="m">Not yet traced.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Alettha Catrina Johanna</b></td><td><b>Aletta Catharina Johanna Venter</b> on the 1908 notice, married in community of property to Stephanus Andries Venter.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Magdalena Johanna</td><td class="m">Not yet traced. The name comes down from <b>Magdalena Elisabeth de Beer</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Hendrick Stefanus Nicolaas</b></td><td><b>Dead five days after his mother</b>, on the same farm. His notice is below.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">
    <b>Where "Ockert Johannes" comes from.</b> This archive has had an <b>Ockert Johannes Stephanus
    Booysen</b> since the 1908 notice was read and no explanation for the name &mdash; it appears nowhere
    else in six generations of Booysens. Line 3 of his mother's death notice answers it:
    <b>Ocken Johannes Oliefier</b>, her father. The name came in with her, and it came from the Cape.
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:36px">And five days later, her son</h3>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:18px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/sterfkennis-1891-hendrik-nicolaas.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Sterfkennis 6361, 1891: Hendrick Stefanus Booijsen, 29, of Brakfontein, district Rustenburg." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Sterfkennis 6361.</strong> The sheet is torn across below question
        eleven, so the signature is gone.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <div class="scroll">
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1. Naam</td><td><b>Hendrick Stefanes Booijsen</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>2. Geboorteplaats</td><td>a farm in <b>district Rustenburg</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>3. Ouders</b></td><td><b>Hendrick Stefanus Booijsen</b> en <b>Anna Catrena, gebooren Oliefier</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>4. Ouderdom</td><td><b>29 jaar 3 maanden en 14 dagen</b> &mdash; born about 31 January 1862</td></tr>
            <tr><td>5. Beroep</td><td>Landbouwer</td></tr>
            <tr><td>6. Staat</td><td><b>ongehuwd</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>7. Overleden</td><td><b>14 Mei 1891</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>8. Waar</b></td><td><b>op de plaas BRAKFONTIJN, district Rustenburg</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>9. Testament</td><td>Niet bekend</td></tr>
            <tr><td>10&ndash;11. Boedel</td><td><b>alleen roerende</b>, <b>niet boven £25</b></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1891-nicolaas-brakfontein.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Questions four to nine of Sterfkennis 6361: 29 jaar 3 maanden en 14 dagen; landbouwer; ongehuwd; 14 Mei 1891; op de plaas Brakfontijn, Destrict Rustenburg." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>"ongehuwd" &middot; "14 Mei 1891" &middot; "op de plaas Brakfontijn,
      Destrict Rustenburg."</strong> The same farm as his mother's, five days on.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1891-nicolaas-parents.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line three of Sterfkennis 6361: Hendrick Stefanus Booijsen en Anna Catrena gebooren Oliefier." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Line three, enlarged. <strong>"Hendrick Stefanes Booijsen en Anna Catrena
      gebooren Oliefier."</strong> The same word, in the same hand, on the second notice.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p><b>A twenty-nine-year-old farmer, unmarried, with nothing but movables worth under twenty-five
      pounds</b>, dead on his father's farm five days after his mother. The archive cannot say what
      killed either of them and the notices do not ask. What it can say is that <b>Brakfontein lost two
      people in one week in May 1891</b>, and that the man left standing was a widower of sixty-two with
      six grown children &mdash; who married again, at Zeerust, eleven months later.</p>
    <blockquote><b>This is what the archive gets back.</b> A wife with a name, her parents, her birth
      district and her seven children; a son nobody knew existed; a farm confirmed as the family's own;
      and the origin of a forename that had no business being there. All of it came out of a page this
      site had already found, already filed, and read wrongly twice.</blockquote>
    <p class="cap">South Africa, Transvaal, Probate Records from the Master of the Supreme Court,
      1869&ndash;1961 &middot; Sterfkennis <span class="m">6332</span> (FamilySearch
      <span class="m">1:1:QPTM-SCPJ</span>, film <span class="m">007897335</span> image
      <span class="m">00647</span>) and Sterfkennis <span class="m">6361</span> (FamilySearch
      <span class="m">1:1:QPRB-R43G</span>, same film image <span class="m">00675</span>).
      Read 13 September 2026.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="rustenburg-register">1862&ndash;1894 &middot; The Rustenburg register, where an index has to stand in for the page</h2>
  <p class="dek">Five entries from the <b>Netherdutch Reformed Church registers of Rustenburg</b>, held at
    the Pretoria archive. <b>Not one of them has an image</b> &mdash; FamilySearch says
    <em>Image Unavailable</em> against every one, so what follows is a volunteer's transcription and
    nothing else. It is set out separately from the read documents for that reason. It also contains the
    single most useful line this archive found today.</p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:9rem" class="m">Date</th><th style="width:20rem">Entry</th><th>What it says</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="m">25 Dec 1862<br /><span class="cap">rec. 1046, p. 185</span></td>
          <td><strong>Baptism &mdash; Hendrik Nicolaas Stephanus Booijsen</strong>, born 31 Jan 1862</td>
          <td>Father <b>Hendrik Stephanus Booijsen</b>. Mother <b><u>Anna Johanna Olivier</u></b>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="m">3 Apr 1869<br /><span class="cap">p. 75</span></td>
          <td><strong>Lidmaat &mdash; Petrus Jacobus Boo&yuml;sen</strong></td>
          <td>Born <b>20 April 1851</b>. Admitted a member at Rustenburg, aged seventeen.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="m">3 Apr 1869</td>
          <td><strong>Lidmaat &mdash; &ldquo;Ceker&rdquo; Johannes Stephanus Boo&yuml;sen</strong></td>
          <td>Born <b>15 September 1852</b>. Admitted <b>the same day as his brother</b>. The forename is
            indexed <em>Ceker</em> and is <b>Ockert</b>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="m">30 Jan 1882<br /><span class="cap">rec. 6134, p. 469</span></td>
          <td><strong>Baptism &mdash; Hendrik Stephanus Nicolaas Booyen</strong>, born 4 Jan 1882</td>
          <td>Father <b>Ockert Johannes Stephanus Booysen</b>, mother <b>Elisabetta Johanna Sophia
            Myburgh</b>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="m">1889 &amp; 4 Nov 1894</td>
          <td><strong>Baptisms &mdash; Petrus Jacobus</strong> (b. 21 Jun 1889) and
            <strong>Ockert Johannes Stefanus</strong> (b. 16 May 1894)</td>
          <td>Father <b>Petrus Jacobus Booijsen</b>, mother <b>Anna Catharina Sofia Benadi</b>.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>The first row is the one that matters.</b> A baptism register at Rustenburg, indexed by somebody
    who never saw this site, gives the parents of a boy born <b>31 January 1862</b> as
    <b>Hendrik Stephanus Booijsen</b> and <b>ANNA JOHANNA OLIVIER</b>.
    <br /><br />
    <b>31 January 1862 is not a date this archive had.</b> It is a date it <em>calculated</em>, an hour
    earlier, by subtracting <em>29 jaar 3 maanden en 14 dagen</em> from <em>14 Mei 1891</em> on a torn
    death notice. The two agree to the day. And the register spells the mother's name in full, in a
    hand a volunteer could read, thirty years before the death notice this archive misread as
    <em>Alberts</em>. <a href="#brakfontein-1891">The 1891 notices &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Two brothers, one Sunday</h3>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>On <b>3 April 1869</b> two sons of the house were admitted to membership at Rustenburg together:
      <b>Petrus Jacobus</b>, born 20 April 1851, and <b>Ockert Johannes Stephanus</b>, born
      15 September 1852. They are the first and second names on their mother's 1891 list, seventeen and
      sixteen at the time, and they are the two who appear again on their father's 1908 notice &mdash;
      one of them dead by then, the other signing it.</p>
    <p><b>Ockert died at Klerksdorp on 16 January 1921, aged sixty-nine.</b> That is a civil
      registration and it names no parents, which is the whole difficulty with civil death records in
      this country and the reason this archive keeps going back to Sterfkennisse.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Where a name goes once it is in</h3>
  <blockquote>
    <b>Ocken Johannes Oliefier</b>, of the district of Cradock, had a grandson called
    <b>Ockert Johannes Stephanus Booysen</b> &mdash; who had a nephew called
    <b>Ockert Johannes Stefanus</b>, baptised at Rustenburg in 1894.
    <b>Three generations of a forename that came into this family with one marriage</b>, and this archive
    could not account for any of them a day ago.
  </blockquote>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:16px">
    <p>The other direction runs the same way. <b>Hendrik Stephanus Nicolaas Booyen was born on
      4 January 1882</b> and named for his uncle &mdash; who was twenty at the time and would be dead on
      his father's farm before the boy was ten. The nephew lived until <b>8 October 1959</b>, at Kempton
      Park, and outlived the man he was named for by <b>sixty-eight years</b>.
      <a href={u("/namesakes/")}>The namesakes &rarr;</a></p>
    <p class="cap">South Africa, Netherdutch Reformed Church Registers (Pretoria Archive), 1838&ndash;1991
      &middot; FamilySearch <span class="m">1:1:QPLV-KSW2</span>, <span class="m">1:1:4ZCG-XRZM</span>,
      <span class="m">1:1:4ZCG-XRN2</span>, <span class="m">1:1:Q57R-8CPN</span>,
      <span class="m">1:1:QPLK-N324</span>, <span class="m">1:1:CSNF-M5N2</span>. Transvaal Civil Death
      1869&ndash;1954 &middot; <span class="m">1:1:W9CB-BNMM</span>. All read 13 September 2026.
      <b>No image is available for any of the church entries.</b></p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="indwe-1908">1908 &amp; 1932 &middot; The second Willem Hermanus &mdash; married at sixteen and twenty-two, dead in Booysens</h2>
  <p class="dek">This archive has had <b>Willem Hermanus Booyzen of Indwe</b> as a name with a beginning
    and no end: a lidmaat at Indwe in 1907, a <em>mijnwerker</em> on two baptisms, a marriage known only
    from an index, and then nothing at all after 1910. <b>Two things are new here.</b> The marriage
    register page itself has now been read, and it is worth reading. And the end of him has been found:
    he died in a Johannesburg hospital on <b>29 July 1932</b>, an <b>ex-miner</b>, and was buried at
    Brixton two days later.</p>

  <figure style="margin:20px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/huwelijk-1908-indwe.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="The Indwe duplicate marriage register sheet for 21 September 1908, one entry to the page." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>DUPLICAAT ORIGINEEL REGISTER &middot; Huwelijk bevestigd te Indwe, in
      de Parochie van Indwe, in het District van Wodehouse.</strong> One marriage to the sheet, and a
      printed warning pasted beside it that the document is damaged.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure style="margin:20px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1908-indwe-entry-13.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Entry thirteen of the Indwe marriage register, 21 September 1908: William Hermanus Booyzen, 22, jongman, mijnwerker, of Dugmore Mijn; Susanna Adriana Roodt, 16, jongedochter, of Dugmore Mijn." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Entry thirteen, <em>Duplicaat Origineel Register</em>, Huwelijk bevestigd te
      <strong>Indwe</strong>, District <strong>Wodehouse</strong>. Certificate 13, reference 3857.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:12rem">Column</th><th>William Hermanus Booyzen</th><th>Susanna Adriana Roodt</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Ouderdom</td><td class="m"><b>22</b></td><td class="m"><b>16</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Staat</td><td>Jongman</td><td>Jongedochter</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Rang of beroep</b></td><td><b>MIJNWERKER</b></td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Woonplaats</b></td><td><b>Dugmore Mijn</b></td><td><b>Dugmore Mijn</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Geboden of licentie</td><td class="m">na huwelijksgeboden</td><td class="m">geboden</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Toestemming</b></td><td class="m">&mdash;</td><td><b>OUDERS</b> &mdash; she was sixteen</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px">Married in the Ned. Geref. Kerk at Indwe. Signed
    <strong>W. H. Booyzen</strong> and <strong>S. A. Roodt</strong>, before
    <strong>D. N. Claase</strong> and <strong>C. J. Slabbert</strong>, and checked against the original
    register by <strong>J. J. Knight</strong>.</p>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <b>Their son was already four months old.</b> <b>Willem Hermanus Booyzen of Dugmore</b> was born on
    <b>31 May 1908</b> and baptised at Indwe on <b>6 December 1908</b> &mdash; born before the wedding,
    baptised after it. The order of those three dates is the whole story of a twenty-two-year-old miner
    and a sixteen-year-old who needed her parents' consent, living on the same mine.
    <a href={u("/who/willem-hermanus-booyzen-of-dugmore/")}>The son &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:36px">And twenty-four years later, Johannesburg</h3>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:18px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/sterfgeval-1932-william-herman.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Form of Information of a Death, 1932: William Herman Booyzen, 47, born Cape Province, married, ex miner underground." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Informasievorm van Sterfgeval</strong>, under Act 17 of 1923.
        Registered at Johannesburg 30 July 1932, entry <span class="m">4009</span>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <div class="scroll">
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Name</td><td><b>WILLIAM HERMAN BOOYZEN</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Age</td><td><b>47 years</b> &mdash; born about mid-1885</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Birthplace</b></td><td><b>Cape Province</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Condition</td><td><b>Married</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Occupation</b></td><td><b>EX MINER (told) UNDERGROUND</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Died</td><td><b>29 July 1932</b>, Johannesburg Hospital</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Residence</b></td><td><b>62 Langford Street, BOOYSENS, Johannesburg</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cause</td><td><b>Perforated duodenal ulcer</b>; general peritonitis; pulmonary tuberculosis</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Last illness</td><td><b>about one day in hospital</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Informant</b></td><td>an <b>occupier</b> of the Johannesburg Hospital. <b>No relative signed.</b></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figure style="margin:20px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1932-ex-miner.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Questions three to eight of the 1932 death form: 47 years, Cape Province, Married, Ex Miner (told) Underground." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>"47 years" &middot; "Cape Province" &middot; "Married" &middot;
      "Ex Miner (told) Underground."</strong> FamilySearch indexes that age as forty-four. The form says
      forty-seven, and the burial register agrees with the form.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 style="margin-top:30px">Why this is him</h3>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What agrees</h3>
      <p><b>Ex miner, underground.</b> He was a <em>mijnwerker</em> at Dugmore Mijn in 1908 and the word
        appears on no other Booyzen document this archive holds.<br />
        <b>Born Cape Province.</b> Indwe.<br />
        <b>Married.</b> Since 1908.<br />
        <b>Forty-seven in July 1932</b> against <b>twenty-two in September 1908</b> &mdash; which makes him
        forty-six. One year.<br />
        <b>Brixton Cemetery, 31 July 1932</b>, where the burial register gives his birth as <b>1885</b>
        and where this family already buries its Johannesburg dead: <b>Anna J. Mary Booyzen</b> in 1914,
        <b>John Barry Booyzen</b> in 1916, <b>George Downing Daschner</b> in 1925.<br />
        And <b>“Sasanna Adriana Booyzen” was buried at Johannesburg in 1968</b> &mdash; his widow, under
        his name, thirty-six years after him.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What does not</h3>
      <p><b>No parents are named</b>, because a South African civil death registration does not ask for
        them unless the deceased is under ten. That is the whole reason this archive keeps going back to
        Sterfkennisse, and it is why this identification is marked
        <span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> and not documented.<br /><br />
        <b>FamilySearch indexes his age as 44.</b> The form says <b>47</b>. Read the image, not the index
        &mdash; a lesson this site learned twice today.<br /><br />
        The occupation itself was <b>“told”</b> to the clerk, by a hospital official who wrote the word in
        brackets. Nobody who knew him was in the room.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Grave 6889 &mdash; and the identification closes</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1968-grave-6889.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Grave 6889 in the Town Council of Johannesburg consecutive grave-number register: Boysen William, folio 49; Susanna Adriana Booyzen, 29.8.68." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>TOWN COUNCIL OF JOHANNESBURG &middot; CONSECUTIVE GRAVE NUMBERS.</strong>
      Grave <strong>6889</strong>: <strong>Boysen, William</strong> &mdash; folio 49; and beneath him
      <strong>Susanna Adriana Booyzen &middot; 29.8.68</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="note">
    <b>They are in the same grave.</b> Everything above is circumstantial &mdash; the occupation, the
    province, the age, the cemetery. This is not. The Johannesburg grave register puts
    <b>William Booyzen</b> and <b>Susanna Adriana Booyzen</b> in <b>grave 6889</b>, thirty-six years
    apart, and Susanna Adriana Roodt is the woman he married at Indwe on 21 September 1908.
    <br /><br />
    Note how the clerk spells him: <b>Boysen</b>. That is the same spelling an English clerk put on
    <a href="#barry-1915">John Barry&rsquo;s 1915 death notice</a> &mdash; <em>&ldquo;Kathleen Mary
    Boyson, born Barry, married in community of property to William Boyson&rdquo;</em> &mdash; for a
    different man of the same family, seventeen years earlier and eight hundred kilometres away.
    <a href={u("/name/")}>The name &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <blockquote><b>His father died of a stomach ulcer at Indwe in 1905, aged fifty-four.</b> This man died
      of a <b>perforated duodenal ulcer</b> at Johannesburg in 1932, aged forty-seven. Twenty-seven years
      and eight hundred kilometres apart, and the same organ.</blockquote>
    <p>He left the coal at Indwe for the gold on the Rand, as the whole family did, and he was living at
      <b>62 Langford Street in Booysens</b> &mdash; a Johannesburg suburb that has nothing to do with this
      family and is spelled like it. He was in hospital about a day. The man who reported his death was
      the hospital's own officer.
      <a href={u("/atlas/")}>The atlas &rarr;</a> &middot;
      <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a></p>
    <p class="cap">South Africa, Civil Marriage Records, 1801&ndash;1974 &middot; FamilySearch
      <span class="m">1:1:6ZHR-PCSV</span>, film <span class="m">007729488</span> image
      <span class="m">02719</span>. South Africa, Transvaal, Civil Death, 1869&ndash;1954 &middot;
      <span class="m">1:1:736J-96W2</span>, film <span class="m">007731503</span> image
      <span class="m">01056</span>. Johannesburg Cemetery Records &middot;
      <span class="m">1:1:6N1B-JVP9</span> and <span class="m">1:1:6J3R-2Q1R</span>.
      Read 13 September 2026.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="grave-9760">1940 &middot; Grave 9760 &mdash; where Kathleen Barry actually is</h2>
  <p class="dek">This archive has carried the great-great-grandmother of the line as
    <b>Catherine Maria Sophia &ldquo;Kathleen&rdquo; Barry, 1859&ndash;1940</b> &mdash; two years and
    nothing else. The <b>City of Johannesburg Register of Persons Buried at New Cemetery</b> gives the
    day, the section and the number of the grave.</p>

  <figure style="margin:20px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/begraafregister-1940-new-cemetery.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="A page of the City of Johannesburg Register of Persons Buried at New Cemetery, August to December 1940." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>CITY OF JOHANNESBURG &middot; REGISTER OF PERSONS BURIED AT NEW
      CEMETERY.</strong> Name, age in years months and days, sex, date of burial, section, grave number,
      remarks. Late August to December 1940, about forty burials to the page.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure style="margin:20px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1940-grave-9760.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="The register row: Booyzen Catherine M.S., 82, female, buried 6 September, section D.R., grave 9760." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Her row, enlarged and sharpened. <strong>Booyzen Catherine M.S. &middot; 82
      &middot; 6 [September] &middot; D.R. &middot; 9760.</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:18px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What the register says</h3>
      <div class="scroll">
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Name</td><td><b>Booyzen, Catherine M.S.</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Age</td><td><b>82</b> &mdash; born about 1858</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Date of burial</td><td><b>6 September 1940</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Section</td><td><b>D.R.</b> &mdash; Dutch Reformed</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Grave number</b></td><td><b>9760</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cemetery</td><td><b>Johannesburg New</b> &mdash; which is <b>Brixton</b></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Why it is her</h3>
      <p>She is entered under her married surname with her own initials: <b>Booyzen, Catherine M.S.</b>
        &mdash; <b>M</b>aria <b>S</b>ophia. The age fits within a year of the birth this archive holds.
        And she is in the <b>Dutch Reformed section</b>, which is where a woman who married
        <b>Willem Hermanus Booyzen</b> in the Ned. Geref. Kerk at Lady Grey in 1879 belongs.</p>
      <p class="cap">The remarks column carries an administrative note about the grave not being
        reopened, which this archive has not fully read.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <blockquote><b>She is an Irishman's daughter from Barkly East, buried in the Dutch Reformed section
      of a Johannesburg cemetery.</b> Her father John Barry called himself <em>an Irishman, born in
      Ireland</em> on his 1903 registration; her mother was a Kolbe out of W&uuml;rttemberg and Soho. She
      married a Booyzen at Lady Grey, buried him at Indwe in 1905, and finished on the Rand
      thirty-five years later.</blockquote>
    <p>Brixton already holds this family. <b>Anna J. Mary Booyzen</b> was buried there in January 1914,
      <b>John Barry Booyzen</b> &mdash; a child of two &mdash; in December 1916, <b>George Downing
      Daschner</b> in June 1925, and the man this archive believes to be
      <b>Willem Hermanus Booyzen of Indwe</b> in July 1932. Her son
      <b>George Downing Mountjoy Booyzen</b> went to <b>West Park</b> instead, in December 1947.
      <a href={u("/places/brixton-cemetery/")}>Brixton &rarr;</a></p>
    <p class="cap">South Africa, Gauteng, Johannesburg, Cemetery Records, 1840&ndash;2019 &middot;
      FamilySearch <span class="m">1:1:6NBS-NR1Q</span>, film <span class="m">107663873</span> image
      <span class="m">00020</span>. Read 13 September 2026.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="charlotta-1902">1902 &middot; Charlotta&rsquo;s own death notice &mdash; three husbands, six children, and none of them his</h2>
  <p class="dek">Death Notice <span class="m">844</span>, filed 11 September 1902 and stamped by the
    Master of the Supreme Court, Transvaal, on 23 September. This archive has carried the second wife as
    <b>Charlotta Elizabetha Dormehl, died before 1908</b>. Here she is entire, and she proves from a
    second direction the thing the 1892 marriage register only implied.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:20px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1902-charlotta.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Death Notice 844 of 1902 for Charlotta Elizabetha Booysen, born Dormehl, of Driefontein, Groot Marico." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Under <em>The Administration of Estates Ordinance, 1901</em> &mdash; the
        ordinance imposed on the Transvaal by the British administration during the war. She died three
        months before the peace.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <div class="scroll">
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1. Name</td><td><b>Charlotta Elizabetta Booysen (born Dormehl)</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>2. Birthplace</td><td><b>Graaff-Reinet, C.C.</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>3. Father</b></td><td><b>Jacob Dormehl</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>3. Mother</b></td><td><b><em>unknown</em></b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>4. Age</td><td><b>60 years 4 months</b> &mdash; born about October 1841</td></tr>
            <tr><td>5. Condition</td><td><b>Farmer&rsquo;s wife</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>6. Married</td><td>surviving spouse <b>Hendrik Stefanus Booysen</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>6b. Previous spouses</b></td><td><b>Jacob van Staden</b> &mdash; date unknown<br /><b>Matthys Roos</b> &mdash; date unknown</td></tr>
            <tr><td>7. Died</td><td><b>6 February 1902</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>8. Where</b></td><td><b>Driefontein, Groot Marico, Marico</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>10. Property</td><td><b>one wagon and household furniture, £51 8s 0d</b>. She left <b>a will</b>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Signed</td><td><b>H. S. Booysen</b>, at <b>Zeerust</b>, 21 August 1902, in the capacity of <b>Husband</b></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Three husbands, in her own notice</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1902-three-husbands.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question six: surviving spouse Hendrik Stefanus Booysen; previous spouses Jacob van Staden, unknown; Matthys Roos, unknown. Died 6 February 1902 at Driefontein, Groot Marico." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>&ldquo;Hendrik Stefanus Booysen &middot; Jacob van Staden. unknown.
      &middot; Matthys Roos. unknown.&rdquo;</strong> Her husband could name both of his predecessors and
      the date of neither.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The 1892 register called her <b>weduwe</b> and wrote her in as <b>Charlotta Elizabeth Roos,
      geboren Dormehl</b>, which told this archive she had been married once before. She had been married
      <b>twice</b> before. <b>Jacob van Staden</b> first, then <b>Matthys Roos</b>, then at the age of
      fifty-one a farmer of Brakfontein.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Six children, and the point of them</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1902-six-children.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question nine: Jacob Alewyn van Staden and Coenrad Sybert van Staden, majors; Gert Johannes Roos, Matthys Johannes Roos, Johannes Sybert Roos, Anna Francisa Roos, majors." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Question nine asks for the children <em>&ldquo;stating separately those born
      of different marriages&rdquo;</em>, and he does &mdash; two van Stadens braced apart from four
      Rooses.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:18px">
    <b>Not one Booysen among them.</b>
    <br /><br />
    Two van Stadens &mdash; <b>Jacob Alewyn</b> and <b>Coenrad Sybert</b>. Four Rooses &mdash;
    <b>Gert Johannes</b>, <b>Matthys Johannes</b>, <b>Johannes Sybert</b>, and <b>Anna Francisa</b>,
    married in community of property to Gert Johannes Laubeschagne. All of age. And after ten years of
    marriage to Hendrik Stefanus Booysen, <b>no child of his is named</b>, because she was fifty-one
    when she married him.
    <br /><br />
    <b>This archive spent a month drawing his four children under her.</b> Earlier today the 1892
    marriage register &mdash; her age, her condition &mdash; made that impossible. Her own death notice
    now says the same thing from the other side, in her husband&rsquo;s handwriting.
    <a href="#brakfontein-1891">The children belong to the first wife &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <figure style="margin:20px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1902-wagon-and-will.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question ten: Yes, one wagon and household furniture valued at £51 8s 0d; the deceased has left a will. Dated at Zeerust, 21 August 1902, signed H. S. Booysen, Husband." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>&ldquo;1 wagon + household furniture valued at £51.8.0&rdquo;</strong>
      &middot; <em>The deceased has left a will.</em> Signed at <strong>Zeerust</strong> on
      <strong>21 August 1902</strong> &mdash; and he signs himself <strong>Booysen</strong>, with an
      <em>s</em>, as he did on the register in 1892.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">And one more parent nobody could name</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1902-name-father.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Questions one to four: Charlotta Elizabetta Booysen born Dormehl; Graaff-Reinet C.C.; father Jacob Dormehl, mother unknown; 60 years 4 months." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>&ldquo;Father: Jacob Dormehl. Mother: unknown.&rdquo;</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <blockquote><b>That is the seventh death notice in this archive that fails to name a parent.</b>
      A husband of ten years knew his wife&rsquo;s father&rsquo;s name and not her mother&rsquo;s.
      It is the same failure as <b>James Montjoy in 1840</b>, as <b>John Barry&rsquo;s son in 1915</b>
      writing <em>Neagle</em> and admitting he had neither her Christian names nor an address, and as the
      1876 Sterfkennis that names eight children and no mother for any of them.</blockquote>
    <p>She never left Marico. In April 1892 she gave her home as <b>Driefontein, Marico</b> and married a
      man of Brakfontein in Rustenburg; ten years later she died at <b>Driefontein, Groot Marico</b>. He
      signed her notice at <b>Zeerust</b>, the town they had married in, six months after her death and
      three months after the peace. <b>She had one wagon and her furniture.</b>
      <a href={u("/atlas/")}>The atlas &rarr;</a></p>
    <p class="cap">South Africa, Transvaal, Probate Records from the Master of the Supreme Court,
      1869&ndash;1961 &middot; Death Notice <span class="m">844</span> of 1902 &middot; FamilySearch
      <span class="m">1:1:7FTM-QD6Z</span>, film <span class="m">007897350</span> image
      <span class="m">00906</span>. Read 13 September 2026.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="diederik-1935">1935 &middot; Looking for Ockert&rsquo;s estate and finding his son&rsquo;s</h2>
  <p class="dek">Ockert Johannes Stephanus Booysen died at Klerksdorp on 16 January 1921. His name appears
    <b>six times</b> in the Transvaal probate index and <b>not once as the deceased</b> &mdash; four of
    those six are duplicate copies of two documents, and in both he is somebody else&rsquo;s relative.
    One of the two, though, is worth the whole search.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:20px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/sterfkennis-1935-diederik-arnoldus.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Sterfkennis 87882 of 1935 for Diederik Arnoldus Booysen of Zwartruggens, karweier." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Sterfkennis 87882</strong>, <em>volgens die voorskrifte vervat in
        die Boedelwet, 1913</em> &mdash; the first document in this archive printed in <b>Afrikaans</b>
        rather than Dutch or English.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <div class="scroll">
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1. Naam</td><td><b>Diederik Arnoldus Booysen</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>2. Geboorteplek</td><td><b>Zwartruggens</b> &middot; Europeaan</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>3. Vader</b></td><td><b>Ockert Johannes Stephanus Booysen</b> &mdash; <em>oorlede</em></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>3. Moeder</b></td><td><b>Elizabeth Johanna Sophia Booysen, gebore MYBURGH</b> &mdash; <em>oorlede</em></td></tr>
            <tr><td>4. Ouderdom</td><td><b>47 jare 7 maande</b> &mdash; born about August 1887</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>5. Beroep</b></td><td><b>KARWEIER</b> &mdash; a transport rider</td></tr>
            <tr><td>6. Woonplaas</td><td>Zwartruggens</td></tr>
            <tr><td>7. Getroud</td><td>in gemeenskap van goedere met <b>Elizabeth Anna Booysen, gebore BRONKHORST</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>8. Sterfdatum</td><td><b>1 Maart 1935</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>9. Sterfplek</b></td><td><b>Voorbedacht No. 401, distrik Rustenburg</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>11&ndash;14</td><td>losse goedere <b>ja</b> &middot; boedel <b>meer as £300</b> &middot; testament <b>ja</b></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">The line that matters</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1935-parents.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question three: Vader Ockert Johannes Stephanus Booysen, oorlede; Moeder Elizabeth Johanna Sophia Booysen gebore Myburgh, oorlede." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>&ldquo;Vader: Ockert Johannes Stephanus Booysen &mdash; oorlede.
      Moeder: Elizabeth Johanna Sophia Booysen, gebore Myburgh &mdash; oorlede.&rdquo;</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Earlier today this archive took the marriage of <b>Ockert Johannes Stephanus Booysen</b> and
      <b>Elisabetta Johanna Sophia Myburgh</b> from a Rustenburg baptism entry that <b>has no image</b>
      &mdash; a volunteer&rsquo;s transcription and nothing more, published on its own page with a warning
      saying so. <b>Here is the same couple on a document that can be read.</b> The index entry stands.</p>
    <p>It also extends them. Their known son <b>Hendrik Stephanus Nicolaas</b> was baptised at Rustenburg
      in January 1882. <b>Diederik Arnoldus was born about August 1887</b>, and both parents were dead by
      March 1935 &mdash; which agrees with Ockert&rsquo;s death at Klerksdorp in 1921.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Two families on one form, and a pencil correction</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1935-two-families.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question ten, the children: two Jouberts braced apart from three Booysens, with a pencil note in the margin." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Question ten, entire, with a <strong>pencil note in the margin</strong>
      against the upper brace &mdash; it reads <em>not deceased&rsquo;s children</em>, and the edge of the
      page cuts off the end of it.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:22rem">On the form</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><b>Josina Adriaan Joubert</b><br /><b>Maria Catharina Jacomina Eden</b>, born Joubert</td>
          <td>The widow&rsquo;s children by her own late husband <b>Joubert</b>, who died
            <b>31 January 1916</b>. <b>Stepchildren</b> &mdash; and somebody at the Master&rsquo;s Office
            picked up a pencil and said so in the margin.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><b>Elizabeth Johanna Sophia Booysen</b>, born 23/9/1918<br />
              <b>Elizabeth Anna Booysen</b>, born 15/3/1922<br />
              <b>Ockert Johannes Stephanus Booysen</b>, born 23/10/1927</td>
          <td>His own three, by Elizabeth Anna Bronkhorst. The eldest two carry their mother&rsquo;s and
            their grandmother&rsquo;s names. <b>The youngest carries his grandfather&rsquo;s.</b></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <b>A fourth generation of the name.</b> <b>Ocken Johannes Oliefier</b> of the district of Cradock had
    a grandson <b>Ockert Johannes Stephanus Booysen</b> (1852&ndash;1921), who had a grandson
    <b>Ockert Johannes Stephanus Booysen born 23 October 1927</b> &mdash; and a great-nephew of the same
    name baptised at Rustenburg in 1894. <b>Four generations, out of one marriage</b>, and yesterday this
    archive could not account for the forename at all.
    <a href="#brakfontein-1891">Where it came from &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
    <h3>And the negative</h3>
    <p><b>Ockert Johannes Stephanus Booysen has no estate file of his own in the indexed Transvaal
      probate records.</b> Searched under <em>Ockert</em>, <em>Okkert</em>, <em>Ocker</em> and
      <em>Ocken</em> against <em>Booysen</em>, <em>Booyzen</em> and <em>Booijsen</em>. Six hits, all of
      them him as a relative: four duplicate copies of this 1935 Sterfkennis, and two of his own
      father&rsquo;s 1908 notice, where he is entered as <em>Child</em>.</p>
    <p>He died at Klerksdorp on <b>16 January 1921</b> and it is a <b>civil death registration</b>, which
      names no parents. If an estate was raised for him it is not name-indexed, and the archive has no way
      to browse to it &mdash; the films are not ordered by estate number.
      <a href={u("/searched/")}>What has been searched &rarr;</a></p>
    <p class="cap">South Africa, Transvaal, Probate Records from the Master of the Supreme Court,
      1869&ndash;1961 &middot; Sterfkennis <span class="m">87882</span> &middot; FamilySearch
      <span class="m">1:1:QPKJ-LLZQ</span>, film <span class="m">007897967</span> image
      <span class="m">00898</span>, and the duplicate at film <span class="m">007820977</span> image
      <span class="m">01397</span>. Read 13 September 2026.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="olivier-line">1824 &amp; 1850 &middot; The Olivier line, and the marriage that puts this family north of the Vaal in 1850</h2>
  <p class="dek">Yesterday <b>Anna Catharina Johanna Olivier</b> did not exist on this site. This morning
    she was recovered from a death notice the archive had misread twice. This afternoon there are two
    more documents: <b>her baptism at Cradock in 1824</b> and <b>her marriage at Potchefstroom in 1850</b>
    &mdash; and the second of them moves the whole family&rsquo;s history twenty-six years.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:30px">Cradock, 4 October 1824</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/doopregister-1824-cradock.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="A page of the Cradock Dutch Reformed baptism register for 1824." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The Cradock <em>doopregister</em> for 1824 &mdash; the church her family
      baptised in, and the district her death notice gives sixty-seven years later.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:12rem">Child</td><td><b>Anna Johanna Olivier</b>, born <b>10 September 1824</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Baptised</td><td><b>4 October 1824</b>, Cradock</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Father</b></td><td><b>Ockert Johannes Stephanus Olivier</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Mother</b></td><td><b>Anna Catharina Botha</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">
    <b>Read her father&rsquo;s name again.</b> Not <em>Ocken Johannes</em>, as the 1891 death notice
    scrawled it. <b>OCKERT JOHANNES STEPHANUS OLIVIER</b> &mdash; all three names, exactly as her son
    carries them. <b>Ockert Johannes Stephanus Booysen</b> was called after his grandfather, entire.
    <br /><br />
    And she had a sister baptised at Cradock eleven months earlier: <b>Aletta Catharina Olivier</b>,
    4 October 1823, same parents. Anna Johanna&rsquo;s own daughter is
    <b>Aletta Catharina Johanna</b>. The names in this family go back up the female line and this archive
    could not see it until today. <a href={u("/namesakes/")}>The namesakes &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:36px">Potchefstroom, 3 July 1850</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/huwelijksregister-1850-potchefstroom.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="A page of the Potchefstroom marriage register, entries 189 to 207, 1850." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Entries <strong>189 to 207</strong>, June and July 1850. Three columns only:
      <em>Namen der Gehuwden</em>, <em>Ouderdom</em>, <em>Wanneer gehuwd</em>. No parents, no birthplaces,
      no occupations &mdash; a frontier register keeping the minimum.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1850-entry-206.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Entry 206 of the Potchefstroom marriage register: Hendrik Stephanus Booijzen, 23; Anna Johanna Olivier, 25." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Entry <strong>206</strong>. <em>Namen der Gehuwden &middot; Ouderdom &middot;
      Wanneer gehuwd.</em> <strong>Hendrik Stephanus Booijzen, 23 &middot; Anna Johanna Olivier,
      25</strong> &mdash; the date carried down by ditto marks from <strong>3 July 1850</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:18px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Her name, written plainly, forty-one years early</h3>
      <p>The 1891 death notice spells her maiden name <b>Oliefier</b> in a hand so bad this archive read
        it as <em>Alberts</em> and threw her out of the family for a week. Here it is in 1850, in a clerk
        who could write: <b>OLIVIER</b>. Four documents now agree &mdash;
        <b>1850 Potchefstroom</b>, <b>1862 Rustenburg</b>, <b>1891 Brakfontein</b>,
        <b>1908 Koedoesfontein</b>. <a href="#brakfontein-1891">How it was misread &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And the ages hold</h3>
      <p><b>He was 23</b> in July 1850, so born about 1827 &mdash; against <em>about June 1828</em> from
        the 1908 notice and <em>63</em> at Zeerust in 1892. <b>She was 25</b>, so born about 1825
        &mdash; against a baptism of <b>10 September 1824</b>. Four independent estimates, all inside two
        years. Their eldest son <b>Petrus Jacobus was born on 20 April 1851</b>, nine and a half months
        after the wedding.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>The date that matters most is the place.</b>
    <br /><br />
    This archive has spent its whole life pushing back the moment this family crossed into the Transvaal.
    It began by saying they went north to Johannesburg by railway in the twentieth century. Then the
    <a href="#sterfkennis-1876">1876 Sterfkennis</a> put the direct ancestor dead in the district of
    Rustenburg &mdash; twenty-five years before the war, a generation before Johannesburg &mdash; and
    that was already the earliest evidence there was.
    <br /><br />
    <b>They were married at POTCHEFSTROOM on 3 JULY 1850.</b> That is twenty-six years earlier again,
    fourteen years after the Great Trek, and two years before the Sand River Convention made the South
    African Republic a state at all. <b>A Booijzen was standing in a Transvaal church before there was a
    Transvaal.</b> <a href={u("/atlas/")}>The atlas &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p><b>What is still open.</b> Ockert Johannes Stephanus Olivier and Anna Catharina Botha have a
      daughter, a second daughter, a town and nothing else. Neither has a death notice in this archive,
      neither has parents, and the Oliviers of Cradock in the 1820s are numerous &mdash; there are at
      least four other men of nearly this name baptising children in that church in those years. This
      archive has not separated them and will not guess.
      <a href={u("/errands/")}>What is still to ask &rarr;</a></p>
    <p class="cap">Baptism: South Africa, Dutch Reformed Church Registers (Cape Town Archives),
      1660&ndash;1994 &middot; FamilySearch <span class="m">1:1:6KRN-K37Y</span>, and the sister at
      <span class="m">1:1:6KRL-Q4H4</span>. Marriage: South Africa, Netherdutch Reformed Church
      Registers (Pretoria Archive), 1838&ndash;1991 &middot; <span class="m">1:1:Q29M-ZJPM</span>,
      entry 206. Read 13 September 2026.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="anna-maria-1806">1806 &middot; Anna Maria van der Merwe stops being a rumour</h2>
  <p class="dek">The ancestor chart carries a wall against her marked <span class="chip open">Disputed</span>,
    and the reason printed beside it is <em>&ldquo;Only a family tree puts her here.&rdquo;</em>
    <b>That is no longer true.</b></p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:12rem">Child</td><td><b>DAVID SCHALK BOOIJZEN</b>, born <b>12 December 1806</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Baptised</td><td><b>4 January 1807</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Father</b></td><td><b>Pieter Jacobus Booijzen</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Mother</b></td><td><b>ANNA MARIA VAN DER MERWE</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">Dutch Reformed Church Registers (Cape Town Archives). The index
    carries an explicit <em>Couple</em> relationship between the parents and <em>ParentChild</em> to the
    boy &mdash; this is not two names that happen to sit near each other on a page.</p>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">
    <b>DAVID SCHALK IS FIRST ON THE 1876 STERFKENNIS.</b> The eight children of Petrus Jacobus Booijsen
    are listed there in order and he heads them, and this archive has never had a date for him. A son
    born in <b>December 1806</b> to a father baptised in <b>September 1788</b> is a man of eighteen
    becoming a father &mdash; which is exactly what the site has always said: <em>&ldquo;married at about
    seventeen&rdquo;</em>.
    <br /><br />
    <b>So Anna Maria van der Merwe is documented.</b> Not as a name on a tree; as a mother on a baptism
    register, married to a Pieter Jacobus Booijzen, with a son who is first on the 1876 list.
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">And it settles a dispute this archive marked Serious</h3>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The <a href={u("/disputed/")}>disputed page</a> has carried this since the first week:</p>
    <blockquote><em>&ldquo;The tree marries her to Petrus Jacobus Booysen in 1805 at Graaff-Reinet and has
      her dead before March 1825. FamilySearch carries a Dutch Reformed marriage of PETRUS JACOBUS
      BOOŸZEN and ANNA MARIA VAN DER MERWE dated 9 MARCH 1825, at Cape Town, indexed three separate
      times. Either the death date was derived from this very record by someone reading it as an end
      rather than a beginning, or the 1825 marriage is a different couple entirely. The archive is not
      going to guess which.&rdquo;</em></blockquote>
    <p><b>It does not have to guess any more.</b> A son born to that couple on <b>12 December 1806</b>
      means they were married <b>by 1806</b>. The 1805 marriage at Graaff-Reinet stands; <b>the 1825
      record is not this couple&rsquo;s wedding</b> &mdash; it is a different pair of the same two very
      common names, six hundred miles away at Cape Town. <b>Resolved.</b></p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">What the wall becomes</h3>
  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What is now proved</h3>
      <p>Anna Maria van der Merwe <b>was married to Pieter Jacobus Booijzen</b> and <b>bore him a son in
        1806</b> who is the eldest of the eight on the 1876 Sterfkennis. She is a real, documented wife
        and mother in this family.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What is still not</h3>
      <p><b>That she is the mother of the 1812 man</b> &mdash; the one in the direct line. No document
        says so. The 1876 notice still names eight children and no mother for any of them, and
        <b>Elizabetha van der Merwe</b> is documented as the mother of a ninth child born about 1828.
        Two van der Merwe wives, and the middle of the family unassigned.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">The wall on the chart stays, because the chart is about <em>this</em>
    descent and the link it needs is still missing. <b>But the reason printed on it was wrong and has
    been rewritten.</b> <a href={u("/tree/")}>The chart &rarr;</a></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:38px">A second baptism, at Lady Grey</h3>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:12rem">Child</td><td><b>PETRUS JACOBUS BOOIJSEN</b>, born <b>29 May 1873</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Baptised</td><td><b>24 August 1873, LADY GREY</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Father</td><td><b>David Schalk Booijsen</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mother</td><td><b>Elizabeth Margaritha Marais</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:16px">
    <p>This is the eldest of the eight on the <a href="#marais-1900">1900 Pretoria death notice</a>
      &mdash; <em>&ldquo;Petrus Jacobus Booijzen (unmarried &mdash; major)&rdquo;</em> &mdash; and he now
      has a birth date and a font. <b>And the font is Lady Grey</b>, which is where his parents married
      and where his uncle Willem Hermanus would marry Kathleen Barry <b>six years later</b>. The family
      was in that small Cape church across at least a decade before any of them went north.</p>
    <p class="cap">South Africa, Dutch Reformed Church Registers (Cape Town Archives), 1660&ndash;1994
      &middot; FamilySearch <span class="m">1:1:6VTL-YJ25</span> and
      <span class="m">1:1:65M7-16ZC</span>. Both read from the index: FamilySearch is refusing this
      archive&rsquo;s record-page requests, so neither image has been opened.
      Read 13 September 2026.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="marais-1900">1900 &middot; A zinc building in Market Street, Pretoria &mdash; and the direct line&rsquo;s own brother</h2>
  <p class="dek">A run of <b>ten index entries</b> under <em>Booijzen</em> in the Transvaal probate
    records carried no dates, no places and nothing to identify them. They all belong to <b>one death
    notice</b>, and it opens a household this archive did not have: the family of
    <b>David Schalk Booysen</b>, born 9 June 1848 &mdash; <b>the brother of the direct ancestor</b>.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:20px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1900-elizabetha-marais.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Death Notice 4795 of 1900 for Elizabetha Margaretha Booijzen, born Marais, of Pretoria." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Death Notice 4795</strong>, filed 18 March 1904 and stamped by the
        Master of the Supreme Court, Transvaal, on 2 May 1904 &mdash; <b>nearly four years after the
        death</b>. Under <em>The Administration of Estates Proclamation, 1902</em>: the proclamation did
        not exist when she died.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <div class="scroll">
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1. Name</td><td><b>Elizabetha Margaretha Booijzen (born Marais)</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>2. Birthplace</td><td><b>District Cradock, Cape Colonial</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>3. Father</b></td><td><b>Stephanus François Marais</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>3. Mother</b></td><td><b>Elizabetha Margaretha Marais</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>4. Age</td><td><b>50 years 2 months</b> &mdash; born about April 1850</td></tr>
            <tr><td>5. Condition</td><td>Housewife</td></tr>
            <tr><td>6a. Spouse</td><td><b>DAVID SCHALK BOOIJZEN</b>, in community of property</td></tr>
            <tr><td>6b. Previous spouses</td><td><em>none</em></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>6c. Married at</b></td><td><b>LADY GREY, near Aliwal North, Cape Colony</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>7. Died</b></td><td><b>1 June 1900</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>8. Where</b></td><td><b>a zinc building in MARKET STREET, PRETORIA</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>10. Movables</td><td><b>none &mdash; compensation claim filed</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>11&ndash;12</td><td>no immovable property; estate <b>under £100</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>13. Will</td><td><b>Yes &mdash; mutual will</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Signed</td><td><b>D. S. Booijzen</b>, at Pretoria, 18 March 1904, as <b>Husband</b></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figure style="margin:20px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1900-marais-parents.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Questions one to four: Elizabetha Margaretha Booijzen born Marais; District Cradock, Cape Colonial; father Stephanus François Marais, mother Elizabetha Margaretha Marais; 50 years 2 months." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>&ldquo;Elizabetha Margaretha Booijzen (born Marais)&rdquo; &middot;
      &ldquo;District Cradock, Cape Colonial&rdquo; &middot; father &ldquo;Stephanus François
      Marais&rdquo;, mother &ldquo;Elizabetha Margaretha Marais&rdquo;.</strong> Mother and daughter under
      the same two forenames &mdash; and the daughter who died a minor makes three.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Lady Grey, which this archive already knows</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1900-lady-grey.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question six: surviving spouse David Schalk Booijzen, in community of property; place of last marriage Lady Grey, near Aliwal North, Cape Colony; died 1st June 1900 in a zinc building in Market Street, Pretoria." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>&ldquo;Lady Grey, near Aliwal North, Cape Colony.&rdquo;</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="note">
    <b>That is the family&rsquo;s own church.</b> <b>Willem Hermanus &ldquo;William Henry&rdquo;
    Booyzen</b> married <b>Catherine Maria Sophia &ldquo;Kathleen&rdquo; Barry</b> at <b>Lady Grey</b> on
    22 December 1879. And <b>David Schalk Booysen, born 9 June 1848, is his brother</b> &mdash; fourth
    on the 1863 death notice of their mother Johanna Catharina Mountjoy, where Willem Hermanus is sixth.
    <br /><br />
    Two brothers married out of one small Cape church; one went east to the coal at Indwe and died there
    in 1905, the other went north to Pretoria. <b>This archive has had David Schalk as a line on a list
    since the beginning</b> &mdash; <em>&ldquo;Fourth on the 1863 list&rdquo;</em>, no wife, no children,
    no death. Here is his whole household. <a href={u("/direct-line/")}>The line &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Eight children</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1900-seven-children.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question nine, the children: Petrus Jacobus, Stephanus François, Johanna Margaretha Delport, Magdalena Johanna Vogel, Rachel Margaretha Williamse, Anna Susanna, Elizabeth Margarietha died a minor, David Schalk 15 years." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Question nine, with a name <b>squeezed in between the lines</b> after the
      form was first written.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:24rem">As written</th><th></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>Petrus Jacobus Booijzen</b></td><td class="m">unmarried &mdash; major</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Stephanus François Booijzen</b></td><td class="m">ditto &mdash; and see below</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Johanna Margaretha Delport</b>, born Booijzen</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Magdalena Johanna Vogel</b>, born Booijzen</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Rachel Margaretha Williamse</b>, born Booijzen</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Anna Susanna</b>, born Booijzen</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Elizabeth Margarietha</b></td><td class="m"><b>died a minor</b> &mdash; the interlineation</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>David Schalk Booijzen</b></td><td class="m"><b>15 years</b> &mdash; born about 1889</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">Her father was <b>Stephanus François Marais</b>, and her second
    son is <b>Stephanus François</b>. Her own names were <b>Elizabetha Margaretha</b>, and the daughter
    who died a minor was <b>Elizabeth Margarietha</b>. The naming runs up the female line again, exactly
    as it does in the Olivier household. <a href={u("/namesakes/")}>The namesakes &rarr;</a></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">She died four days before Pretoria fell</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1900-compensation-claim.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question ten: has the deceased left any movable property? — none, compensation claim filed." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>&ldquo;none. Compensation claim filed.&rdquo;</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>1 June 1900.</b> Lord Roberts entered Pretoria on <b>5 June 1900</b>. She died in
      <b>a zinc building in Market Street</b> &mdash; the main street of the capital &mdash; four days
      before the British took the town, and her husband did not file her death notice for
      <b>three years and nine months</b>, under a proclamation that did not exist when she died.</p>
    <p>And against the question about movable property he wrote <b>&ldquo;none. Compensation claim
      filed.&rdquo;</b> This archive has a page about the <b>War Losses Compensation Commission</b> and
      has recorded that it found no Booyzen claim &mdash; while noting that the Commission&rsquo;s
      records are catalogued by <b>sub-commissioner</b> and not by claimant, so the zero was weak.
      <b>Here is a Booijzen claim, named on a different document entirely.</b>
      <a href={u("/searched/")}>The searches &rarr;</a> &middot;
      <a href={u("/rebels/")}>The war &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <b>And it unsettles something this archive inferred.</b>
    <br /><br />
    This site carries a <b>Stephanus Francois Booyzen (c. October 1874 &ndash; 14 May 1940, East Lynne,
    Pretoria)</b>, a <em>messelaar</em>, placed as a son of <b>Petrus Jacobus Booysen and his second
    wife Johanna Margaritha Pretorius</b> &mdash; marked <span class="chip inf">inferred</span>, because
    his own 1940 Sterfkennis names neither parent: his widow wrote only <em>oorlede</em> for both. He was
    put there on his birth date alone.
    <br /><br />
    <b>This notice names a Stephanus François Booijzen as a son of David Schalk Booijzen and Elizabetha
    Margaretha Marais</b> &mdash; an unmarried major at Pretoria in 1904, where the 1874 man died in
    1940. And the forename is his mother&rsquo;s father&rsquo;s: <b>Stephanus François Marais</b>.
    <br /><br />
    That is a better argument than the one this archive was running, and it is <b>still not proof</b>.
    Flagged on the disputed page, not resolved. <a href={u("/disputed/")}>Disputed &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:16px">South Africa, Transvaal, Probate Records from the Master of the
    Supreme Court, 1869&ndash;1961 &middot; Death Notice <span class="m">4795</span> &middot; FamilySearch
    <span class="m">1:1:QPLV-G1R4</span> and nine sibling personas on the same record, film
    <span class="m">007897354</span> image <span class="m">00802</span>. Read 13 September 2026.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="pretoria-1905">1905 &middot; David Schalk marries again &mdash; and the register cites the estate number</h2>
  <p class="dek">This archive lost the direct ancestor&rsquo;s brother in March 1904, signing his
    wife&rsquo;s death notice at Pretoria. Here he is nineteen months later, in the same city, marrying
    again &mdash; and the <em>Remarks</em> column does something this archive has never seen a marriage
    register do.</p>

  <figure style="margin:20px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/huwelijk-1905-pretoria.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="The Pretoria duplicate marriage register sheet for 27 November 1905." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>DUPLICATE ORIGINAL REGISTER &middot; Marriage Solemnized at Pretoria,
      in the District of Pretoria.</strong> An English form now, where Zeerust in 1892 was Dutch &mdash;
      three years after the British took the town.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure style="margin:20px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1905-entry-105.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Entry 105 of the Pretoria marriage register, 27 November 1905: David Schalk Booyzen, 56, Cradock, Cape Colony, widower, of Goede Hoop, Pretoria; Johanna Elena Lowies born Stols, 51, Potchefstroom district, widow, of Skinners Court, Pretoria." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Entry <strong>105</strong> (RM 524), <em>Duplicate Original Register</em>,
      marriage solemnized at Pretoria. Eleven columns, and the last one is what matters.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:11rem">Column</th><th>David Schalk Booyzen</th><th>Johanna Elena Lowies</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Age</td><td class="m"><b>56</b></td><td class="m"><b>51</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Birthplace</b></td><td><b>CRADOCK, Cape Colony</b></td><td>Potchefstroom District, Transvaal</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Condition</b></td><td><b>Widower</b></td><td><b>Widow</b>, born <b>Stols</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Antenuptial contract</td><td class="m">without</td><td class="m">without</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Residence</td><td><b>Goede Hoop, Pretoria</b></td><td><b>Skinners Court, Pretoria</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Banns or licence</td><td class="m">Banns</td><td class="m">Banns</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consent</td><td class="m">Own</td><td class="m">Own</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Remarks</b></td><td><b>&ldquo;Remarriage Certificate from Master Supreme Court No. 4795 dated 4/11/05&rdquo;</b></td><td>&ldquo;&hellip; No. 7081 dated 7/11/05&rdquo;</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <figure style="margin:22px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1905-certificate-4795.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="The remarks column: Remarriage Certificate from Master Supreme Court No. 4795 dated 4/11/05." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>&ldquo;Remarriage Certificate from Master Supreme Court No. 4795,
      dated 4/11/05.&rdquo;</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="note">
    <b>4795 is the number of his first wife&rsquo;s death notice.</b>
    <br /><br />
    It is the document read on <a href="#marais-1900">this same page</a> a few sections up: the notice for
    <b>Elizabetha Margaretha Booijzen, born Marais</b>, who died in a zinc building in Market Street on
    1 June 1900 and whose file he did not open until March 1904. The Master would not let a widower
    remarry until the first estate was in order, so he issued a certificate out of that file on
    <b>4 November 1905</b> &mdash; and the marriage clerk copied the estate number into the register
    three weeks later.
    <br /><br />
    <b>This is not an inference. It is a reference number written on one document pointing at another.</b>
    The David Schalk Booyzen who married at Pretoria in 1905 is the David Schalk Booijzen who was widowed
    there in 1900, and both of them are the David Schalk Booysen born 9 June 1848 &mdash; fourth on the
    1863 list, the direct ancestor&rsquo;s brother.
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p><b>And the birthplace is CRADOCK.</b> His mother Johanna Catharina Mountjoy was married at Cradock
      in November 1838; his first wife was born in the district of Cradock about April 1850; and his
      brother Willem Hermanus was born <em>achter Sneeuberg</em> three years after him. The family was
      moving around one corner of the Karoo and this is the first document to say where <b>he</b> started.</p>
    <blockquote><b>A method worth keeping.</b> The Remarks column of a Transvaal civil marriage register
      carries <b>the Master&rsquo;s estate number</b> whenever a widow or widower remarries. That is a
      direct index from a marriage to an estate file &mdash; and it works in the direction this archive
      usually cannot go, from a person who is alive to a file about somebody who is dead.</blockquote>
    <p><b>What is still missing:</b> his own death. He was alive and fifty-six in November 1905, at
      Goede Hoop in Pretoria, and this archive still cannot find the end of him.
      <a href={u("/errands/")}>What is still to ask &rarr;</a></p>
    <p class="cap">South Africa, Transvaal, Civil Marriages, 1870&ndash;1931 &middot; entry
      <span class="m">105</span>, RM <span class="m">524</span>, reference <span class="m">3149</span>
      &middot; FamilySearch <span class="m">1:1:QGR1-Q7WY</span>. Solemnized by
      <b>J. B. Klopper</b>, minister; witnesses <b>J. P. Stols</b> and <b>A. Vaughan</b>.
      Read 13 September 2026.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="estate-7081">1905 &middot; Estate 7081 &mdash; the other number, and the same address</h2>
  <p class="dek">The Pretoria marriage register of 27 November 1905 cites <b>two</b> remarriage
    certificates from the Master, one for each party. <b>4795</b> was David Schalk&rsquo;s and led back to
    his first wife&rsquo;s death notice. This is <b>7081</b>, read entire &mdash; and it is not the
    document this archive expected.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:20px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1905-lowies.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Death Notice 7081 of 1905 for Charles Petrus Barend Lowies of Goedehoop, Pretoria." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Death Notice 7081</strong>, filed <strong>28 October 1905</strong>.
        Under <em>The Administration of Estates Proclamation, 1902</em> &mdash; the same wartime
        proclamation as the Marais notice.</figcaption>
