  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">The address</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1905-goedehoop.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line one: Charles Petrus Barend Lowies, of Goedehoop, Pretoria, Transvaal Colony; line two: Dutch, Cradock District, Cape Colony." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>&ldquo;of GOEDEHOOP, Pretoria, Transvaal Colony&rdquo;</strong> &middot;
      <strong>&ldquo;Dutch, Cradock District, Cape Colony&rdquo;</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="note">
    <b>GOEDEHOOP is the address David Schalk Booyzen gave on the marriage register three weeks later.</b>
    <br /><br />
    This archive read that register this morning and wrote down <em>&ldquo;a widower of Goede Hoop,
    Pretoria&rdquo;</em> without knowing what it meant. <b>The two men lived in the same place.</b> David
    Schalk did not marry a widow from across the town; he married the widow of a man from his own
    address &mdash; and the two of them were born in the same Cape district within four months of each
    other, he in June 1848 and Lowies about October.
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">And where he died</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1905-lunatic-asylum.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question eight: where the person died — House, Lunatic Asylum; Town or place, Pretoria; District, Pretoria District." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>&ldquo;House: Lunatic Asylum. Town or place: Pretoria.&rdquo;</strong>
      The form&rsquo;s own words.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">The twelve</h3>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:9rem" class="m">Status</th><th>Named on the notice</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m"><b>Majors</b></td><td>Jan Jacobus &middot; <b>Johanna Elena</b> &middot;
          Gabriel Jacobus &middot; <b>Carl Petrus Barend</b> &middot; Willem Jacobus &middot;
          Gideon Daniel Rudolph &middot; <b>Elizabetha Maria</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><b>Minors</b></td><td>Matthys Andries &middot; Radolph Lodewyk &middot;
          Abel Hermanus Gerhardus &middot; Lourens Daniel Albertus &middot; Johannes Jacobus</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">Three of the twelve carry the grandparents&rsquo; names straight
    through &mdash; <b>Jan Jacobus</b> for Jan Lowies, <b>Elizabetha Maria</b> for Elizabetha Maria born
    Lubbe, and <b>Carl Petrus Barend</b> for his father. <b>Johanna Elena</b> carries her mother&rsquo;s.
    None of the twelve is a Booysen and none of them is kin to this family; they are set down because
    David Schalk Booysen became their stepfather a month after their mother signed this page.</p>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>A <b>hawker</b> of Goedehoop with twelve children, six of them still minors, who died on
      <b>5 June 1905</b> in the Pretoria asylum. His widow signed the notice on <b>27 October</b> and
      <b>married David Schalk Booyzen one month later, on 27 November</b>. The Master cleared her
      remarriage certificate on the 7th of that month and David Schalk&rsquo;s on the 4th.</p>
    <blockquote><b>Between the two certificates stand twenty children</b> &mdash; eight Booijzens and
      twelve Lowieses, six of the Lowieses under age. That is what the Remarks column of a marriage
      register is protecting, and it is why the Master would not let either of them marry until the
      estates were settled.</blockquote>
    <p><b>This archive published this estate from the index a few hours ago</b> and said so at the time.
      The index gave the name, the district, the parents and the twelve children. It did not give
      Goedehoop, the asylum, the trade, or the eight months on his age &mdash; and Goedehoop is the whole
      point. <a href="#pretoria-1905">The marriage &rarr;</a></p>
    <p class="cap">South Africa, Transvaal, Probate Records from the Master of the Supreme Court,
      1869&ndash;1961 &middot; Death Notice <span class="m">7081</span> &middot; FamilySearch
      <span class="m">1:1:QLQ7-PFC4</span>. Read 13 September 2026.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
