  <h3 style="margin-top:36px">And then October 1918</h3>
  <div class="note">
    <b>Richard Peter Booyzen, twenty-four years old, buried at Brixton on 27 October 1918.</b>
    <br /><br />
    That is the exact centre of what Johannesburg called <b>Black October</b> &mdash; the second wave of
    the influenza, which killed something like fourteen thousand people on the Rand in six weeks.
    <br /><br />
    <b>This archive does not have a Richard Peter Booyzen.</b> It should. <b>Richard Peter Barry</b> is
    Kathleen Barry&rsquo;s brother, named on their father&rsquo;s 1903 registration and on the Cape Rebel
    roll; and a boy born about 1894 falls exactly in the gap in Willem Hermanus and Kathleen&rsquo;s
    children, between <b>Catharina Maria Sophia in 1891</b> and <b>George Downing Mountjoy in 1901</b>.
    A Booyzen child named after his mother&rsquo;s brother, buried in the cemetery where this family
    buries. <span class="chip inf">Inferred</span>, and added as a ninth child with the reasoning on
    his page &mdash; a burial register names no parents and this one is no exception.
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p>He is not alone in that register. <b>John Zacharia Booysen</b>, twenty-nine, was buried at Brixton
      on <b>26 November 1918</b>, and <b>Johanna A. Booysen</b>, <b>nought days old</b>, on
      <b>19 December 1918</b>. This archive <b>cannot show</b> that either is kin &mdash; there is no
      forename in this family that reaches them, and Booysen is a common name on the Rand. They are set
      down here because the shape is the point: <b>three Booysens into one cemetery in eight weeks</b>,
      in the worst two months South Africa has had.</p>
    <p class="cap">South Africa, Gauteng, Johannesburg, Cemetery Records, 1840&ndash;2019. 298 entries
      read under six surnames, 13 September 2026. Grave numbers are given where the register carries
      them; most of these entries are name-and-date index rows whose images have not yet been read one by
      one. <a href={u("/searched/")}>What has been searched &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
