<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="dragoons-cape">1806&ndash;1817 &middot; The regiment James Mountjoy left, tracked through the London papers</h2>
  <p class="dek">Question 12 has been blocking since it was written. A memoirist put James Mountjoy in
    the <b>21st Light Dragoons</b>, discharged at the Cape; his own death notice answers
    <em>not known</em> where his parents are concerned and his marriage register rules a line through
    the column that should say where he came from. <b>The regiment itself can be followed, day by day,
    in the British press.</b> Sixty-seven articles, read in order.</p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m" style="width:9rem">Date</th><th>What the papers say</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">5&ndash;6 May 1806</td>
          <td>The Scots Greys relieve them at York. <em>&ldquo;That fine regiment, the 21st Light
            Dragoons, lately passed through [Egham] from York, on their way to Portsmouth
            <b>to embark for the Cape of Good Hope</b>.&rdquo;</em>
            <span class="cap">Bath Journal; Limerick Gazette</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">19 May 1806</td>
          <td><em>&ldquo;The remaining three troops of the 21st light dragoons&hellip;&rdquo;</em> embark
            for the Cape and the East Indies. <span class="cap">Hampshire Chronicle</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">2 Aug 1806</td>
          <td>Admiral Stirling to sail for the Cape, <em>&ldquo;there to take on board <b>part of the
            21st light dragoons</b>, and proceed to <b>South America</b>&rdquo;</em>.
            <span class="cap">Caledonian Mercury</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">10 Dec 1807</td>
          <td><em>&ldquo;Ten Transports with the 21st Light Dragoons, 47th and 87th foot had
            <b>arrived from South America at the Cape</b>&hellip;&rdquo;</em>
            <span class="cap">Government Gazette (India)</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">4 Mar 1809</td>
          <td>In Parliament: <em>&ldquo;it was thought an unaccountable circumstance, that the 21st
            light dragoons should [be] sent to the Cape of Good Hope, <b>where they could have nothing
            to do</b>&rdquo;</em>. <span class="cap">Globe</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">1&ndash;5 Sep 1810</td>
          <td>The Cape garrison under Lieutenant-General the Hon. Grey: the 1st battalions of the 24th,
            72nd, 83rd, 87th and 93rd, <b>with the 21st Light Dragoons</b>. Beef 3d. a pound, bread 2d.
            <span class="cap">Star; Johnson&rsquo;s Sunday Monitor; Caledonian Mercury; Bury and Norwich Post</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">27 Oct 1810</td>
          <td><em>&ldquo;A large reinforcement of officers and men are to be sent out by the first ship
            to that excellent and highly-disciplined regiment&hellip;&rdquo;</em>
            <span class="cap">Oxford Journal</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">2 Jan 1812</td>
          <td><em>&ldquo;CAPE OF GOOD HOPE. <b>The 21st Light Dragoons are in the highest state of
            discipline.</b> The following is a Copy of General Orders given [by] <b>SIR JOHN
            CRADOCK</b>, after reviewing that regiment&hellip;&rdquo;</em>
            <span class="cap">London Courier and Evening Gazette</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">9 Nov 1812</td>
          <td><em>&ldquo;<b>General TARLETON&rsquo;s regiment</b>, the 21st Light Dragoons, which have
            been stationed some time back at the Cape of Good Hope&hellip;&rdquo;</em>
            <span class="cap">Sun (London)</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">4 Oct 1813</td>
          <td>Detachments of the 21st Light Dragoons and 83rd Foot arrive at <b>Hilsea depot</b> to wait
            embarkation for the Cape. <span class="cap">Star; Hampshire Chronicle</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">19&ndash;20 Oct 1813</td>
          <td><b>Lord Charles Somerset&rsquo;s</b> military command at the Cape is to consist of the
            21st Light Dragoons and the 1st battalions of the 59th, 60th, 83rd and 93rd.
            <span class="cap">Saint James&rsquo;s Chronicle; Public Ledger</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">26 Oct 1813</td>
          <td><b><em>&ldquo;a detachment of 140 men for the 21st Light Dragoons, and about the same
            number for the 81st Foot, embarked for the Cape of Good Hope.&rdquo;</em></b>
            <span class="cap">Star (London)</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">Oct 1817</td>
          <td>At <b>St Helena</b>, guarding Napoleon: the 66th Regiment of about 1,000 men,
            <b>a detachment of the 21st Light Dragoons of about 100</b>, and two or three companies of
            Royal Artillery. <span class="cap">Star; Oxford Journal; Belfast News-Letter</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><b>14&ndash;17 Nov 1817</b></td>
          <td><b>Extract of a letter from the Cape of Good Hope, dated 1 and 10 September 1817:
            <em>&ldquo;THE 21ST LIGHT DRAGOONS HAVE EMBARKED FOR INDIA.&rdquo;</em></b>
            <span class="cap">Sun; Star; Public Ledger; Morning Herald; Hampshire Chronicle</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:22px">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0">And this is the sentence the whole table was read for</h3>
    <p style="font-size:17px;line-height:1.7"><b>James Montjoy married Anna Catharina Slier at
      Graaff-Reinet on 9 March 1817. His regiment sailed for India six months later.</b></p>
    <p style="margin-top:14px">A soldier who married in March 1817 and was keeping a house and a saddler&rsquo;s
      trade at Cradock by January 1822 <b>was discharged at the Cape before September 1817</b>, and the
      thing he was choosing between was <b>a wife at Graaff-Reinet and a transport to India</b>.
      This archive has been trying to date his discharge from the Cape end for weeks. <b>The London end
      dates it in one line.</b></p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">Three windows, not one</h3>
      <p>He could have come out <b>with the regiment in 1806</b> at about twenty-four, <b>with the 1810
        reinforcement</b>, or <b>with the draft of 140 men in October 1813</b>.</p>
      <p class="cap">All three are now dated, and the WO 97 attestation years for the regiment cluster
        at 1799&ndash;1801 and 1805 &mdash; which favours the first.
        <a href={u("/documents/#fmp-negatives")}>The WO 97 sweep &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">Why a cavalryman would take his discharge</h3>
      <p>Parliament said it out loud in <b>March 1809</b>: the 21st were at the Cape
        <b><em>&ldquo;where they could have nothing to do&rdquo;</em></b>. Eleven years of garrison
        duty, one review, and a colonel &mdash; <b>Banastre Tarleton</b> &mdash; who never saw them.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px">A trade and a marriage at Graaff-Reinet against another
        eleven years in Madras is not a difficult choice.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">A name that turns up twice</h3>
      <p>The regiment was reviewed at the Cape by <b>Sir John Cradock</b>, Governor, and pronounced in
        the highest state of discipline.</p>
      <p class="cap"><b>The town James Mountjoy settled in is named after the man who reviewed his
        regiment.</b> There is nothing in that but a coincidence of governors and place-names &mdash;
        and it is still worth writing down.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><b>What this does not do.</b> It does not name him, and it
    does not touch his parentage. WO 97 still holds no Mountjoy of that regiment, and the reason still
    stands: <b>the pre-1855 discharge papers hold only men discharged to a pension</b>, and a man who
    took his papers at the Cape to marry and make saddles is not one.
    <b>What has changed is that his discharge now has a deadline</b>, and the muster books
    &mdash; <span class="m">WO 12</span> at Kew, which list every man by name, quarter by quarter
    &mdash; would show him leaving it.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Question 12 &rarr;</a></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:30px">17 September &middot; The errand is one piece, and the piece this archive
    had written down was the wrong one</h3>
  <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px">The Kew catalogue was searched again before the letter went.
    It gives the regiment a <b>continuous run of muster books</b>, and the run ends where the regiment
    does &mdash; disbanded in 1820, with nothing again until the 1860s.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m" style="width:11rem">Piece</th><th class="m" style="width:9rem">Covering</th><th>What was happening</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">WO 12/1442&ndash;1445</td><td class="m">1798&ndash;1804</td><td>Home service. The WO 97 attestations cluster here.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">WO 12/1446</td><td class="m">1805&ndash;06</td><td>York, then Portsmouth, then the Cape</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">WO 12/1447</td><td class="m">1807&ndash;09</td><td>Back from South America; &ldquo;nothing to do&rdquo;</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">WO 12/1448</td><td class="m">1810&ndash;12</td><td>The reinforcement; Cradock&rsquo;s review</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">WO 12/1449</td><td class="m">1813&ndash;15</td><td>The draft of 140 men</td></tr>
        <tr style="background:rgba(127,127,127,.09)"><td class="m"><b>WO 12/1450</b></td><td class="m"><b>1816&ndash;17</b></td>
          <td><b>THE MARRIAGE, AND THE EMBARKATION FOR INDIA.</b> One piece covers both.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">WO 12/1451&ndash;1452</td><td class="m">1818&ndash;20</td><td>India, then disbandment</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:8px">Held at The National Archives, Kew, with no access conditions
    recorded. Not name-indexed &mdash; they have to be read, and <b>a man&rsquo;s disappearance from a
    muster dates his discharge</b>.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:20px">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0">And the correction, which is the reason for going back at all</h3>
    <p>On 15 September this archive searched the same catalogue, concluded correctly that WO 97 would
      not hold him, and named the piece to read instead: <b><span class="m">WO 12/1430</span></b>. That
      went into a letter drafted to Kew.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:12px"><b>WO 12/1430 is the 20th and 21st Dragoons for 1867&ndash;1868.</b>
      Fifty years late, and the re-raised regiment rather than the one that garrisoned the Cape.</p>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:12px">The letter was never posted. It now names WO 12/1450, gives
      the newspaper evidence for the September 1817 departure, and tells them in as many words that an
      earlier version had the wrong piece. <b>A letter that asks the wrong archivist for the wrong box
      gets one answer and burns a month</b>, and the only thing that caught it was searching a
      catalogue twice. <a href={u("/corrections/")}>The corrections &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="kolbe-freestate">1868&ndash;1914 &middot; Leeuwberg, a commando, and a farmers&rsquo; president &mdash; the Free State Kolbes in the British press</h2>
  <p class="dek">Commandant <b>John Gerson Eliezar Kolbe</b>, the Settler&rsquo;s eldest child, died of
    typhus caught on commando on <b>3 April 1868, on the farm Leeuwberg</b>, leaving a widow and
    <b>twelve children &mdash; eight sons and four daughters</b>. This archive can put a hand on two of
    the twelve. The prisoner list and the newspapers between them now put Kolbes back on that farm, in
    the field, and at the head of the Free State&rsquo;s farmers.</p>

  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">1901 &middot; A Kolbe still on Leeuwberg</h3>
      <p><b>Andries Frederick Kolbe, aged 45</b>, taken prisoner and entered on the War Office list as
        of <b>LEEUWBERG, Bloemfontein</b> &mdash; <span class="m">WO 108/368 folio 431</span>, sent to
        Cape Town and Natal.</p>
      <p class="cap"><b>Thirty-three years after the Commandant died there, a Kolbe is farming it.</b>
        Born about 1856, he would have been twelve when his father&mdash;or whoever it was&mdash;died at
        Leeuwberg, and he is inside the age bracket of the eight sons.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px"><b>No descent is documented and none is claimed.</b> What
        is documented is a farm, a surname and a date.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">1901 &middot; A Kolbe commando in the field</h3>
      <p><b>May 1901, Colesberg.</b> <em>&ldquo;Kritzinger, with 200 men, has passed through this
        district. The <b>Hertzog, Brand and Kolbe</b> commandos&hellip;&rdquo;</em> &mdash; London
        Evening Standard, Globe and Westminster Gazette, 21 May; Leominster News, 24 May.</p>
      <p><b>September 1901.</b> <em>&ldquo;<b>Kolbe&rsquo;s attitude is doubtful. He is reported to have
        expressed a wish to surrender.</b>&rdquo;</em> &mdash; Derby Daily Telegraph and Derby and
        Chesterfield Reporter, 5&ndash;6 September.</p>
      <p><b>November 1901.</b> <em>&ldquo;<b>GENERAL KOLBE CAPTURED.</b> Bloemfontein, Wednesday.
        &mdash; Hamilton&rsquo;s column, operating in the district of <b>Reddersburg</b>&hellip;&rdquo;</em>
        &mdash; Birmingham Mail, 21 November; Warwick and Warwickshire Advertiser, 23 November.</p>
      <p class="cap">Colesberg, Bethulie, Reddersburg, Bloemfontein. <b>That is Leeuwberg country</b>,
        and 1901 is exactly when the Commandant&rsquo;s sons would have been commanding.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">1914 &middot; A Kolbe at the head of the Free State&rsquo;s farmers</h3>
      <p><em>&ldquo;A cordial welcome was given to the South African farmers at the general meeting, and
        at luncheon in the yard <b>Mr G. A. KOLBE, President of the Orange Free State Agricultural
        Association and Chairman of the Orange Free State delegation</b>&hellip;&rdquo;</em> &mdash;
        Western Daily Press, Dundee Courier, Cork Free Press and Exeter and Plymouth Gazette,
        <b>2 July 1914</b>.</p>
      <p>Named in the delegate list of <b>26 June 1914</b> in the Daily Telegraph as
        <b>&ldquo;Mr. G. A. Kolbe (Bethulie)&rdquo;</b>, beside Mr E. W. Hunt of Pretoria and
        Mr J. H. Perks of Koingha. On <b>22 July</b> the Daily Malta Chronicle has him sending
        <b>a telegram of respectful greeting to Queen Wilhelmina</b> on the delegation&rsquo;s behalf.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px"><b>And the War Office list of 1901 holds a
        GEORGE AUGUSTUS KOLBE of Sterkfontein, BETHULIE, aged 32, a Free Stater, sent to Natal</b>
        &mdash; <span class="m">WO 108/368 folio 581</span>. Same initials, same district, and thirteen
        years between them.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:22px">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0">What this is and what it is not</h3>
    <p>It is <b>four documents and a district</b>. A man on the Commandant&rsquo;s farm in 1901; a
      commando under the Commandant&rsquo;s surname in the Commandant&rsquo;s own country in the same
      year; a general of that name taken at Reddersburg in November; and a farmers&rsquo; president of
      Bethulie in 1914 carrying the Settler&rsquo;s forenames and, very probably, a prisoner&rsquo;s
      number thirteen years earlier.</p>
    <p><b>It is not a pedigree.</b> The document that would make it one is
      <span class="m">VAB MHG M777</span>, the 1896 estate of the Commandant&rsquo;s widow
      <b>Anna Jacoba van der Merwe</b>, filed under her second husband&rsquo;s name McCabe &mdash;
      <b>which would name all twelve children</b>. It is identified, it is unread, and it is in
      Bloemfontein. <a href={u("/documents/#commandant-kolbe-1868")}>The Commandant &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:16px"><b>One limit worth stating.</b> FindMyPast&rsquo;s newspaper
    archive is British and Irish. Its own coverage facet lists England, Scotland, Wales, both Irelands,
    India, Barbados, China, Jamaica, Australia and New Zealand &mdash; <b>and no South Africa at all</b>.
    Everything above is the Cape and the Free State seen from Fleet Street, through Reuter&rsquo;s wire
    and a war correspondent. <b>It is not a substitute for the South African papers</b>, and the
    eGGSA newspaper index remains the only route into those.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="burgersdorp-605">17 September &middot; The rest of the Burgersdorp register &mdash; 214 images, 2,046 baptisms, and three of the five were already here</h2>
  <p class="dek">The standing errand of this project. <b>Film 008039088</b>, the Burgersdorp Dutch
    Reformed baptism register, was swept by hand from image 338 to image 604 on 14 September and then
    stopped &mdash; twice &mdash; because a FamilySearch session is shorter than a 214-image sweep.
    <b>It is finished.</b></p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:18px">
    <p><b>What the last 214 images hold:</b> <b>2,046 baptisms</b>, running from <b>1858 to 1866</b>
      &mdash; 191 in 1859, 309 in 1860, 279 in 1861, 212 in 1862, 325 in 1863, 273 in 1864, 328 in
      1865, 123 in 1866. <b>391 distinct parent surnames.</b> No image failed and no image was skipped.
      It is a complete demographic slice of one Karoo town across eight years, and this archive wanted
      five rows of it.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">Five rows carrying this family &mdash; and two of them this archive already had</h3>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:6px">Written that way round deliberately. <b>The first draft of this
    section called all five new</b>, and two of them were in the register here already. What the sweep
    actually did for those two is smaller and is said below.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m" style="width:5rem">Image</th><th style="width:17rem">Child</th><th>Parents</th><th class="m">Born &middot; baptised</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">637</td><td>Johanna Catharina Mountjoy<br /><span class="cap">indexed &ldquo;Joha Catha&rdquo; &middot; ALREADY HELD</span></td>
          <td>George Mountjoy and Maria Sophia Botha</td>
          <td class="m">28 Jan 1860<br />29 Feb 1860</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">673</td><td><b>Fredrik Fortunatus Steenkamp Werdmuller</b><br /><span class="cap">the man was held; the birth and the mother are new</span></td>
          <td><b>Coenraad Johannes Otto Werdmuller</b> and <b>Augusta Louisa Kolbe</b></td>
          <td class="m">25 Apr 1861<br />19 May 1861</td></tr>
        <tr style="background:rgba(127,127,127,.09)"><td class="m">685</td><td><b>GEORGE AUGUSTUS KOLBE</b></td>
          <td><b>G. A. Kolbe</b> and <b>ANNA MARIA VILJOEN</b></td>
          <td class="m"><b>2 Oct 1861</b><br />12 Jan 1862</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">745</td><td><b>Anna Johanna Kolbe</b><br /><span class="cap">indexed &ldquo;Anna Joha&rdquo;</span></td>
          <td>George Augustus Kolbe and Anna Maria Viljoen</td>
          <td class="m">29 Dec 1863<br />28 Feb 1864</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">795</td><td><b>Petrus Jacobus Kolbe</b><br /><span class="cap">indexed &ldquo;Petrus Jacs&rdquo;</span></td>
          <td>George Augustus Kolbe and Anna Maria Viljoen</td>
          <td class="m">11 Aug 1865<br />8 Oct 1865</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:8px">All five at Burgersdorp, all five with citable references &mdash;
    <span class="m">6FHW-89ND</span>, <span class="m">6FW6-WXGS</span>,
    <span class="m">6FWF-YVJT</span>, <span class="m">6FWV-27JX</span>, <span class="m">6F4H-63P2</span>.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The one that lands on something published yesterday</h3>
      <p>On 16 September this archive read the Wakkerstroom gravestones and separated two men of one
        name. One of them: <b>GEORGE AUGUSTUS KOLBE, born 2 October 1861 at Burgersdorp, died
        8 November 1934</b> &mdash; and the age arithmetic made him the prisoner of war of
        <span class="m">WO 108/368 folio 709</span>. The record then said, in terms,
        <em>&ldquo;the descent is not documented here and is not claimed&rdquo;</em>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px"><b>Here is his baptism.</b> 2 October 1861, Burgersdorp, father
        <b>G. A. Kolbe</b>, mother <b>ANNA MARIA VILJOEN</b>. The stone gave a date and a town; the
        register gives him parents.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px"><b>Whether his father is the Settler&rsquo;s twin son</b>
        &mdash; the G.A. Kolbe born 23 February 1836, who would have been twenty-five &mdash;
        <b>is the next step and is not taken here.</b> What is documented is a couple and three
        children.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">A sentence withdrawn, which is the better kind of find</h3>
      <p>This archive already held a <b>Frederick Fortunatus Werdmuller</b> &mdash; from a Cape Supreme
        Court petition of 1907 and an estate of 1953 &mdash; with no birth, no parents, and a line
        saying <em>&ldquo;NOT SHOWN TO BE HER DESCENDANT&rdquo;</em> of Augusta Louisa Kolbe.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px"><b>Image 673 shows it.</b> Fredrik Fortunatus Steenkamp Werdmuller,
        born 25 April 1861, baptised 19 May, father C. J. O. Werdmuller, <b>mother Augusta Louisa
        Kolbe</b>. The Settler&rsquo;s fourth daughter gave her husband&rsquo;s child <b>her
        brother&rsquo;s name</b> &mdash; which is how Frederik Fortunatus reached a fourth surname.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px">It also dates her. C.J.O. Werdmuller married again at
        Burgersdorp in 1874, and this archive could not tell whether his son Charles Andrew belonged to
        that marriage or to Augusta Louisa. <b>She was bearing his children in 1861.</b>
        <a href={u("/documents/#werdmuller-1874")}>The Werdmullers &rarr;</a></p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px"><b>One step not taken:</b> that the child of 1861 is the man
        of the 1907 petition. The name is rare and both are Cape &mdash; but the 1907 man is of
        <b>Malmesbury</b> and the child was baptised at <b>Burgersdorp</b>, and nothing read here joins
        them.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><b>Two blank-name rows in 2,046, and neither is ours.</b>
    Image 666, a child baptised 17 February 1861 whose only named parent is
    <em>Fredrica Geertruida Elizabeth Kezar</em>; image 792, a child baptised 27 August 1865 whose only
    named parent is <em>Francina Helena Magda Steenkamp</em>. <b>That failure &mdash; the empty Name
    column &mdash; is the whole reason this register had to be swept rather than searched</b>, because
    it is what hid Catherine Mary Sophia Barry in 1855 and the Werdmuller child in 1857. Twice more in
    this stretch, and both times to other families.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:20px">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0">And the one that was not a find at all</h3>
    <p><b>Johanna Catharina Mountjoy, born 28 January 1860, has been in this archive for days</b> &mdash;
      a daughter of George Mountjoy and Maria Sophia Botha, baptised on a leap day, and the woman who
      married William Henry Keys. She is one of the thirteen Mountjoy children.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:12px">The sweep reached her from the other direction and returned the same two
      dates. <b>That is a confirmation and a citable reference, and it is not a discovery.</b> The
      duplicate record this sweep created was caught by the build&rsquo;s own name-collision check
      before it reached a page. <a href={u("/documents/#mountjoy-thirteen")}>The thirteen &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px"><b>Recorded so that they are not later adopted:</b> two
    Booysen entries at image 691 &mdash; Jacobus Alewyn, born 5 January 1862 to Andries Johannes Daniel
    Kruger and <em>Cornelia Etresia Booysen</em>, and Maria Helena Charlina, born 8 December 1861 to
    <em>Jan Jonathan Booysen</em> and Cornelia Margaretha de Villiers. <b>Neither couple is in this
    archive and neither is claimed.</b> Burgersdorp had Booysens who were not these Booysens.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="kolbe-counted">1841&ndash;1871 &middot; The whole English Kolbe family, counted &mdash; and a gap that is the record failing, not the family ending</h2>
  <p class="dek">A surname this rare can be measured rather than sampled. <b>Every Kolbe in the British
    census, and every Kolbe in the civil registers, for thirty years.</b> It comes to eight records and
    four people, and the shape of it says something the individual records do not.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:18px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The census, England, Wales and Scotland</h3>
      <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:10px">
        <table><tbody>
          <tr><td class="m">1841</td><td class="m"><b>4</b></td><td><b>and they are one household</b> &mdash; John G (b. 1765), Marianne (1806), Charlotte (1811), Amelia A (1816), all at <b>St George Hanover Square</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">1851</td><td class="m"><b>1</b></td><td>J Kolbe, b. 1826, <b>Lambeth</b> &mdash; none of the four</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">1861</td><td class="m">1</td><td>John Kolbe, b. 1836, St Marylebone</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">1871</td><td class="m">7</td><td>a later, unrelated arrival</td></tr>
        </tbody></table>
      </div>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:8px">In 1841 <b>the Conduit Street household was the entire
        surname in Britain</b>. Not the family this archive happens to hold &mdash; the whole of it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The civil registers, 1837&ndash;1867</h3>
      <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:10px">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th class="m" style="width:5rem">Year</th><th>Deaths</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="m">1845</td><td><b>John Gottlob Kolbe</b> &mdash; St George Hanover Square</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="m">1848</td><td>George Kolbe &mdash; Westminster St Margaret</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="m">1852</td><td>Elizabeth Kolbe &mdash; Strand</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="m">1867</td><td><b>Charlotte Kolbe &mdash; Lambeth</b></td></tr>
          </tbody>
          <thead><tr><th class="m"></th><th>Marriages</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="m">1838</td><td>Maria Kolbe &mdash; Stepney</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="m">1847</td><td>Mary Ann Maria Ford Kolbe &mdash; Whitechapel</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="m">1853</td><td><b>Jane Day Kolbe</b> &mdash; City of London</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="m">1854</td><td><b>Amelia Ann Kolbe</b> &mdash; St Pancras</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:8px"><b>Eight records in thirty years, all of them London.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:22px">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0">And the thing the two tables say together</h3>
    <p><b>Three women disappear from the census between 1841 and 1851, and at least two of them were
      demonstrably alive after it.</b> Amelia Ann married at St Pancras in <b>1854</b>. A Charlotte
      Kolbe died at Lambeth in <b>1867</b> &mdash; and the one Kolbe in the whole 1851 census is at
      Lambeth too.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:12px"><b>So the 1851 blank is not a family ending. It is the record failing to
      hold them</b> &mdash; mis-transcribed, or living in a household entered under somebody else's
      name. A census absence reads like an extinction and is very often an indexing accident.</p>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:12px">This archive learned the same lesson about itself this
      morning, from the other side: seventeen of its own people had pages saying they were discussed
      nowhere, because a build step dropped them. <b>An absence is a claim about the record before it is
      a claim about the person.</b> <a href={u("/method/#evidence-dropped")}>The method &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><b>One thing that got harder, not easier.</b> The three women
    at Conduit Street &mdash; Marianne, Charlotte and Amelia &mdash; <b>match no baptism in the family&rsquo;s
    own register run</b>. Their 1841 ages put them born about 1802&ndash;06, 1807&ndash;11 and
    1812&ndash;16, and the eleven known children leave gaps at exactly <b>1808&ndash;1812</b> and
    <b>1814&ndash;1818</b>. Those are the same two gaps the three missing sons have to sit in. The 1841
    census gives no relationships &mdash; that column starts in 1851 &mdash; <b>so their place in this
    family is an inference from living in the house, and is recorded as one.</b>
    <a href={u("/documents/#savoy-locked")}>The register that would settle it &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="kolbe-1834">April 1834 &middot; Kolbe and Fletcher, Conduit-street, in a list of three hundred master tailors</h2>
  <p class="dek">This archive holds a printed line from December 1799: <b>&ldquo;Kolbe, John, 18
    Broad-street, Soho&rdquo;</b>, one of <b>234 master tailors</b> who met over the Combination Act.
    It has just found the same man in the same kind of list <b>thirty-five years later</b> &mdash; and
    the second list closes a gap this archive had written down as unclosable.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:18px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9"><b>Morning Post &middot; Wednesday, 30 April
      1834</b><br />
      &ldquo;MEETING OF MASTER TAILORS. Yesterday morning a Meeting of considerably more than
      <b>three hundred of the Master Tailors of the metropolis</b> was held in <b>Willis&rsquo;s Rooms,
      St. James&rsquo;s-street</b>&hellip;&rdquo;</p>
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9;margin-top:12px">&hellip;Thomson and Whitelaw
      &middot; J. Beaumont, Finch-lane &middot; <b>KOLBE AND FLETCHER, CONDUIT-STREET</b> &middot;
      W. Pugh, Regent-street&hellip;</p>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:16px">The roll was reprinted across the London press over a fortnight and the
    entry survives in all of it, mangled differently each time by the scanner and identically by the
    compositor: <b>Globe</b> 30 April and 10 May (&ldquo;Kerr, James, Vine street &middot; Kolbe and
    Fletch&hellip;&rdquo;), <b>Old England</b> 4 May, <b>Morning Herald</b> 10 May, <b>London Courier
    and Evening Gazette</b> 10 May, <b>True Sun</b> 10 May, <b>Public Ledger and Daily Advertiser</b>
    12 May (&ldquo;Kolbs and Fletcher&rdquo;). <b>Seven newspapers, one line.</b></p>

  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">What it closes</h3>
      <p>The Westminster rate books have him at <b>Church Street South, Soho, to 1831</b>. The London
        Gazette has him at <b>Conduit Street by September 1836</b>. This archive wrote that the move
        happened <em>somewhere in between</em> and left it there.</p>
      <p class="cap"><b>He was at Conduit Street, with Fletcher, by 29 April 1834.</b> The gap is now
        1831 to early 1834, and the partnership is two and a half years older than its dissolution
        notice.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">What they were signing</h3>
      <p>Not a trade directory. A <b>side</b>. The journeymen tailors had struck, and the masters met to
        resist <b>the Grand National Consolidated Trades&rsquo; Union</b> &mdash; in the masters&rsquo;
        own words, reprinted by the <em>Poor Man&rsquo;s Guardian</em>, <em>&ldquo;the present is not a
        contest between the master tailors and the journeymen, but the contest between the master
        tradesmen and The Grand National Consolidated Trades&rsquo; Union.&rdquo;</em></p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px">Spring 1834. The same season the Tolpuddle labourers were
        transported.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">And what it rhymes with</h3>
      <p>In <b>December 1799</b> he is one of 234 masters over the <b>Combination Acts</b>, a young man
        at 18 Broad-street with no partner and no Bond Street.</p>
      <p>In <b>April 1834</b> he is <b>Kolbe and Fletcher of Conduit-street</b>, sixty-nine or seventy
        years old, a firm rather than a name.</p>
      <p class="cap"><b>Two printed lists thirty-five years apart, the same trade and the same kind of
        fight</b> &mdash; and this archive found the first one months ago and never thought to look for
        a second.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="kolbe-press">1824&ndash;1832 &middot; Four more things the London press says about this family</h2>
  <p class="dek">The newspaper archive was opened for one question and answered several. All four below
    come from the same search as the master tailors&rsquo; list, and none of them was looked for.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:18px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">1 &middot; The son&rsquo;s trade, at last</h3>
      <p class="m" style="font-size:13px;line-height:1.85"><b>Law Chronicle, Commercial and Bankruptcy
        Register &middot; 13 January 1825</b><br />
        &ldquo;<b>KOLBE John Henry, of Norfolk-street, Middlesex-hospital, last of Mansfield-st.,
        Kentish town, TAILOR, DRAPER</b>&hellip;&rdquo;</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">The Gazette gave his addresses and not his trade. <b>He was a tailor
        and draper on his own account</b> &mdash; and the Gazette&rsquo;s &ldquo;Mansfield-Place&rdquo;
        is <b>Mansfield Street, Kentish Town</b>. He left his father&rsquo;s firm in May 1824 and was in
        the insolvent debtors&rsquo; lists by October.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">2 &middot; The 1824 dissolution, in six papers</h3>
      <p>&ldquo;<b>J. H. Kolbe and J. G. Kolbe, Church-street, Soho, tailors</b>&rdquo; &mdash;
        <em>The News</em> 23 and 24 May, <em>New Times</em> 17 May, <em>Nation</em> 17 May,
        <em>Globe</em> 17 May, <em>Sunday Morning Herald</em> 16 May, <em>Fleming&rsquo;s Weekly
        Express</em> 16 May 1824.</p>
      <p class="cap">A third independent witness to the Soho address after the rate books and the
        son&rsquo;s own deposition in the Old Bailey &mdash; and the papers put <b>the son&rsquo;s name
        first</b>, where the Gazette put the father&rsquo;s.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">3 &middot; A house number</h3>
      <p class="m" style="font-size:13px;line-height:1.85"><b>Morning Herald &middot; 6 June 1832</b><br />
        &ldquo;&hellip;the most respectable references will be given. &mdash; Letters addressed A. Z.,
        <b>Mr. Kolbe, 5, Church-street, Soho</b>, will be punctually attended to.&rdquo;</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">A situations-wanted advertisement using the tailor&rsquo;s shop as an
        accommodation address, which is what a respectable tradesman&rsquo;s counter was for.
        <b>The rate books give the street. This gives the number.</b></p>
      <p class="cap">And it puts a Kolbe still in Church Street in June 1832, after the last rate book
        entry.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">4 &middot; A boy who ran away, and the rejection that holds</h3>
      <p class="m" style="font-size:13px;line-height:1.85"><b>Morning Advertiser &middot; 31 December
        1832</b><br />
        &ldquo;<b>LEFT his HOME, Monday, December 10, FREDERICK KOLBE, about 16, dark hair and eyes,
        with an impediment in his sight.</b> Any information addressed <b>Mrs. Kolbe, No. 18,
        Gerrard-street, Soho</b>, will be rewarded; if he will return, will&hellip;&rdquo;</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">This archive <b>rejected</b> a Frederick Kolbe baptised at Westminster
        on <b>16 December 1816</b> &mdash; father George, mother Elizabeth &mdash; because the parents
        were wrong. <b>&ldquo;About 16&rdquo; in December 1832 is exactly that boy</b>, and
        Gerrard Street is two streets from Church Street.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px"><b>So the rejection was right and it was not a dead end.</b>
        There were <b>two Kolbe households in Soho at once</b>, and every Soho Kolbe record on this site
        has to be read against that. Whether the two families were related is not known here.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><b>And one for the Settler.</b>
    <em>London Courier and Evening Gazette</em>, 12 November 1819, and the <em>British Press</em> the
    next day: <em>&ldquo;the Ministers are preparing to receive Settlers proceeding to the Cape of Good
    Hope. In the Port of London &mdash; Chapman, 558 tons; <b>NAUTILUS, 403</b>; Agamemnon, Diadem, and
    Ocean. At Liverpool &mdash; Thetis, 530 tons&hellip;&rdquo;</em> <b>The ship George Augustus Kolbe
    sailed on, listed by tonnage in a London paper five months before it left</b>, while he was still a
    boy in his father&rsquo;s house in Soho.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="savoy-locked">16 September 2026 &middot; The Savoy register is filmed, digitised, and padlocked</h2>
  <p class="dek">Five of the eight sons are now named. <b>Three are not</b>, and the gaps in the
    Westminster baptism run &mdash; <b>1808 to 1812</b> and <b>1814 to 1818</b> &mdash; are where they
    must be. The burials of the ones who died are in the German Lutheran Church register at
    <span class="m">RG 4/4631</span>. <b>So the baptism register of the same church is the document that
    finishes the family</b>, and this archive went looking for it.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:18px">
    <p><b>It exists, and it is exactly what was wanted.</b> FamilySearch catalogue entry
      <span class="m">koha:176759</span> &mdash; <em>&ldquo;Church records of German Lutheran Church,
      1694&ndash;1840&rdquo;</em>, author <b>German Lutheran Church (Savoy, Westminster)</b>, filmed by
      the Genealogical Society of Utah in 1967 from <b>the original records at the Public Record
      Office</b>. The cover sheet calls it <em>&ldquo;Die Deutsche Evangelisch Lutherische S. Marien
      Kirche in der Savoy&rdquo;</em>. <b>Text in German. All volumes have a name index.</b></p>
  </div>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m" style="width:9rem">Film / DGS</th><th>What is on it</th><th class="m">Images</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m"><b>008004264</b></td><td><b>Births and baptisms 1694&ndash;1840</b>, marriages 1694&ndash;1754, burials 1694&ndash;1771</td><td class="m">537</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">008087181</td><td>Deaths and burials 1722&ndash;1840; births and baptisms <b>arranged by family</b> 1719&ndash;1782</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">008220012</td><td>A second filming, 1986, of baptisms 1694&ndash;1771 and 1781&ndash;1840</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><b>And every one of the 537 images carries a padlock.</b> Not
    some of them, not the recent ones &mdash; <b>image 1 to image 537</b>, on a signed-in account with a
    full subscription elsewhere. The film is digitised and the images are withheld: viewable only inside
    a <b>FamilySearch Center or an affiliate library</b>, because the record custodian is The National
    Archives and the contract says so.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Why this is a good result and not a bad one</h3>
      <p>Before today the three missing sons had <b>no route at all</b>. Now they have one, and it is
        short: <b>film 008004264, 537 images, a German-language register with its own name index, at any
        FamilySearch affiliate library.</b> The index means it is a half-hour job at the machine rather
        than a page-by-page sweep.</p>
      <p class="cap">And the same film holds the <b>marriages to 1754 and burials to 1771</b>, which
        would reach back past this family into whoever the Kolbes were before Broad Street.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">A seventh kind of shut door</h3>
      <p>This site keeps a list of the ways a source can be closed. <b>This is a new one:
        <em>digitised and withheld</em></b> &mdash; not lost, not undigitised, not paywalled, not
        bot-blocked. Photographed, catalogued, counted, and locked by contract, with the padlock drawn
        on every frame so you can see exactly what you are not allowed to read.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px">It is the most answerable of the seven, because the fix is
        a bus fare rather than a letter. <a href={u("/sources/#shut")}>The other six &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="pow-1901">1901 &middot; Three Booyzens on the <em>City of Cambridge</em> &mdash; the war, at last with a document behind it</h2>
  <p class="dek">This archive has carried the family's own account of the Anglo-Boer War for the whole
    project and never had a record for it. It has also established, carefully, that <b>no Booysen is on a
    Cape Rebel roll</b>. Both of those things were true and neither was the answer, because the question
    was being asked in the wrong colony.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin:20px 0">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0">The list, as printed</h3>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">No.</th><th>Surname</th><th>Christian name</th><th class="m">Ship</th><th class="m">Sailed</th><th class="m">Age</th><th>Entered from</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="m">18463</td><td><b>Booyzen</b></td><td><b>Stephanus Francois</b></td><td class="m">City of Cambridge</td><td class="m">3-6-01</td><td class="m"><b>28</b></td><td>Pretoria, Pretoria</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">18464</td><td><b>Booyzen</b></td><td><b>Petrus Jacobus</b></td><td class="m">do.</td><td class="m">do.</td><td class="m"><b>29</b></td><td>do. do.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">18465</td><td><b>Booyzen</b></td><td><b>David Schalk</b></td><td class="m">do.</td><td class="m">do.</td><td class="m"><b>54</b></td><td>do. do.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px"><strong>Source.</strong> "De List", Military Records
      1901&ndash;1903, South Africa Government Archives (Pretoria), volume no. 10469&ndash;32561, image
      group <span class="m">008019246</span> (item 3 of 9), <b>image 51 of 69</b>. Found through
      FamilySearch's full-text search, which reads the images themselves; read from the image, not from
      the transcript.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>Three consecutive numbers, one ship, one day, one district.</b> That is what the list says and
      it is the whole of what it says &mdash; there are no parents on it, no farm, and no commando. But
      consecutive prisoner numbers on a single transport are not a coincidence of filing: these three men
      were taken together and processed together, and then put on the same ship out of the country on
      <b>3 June 1901</b>.</p>

    <blockquote><b>The archive was asking the wrong question.</b> <a href={u("/rebels/")}>The rebels
      page</a> answers, correctly and at length, whether a Booysen appears on a <em>Cape Rebel</em> roll.
      He does not. A Cape Rebel is a British subject who took up arms against his own government. These
      men were <b>Transvaal burghers</b> fighting for their own republic, which is not rebellion and does
      not appear on that roll at all. Months of careful negative work, all of it sound, all of it aimed
      one colony to the south.</blockquote>

    <h3>What can be claimed, and what cannot</h3>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:11rem">Claim</th><th>Standing</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>Three men called Booyzen were Transvaal prisoners of war, deported 3 June 1901</b></td>
              <td><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> &mdash; it is a printed government list and it is legible.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>They were taken together</td>
              <td><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> from consecutive numbering on one transport. Strong, and an inference.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The David Schalk is <a href={u("/who/david-schalk-booysen/")}>this archive's David Schalk Booysen</a>, born Cradock 1848</td>
              <td><span class="chip open">Unproven.</span> The age is given as <b>54</b>; he would have been <b>52</b>, six days short of 53, when the ship sailed. He was demonstrably at Pretoria four years later, where he remarried. Nothing on the list names a parent or a birthplace.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The Stephanus Francois is <a href={u("/who/stephanus-francois-booyzen/")}>this archive's Stephanus Francois Booyzen</a></td>
              <td><span class="chip open">Unproven.</span> The age is <b>28</b>; this archive has him born about October 1874, which would make him <b>26</b>. He died at <b>East Lynne, Pretoria</b> &mdash; the district all three were entered from.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p><b>Both testable ages run high by the same margin</b>, one to two years. That is ordinary on lists
      taken in the field from men who had been on commando, and it is worth saying rather than
      smoothing.</p>

    <h3>And it touches the oldest dispute on the site</h3>
    <p>Whether <b>Stephanus Francois</b> is a son of David Schalk Booysen is marked
      <span class="chip open">Serious</span> on <a href={u("/disputed/")}>Disputed</a>, and the three
      arguments for it have all been <em>naming</em> arguments &mdash; the Marais forename, a bachelor of
      thirty at Pretoria in 1905, an eldest son called David Schalk. Naming arguments in this family are
      weak, as <a href={u("/namesakes/")}>the namesakes page</a> keeps demonstrating.</p>
    <blockquote><b>This is the fourth argument and the first that is not about a name.</b> He is 18463.
      David Schalk is 18465. Two apart, on one transport, on one day, out of one district &mdash; the
      district he would die in thirty-nine years later. <b>He has not been moved on Disputed</b>, because
      a transport list is not a document that names a parent. But the dispute no longer rests entirely on
      what people were called.</blockquote>
    <p class="cap">Where the ship went is not on the De List and has not been established here. The
      transports of 1901 carried Transvaal prisoners to Ceylon, India, Bermuda and St Helena.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="tolosberg-1901">1901 &middot; Tolosberg, 25 April &mdash; the register that names the commando</h2>
  <p class="dek">The De List gives a name, an age and a ship. <b>Item 1 of the same film gives the rest</b>
    &mdash; and its ages match this archive's own dates, where the De List's did not.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin:20px 0">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0">The register, as ruled</h3>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">No.</th><th>Surname</th><th>Christian name</th><th class="m">Age</th><th>Home address</th><th>Town or district</th><th><b>Commando</b></th><th><b>Where captured</b></th><th class="m">Date of capture</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="m">18462</td><td>Booysen</td><td>Adrian Jacobus</td><td class="m">33</td><td>Blaauwboschbank</td><td>Fauresmith</td><td>Edenburg</td><td>Springhaan Nek</td><td class="m">14 Dec 1900</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m"><b>18463</b></td><td><b>Booysen</b></td><td><b>Stefanus Francois</b></td><td class="m"><b>26</b></td><td><b>Pretoria</b></td><td><b>Pretoria</b></td><td><b>Pretoria</b></td><td><b>Tolosberg</b></td><td class="m"><b>25 April 1901</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m"><b>18464</b></td><td><b>Booysen</b></td><td><b>Petrus Jacobus</b></td><td class="m"><b>27</b></td><td class="m">do.</td><td class="m">do.</td><td class="m">do.</td><td class="m">do.</td><td class="m"><b>25 April 1901</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m"><b>18465</b></td><td><b>Booysen</b></td><td><b>David Schalk</b></td><td class="m"><b>53</b></td><td class="m">do.</td><td class="m">do.</td><td class="m">do.</td><td class="m">do.</td><td class="m"><b>25 April 1901</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">18466</td><td>Booysen</td><td>Gert</td><td class="m">25</td><td>Bloemfontein</td><td>Bloemfontein</td><td>Bloemfontein</td><td>Kaffirskraal</td><td class="m">2 Oct 1900</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px"><strong>Source.</strong> Same film, <b>item 1 of 9</b>, image
      <b>126 of 292</b> &mdash; "Prisoners of War at Cape Town and Natal", in the batch headed
      <em>"arriving in Cape Town and Natal from 5th April, 1901, to 27th May, 1901. Nos. 18,382 to
      19,430"</em>, whose title page is image 124. Found by bisecting the number column, then reading the
      B pages of the batch whose arrival dates bracket the sailing.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <blockquote><b>All three lived at PRETORIA, all three rode with the PRETORIA commando, and all three
      were taken at TOLOSBERG on 25 APRIL 1901.</b> Six weeks later they were on a ship.</blockquote>

    <p><b>Two Booysens sit beside them and are not with them.</b> 18462 Adrian Jacobus is a Free State man
      of Blaauwboschbank, Fauresmith, Edenburg commando, taken at Springhaan Nek in December 1900; 18466
      Gert is of Bloemfontein, taken at Kaffirskraal in October 1900. Neither shares the district, the
      commando, the place or the date. <b>The register separates those two as cleanly as it groups our
      three</b>, which is what makes the grouping worth anything.</p>

    <h3>The ages, which are the reason this matters</h3>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:13rem">Man</th><th class="m">This archive</th><th class="m">De List</th><th class="m">This register</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><a href={u("/who/david-schalk-booysen/")}>David Schalk Booysen</a></td><td class="m">b. 9 Jun 1848 &rarr; <b>52</b>, turning 53 that June</td><td class="m">54</td><td class="m"><b>53</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href={u("/who/stephanus-francois-booyzen/")}>Stephanus Francois Booyzen</a></td><td class="m">b. c. Oct 1874 &rarr; <b>26</b></td><td class="m">28</td><td class="m"><b>26</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Stefanus Francois is <b>exact</b>. David Schalk is within weeks of his fifty-third birthday.
      <b>The note published earlier that "both ages run one to two years high" described the De List and
      not the record</b>, and is withdrawn on <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Corrections</a>. The
      identification is no longer strained by the arithmetic &mdash; it is supported by it.</p>

    <blockquote><b>And the group is now hard to read as strangers.</b> A man of fifty-three and two men in
      their twenties, one address, one commando, taken in one action on one day, numbered consecutively,
      shipped together. <a href={u("/disputed/")}>Whether Stephanus Francois is David Schalk's son</a> is
      still not settled by a document naming a parent &mdash; and this is the nearest thing to one this
      archive has.</p>

    <h3 id="tolosberg-what">What happened at Tolosberg &mdash; and who Stephanus Francois turns out to be</h3>
    <p><b>Tolosberg is not in any gazetteer this archive could reach</b>, nor in the standard battle
      lists, nor in a day-by-day war diary of 1901. It is in one place: the medal-roll database at
      angloboerwar.com, kept by Dr David Biggins &mdash; and the entry there is about <b>this man, by
      this prisoner number</b>.</p>
    <blockquote>"Booysen was one of <b>42 Boers captured on 25 April 1901 at Tolosberg in the Waterval
      Valley, Eastern Transvaal</b>, together with 2 guns and much stock. He was sent to <b>India</b> as
      PoW <b>No 18463</b>."</blockquote>
    <p><b>18463 is the number in the register above.</b> Two record series, compiled for different
      purposes, agree on the man, the day, the place and the number.</p>

    <p>The same entry, drawn from a <b>City Coins auction catalogue of November 2020</b> describing his
      medal, says what he was:</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td style="width:11rem"><b>Unit</b></td><td>A member of the <b>ZARPs</b> &mdash; the
            Zuid-Afrikaansche Republiek Politie, the ZAR's police. Based at <b>Pretoria</b>, which is the
            home address the register gives all three men.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Commanding officer</b></td><td><b>Commandant J. P. de la C. Schroeder</b>, who
            completed Booysen's medal applications in full.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>In action at</b></td><td><b>Talana</b> &middot; <b>Modder Spruit</b> &middot;
            <b>Colenso</b> &middot; <b>Platrand (Wagon Hill)</b> &middot; <b>Spion Kop</b> &middot;
            <b>Dalmanutha</b> &mdash; the last of these the famous last stand of the ZARPs.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Wounded</b></td><td>At <b>Platrand (Wagon Hill)</b>, in the <b>right leg above the
            knee</b>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Decorations</b></td><td>The <b>ABO</b>, named <em>Burger S. F. Booysen</em>, with the
            <b>Lint voor Verwonding</b> &mdash; the wound ribbon.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>After capture</b></td><td>Sent to <b>India</b>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p><b>What this is and is not.</b> It is a specialist database entry quoting an auction catalogue
      describing a medal and the papers that came with it. This archive has <b>not</b> seen the medal, the
      ABO application, or Schroeder's signature. It is <span class="chip inf">strong secondary</span>
      evidence, and the prisoner number is what ties it to the register rather than to a name alone.</p>

    <blockquote><b>The family's own war testimony now has something to be tested against.</b> This archive
      has quoted <a href={u("/rebels/")}>Tersia Booyzen's account</a> in full for the whole project and
      measured it only against surname distributions and rebel district figures, because there was no
      record. There is one now: a policeman of Pretoria who was at Talana in the first week of the war,
      wounded at Wagon Hill, present at Spion Kop and at the ZARPs' last stand, taken at Tolosberg with
      forty-one others and two guns, and shipped to India.</p>
    <p class="cap"><b>Tolosberg is still not on <a href={u("/atlas/")}>the atlas</a></b> &mdash; "the
      Waterval Valley, Eastern Transvaal" is the only location this archive has for it, and no map
      consulted so far carries the name.</p>
  </div>
</section>


<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="turffontein-1918">1918 &middot; Richard Peter Booyzen at Turffontein &mdash; and a good idea that does not survive the document</h2>
  <p class="dek">He came into this archive at lunchtime, off a burial register, as an <em>inferred</em>
    ninth child. His death registration is here, and it does two things: it makes the identification a
    great deal stronger, and it <b>kills the best theory this archive had</b> about his mother.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:20px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/sterfgeval-1918-richard-peter.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Form of Information of a Death, 1918: Richard Peter Booyzen, 24, of 18 Anderson Street, Turffontein, European, born Cape of Good Hope, married, died of influenza and pneumonia." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Form of Information of a Death</strong>, Ordinance 19 of 1906
        (Transvaal), no. <span class="m">11487</span>. Registered at Johannesburg
        <strong>27 October 1918</strong>, entry <span class="m">4983</span>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <div class="scroll">
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1. Name</td><td><b>Richard Peter Booyzen</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>3. Residence</td><td><b>18 Anderson Street, TURFFONTEIN</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>4. Age</td><td><b>24 years</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>5. Birthplace</b></td><td><b>C. of G. Hope</b> &middot; European</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>6. Condition</b></td><td><b>MARRIED</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>7. Occupation</b></td><td><b>Sawyer</b> <span class="cap">&mdash; the initial is ambiguous and could be read Lawyer; at 18 Anderson Street it will not be</span></td></tr>
            <tr><td>8. Died</td><td><b>25 October 1918</b>, at that address</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>11. Cause</b></td><td><b>INFLUENZA &amp; PNEUMONIA</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>11a. Duration</td><td><b>eleven days</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>10. Burial</td><td>New Cemetery, Johannesburg &mdash; he went to <b>Brixton</b> on the 27th</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figure style="margin:20px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1918-influenza-pneumonia.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Lines five to eleven: European, born C. of G. Hope; Married; Occupation Sawyer; died twenty-fifth October 1918; causes of death influenza and pneumonia; duration eleven days." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>&ldquo;C. of G. Hope&rdquo; &middot; &ldquo;Married&rdquo; &middot;
      &ldquo;Influenza &amp; Pneumonia&rdquo; &middot; &ldquo;11 days&rdquo;.</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What it strengthens</h3>
      <p><b>Born at the Cape of Good Hope, dead at Johannesburg in 1918.</b> That is the migration of
        every one of Kathleen Barry&rsquo;s children &mdash; born in the north-eastern Cape, dead on the
        Rand. With the forename that is her own brother&rsquo;s, and a birth about 1894 that falls exactly
        in the gap between her daughter of 1891 and her son of 1901, the inference is now a good deal
        firmer than it was at lunchtime. It is <b>still not documented</b>: this form names no parents,
        because a Transvaal death registration does not ask.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What it kills</h3>
      <p>This archive has an open question &mdash; <b>why was Kathleen Barry a &ldquo;Military
        Pensioner&rdquo;</b> at her death in 1940? Her husband died in 1905 of a stomach ulcer, so the
        pension is not his, and the page has said for months that <em>&ldquo;no son of hers is among the
        First World War dead&rdquo;</em>.
        <br /><br />
        <b>A son of hers dead in October 1918 looked like the answer.</b> Enormous numbers of South
        African servicemen died in that epidemic and their dependants were pensioned.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">
    <b>He was not a soldier.</b> Occupation: a trade. Condition: <b>married</b> &mdash; so he had a wife,
    and a married son&rsquo;s death does not pension his mother in any case. He died in his own house in
    Turffontein of influenza and pneumonia after eleven days, and the man who reported it gave an address
    in Market Buildings in town.
    <br /><br />
    <b>The theory is rejected.</b> It was a good theory &mdash; the right son, the right age, the right
    month &mdash; and the document says no. <b>The open question stands, and it is now better specified:
    the pension belongs to a son who SERVED AND SURVIVED</b>, which is exactly what the letter already
    sitting at the SANDF Documentation Centre asks about.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open question &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <h3>Three more Booyzens at Johannesburg, and why none of them is claimed</h3>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:20rem">Who</th><th>Why not</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>Petrus Jacobus Booyzen</b><br /><span class="cap">b. May 1882, d. May 1961, buried Johannesburg 20 May</span></td>
              <td>The best of the three. A birth in May 1882 would make him Kathleen Barry&rsquo;s eldest,
                ahead of John Barry in 1884, and Petrus Jacobus is the most-repeated name this family has.
                <b>And that is the whole case</b> &mdash; a name and a gap. There is no document.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Petrus Jacobus Van Blerk Booyzen</b><br /><span class="cap">b. 1872, d. 17 Mar 1949, buried West Park</span></td>
              <td><b>Van Blerk</b> is a mother&rsquo;s surname carried as a forename, and there is no Van
                Blerk anywhere in this family. A different Booyzen household on the Rand.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Jan Gerhardus Lodewikus Booysen</b><br /><span class="cap">buried Johannesburg 17 Dec 1943</span></td>
              <td>The name is exactly right &mdash; it runs from Gerthardus Lodewikus at the top of this
                line down to the man who died at Vaalbank in 1885. But the 1855 child of that name would
                be eighty-eight in 1943, the register gives no age at all, and the name is common enough
                in the wider family that this archive will not guess.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>John Zacharia Booysen</b><br /><span class="cap">d. 25 Nov 1918, buried Brixton the 26th, aged 29</span></td>
              <td><b>Rejected.</b> ZACHARIA reaches nothing in this family, and his death is a civil
                registration, which names no parents &mdash; so nothing could ever tie him either way. He
                is set down because three Booysens went into Brixton in eight weeks and the shape of that
                is worth recording.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="cap">South Africa, Transvaal, Civil Death, 1869&ndash;1954 &middot; FamilySearch
      <span class="m">1:1:7D96-H9N2</span>, with <span class="m">1:1:7DQQ-B7ZM</span>,
      <span class="m">1:1:WDD4-33T2</span> and <span class="m">1:1:6J4Y-782H</span>.
      Read 13 September 2026.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="kolbe-1291">1915 &middot; Estate 1291 &mdash; Margaretha Kolbe&rsquo;s own notice, and a ninth Barry child</h2>
  <p class="dek">This archive looked for her estate on the films either side of <b>925</b>, her
    husband&rsquo;s, and did not find it. It is <b>1291</b>, and it is indexed under
    <em>Cape, KIMBERLEY, Probate Records of the Supreme Court</em> while the probate itself was granted
    at <b>Grahamstown</b> &mdash; which is exactly how a record hides.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:20px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1915-margaretha-kolbe.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Death Notice 1291 of 1915 for Margaret Louisa Elizabeth Barry, born Kolbe, of Manorowen, Barkly East." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Form 52C, stamped <strong>25 May 1915</strong>. The left-hand page is the
        Afrikaans <em>Sterfkennis</em>, left entirely blank; this family filled in the English side.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <div class="scroll">
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1. Name</td><td><b>Margaret Louisa Elizabeth Barry, born Kolbe</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>2. Birthplace</td><td><b>&ldquo;Believe Colesberg District&rdquo;</b> &middot; nationality <b>&ldquo;Afrikander of European extraction&rdquo;</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>3. Father</b></td><td><b>&ldquo;Kolbe, thinks James was Christian name&rdquo;</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>3. Mother</b></td><td><b>&ldquo;Unknown&rdquo;</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>4. Age</td><td><b>84 years</b> &mdash; born about 1828</td></tr>
            <tr><td>5. Occupation of her husband</td><td><b>Farmer</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>6. Residence</td><td><b>Manorowen</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>7. Condition</td><td><b>Widow</b> &mdash; of <b>&ldquo;John Barry in August 1904&rdquo;</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>7c. Place of marriage</td><td><b>&ldquo;Unknown&rdquo;</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>8. Died</td><td><b>2 August 1912</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>9. Where</b></td><td><b>the house of her son R. P. Barry</b>, Manorowen, Barkly East</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>10. Children</b></td><td><b>NINE</b>, all majors</td></tr>
            <tr><td>11&ndash;14</td><td>no movable property, no immovable, estate <b>Nil</b>, <b>no will</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Signed</td><td><b>R. P. Barry</b> at Barkly East, 13 May 1915, as <b>&ldquo;Son in whose house deceased died&rdquo;</b></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figure style="margin:20px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1915-kolbe-parents.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Questions one to three: Margaret Louisa Elizabeth Barry born Kolbe; Believe Colesberg District, Afrikander of European extraction; father Kolbe, thinks James was Christian name; mother Unknown." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>&ldquo;Afrikander of European extraction&rdquo;</strong> &middot;
      <strong>&ldquo;Kolbe, thinks James was Christian name&rdquo;</strong> &middot;
      <strong>&ldquo;Unknown&rdquo;</strong>. Her father was <b>George Augustus Kolbe</b> and her mother
      <b>Margaret Downing</b>, and both names are cut into a standing gravestone at Burgersdorp.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Nine children, and one of them is new</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1915-nine-children.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question ten of the 1915 notice: John, James, Henry, George, Frederick, William, Catharina, Margaret, and Richard Peter — all majors." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Question ten, entire. <strong>John &middot; Richard Peter &middot; James
      &middot; Henry &middot; George &middot; Frederick &middot; WILLIAM &middot; Catharina &middot;
      Margaret</strong> &mdash; &ldquo;all majors&rdquo;.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:14rem">1915, hers</th><th style="width:22rem">1915, his (estate 925)</th><th></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>John</td><td>John Augustus Barry</td><td class="m">&#10003;</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Richard Peter</td><td>Richard Peter Barry</td><td class="m">&#10003;</td></tr>
        <tr><td>James</td><td>James Henry Barry</td><td class="m">&#10003;</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Henry</td><td>Henry Nicholas Barry</td><td class="m">&#10003;</td></tr>
        <tr><td>George</td><td>George Augustus Barry</td><td class="m">&#10003;</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Frederick</td><td>Frederick Fortunatus Barry</td><td class="m">&#10003;</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Catharina</td><td>Kathleen Mary Boyson, born Barry</td><td class="m">&#10003;</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Margaret</td><td>Margaret Mary Bedford, born Barry</td><td class="m">&#10003;</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>WILLIAM</b></td><td class="m">&mdash; not on it &mdash;</td><td><b>NEW</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">
    <b>&ldquo;Eight is not eleven&rdquo; becomes &ldquo;nine is not eleven&rdquo;.</b>
    <br /><br />
    John Barry&rsquo;s 1903 registration answers the printed question <em>&ldquo;the total number of
    children deceased has had&rdquo;</em> with <b>ELEVEN</b>. His own 1915 death notice named
    <b>EIGHT</b>, and this archive has been carrying three unnamed children since.
    <b>Her notice names a ninth: WILLIAM.</b> He is on no other document here. Two are still missing, and
    one of them may be <b>Fred J. Barry</b> or <b>Ruben Jacobus Josuah Barry</b>, both of whom this site
    already holds as <span class="chip open">open</span>.
    <a href="#barry-1915">The 1915 notice &rarr;</a> &middot;
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">And two things the family had lost</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1915-john-barry-1904.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question seven: name and date of death of previous spouse — John Barry in August 1904; place of last marriage, Unknown; died 2nd August 1912 at the house of son R. P. Barry, Manorowen, Barkly East." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>&ldquo;John Barry in August 1904&rdquo;</strong> &middot;
      <strong>place of last marriage: &ldquo;Unknown&rdquo;</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>John Barry died on 25 August 1903</b> &mdash; his own registration says so, the one where he is
      called <em>an Irishman, born in Ireland</em>. His son, writing twelve years later, puts it in
      <b>August 1904</b>. The month is right and the year is wrong by one.</p>
    <p>And where his parents were married, the same son writes <b>Unknown</b> &mdash; while on his
      father&rsquo;s notice, three months later, he writes <b>&ldquo;Burghersdorp (probably)&rdquo;</b>.
      <b>Two documents by one man in one year, and he cannot place his own parents&rsquo; wedding on
      either.</b> Her father is <em>&ldquo;Kolbe, thinks James was Christian name&rdquo;</em>; her mother
      is <em>&ldquo;Unknown&rdquo;</em>, when the archive knows both names stand cut into a gravestone at
      Burgersdorp. <a href={u("/disputed/")}>What that is worth &rarr;</a></p>
    <figure style="margin:20px 0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1915-nothing-at-all.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Questions eleven to fourteen: no movable property, no immovable property, estate Nil, no will; signed R. P. Barry, son in whose house deceased died." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>No &middot; No &middot; Nil &middot; No.</strong> And the capacity
        in which he signs: <strong>&ldquo;Son in whose house deceased died.&rdquo;</strong></figcaption>
    </figure>

    <blockquote><b>She left nothing at all.</b> No movable property, no immovable property, estate
      <b>Nil</b>, no will. A farmer&rsquo;s widow of eighty-four, dead in her son&rsquo;s house at
      Manorowen, and the file exists only because the law required one.</blockquote>
    <p class="cap">South Africa, Cape, Kimberley, Probate Records of the Supreme Court, 1871&ndash;1937
      &middot; estate <span class="m">1291</span>, probate granted at Grahamstown 1915 &middot;
      FamilySearch <span class="m">1:1:QG2S-F1QT</span>. Read 13 September 2026.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="barry-fonts">1847&ndash;1866 &middot; The Barry children at the font &mdash; and William, found</h2>
  <p class="dek">Yesterday <b>William Barry</b> existed on one line of his mother&rsquo;s death notice and
    nowhere else. Here is his baptism. And the page it is on does something else: it puts the two Kolbe
    sisters this archive had to separate <b>side by side in one church on one afternoon</b>.</p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:17rem">Child</th><th class="m" style="width:8rem">Born</th><th>Baptised</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>John August Barry</b> <span class="cap">&mdash; John Augustus Barry II</span></td><td class="m">13 Mar 1847</td><td>12 Apr 1847</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>George Augustus Barry</b></td><td class="m">26 Apr 1850</td><td>9 Jun 1850, <b>Graaff-Reinet</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>WILLIAM FREDRIK BARRY</b></td><td class="m"><b>23 Aug 1853</b></td><td><b>23 October 1853, BURGERSDORP</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Catherina Mary Sophia Barry</b> <span class="cap">&mdash; &ldquo;Kathleen&rdquo;, the direct line</span></td><td class="m"><b>4 Aug 1859</b></td><td>1859, Graaff-Reinet &middot; and again 25 Dec 1859, Dordrecht</td></tr>
        <tr><td>William Frederick Thomas Barry <span class="chip inf">a second William</span></td><td class="m">&mdash;</td><td>29 Sep 1861</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Frederik Fortunatus Barry</b></td><td class="m">10 Nov 1863</td><td>10 Jul 1864, <b>Dordrecht</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pieter Richard Barry <span class="chip inf">probably Richard Peter</span></td><td class="m">13 Jun 1866</td><td>11 Oct 1866, <b>Lady Grey</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">All from the Dutch Reformed registers, under <b>John Barry</b>
    and <b>Margaret(ha) Wilhelmina Kolbe</b> &mdash; spelled Kolbe, Kolber, Colbe, Colby and Coetzee-adjacent
    nonsense across seven entries. The last two are marked <span class="chip inf">inferred</span>: the
    1861 child is a <em>second</em> William and the archive does not know whether the first died or the
    clerk erred, and the 1866 entry gives the mother a surname this archive cannot read as Kolbe.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Entry 2187, and the entry above it</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/doopregister-1853-burgersdorp.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="A double page of the Burgersdorp Dutch Reformed baptism register for 1853, entries 2184 to 2192." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Het Jaar 1853.</strong> Nine baptisms to the opening, each one set out
      the same way: the child, the birth, the christening, the father, the mother, and the
      <em>getuigen</em> who stood for it.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1853-two-sisters.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Entries 2186 and 2187 of the Burgersdorp baptism register, 23 October 1853: Augustus Kolbe, son of George Fredk Enslin and Mary Elizabeth Kolbe; and William Fredrik, son of John Barry and Margareth Kolbe." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Two entries, one page, one afternoon &mdash; <strong>23 October 1853</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">2187 &mdash; the Barry child</h3>
      <p><b>Het Kind is: WILLIAM FREDRIK</b><br />
        Geb. <b>23 Aug 1853</b> &middot; Ged. <b>23 Oct 1853</b><br />
        V. <b>John Barry</b> &middot; M. <b>Margareth Kolbe</b><br />
        Get. <b>John Kolbe &middot; Anna van der Merwe &middot; George Kolbe</b></p>
      <p class="cap">Three godparents, and two of them are her brothers. <b>Anna van der Merwe</b> stands
        at a Barry font in 1853 &mdash; a surname this archive has been chasing on the other side of the
        family entirely.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">2186 &mdash; two entries above</h3>
      <p><b>Het Kind is: AUGUSTUS KOLBE</b><br />
        Geb. 7 Sept 1853 &middot; Ged. <b>23 Oct 1853</b><br />
        V. <b>George Fredk ENSLIN</b> &middot; M. <b>MARY ELIZABETH KOLBE</b><br />
        Get. Petrus Joubert &middot; <b>Catharine Kolbe</b> &middot; Margaretha Maria Enslin</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <b>That is the Enslin problem, settled a second time and from a completely different direction.</b>
    <br /><br />
    This archive carried a contradiction marked <span class="chip inf">Serious</span>: Margaretha Kolbe
    appeared to marry <b>George Frederick Enslin</b> in 1850 while married to John Barry, and to bear
    Barry and Enslin children three and a half months apart in 1851. It was resolved from
    <b>Margaret Downing&rsquo;s 1867 death notice</b>, which lists among her children
    <em>&ldquo;Mary Elizabeth (dead) who was married to George F. Enslin&rdquo;</em>. The Enslin marriage
    belongs to Margaretha&rsquo;s <b>sister</b>.
    <br /><br />
    <b>Here are the two sisters, on one page, on one day, baptising a son each.</b> Mary Elizabeth Kolbe
    with her Enslin, Margareth Kolbe with her Barry, two entries apart in the Burgersdorp register on
    23 October 1853. <a href={u("/disputed/")}>The contradiction &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p><b>And it puts them at Burgersdorp.</b> The 1915 death notice says the marriage was at
      <em>&ldquo;Burghersdorp (probably)&rdquo;</em> &mdash; a son guessing, sixty-five years on, at
      something nobody had told him. They were baptising a child in that church in 1853, and the
      godparents were her own brothers. The guess was right.</p>
    <blockquote><b>Three more Kolbes come out of these two entries</b> &mdash; <b>John Kolbe</b> and
      <b>George Kolbe</b>, godfathers at the Barry font, and <b>Catharine Kolbe</b>, godmother at the
      Enslin one. Margaretha and Mary Elizabeth had a family around them at Burgersdorp, and this archive
      has been treating Margaretha as though she arrived alone.</blockquote>
    <p class="cap">South Africa, Dutch Reformed Church Registers (Cape Town Archives), 1660&ndash;1994
      &middot; Burgersdorp, <em>Het Jaar 1853</em>, entries <span class="m">2186</span> and
      <span class="m">2187</span> &middot; FamilySearch <span class="m">1:1:6FWQ-6YHH</span>, with six
      sibling baptisms at <span class="m">6VY5-78QK</span>, <span class="m">6VPS-NL5M</span>,
      <span class="m">6KNX-8WY8</span>, <span class="m">623R-9MM9</span>,
      <span class="m">62R5-6QHP</span> and <span class="m">653H-1PL6</span>.
      Read 13 September 2026.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="mountjoy-thirteen">1852&ndash;1871 &middot; Eight of the thirteen Mountjoy children, and the Keys question answered</h2>
  <p class="dek">This archive has known since the marriage certificate turned up that
    <b>George Mountjoy and Maria Sophia Botha</b>, married at Cradock on 20 August 1848, had
    <b>thirteen children</b> &mdash; and it could name <b>one</b>. The Dutch Reformed baptism registers
    name eight, and one of the eight settles an open question.</p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:20rem">Child</th><th class="m" style="width:9rem">Born</th><th>Baptised</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>Anna Catharina Mountjoy</b></td><td class="m">5 Aug 1852</td><td>11 Jan 1854</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Maria Elizabeth Mountjoy</b></td><td class="m">12 Jun 1858</td><td>14 Jun 1858, <b>Graaff-Reinet</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Johanna Catharina Mountjoy</b></td><td class="m">28 Jan 1860</td><td><b>29 February 1860, Burgersdorp</b> &mdash; a leap day</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>George Herkules Johannes Mountjoy</b></td><td class="m">16 Jan 1862</td><td>27 Apr 1862, <b>Smithfield, Orange Free State</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Elsje Petronella Frederika Mountjoy</b></td><td class="m">10 Jun 1864</td><td>17 Jul 1864, <b>Smithfield</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hercules Johannes Mountjoy <span class="cap">already held</span></td><td class="m">16 Jul 1866</td><td>29 Jul 1866</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Jacoba Regina Mountjoy</b></td><td class="m">14 Jun 1868</td><td>15 Aug 1868</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Petronella Christina Aletta Mountjoy</b></td><td class="m">10 Oct 1870</td><td>3 Mar 1871, <b>Swellendam</b> &mdash; <span class="chip inf">doubted</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">Seven of these sit in one country &mdash; Graaff-Reinet,
    Burgersdorp and Smithfield are within a hundred miles of each other on the Cape&ndash;Free State
    border, and the family was crossing it. <b>Swellendam is six hundred miles west</b> and this archive
    does not accept that one; the parents&rsquo; names match and the geography does not.</p>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <b>And the Keys question is answered.</b> This site has carried a <b>William Henry Keys</b> who
    married <b>a Johanna Catharina Mountjoy</b> and baptised children at Dordrecht, Burgersdorp and
    Barkly East between 1878 and 1898 &mdash; among them <b>James Jonathan Keys</b> and <b>Herklaas
    Edward Johannes Keys</b>, Mountjoy forenames crossing into a third surname. It has been asking
    <em>which</em> Johanna Catharina Mountjoy.
    <br /><br />
    <b>This one.</b> The Keys marriage record estimates her birth at <b>1859</b>; the Burgersdorp font
    gives <b>28 January 1860</b>. She is <b>George Mountjoy&rsquo;s daughter</b>, and the Mountjoy
    forenames that turn up in the Keys children are her father&rsquo;s and her brother&rsquo;s.
    <a href={u("/namesakes/")}>The namesakes &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p>Their children, from the registers: <b>Robert Byron</b> (1878), <b>William Henry</b> (1882),
      <b>James Jonathan</b> (1883), <b>George</b> (1884), <b>Lancelott Byron</b> (1888),
      <b>Cecil Frederik</b> (1891), <b>Rachel Anna Maria</b> (1895) and <b>Herklaas Edward Johannes</b>
      (1898) &mdash; eight, baptised across Dordrecht, Barkly East and Barkly West. <b>Byron</b> twice,
      which is not a name this archive has met anywhere else.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="barry-eleven">1847&ndash;1863 &middot; The two missing Barry children, found &mdash; and an API that lies by wrapping</h2>
  <p class="dek">Open question 13 has been on this site since it was written: the 1903 death
    registration says John Augustus Barry left <b>eleven</b> children, his own death notice names
    <b>eight</b>, his wife&rsquo;s names <b>nine</b>, and two have never been identified. The question&rsquo;s
    own note said the index route was closed. <b>It was closed to a name search. It was never closed to
    a relationship search.</b></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:22px">The nine baptisms, in order</h3>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:9rem">Born</th><th style="width:8rem">Baptised</th><th style="width:15rem">As indexed</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">13 Mar 1847</td><td class="m">12 Apr 1847</td><td>John August Berry</td><td class="cap">Cape Colony &mdash; known</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">13 Jul 1848</td><td class="m">13 Nov 1868</td><td>Henry Jaonas / Hennery James</td><td class="cap">baptised at twenty &mdash; known</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">26 Apr 1850</td><td class="m">9 Jun 1850</td><td>George Augustus Barry</td><td class="cap">Graaff-Reinet &mdash; known</td></tr>
        <tr style="background:var(--panel)"><td class="m"><b>5 Apr 1852</b></td><td class="m"><b>5 Apr 1852</b></td><td><b>Margrith Louisa Maria</b><br /><span class="cap">also Margreth Louisa Mary</span></td><td><b>BURGERSDORP &mdash; NEW. No surname of her own in the index.</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">23 Aug 1853</td><td class="m">23 Oct 1853</td><td>William Fredrik Barry</td><td class="cap">Burgersdorp &mdash; known</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">10 Jul 1855</td><td class="m">22 Jul 1855</td><td>Catherine Mary Sophia</td><td class="cap">Burgersdorp &mdash; found 14 September; died young</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">4 Aug 1859</td><td class="m">25 Dec 1859</td><td>Catharina Mary Sophia Barry</td><td class="cap">Dordrecht &mdash; Kathleen, who married into the Booyzens</td></tr>
        <tr style="background:var(--panel)"><td class="m"><b>29 Sep 1861</b></td><td class="m"><b>29 Sep 1861</b></td><td><b>William Frederick Thomas Barry</b></td><td><b>GRAAFF-REINET &mdash; NEW. A SECOND William Frederick.</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">10 Nov 1863</td><td class="m">10 Jul 1864</td><td>Frederik Fortutaums Barry</td><td class="cap">Dordrecht &mdash; Frederick Fortunatus, known</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Why neither could be found by name</h3>
      <p><b>Margrith Louisa Maria has no surname in the index.</b> Two separate index passes of the same
        register give &ldquo;Margrith Louisa Maria&rdquo; and &ldquo;Margreth Louisa Mary&rdquo;, and the
        Name column stops there. <b>It is the blank Name column again</b> &mdash; the same failure that
        hid Catherine Mary Sophia of 1855 until a fortnight ago.</p>
      <p><b>William Frederick Thomas is invisible for the opposite reason.</b> He is not missing a name;
        he has <b>the same one as his brother</b>. A search for William Barry returns the 1853 child and
        stops, and nothing about the result says there is a second.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px">Both were found by asking the index for a
        <b>relationship</b> &mdash; father John Barry, mother a Kolbe &mdash; and letting it return
        whatever the child was called. <a href={u("/method/#relationship-not-person")}>The method &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And the count comes right</h3>
      <p><b>Nine baptisms in the registers, plus Hendrik Nicholaas and Richard Peter</b> &mdash; both
        named on their parents&rsquo; death notices and in no register this archive can find &mdash;
        <b>makes eleven</b>, which is exactly the number the 1903 death registration gives.</p>
      <p><b>Margaret Mary</b>, named on the death notices and never placed, is almost certainly
        <b>Margrith Louisa Maria</b>: it is her mother&rsquo;s name, and there is no other candidate in
        the sibling group. <span class="chip inf">Inferred from the count and the name</span></p>
      <p class="cap">And two Williams eight years apart means, by this family&rsquo;s own practice, that
        <b>the first William died</b> &mdash; which is what the archive concluded about the two Catherine
        Mary Sophias. A baptism is not done twice. The &ldquo;WILLIAM who appears on nothing else&rdquo;
        on the wife&rsquo;s death notice is one of these two, and the notice does not say which.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><b>The citations.</b> Margrith Louisa Maria:
    <span class="m">ark:/61903/1:1:6VYG-SS94</span> and <span class="m">1:1:6F4L-76PK</span>, images
    <span class="m">3:1:3Q9M-CSVC-XPSY</span> and <span class="m">3:1:3Q9M-CSFQ-QLQN</span> &mdash; the
    second of which is on <b>film 008039088</b>, the Burgersdorp register this archive has been sweeping
    by hand. William Frederick Thomas: <span class="m">1:1:623R-9MM9</span> and
    <span class="m">1:1:62GJ-SD8Z</span>, images <span class="m">3:1:3Q9M-CSK6-9S81-J</span> and
    <span class="m">3:1:3Q9M-CSVD-5VNZ</span>. <b>Each child is indexed twice, and neither index pass
    agrees with the other about the spelling.</b></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">And a trap in the tool, which is worth as much as the finding</h3>
  <p>The search API accepts <span class="m">q.filmNumber</span>, and for film 008039088 it reports
    <b>22,059 records</b> &mdash; the whole indexed film, which would have made the hand sweep
    unnecessary. It also accepts an <span class="m">offset</span>, and pages correctly to
    <b>offset 1000</b>.</p>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:12px">At <b>offset 1001 it silently returns page one again</b>. No
    error, no warning, no change in the reported total. The response still says
    <span class="m">"results": 22059</span> and still says <span class="m">"index": 0</span>.</blockquote>
  <p class="note" style="margin-top:12px"><b>An unchecked sweep would have re-read the first thousand
    records twenty-two times and reported the film as holding 1,400 entries.</b> It was caught by
    comparing the first name returned at offset 0 against offset 1400 &mdash; they were identical
    &mdash; and then bisecting to find the edge. <b>This is the fourth undocumented boundary this
    archive has hit in a week</b>, after the church films beginning in 1843, the estate inventories
    stopping in the 1830s, and NAAIRS defaulting to OR. It is the only one so far that answers
    <em>wrongly</em> rather than emptily.</p>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px"><b>Which is why the film sweep is not abandoned.</b> The API
    will show 1,001 of 22,059 records and no more, so the remaining 214 images of the hand sweep still
    have to be stepped. What the API is good for is exactly what it did here: <b>a targeted relationship
    query, where the answer is small enough to fit inside the first thousand.</b></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="graves-and-memoir">The gravestone collection swept, and what a memoir is worth</h2>
  <p class="dek">Two of the five surnames are simply not in the Genealogical Society&rsquo;s gravestone
    data, and this time the zero can be trusted &mdash; because three controls were run first.</p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:12rem">Surname</th><th style="width:7rem">Stones</th><th>What that is worth</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>BARRY</b></td><td class="m">54</td><td class="cap">The control. The database answers, and answers a lot.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>KOLBE</b></td><td class="m">4</td><td class="cap">Three of them new to this archive.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>BOOYZEN</b></td><td class="m">3</td><td class="cap">All three from one family cemetery.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>MOUNTJOY</b></td><td class="m">0</td><td class="cap"><b>A real absence</b>, not a broken search.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>DASCHNER</b></td><td class="m">0</td><td class="cap"><b>The same.</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px"><b>This is the method working as intended.</b> A month ago this
    archive would have written &ldquo;no Mountjoy gravestones&rdquo; and moved on. The number that makes
    that sentence mean anything is not nought &mdash; it is <b>fifty-four</b>.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:26px">The three Kolbe stones</h3>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:12px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:13rem"><b>Kolbe, GE</b></td><td>Concentration Camp cemetery, <b>Volksrust</b>, died before 1899&ndash;1902. This archive knew of a Kolbe dead in that camp; <b>it now has the initials</b>, and they are two of the Commandant&rsquo;s three.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Van der Spuy, MH</b></td><td>Old Utrecht Cemetery, KwaZulu-Natal, d. <b>13 August 1946</b> &mdash; <b>Margaret Harriet</b>, born Kolbe. Her Natal estate, NAB MSCE 1639/1946, was found earlier the same afternoon. <b>Now she has a grave as well as a file.</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Fourie, MD</b></td><td>New Gelderland Cemetery, Natal, <b>29 September 1899 &ndash; 8 December 1979</b>. <b>M.D.</b> on a Kolbe-connected stone, eighty years after Margaret Downing died at Burgersdorp.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><b>And the three Booyzen stones are one household.</b>
    <b>Viljoen, JCE</b> (2 July 1807 &ndash; 11 December 1861), <b>Viljoen, BC</b> (8 January 1807 &ndash;
    25 November 1859) and <b>Bezuidenhout, JCE</b> (30 October 1831 &ndash; 11 January 1904), all in the
    <b>Bezuidenhout Family Cemetery at Bezuidenhout Park, Johannesburg</b>. That is
    <b>Judith Cornelia Theresia Booijsen</b> &mdash; b3c4d5e6, baptised 16 August 1807, the woman whose
    three names came from her Viljoen mother &mdash; <b>with her husband and her daughter</b>. This
    archive had her stone. It did not have the other two, or know they were in the same ground.
    <a href={u("/namesakes/")}>Namesakes &rarr;</a></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">Cradock in January 1822, because he counted it</h3>
  <p>This archive keeps saying it names the people documents leave out. Here is a town, complete, in one
    sentence &mdash; five years after James Mountjoy married in it and fifteen before the Booyzens
    arrived to marry there too.</p>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:12px">At that time there were <b>only about half a dozen inhabitants in
    the place</b>. All that I can remember are Messrs. <b>SOLOMON</b> and <b>S.M. DE BEER</b>, the
    <b>Rev. Mr. EVANS</b> (D.R. Church), Mr. <b>[P. GIE]</b>, <b>James MOUNTJOY</b> (a saddler, who was
    discharged from the 21st Light Dragoons), the two Messrs. <b>ZIERVOGEL</b> (one was Secretary to
    Government), Mr. <b>HEILBERG</b>, Fieldcornet <b>B.J. VORSTER</b>, and <b>Gert VAN DEVENTER</b>.</blockquote>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">Nine names and a constable who demanded his pass in the street.
    <b>Solomon is the constable</b> &mdash; Montgomery refuses to show him the pass, walks to the
    Landdrost&rsquo;s office four miles out at <b>Driefontein</b> across the Fish River, and arrives
    &ldquo;just in time to prevent myself being brought up a prisoner&rdquo;, because Solomon already had
    the warrant. <b>Captain HARDING</b> is the Landdrost, &ldquo;a little cross-grained Irishman&rdquo;.
    <b>It is the only picture this archive has of the place its people were living in.</b></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">And what a memoir is worth &mdash; a position, since two of them cost walls</h3>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>John Montgomery was recalling <b>forty-seven years back</b> when he wrote that, and forty-five
      when he placed George Augustus Kolbe at a mission school. This archive has taken two substantial
      claims from him. It should say what that kind of source is.</p>
    <p><b>A memoir is a witness, not a record.</b> It has no clerk, no form, no oath and no date of
      composition close to the event. What it has instead is <b>circumstantial specificity</b> &mdash;
      Montgomery does not say &ldquo;a saddler lived there&rdquo;, he says which regiment, and he slept
      in the man&rsquo;s house and ate his breakfast.</p>
    <blockquote>The test this archive is using: <b>does the memoir claim something it had no reason to
      invent, and does anything independent touch it?</b></blockquote>
    <p><b>On Koolskop, something does.</b> A Cape Town library catalogue written from the papers
      themselves calls George Augustus Kolbe an <b>LMS missionary</b>, and this page had already deduced
      a mission circuit from where his children were born. Three lines of evidence, none of them aware
      of the others. <b>That one is as good as an unverified claim gets.</b></p>
    <p><b>On the 21st Dragoons, nothing does &mdash; yet.</b> The regiment is real, it was at the Cape
      from 1806 to 1816, and the man married there in 1817. But he is <b>not</b> in any name-catalogued
      discharge series at Kew, and the reason may be structural or may be that Montgomery misremembered
      a regiment forty-seven years on. <b>Both remain open</b>, and the site says so rather than letting
      a good sentence do the work of a document.</p>
    <p class="cap"><b>The asymmetry is the point.</b> One memoir claim is corroborated and one is not,
      and they are written up differently on this site for exactly that reason &mdash; not because one
      is a better story.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="nautilus-storm">December 1819 &middot; What the voyage was like, from a man on the same deck</h2>
  <p class="dek">This archive has had the <em>Nautilus</em> as a set of dates since the settler roll was
    checked: Gravesend on 3 December 1819, Table Bay on 17 March 1820, Algoa Bay on 14 April. George
    Augustus Kolbe was sixteen and his wife was sixteen, and the roll says twenty and nineteen. What
    none of it said was what the crossing was.</p>

  <p style="margin-top:16px"><b>John Penny Mandy led another party on the same ship</b> and wrote home
    to his mother in Kent. His letters survive in a descendant's copy &mdash; the originals went to the
    Albany Museum at Grahamstown and <b>were destroyed in a fire in the 1920s</b> &mdash; and a
    newspaper printed them in 1970.</p>

  <blockquote style="margin-top:14px">In one letter, written in January 1820, he describes the
    <b>Nautilus disaster in the Downs</b>. After they dropped anchor in the Queen&rsquo;s Channel,
    <b>a day after leaving Gravesend</b>, &ldquo;it came on to blow tremendously hard, the sea running
    mountains high. We could not weigh anchor till Sunday afternoon, when our troubles began, the sea
    breaking over us in all directions, tables, chairs, boxes, plates and dishes; <b>men, women and
    children all mixed together, tumbling over one another</b>, and all dreadfully seasick, except
    myself and SMITH, who was on deck working the ship; I below, basin holder.&rdquo;</blockquote>

  <blockquote style="margin-top:12px">&ldquo;In the midst of this <b>the sea broke into our cabin
    windows</b>, dashing glass and frame in, the things that were below rolling and sliding, took to
    swimming.&rdquo; &hellip; When <b>the ship struck on the sands</b> all was confusion and dismay
    &mdash; <b>&ldquo;even the sailors seemed panic struck.&rdquo;</b> After an hour and a half a heavy
    sea set them afloat without much damage, and five or six boats went to their help.</blockquote>

  <blockquote style="margin-top:12px">Then another storm, which lasted three days. &ldquo;The sea was
    running <b>as high as our masthead</b>, and two of the waves broke over us; the forepart of the ship
    had <b>three tons of water in</b>, which swamped almost every person in their beds, Joseph was
    washed out of his cot.&rdquo;</blockquote>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What this is, and what it is not</h3>
      <p><b>It is not about this family.</b> Mandy led a different party and there is no evidence he
        and Kolbe ever spoke. Ships carried several parties and a hundred people apiece.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>They were on the same deck in the same storm</b>, a day out of Gravesend, and
        the ship went aground on the sands before it had properly started. A boy four days short of his
        seventeenth birthday and a girl of sixteen, both of whom had sworn on paper that they were older
        than they were, were somewhere in that cabin among the tables and chairs.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And it dates the voyage properly</h3>
      <p>Mandy's party reached <b>Algoa Bay in April 1820</b>, and he writes from there on
        <b>20 April</b>: &ldquo;I landed on Sunday night to get ready for Mary Anne and the
        children.&rdquo; This archive has the Nautilus at Algoa Bay on 14 April. <b>They agree.</b></p>
      <p class="cap">And a child was born aboard. <b>William Nautilus Mandy</b>, on 1 March 1820,
        &ldquo;in latitude 18 degrees, longitude six degrees&rdquo; &mdash; a fortnight short of Table
        Bay, and named for the ship. He died at Lushington Valley in 1887 in his sixty-seventh year.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px"><b>A note on how this was found, because it closes an item
    rather than opening one.</b> The plan was to read John Montgomery's serialised memoir entire &mdash;
    it had already produced the Koolskop mission school and the 21st Dragoons, and it runs through The
    Friend across 1868, 1869 and 1870 under two titles. <b>That turns out to be unnecessary.</b> Both
    finds came out of sweeping a <em>surname</em> across the whole newspaper collection, and those
    sweeps are done: KOLBE returns 43 documents and MOUNTJOY six, across every paper in the archive.
    <b>Koolskop appears exactly once in the entire collection</b> &mdash; in the passage already read.
    Reading a long memoir to find what a name search has already found is work that looks like
    diligence. This was found instead by searching for the <b>ship</b>.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="natal-opened">The Natal depot, opened &mdash; and a connection this archive nearly invented</h2>
  <p class="dek">NAB had never been searched. It had only ever been asked for six references somebody
    else had found. Opened properly it holds thirty-nine Kolbes, nine Mountjoys, four Booyzens and no
    Daschners at all &mdash; and one document that looked for ten minutes like the link this archive has
    been hunting for a fortnight.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:22px">The one that was not what it looked like</h3>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:12px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9"><b>NAB &middot; CSO 1860 &middot; 1908/4469</b><br />
      <b>CK BARRY, WAKKERSTROOM. MR. GS KOLBE ASKS TO BE SUPPLIED WITH A COPY OF THE ROAD BOARD LAW.</b></p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:14px"><b>A Barry and a Kolbe in one sentence, at Wakkerstroom, in 1908.</b>
    Wakkerstroom is the district whose Kolbes this archive has spent weeks trying to attach to the 1820
    Settler. This archive holds three Barrys carrying <b>Kolbe as a forename</b> &mdash; Henry James
    Kolbe Barry, Richard Pieter Kolbe Barry. <b>C.K. Barry</b> read, for about ten minutes, like a
    fourth.</p>
  <p class="note" style="margin-top:12px"><b>He is CYRIL KERSHAW BARRY, and he is an attorney.</b> Four
    further documents settle it: he asks about the appointment of a Commissioner of Oaths at
    Wakkerstroom; he enquires whether a certificate of domicile has been issued to <b>Mahomed Mamoojee
    Motala of Ladysmith</b>; he forwards quitrent <b>on behalf of Mr Moll</b>; he writes about the
    division of ground in Natal. <b>That is a country solicitor&rsquo;s week, and G.S. Kolbe is a
    client.</b> The K is Kershaw. <span class="chip lore">Nearly published as a family link</span></p>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">It is set down at length because the mistake was a good one to
    almost make: <b>two surnames from this archive, in the right district, in one line of a catalogue</b>
    &mdash; and the thing joining them is a professional relationship that would have been invisible if
    the other four documents had not been read.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">What Natal actually gave</h3>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:12rem">Reference</th><th>Entry, and what it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">NAB MSCE 8509/1923</td><td><b>KOLBE, ELIZABETH JOHANNA, born DU TOIT</b>, surviving spouse <b>FREDERICK FORTUNATUS</b>. This archive derived that marriage from Dutch Reformed baptism registers. <b>Here it is in an estate index, from the other end.</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">NAB MSCE 15161/1929</td><td><b>KOLBE, FREDERICK FORTUNATUS</b>, cross-referenced to hers. Both their estates are in Natal.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">NAB MSCE 21335/1934</td><td><b>KOLBE, JOHANNA MARIA, born POTGIETER</b>, spouse <b>KOLBE, HENDRIK JOHANNES HOFMEYR</b> &mdash; who is one of the two Wakkerstroom men on the unsent Pietermaritzburg letter. <b>He now has a wife, and she has an estate of her own.</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">NAB MSCE 581/1973</td><td><b>SHOOTER, MATHILDA ELIZABETH, born MOUNTJOY, BORN IN BARKLY EAST.</b> A second Barkly East Mountjoy who died in Natal, and new.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">NAB MSCE 4458/1973</td><td><b>BOTHMA, WRAY, born MOUNTJOY, born in KOKSTAD</b> &mdash; the bride of the 1957 marriage found in the newspaper transcriptions, now with a birthplace and a death.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">NAB MSCE 4064/1974</td><td><b>BOOYZEN, NICOLAAS THEODORUS, BORN IN WAKKERSTROOM.</b> A Booyzen born in the Kolbe district, which is not a combination this archive has met before.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:26px">And two court cases from before the Republic</h3>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:12px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9"><b>NAB &middot; RSC 1/5/3 &middot; 46/1846</b><br />
      ILLIQUID CASE. <b>PETRUS GERHARDUS BOOYZEN VERSUS DAVID SCHALK VAN DER MERWE.</b><br /><br />
      <b>NAB &middot; RSC 1/5/7 &middot; 95/1848</b><br />
      ILLIQUID CASE. ANDRIES EVERT GOUWS&hellip; VERSUS <b>PETRUS GERHARDUS BOOYZEN</b>.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px"><b>1846 is the oldest Booyzen document this archive has found
    outside the Cape.</b> And the defendant's name is the one this site spent yesterday proving is a van
    der Merwe name that entered the Booyzen line by marriage &mdash; <b>here the two surnames are on
    opposite sides of a lawsuit two years before this family's own David Schalk turned forty.</b>
    <b>No connection is claimed.</b> Petrus Gerhardus is not a name in this archive, and a Booyzen in
    Natal in 1846 is more likely one of the trekker families than one of ours. Recorded because it is
    early and because the pairing is worth somebody's attention, not because it is a lead.</p>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><b>And the Kolbes of Wakkerstroom kept stock.</b> Six
    veterinary files, 1905&ndash;1912: F.F. Kolbe refusing a cheque for sixteen shillings for the rent of
    a sheep kraal; a claim for &pound;25 for a cow lost to poisoning; G.S. Kolbe asking permission to
    move two bulls; scab in J. Kolbe's sheep; and permission to move one bull <b>from Wakkerstroom to
    Saxony, Utrecht</b>. <b>It is the most ordinary material in this archive and it is the first time
    these people appear doing their actual work.</b></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">Three smaller returns from the same afternoon</h3>
  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:14px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">A farm called Mountjoy</h3>
      <p class="cap"><b>TAB SS 2932, R8960/91</b>: the Registrar of Deeds sends title deeds for the farms
        <b>&ldquo;MOUNTJOY&rdquo;</b>, Smaldeel, Bloemhof, Krausberg and Gossard, 1891. And
        <b>TAB PM 29, 66/6/1907</b>: <b>&ldquo;LANDS &mdash; MOUNTJOY AND PHILLAMORE&rsquo;S OCCUPATION
        &mdash; RUSTENBURG DISTRICT&rdquo;</b>. Rustenburg is this archive's own district. Also
        <b>W. Mountjoy&rsquo;s application to capture ostrich chicks</b>, 1906.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">Daschner, worked entire</h3>
      <p class="cap">Twelve in the Transvaal and none in Natal. <b>William Herman Daschner</b>, d. 1963,
        now has a wife: <b>Mildred</b>. A <b>Heinrich Daschner</b> died in 1969 and the index marks him
        <b>CHILD</b>. And a <b>HENRY JAMES DASCHNER</b> appears twice &mdash; which is the Barry and
        Booyzen forename string, arriving on a fourth surname by way of Catherine Mary Sophia.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">A Kolbe on a school paper</h3>
      <p class="cap"><b>TAB OD OR3052/99</b>: declarations from the school at <b>Martinus
        Wesselstroom</b> &mdash; the old name for Wakkerstroom &mdash; <b>&ldquo;ondertekend door de
        heeren GA KOLBE en Francois L&hellip;&rdquo;</b> A George Augustus Kolbe signing for a school
        commission in 1899, in the district this archive cannot yet attach to the Settler.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px"><b>And a sweep that was abandoned, deliberately.</b> The plan
    was to put this family's farms through the education series as well as its surnames.
    <b>Brakfontein returns 77 documents, Koedoesfontein 188, Goedehoop 41</b> &mdash; they are among the
    commonest farm names in the country and the results are other people's schools in other districts.
    One is worth keeping: <span class="m">OD OR150/95</span>, <b>&ldquo;CAB MEYER, KOEDOESFONTEIN, WYK
    ZWARTRUGGENS, RUSTENBURG&rdquo;</b>, is unmistakably this archive's Koedoesfontein &mdash; the farm
    where Hendrik Stefanus Booijsen died in 1908, in the ward of Zwartruggens. <b>It had a school in
    1895 and no Booyzen is anywhere on its papers.</b> A farm-name sweep without a surname is not a
    search, and the rest of it is recorded as void.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="vab-swept">The Free State depot, swept at last &mdash; and the Settler was a missionary</h2>
  <p class="dek">VAB had been opened twice in this project's life, both times for a single reference.
    Swept properly it holds <b>146 Kolbe documents and thirteen McCabes</b>, and among them is the
    catalogue entry that turns this morning's inference into a fact.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:22px">First, the thing nobody was looking for</h3>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:12px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9"><b>SA Library, Cape Town &middot; Manuscript &middot; MSB751</b><br /><br />
      <b>GA KOLBE COLLECTION.</b> Donated by Dr Jean Griffiths, Somerset West, 1985. Photocopy, 4 pages.<br /><br />
      &ldquo;George Augustus Kolbe, <b>pharmacist</b>, 1820 settler, <b>missionary</b>, married Margaret
      Downing. + <b>Letter, 1830 Aug. 23, from Johan Kolbe, London, to his son, George Augustus Kolbe on
      his ordination.</b>&rdquo;<br /><br />
      And on the second entry: &ldquo;George Augustus Kolbe, <b>1802&ndash;44. LMS missionary.</b> +
      Letter from his father, JG Kolbe, London.&rdquo;</p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">It corroborates a memory this archive published as an inference</h3>
      <p>This morning John Montgomery&rsquo;s reminiscence put the Settler at a <b>Bushman mission school
        at Koolskop</b> about 1824, and this archive published it hedged: a man recalling forty-five
        years back, naming somebody only as a Commandant&rsquo;s father.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>A library catalogue, written from the papers themselves, calls him an LMS
        missionary.</b> Two sources that have nothing to do with each other &mdash; an old
        man&rsquo;s memoir in a Bloemfontein newspaper and a manuscript donated to a Cape Town library
        in 1985 &mdash; and they agree. The inference stands up.</p>
      <p class="cap">It also settles the occupation. The 1820 settler roll entered him as a
        <b>CHEMIST</b>; the catalogue says <b>pharmacist</b>; the 1844 obituary describes a medical
        practice. They are one man and one trade, and the missionary work is the part nobody had.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And it is the London end, in four photocopied pages</h3>
      <p><b>A letter from Johan Kolbe in London to his son at the Cape, dated 23 August 1830</b>,
        written on his ordination. This archive has spent a fortnight failing to connect the tailor of
        Conduit Street to the settler of Colesberg by way of directories, census returns and a
        newspaper obituary.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px"><b>The two men wrote to each other, and the letter
        survives.</b> A letter from London in 1830 is very likely to carry an address at the head of
        it &mdash; which is the exact question the Brunswick Square muddle turns on, and which Google
        Books, HathiTrust and archive.org between them could not answer.</p>
      <p class="cap"><b>It is a photocopy of something.</b> Where the original is, and whether more of
        it survives with Dr Griffiths&rsquo;s descendants, the catalogue does not say.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">Francis McCabe&rsquo;s own estate, and a first wife</h3>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:11rem">Reference</th><th style="width:5rem">Year</th><th>Entry</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">VAB MHG C129</td><td class="m">1872</td><td><b>MCCABE, FRANCIS.</b> Eggenotes 1) <b>MARIA PETRONELLA ELISABETHA ANTOINETTA RAATH</b> en 2) <b>ANNA (gebore VAN DER MERWE)</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">VAB 1/3/1/12 BC11</td><td class="m">1872</td><td><b>MCCABE, FRANCIS. INSOLVENTE BOEDEL.</b> Remarks: <b>BEVAT GEEN STERFKENNIS NIE</b> &mdash; contains no death notice.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">VAB MHG M777</td><td class="m">1896</td><td><b>MCCABE, ANNA JACOBA.</b> Nooiensvan VAN DER MERWE. Eggenote <b>JOHN GHERSON ELEASAR KOLBE EN FRANCIS MCCABE.</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">VAB MHG C30</td><td class="m">1860</td><td>MCCABE, JOHN. Eggenote Magdalena Maria Antoinette (gebore <b>LIESCHING</b>) &mdash; the brother the obituary says &ldquo;died here in 1860&rdquo;.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">VAB MHG C63</td><td class="m">1865</td><td>MCCABE, JOSEPH &mdash; the trader and traveller who died in the interior.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><b>Three things.</b> His widow&rsquo;s own estate index entry
    <b>names both her husbands</b>, so the remarriage is documented in the archives and not only in a
    newspaper. <b>He died insolvent</b> &mdash; the Landdrost of Bloemfontein, buried with the State
    President walking behind him, left an insolvent estate. And the insolvency file <b>says outright
    that it holds no death notice</b>, which is the depot telling a researcher not to travel for it.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">The Kolbe&ndash;McCabe marriage that was &ldquo;perhaps a coincidence&rdquo;</h3>
  <p>This archive recorded, and flagged as unresolved, that a <b>Rosa Magdalena Kolbe</b> was baptised at
    Bloemfontein in 1890 to Frederik Fortuna Kolbe and <b>Maria Petronella Elizabeth Antoinetta
    McCabe</b> &mdash; &ldquo;a second Kolbe&ndash;McCabe marriage in the same small Free State world,
    and this archive does not yet know whether that is a coincidence.&rdquo;</p>
  <p class="note" style="margin-top:12px"><b>Francis McCabe&rsquo;s first wife was Maria Petronella
    Elisabetha Antoinetta Raath.</b> A woman carrying that entire four-name string, with McCabe as her
    surname, marrying a Kolbe in the same town, is his daughter by that first marriage, named for her
    mother. <b>So the two families joined twice</b>: he married the Commandant&rsquo;s widow, and his
    daughter married a Kolbe. <span class="chip inf">Inferred on the name, and the name is four names
    long</span> His estate file would confirm it in a line.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">Downing is a Kolbe forename, for four generations and three provinces</h3>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:11rem">Reference</th><th>Who</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB MHG 40293</td><td><b>KOLBE, FREDERICK FORTUNATUS DOWNING</b>, d. 1918 &mdash; his son&rsquo;s name and his wife&rsquo;s surname in one man. Surviving spouse Hester Gertina Susanna born Joubert.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">NAB MSCE 1229/1960</td><td>That widow, remarried: MEIRING, Hester Gertina Susanna, <b>formerly KOLBE</b>, born Joubert, <b>born in VOLKSRUST</b> &mdash; which ties the Downing name to the Wakkerstroom and Volksrust Kolbes this archive found on gravestones.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">VAB MHG 23877</td><td>FICK, Lambertus Hendrik, spouse <b>MARGARET DOWNING (born KOLBE)</b>, 1932.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">VAB MHG 801/49</td><td>The same woman&rsquo;s own estate: <b>KRUGER, MARGARET DOWNING, nooiensvan KOLBE, voorheen FICK</b> &mdash; twice widowed, d. 1949.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">VAB MHG 33459</td><td><b>DU PLESSIS, MARGARET DOWNING, born KOLBE</b>, 1941 &mdash; a second woman of the same three names.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB MHG 5450/68</td><td>ROUSSEAU, surviving spouse <b>MARIA MARGARETHA DOWNING ROUSSEAU</b>, 1968.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">VAB, civil</td><td><b>PIETER DOWING KOLBE LUBBE</b>, twice in 1959 &mdash; by then the surname is Lubbe and Kolbe has become a forename beside Downing.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px"><b>Margaret Downing signed her marriage allegation with a
    mark in 1819</b>, aged fifteen, swearing she was twenty-one. A hundred and thirty years later women
    in three provinces were being christened with the whole of her name.
    <a href={u("/namesakes/")}>Namesakes &rarr;</a></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">And six of them in one court application</h3>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:12px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9"><b>VAB &middot; HG 4/2/1/1/456 &middot; 702/1931</b><br /><br />
      APPLICATION: <b>LEONORA STEENKAMP, BORN KOLBE</b> &middot; <b>MARGARET DOWNING FICK, BORN
      KOLBE</b> &middot; <b>GEORGINA AUGUSTA DE WET, BORN KOLBE</b> &middot; <b>JACOBUS WYNAND LOUW
      KOLBE</b> &middot; <b>GEORGE AUGUSTUS KOLBE</b> &middot; <b>CATHARINA ELIZABETH MALAN, BORN
      KOLBE.</b></p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:14px"><b>Six people of one surname applying to a court together is a sibling
    group.</b> Four married daughters, two sons, and among the six forenames are <b>Margaret Downing</b>,
    <b>George Augustus</b> and <b>Georgina Augusta</b>. It is a generation too late to be the
    Commandant&rsquo;s twelve &mdash; these are more likely his grandchildren &mdash; but <b>it is the
    first Free State Kolbe sibling group this archive has been able to name at all</b>, and the file
    will say what they were applying about, which is usually a parent&rsquo;s estate.
    <a href={u("/errands/")}>On the errands list &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="slier-pietersen">1780&ndash;1810 &middot; Anna Catharina Slier gets parents, and this archive had two men under one name</h2>
  <p class="dek">Volume 3 of the Cape genealogies was opened to look up the six Slier entries the index
    reported. One of them is a whole family, and it answers a woman who has been on this site as a birth
    year and five spellings. It also shows that what this archive has been calling her first husband is
    two people.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:18px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.95"><b>876.&mdash;SLIER.</b><br /><br />
      <b>Willem Slier, van Hamburg</b>, gehuwd 19 November 1780 met <b>Anna Catharina Fortman</b>.<br />
      &nbsp;(b) 1 <b>Anna Catharina, gedoopt 14 October 1787, gehuwd met Hermanus Pieterse</b><br />
      &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2 Wilhelmina Christina, gedoopt 27 December 1789, gehuwd 5 Januari 1811 met Jan Abraham van Wijk, van Amsterdam<br />
      &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;3 Geertruida Johanna, gedoopt 2 Juli 1791<br />
      &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;4 Wilhelmus Josephus, gedoopt 5 Maart 1797</p>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:16px">This archive has carried her as <b>&ldquo;Anna Catharina Sleer &mdash;
    Slier &middot; Schleher &middot; Sher &middot; Hier, five renderings, five sources&rdquo;</b>, born
    1787, died 1885 aged ninety-eight, first the widow of a Pietersen and then the wife of James Montjoy.
    <b>Every one of those facts holds and three more arrive.</b> She was baptised on <b>14 October
    1787</b>. Her father was <b>Willem Slier of Hamburg</b> and her mother <b>Anna Catharina Fortman</b>.
    And she had three siblings, one of whom married an Amsterdammer.</p>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><b>The five spellings stop being a mystery.</b> A Hamburg
    name &mdash; <em>Schlier</em>, <em>Schleer</em> &mdash; written down by Cape clerks who had never seen
    it, which is exactly how Slier, Schleher, Sher and Hier all come out of one family in one generation.
    <b>This archive has a whole page arguing that a spelling is whatever the clerk had</b>, and here is
    the argument again, on a different surname, from a different country.
    <a href={u("/name/")}>The Name &rarr;</a></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">And then the Pietersens, two pages later</h3>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:12px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.95"><b>Hermanus Pietersen, namen van ouders
      onbekend</b>, gehuwd <b>5 April 1807</b> met <b>Anna Catharina Slier</b>.<br />
      &nbsp;(b) 1 <b>Willem Hermanus, gedoopt 12 Maart 1809</b><br />
      &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2 <b>Johan Christiaan, gedoopt 28 April 1810</b></p>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:16px"><b>This archive has been running a father and a son together.</b> The entry
    here reads &ldquo;Willem Hermanus Pieterzen &mdash; Anna Catharina Slier&rsquo;s first husband&rdquo;,
    built out of two documents that spell him differently: the 1817 marriage register, which calls him
    <em>Harmanus Pieterze</em>, and the 1840 death notice, which calls him <em>William Pietersen</em>.
    Two spellings looked like one man with a long name. <b>They are two men.</b> The husband is
    <b>Hermanus</b>, married on 5 April 1807. <b>Willem Hermanus is his son</b>, baptised 12 March 1809
    &mdash; and this archive already half knew it, because its own note says the 1851 godfather who
    carries both forms in full <em>was his son</em>.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">A person nobody had</h3>
      <p><b>Johan Christiaan Pietersen, baptised 28 April 1810</b> &mdash; a second son, and a second
        half-brother to the Montjoy children. He is on nothing else this archive holds.</p>
      <p class="cap">And the marriage is dated. She married Hermanus Pietersen on <b>5 April 1807</b>,
        at nineteen; was a widow by <b>9 March 1817</b>, when she married James Montjoy; was a widow
        again by <b>September 1827</b>; and lived until <b>1885</b>. Fifty-eight years a widow.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Where &ldquo;Willem Hermanus&rdquo; comes from, at last</h3>
      <p>This site has said for weeks that the name <b>Willem Hermanus</b> &mdash; carried by two
        Booyzens in the direct line &mdash; entered through a step-relation with no blood tie. That was
        right, and it can now be traced one generation further back.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px">The boy baptised in 1809 is <b>Willem</b>, for his
        mother&rsquo;s father <b>Willem Slier of Hamburg</b>, and <b>Hermanus</b>, for his own father.
        <b>The whole name is accounted for, and half of it is German.</b>
        <a href={u("/namesakes/")}>Namesakes &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px"><b>One more, and it is a negative worth the space.</b> The
    Dormehls are in volume 2 at entry 393 &mdash; <b>Siebert Dormehl, van Marburg</b>, married 29 January
    1792 to Johanna Israel, widow of Johan Godlieb Carstedt, and twice more after that. His sixth child
    is <b>Charlotta Elisabeth, baptised 18 June 1802</b>. This archive holds a <b>Charlotta Elizabetha
    Dormehl</b> who married Hendrik Stefanus Booijzen at Zeerust in 1892. <b>A woman baptised in 1802
    cannot be marrying in 1892</b>, so these are two women ninety years apart, and the only thing the
    book gives is that the name <em>Charlotta Elisabeth</em> was already in the founding Dormehl
    household. <b>Not a link, and recorded so that it does not become one later.</b></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="mountjoy-dragoons">January 1822 &middot; A man who lodged in James Mountjoy&rsquo;s house says where he came from</h2>
  <p class="dek">Question 12 on this site has been marked <b>Blocking</b> since it was written: who were
    James Montjoy&rsquo;s parents? His own death notice answers <em>not known</em>. His marriage register
    leaves the column ruled through and empty. And the question&rsquo;s own note concluded that
    <b>&ldquo;the way in is a Cape arrival record, not an English birth record&rdquo;</b>. It is, and one
    has just been found in a newspaper.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:18px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9"><b>The Friend of the Free State and
      Bloemfontein Gazette &middot; Thursday, 25 November 1869</b><br />
      &ldquo;How I arrived in Cradock in January 1822, and narrowly escaped incarceration&hellip;&rdquo;<br />
      <b>By Mr. John MONTGOMERY, Founder of Burghersdorp, Cape Colony</b></p>
  </div>

  <blockquote style="margin-top:14px">I arrived in Cradock, tired and jaded, on a knocked-up horse, on a
    very cold, stormy afternoon in January 1822. <b>At that time there were only about half a dozen
    inhabitants in the place.</b> All that I can remember are Messrs. SOLOMON and S.M. DE BEER, the Rev.
    Mr. EVANS (D.R. Church), Mr. [P. GIE], <b>James MOUNTJOY (a saddler, who was discharged from the 21st
    Light Dragoons)</b>, the two Messrs. ZIERVOGEL (one was Secretary to Government), Mr. HEILBERG,
    Fieldcornet B.J. VORSTER, and Gert VAN DEVENTER. <b>After some trouble I obtained lodgings for the
    night from Mr. MOUNTJOY.</b></blockquote>

  <p style="margin-top:16px">He goes on to be challenged for his pass in the street outside the house by
    Solomon the constable, to be asked to breakfast by Mountjoy, to be told by him that the
    Landdrost&rsquo;s office was four miles out at Driefontein across the Fish River, and to walk there
    and arrive <em>&ldquo;just in time to prevent myself being brought up a prisoner&rdquo;</em>.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Is it our man</h3>
      <p>This archive holds <b>James Montjoy, saddler</b>, born in &ldquo;Europe&rdquo; about 1782, who
        married Anna Catharina Slier at Graaff-Reinet on <b>9 March 1817</b> and died <b>at Cradock in
        September 1827</b>. Montgomery names <b>a James Mountjoy, a saddler, at Cradock in January
        1822</b> &mdash; inside that window, in that town, in a place with <b>six inhabitants</b>.</p>
      <p class="cap">There is no room in a village of six for two saddlers of one name. <b>It is him.</b></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And what it gives</h3>
      <p><b>A regiment.</b> The <span class="m">21st Light Dragoons</span> were the cavalry of the Cape
        garrison &mdash; they came out with the 1806 expedition and were not disbanded until the end of
        the 1810s. <b>A man discharged from them at the Cape is a man who arrived in uniform</b>, which
        is why no Cape document can say where he was from: he did not come as a settler and no ship&rsquo;s
        list records him as a passenger.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px">It also dates him. Discharge would put him a civilian in
        the colony by about 1817 &mdash; <b>the year he married</b>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><b>The document that answers the question is now named.</b>
    British Army discharge papers for the period are <span class="m">WO 97</span> at The National
    Archives, Kew &mdash; Royal Hospital Chelsea soldiers&rsquo; documents, arranged by regiment before
    1855 &mdash; and they give <b>the man&rsquo;s parish and county of birth, his age on enlistment, his
    trade and his service</b>. The muster books and pay lists are <span class="m">WO 12</span>. Neither is
    digitised for this regiment. <b>Question 12 stops being unanswerable and becomes an errand at Kew</b>,
    which is a different kind of problem and a much better one. <a href={u("/errands/")}>Errands &rarr;</a></p>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:16px"><b>The same man, twice in one week.</b> John Montgomery&rsquo;s
    serialised reminiscences in The Friend have now broken two walls on this site that had nothing to do
    with each other. His &ldquo;Forty-five years ago&rdquo; piece put George Augustus Kolbe at a
    <b>Bushman mission school at Koolskop</b>, on the ground where Colesberg was later laid out. This one
    puts James Mountjoy in the <b>21st Light Dragoons</b>. <b>Neither was found by searching for a
    Kolbe or a Mountjoy.</b> They were found by reading a surname sweep to the bottom instead of
    skimming it, and both were sitting in the memoir of one old man in Burgersdorp who happened to write
    down who he met. <a href={u("/documents/#mccabe-1872")}>The Koolskop passage &rarr;</a></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">Two others from the same sweep, neither of them this family</h3>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:14px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">1859 &middot; An intestate estate at Middelveld</h3>
      <p class="cap">The Friend: <em>&ldquo;In den intestaten boedel van wyle <b>Jan Lodewyk BOOIJSEN,
        van het Middelveld, District Bloemfontein</b>&rdquo;</em> &mdash; who married <b>about 1819</b>
        Christina Magdalena de Buijs, widow of Cornelius Faber of the Cape Colony, and whose estate had
        become mixed with Faber&rsquo;s. A <b>Jan Lowies Booijsen</b> also has a Cape inventory of 1831,
        at Onder Roggeveld. Not connected here, and recorded because the forename pair
        <b>Jan Lodewyk / Jan Lowies</b> keeps appearing beside this family without joining it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">1862 &middot; A trek-farmer near the Orange River</h3>
      <p class="cap">The Eastern Province Herald reports a Booyzen, <b>a trek-farmer</b> digging for
        water at Deep Fountain between the Hartebeest River and Hope Town, who recognised and seized a
        party of Bushmen accused of shooting a farmer &mdash; <b>&ldquo;six men, seven women and seven
        children&rdquo;</b>. The dispute behind it was water: ground the Bushmen had used
        <em>&ldquo;from time immemorial&rdquo;</em> and which the Government had let to the dead man for
        &pound;70 a year. Nothing connects this Booyzen to this family. <b>It is set down because the
        paper recorded the numbers and this archive is not going to be the place that drops them.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="gendatabase-two">Three questions put to the same search engine, and what each one cost</h2>
  <p class="dek">The Baptism Finder, the Land &amp; Deeds finder and the marriage transcriptions, asked
    in that order. One returned a hard negative that closes a route, one returned four exact dates for
    nothing, and one turned out not to be an index at all.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:22px">1. The 1788 baptism is not on this route, and now this archive knows why</h3>
  <p>The decisive errand on this site is the Dutch Reformed baptism entry of <b>21 September 1788</b>,
    which would name Petrus Jacobus Booijsen's father in a contemporary hand and end the oldest question
    here. The Baptism Finder lists, town by town, which registers the FamilySearch project holds. The
    three candidate congregations were asked:</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:16rem">Congregation</th><th style="width:8rem">Registers</th><th>Earliest held</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>Paarl (Drakenstein)</b> &mdash; where b3c4 Jan was a burgher</td><td class="m">54</td><td class="m">1843&ndash;1844</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Graaff-Reinet</b></td><td class="m">48</td><td class="m">1843&ndash;1844</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Stellenbosch</b></td><td class="m">55</td><td class="m">1843&ndash;1844</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><b>Every register in the collection begins in 1843</b>, in
    every town, sorted earliest first. This is the post-1842 Nederduitse Gereformeerde Kerk archive
    series, and it does not reach the eighteenth century at all. <b>The 1788 baptism cannot be got from
    this collection, and neither can anything else this archive wants from before 1843</b> &mdash; which
    is a fact about the filming project and not about the registers, because the originals exist. It also
    explains, retrospectively, why weeks of FamilySearch work on this family has produced nothing older
    than the 1840s. <b>The errand stands, and it is a Cape Town errand, not a screen one.</b></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:26px">2. Four Mountjoy children, with the day of birth, for nothing</h3>
  <p>The Baptism Database &mdash; a different thing from the Finder &mdash; holds six Mountjoys, four of
    them nineteenth-century, and the free layer of each record gives an exact date of birth:</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:22rem">Name</th><th style="width:10rem">Born</th><th>Baptised</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>Maria Elizabeth Mountjoy</b></td><td class="m">12 June 1858</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Johanna Catharina Mountjoy</b></td><td class="m">28 January 1860</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Georg Herkules Johannes Mountjoy</b></td><td class="m">16 January 1862</td><td class="m">27 April 1862</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Elsje Petronella Frederika Mountjoy</b></td><td class="m">10 June 1864</td><td class="m">17 July 1864</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:14px"><b>Four children at roughly two-year intervals is a sibling group</b>, and
    the names are the interesting part: <em>Georg Herkules Johannes</em> and <em>Elsje Petronella
    Frederika</em> are thoroughly Cape Dutch name-strings sitting on an English surname, baptised in the
    Dutch Reformed Church. This archive already holds a <b>Hercules Johannes Mountjoy</b>, born
    16 July 1866 &mdash; the same forenames and the same day of the month, four years later.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:16px">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0">And one of them may be the woman who died at Indwe</h3>
    <p>This archive holds <b>&ldquo;Johanna Catharina Mountjoy of Indwe&rdquo;</b> with <b>no birth date
      at all</b> &mdash; she joined the Dutch Reformed congregation at Indwe on 23 July 1907, arriving
      from Barkly Oost six weeks after Willem Hermanus Booijzen was confirmed in the same church, and the
      register's last column records her dead there in June 1911. <b>A Johanna Catharina Mountjoy born
      28 January 1860 would be fifty-one in June 1911.</b></p>
    <p class="note"><b>It is a candidate and it is not being entered.</b> This family already contains one
      Johanna Catharina Mountjoy, born 1819 and dead in 1863, so the name repeats within it. What would
      decide it is the <b>congregation</b> the 1860 baptism was registered in &mdash; if it is Barkly
      East or anywhere in that district, the two women are almost certainly one. <b>And the congregation
      is the field the site charges for.</b></p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:26px">3. Where exactly the paywall falls</h3>
  <p>Worth setting down precisely, because it decides whether a subscription is worth buying for this
    project. On a baptism record the free layer gives:</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:14px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">Free</h3>
      <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9">Names<br />Surname<br /><b>Date of birth</b><br /><b>Baptism date</b></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">&ldquo;Sign in to view&rdquo;</h3>
      <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9"><b>Baptism place</b><br />Source<br />Image<br />Entry<br /><b>Father's names and surname</b><br /><b>Mother's names and surname</b><br /><b>Parents</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px"><b>The free layer gives dates; the paid layer gives parentage
    and the citation.</b> For a project that already has most of its dates and is short of parents, that
    is the wrong half &mdash; which is exactly why it is worth knowing before paying rather than after.
    Nothing has been bought and no account has been made.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:26px">4. Land &amp; Deeds turned out not to be an index</h3>
  <p>It was opened to look for Vaalbank and Kwaggafontein. It does not take a farm name or a surname: it
    takes <b>a year and an area</b>, and the areas are <b>Natal and the Transvaal only</b> &mdash; no
    Cape, no Free State, so Leeuwberg and Kaalspruit are out of reach by this road anyway.</p>
  <p>What it returns for <span class="m">1898, Transvaal</span> is <b>fifty-seven registers</b> &mdash;
    <span class="m">Farm Records</span>, volumes RAK 89, RAK 243, RAK 274, RAK 275, RAK 279, RAK 281,
    RAK 387 and more &mdash; each with a link to view the film online. <b>It is a film finder, not a
    name index.</b> To find Vaalbank in it somebody has to open a volume and read. That is a real errand
    and it is now a specified one, which it was not this morning.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:26px">And the marriages, since they were free</h3>
  <p><b>MOUNTJOY: twenty-two</b>, 1895 to 1969. Two are worth naming. <b>1937 &mdash; WILLEM HERMANUS
    MOUNTJOY &times; ELSIE SUSANNA DU PLESSIS</b>: Willem Hermanus is this archive's own name-string,
    carried by two Booyzens in the direct line, here on a Mountjoy. And <b>1919 &mdash; FRANK
    MOUNTJOY-BENTLEY &times; KATHLEEN RITA MILEY FITZPATRICK</b> &mdash; a second hyphenated compound in
    this archive's own surnames, after Kolbe-Booysen.</p>
  <p><b>DASCHNER: three.</b> <b>1900 &mdash; CARL AXEL MARTENSON &times; MINNIE DASCHNER</b>;
    <b>1935 &mdash; JOSEPH CHARLES DASCHNER &times; ELIZABETH HENDRINA MURRAY</b>; and
    <b>1935 &mdash; GERT ANDRIES VAN JAARSVELD &times; MARGARET WILHELMINA ELIZABETH JEANETTE
    DASCHNER</b>. The whole surname holds forty-five records in the entire aggregate, which is the
    smallest of this archive's five families by a long way.</p>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px"><b>None of the above is entered as fact.</b> Every line is an
    index entry from a commercial aggregator, and the archive's rule has not changed for being given a
    better search box: <b>a transcription is a lead, and the register is the record.</b></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="mooc8-inventories">1781 &middot; The people this family owned, and the fact that this archive had never looked</h2>
  <p class="dek">The MOOC8 finder searches the inventories taken of Cape estates at death. Thirteen of
    them name a Booijsen. The oldest belongs to <b>Gerrit Booijsen</b> &mdash; the man at the top of this
    archive's Cape line &mdash; and like every inventory of its kind it lists the enslaved people of the
    household by name, because under Cape law they were property and property is what an inventory is for.</p>

  <p style="margin-top:16px">This project has been running for weeks. It reaches back to a marriage of
    1733. It has a page about who this family named their children after and a page about what its
    documents fail to record. <b>Until this afternoon the word slavery appeared nowhere on it</b>, and
    that was not a judgement about the family &mdash; it was an artefact of which records had been read.
    Estate inventories had never been opened.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:18px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.9"><b>MOOC 8/18.11 &middot; 13 December 1781</b><br />
      <b>Gerrit Booijsen</b> &mdash; St Martijn, Eselscloof, <b>Drakenstein</b></p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:22px">The eleven people named in it</h3>
  <p>The Cape formula gives a name and where the person was taken from. It is set out here in full,
    because a list of eleven names is the only thing that survives of eleven people and shortening it
    would be the second theft.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:14rem">Name</th><th>As written</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>Silvia</b></td><td class="m">Silvia van de Caab</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Lena</b></td><td class="m">Lena van de Caab</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Sara</b></td><td class="m">Sara van de Caab</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Janna</b></td><td class="m">Janna van de Caab</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Dorinda</b></td><td class="m">Dorinda van de Caab</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Fortuijn</b></td><td class="m">Fortuijn van de Caab</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Meij</b></td><td class="m">Meij van de Caab</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Februarij</b></td><td class="m">Februarij van de Caab</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Dijk</b></td><td class="m">Dijk van <b>Madagascar</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Farla</b></td><td class="m">Farla van <b>Madagascar</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Achilles</b></td><td class="m">Achilles van <b>Mallabaar</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px"><b>Eight born at the Cape, two taken from Madagascar, one from
    the Malabar coast.</b> The names are the ones given to them, not the ones they were born with:
    <em>Fortuijn</em> is Fortune, <em>Februarij</em> and <em>Meij</em> are the months they were bought
    or landed in, <em>Achilles</em> is the classical joke a slave-owner made. That is the whole
    vocabulary of the practice, in one column.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:26px">Is this the right Gerrit</h3>
  <p>De Villiers has <b>b3 Gerrit Booijsen</b>, married <b>Johanna du Rand in 1733</b>, father of
    <b>c4 Jan</b>, who was a burgher <b>at Drakenstein</b> &mdash; and this inventory is at Drakenstein.
    A man who married in 1733 and died in 1781 is seventy-odd, which is ordinary. The other Gerrit in the
    printed genealogy, b3c4d1, was not baptised until 1771 and cannot have an inventory in 1781.</p>
  <p class="note" style="margin-top:12px"><b>So it is almost certainly him, and it is published as
    almost certainly.</b> What would settle it is the inventory itself, which names the heirs &mdash;
    and if the heirs include a Jan, this archive's direct line runs straight through this document. It
    has gone on the errands page for Cape Town. <b>The identification is the uncertain part. The eleven
    names are not.</b></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:26px">The other twelve</h3>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:12px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:9rem">Reference</th><th style="width:7rem">Date</th><th>Estate</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">MOOC 8/18.10</td><td class="m">26 Jun 1781</td><td><b>Gijsbert Verweij</b>, Tijger-bergen &mdash; and de Villiers records a Booijsen daughter, Hermina, baptised 1789, married to a Gijsbert Verweij. Names <b>Alexander van de Caab, April van Macasser, Slammat van Mandaar, Dam van Balij, Candasa van Mallabaar</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">MOOC 8/49.6</td><td class="m">15 Mar 1781</td><td>Jacobus Petrus Kriel</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">MOOC 8/20.6</td><td class="m">9 Mar 1790</td><td>Tafelvalleij, Block 12 &mdash; an inventory naming <b>more than twenty</b> people from Ceylon, Bugis, Bengal, Malabar, Timor and the Cape</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">MOOC 8/51.8b</td><td class="m">1 Nov 1792</td><td>Hendrik du Plessies, Slangerivier</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">MOOC 8/51.8a</td><td class="m">25 Oct 1793</td><td>Hendrik du Plessies</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">MOOC 8/53.32a</td><td class="m">17 Jan 1800</td><td>Alletta Catarina Karsten</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">MOOC 8/53.32b</td><td class="m">8 Aug 1799</td><td>&mdash; no name carried in the index</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">MOOC 8/55.38a / b</td><td class="m">18 May 1804</td><td>&mdash; no name carried in the index</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">MOOC 8/56.16b</td><td class="m">30 Mar 1805</td><td><b>Zacharias Booijsen</b>, Graaff Reinet</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">MOOC 8/56.16a</td><td class="m">12 Apr 1806</td><td><b>Zacharias Booijsen</b>, Graaffe Reinet</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">MOOC 8/42.46</td><td class="m">18 Feb 1820</td><td><b>Aletta Booyzen</b>, Graaff Reinet</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">MOOC 8/46.59</td><td class="m">5 Oct 1831</td><td><b>Jan Lowies Booijsen</b>, Vondelings Fontein, Onder Roggeveld, Clanwilliam &mdash; naming <b>Castoor, Jan, Piet, Eva, Malatie, Eva, Marie, Catryn, Clara, Roset</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">MOOC 8/48.32</td><td class="m">6 Dec 1832</td><td><b>Piet Booijsens</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px"><b>A caution about the last one.</b> The index line for
    MOOC 8/46.59 runs personal names and farm names together in one string, so <em>Knue</em> and
    <em>Onder Roggeveld</em> sit in the same list as <em>Eva</em> and <em>Castoor</em>. The ten names
    above are the ones that are unambiguously people. <b>The document has to be read before anyone is
    counted.</b> 1831 is three years before emancipation at the Cape.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:26px">Two things this archive owes</h3>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:14px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">It did not find these people. It found them by accident.</h3>
      <p>The MOOC8 finder was opened this afternoon to look for <b>Mountjoy</b> inventories, because the
        Mountjoy wills are on the Cape Town errands list. Mountjoy returns <b>zero</b>. Booijsen returns
        thirteen. <b>Nobody here went looking for the eleven names; they were on the way to something
        else</b>, and that is worth admitting rather than dressing up.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And the zeros are not yet negatives</h3>
      <p><b>MOUNTJOY 0 &middot; MONTJOY 0 &middot; SLIER 0 &middot; KOLBE 0.</b> Every one of those
        families arrived at the Cape in the nineteenth century, and MOOC8 as indexed here runs from the
        1780s to the 1830s. <b>A zero from an index that stops before your people arrive is not a
        finding.</b> This archive published exactly that mistake this morning about LUUS and is not
        going to publish it again four hours later. Recorded as untested.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><b>Where the full text is, and why it is not here.</b> The
    MOOC8 inventories were transcribed in full by the <b>TANAP</b> project, and its database is
    <b>off the air</b> &mdash; <span class="m">databases.tanap.net</span> does not resolve, and the
    Internet Archive was itself down when this was written. So what is above is read off index entries,
    not off the documents. The documents are at the <b>Western Cape Archives, 72 Roeland Street</b>,
    and are already on the errands page. <b>Eleven people are named here on the strength of a catalogue
    line, and the line is not the record.</b> What is wanted from Roeland Street is the inventory of
    MOOC 8/18.11 entire: the heirs, and every name and valuation on the list.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="gendatabase">A search engine this project had never opened, and a negative it took back in ten minutes</h2>
  <p class="dek">The user asked whether gendatabase.com had been tried. It had not. The word appears
    nowhere in this repository. It aggregates ten South African datasets in one query, four of which
    this archive has never touched at all &mdash; and inside the first half hour it produced a marriage
    this archive had published as not existing.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:22px">The withdrawal first</h3>
  <p>On 14 September this archive published, about the twelve household names on the Silverton pillar:</p>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:12px"><b>&ldquo;LUUS with BOOYZEN returns NOTHING.&rdquo;</b> &hellip; Five
    of the twelve Silverton children remain unplaced and this archive would rather say so than fill them
    in.</blockquote>
  <p style="margin-top:12px">The Marriage Transcription Database on gendatabase:</p>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:12px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.9;text-align:center"><b>1928 &middot; CORNELIUS
      ABRAHAM LUUS &times; SUSANNA FREDERIK ALETTHA BOOYZEN</b></p>
  </div>
  <p class="note" style="margin-top:14px">The negative was true of <b>NAAIRS</b>, which is the only index
    this archive had asked. It was published as though it were true of the record. <b>A marriage register
    is not an estate index, and a sentence that says &ldquo;returns nothing&rdquo; has to name what was
    asked.</b> Withdrawn. Whether this Susanna is the &ldquo;Ousie&rdquo; of the pillar is a separate
    question and is not being assumed &mdash; but she is a Booyzen who married a Luus, which is the thing
    this archive said did not exist. <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Corrections &rarr;</a></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">What the site actually is</h3>
  <p>One search box over ten datasets, with facet counts returned before you pay for anything. For
    <b>BOOYZEN</b> it gives 569 records:</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:18rem">Database</th><th style="width:6rem">BOOYZEN</th><th>What this archive had been using instead</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>Verified Death Database</b></td><td class="m">190</td><td class="cap">Nothing. Post-war deaths, verified.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>National Archives</td><td class="m">152</td><td class="cap">NAAIRS itself &mdash; already swept, and this is a far better search over it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Estate File Database</td><td class="m">122</td><td class="cap">The estate indexes, reached the hard way.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Marriage Transcription Database</b></td><td class="m">54</td><td class="cap"><b>Nothing at all.</b> This is the gap. Fifty-four marriages with both spouses' full names and a year.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Baptism Database</b></td><td class="m">22</td><td class="cap"><b>Nothing at all.</b> 1812&ndash;1835.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>FamilySearch</td><td class="m">22</td><td class="cap">Tree data. Not evidence, and treated as such below.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Concentration Camps</b></td><td class="m">3</td><td class="cap">Reached once, through NAAIRS, by accident.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>1925&ndash;1931 Voter Database</b></td><td class="m">2</td><td class="cap"><b>Nothing at all.</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Civil Death / Unconfirmed Death</td><td class="m">2</td><td class="cap">&mdash;</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">It also carries specialist finders this archive has not yet
    used: <b>Cape Estates</b>, <b>Transvaal Estates</b>, <b>MOOC8 Inventories</b>, <b>Land &amp; Deeds</b>,
    <b>Old Voter Rolls</b>, <b>Boer War Soldiers</b>, <b>Legal Gazettes</b>, and a <b>Baptism Finder</b>
    that lists which Dutch Reformed registers survive for each of 181 towns.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">Four things it confirmed in the first pass</h3>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">1921 &middot; David Kolbe Booyzen &times; Maria Jacoba Smit</h3>
      <p class="cap">This morning this archive published the Kolbe-Booysen household of Port Elizabeth
        off two Eastern Province Herald notices, and noted that the 1921 marriage behind it came from a
        family tree with no document. <b>It is in the marriage transcriptions.</b> Same names, same year.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">1933 &middot; Michael Johannes Vermeulen &times; Beatrix Magdalena Catharina Booyzen</h3>
      <p class="cap"><b>That is Betie of the Silverton pillar</b>, found last week only through a 1951
        estate index that named her as a surviving spouse. <b>Here is the year she married him</b>, from
        the other end of the marriage, and she is his second wife.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">1850 &middot; Hendrik Stephanus Booijzen &times; Anna Johanna Olivier</h3>
      <p class="cap">The marriage that puts this family north of the Vaal in 1850, already on this site
        &mdash; now in an independent transcription. It gives her as <b>Anna Johanna</b> where this
        archive has <b>Anna Catharina Johanna</b>. A variant, recorded, not resolved.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Three Booyzens in the camps, and two on a voters' roll</h3>
      <p class="cap"><b>Gert Jacobus</b>, <b>Gertruida Magdalena</b> and <b>Jan Gerhardus Lodewicus
        Booyzen</b> in the University of Cape Town's Born in the Camps database &mdash; this archive had
        reached one of them, once, through NAAIRS. And on the 1925&ndash;31 voters' roll:
        <b>Jacobus Nicolas Booyzen</b> and <b>John Barry Booyzen</b>, both of whom are on the list of six
        estate files now with a researcher in Pretoria. <b>A voters' roll gives an address.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">And what it is not</h3>
  <p>A search for <b>KOLBE</b> returns <b>2,308</b> records, and of those, <b>thirty-one</b> are people
    born between 1843 and 1869 &mdash; which is exactly the window of the Commandant's twelve children,
    between his marriage on 3 April 1843 and his death on 3 April 1868. It looks for a moment like the
    answer to the oldest question on the Kolbe side of this family.</p>
  <p class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><b>Every one of the thirty-one comes from the FamilySearch
    slice, which is tree data.</b> Not a register, not an estate, not a death notice &mdash; other
    people's trees, indexed. <b>This archive does not take a child from a tree, and thirty-one plausible
    ones do not become evidence by being plausible together.</b> They are worth exactly what they cost,
    which is a list of names to test against documents, and they are not being entered.</p>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">Two of them are worth naming anyway, because they are testable.
    <b>GERSOM ELIEZÄR KOLBE, born 1868, died 1902</b> carries the Commandant's own forenames and was born
    in the year he died &mdash; and the obituary says the youngest child was an infant. And the National
    Archives rows, which <em>are</em> documents, show the name running on: a <b>John Gerson Eliezer
    Kolbe</b> sued by E. Wille and Sons, by John William Pearce and by the Nelspruit Roller Mills,
    divorced by Hester Johanna Catherina born Hammon, and tried with one Adam Barnard in a criminal case.
    <b>Four generations of the same three names, and this archive has documents for the first and the
    last of them and none in between.</b></p>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px"><b>Terms.</b> It is a commercial service: the result lists are
    free, the documents behind them are not. Nothing here was bought, no account was made, and everything
    quoted above came off the public result pages. <b>What it is worth to this project is not the
    documents &mdash; it is that four of its ten datasets are sources this archive did not know it had
    not searched.</b></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="question-zero">Question 0, answered by two people who own the book this archive does not</h2>
  <p class="dek">The oldest open question on this site is one line of one form. The 1876 Rustenburg
    Sterfkennis of Petrus Jacobus Booijsen names his father <b>Gerthardus L&ouml;dewikus Booijsen</b>,
    and the printed Cape genealogy of 1893 names him <b>Jan</b>. One of them is wrong, and this archive
    could not say which without a book it has never seen.</p>

  <p style="margin-top:16px">The escape route was always the same: de Villiers&rsquo;s 1893 original was
    revised by Pama in 1981, and a revision can move a man. If the 1981 edition had quietly relocated
    Petrus Jacobus, the death notice would stand and the 1893 entry would be a superseded reading.
    <b>That was the question put to the WikiTree forum, and it came back in nine words.</b></p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:16px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.9"><b>Ri&euml;l Smit</b> &middot; 14 September 2026<br /><br />
      &ldquo;Yes, <b>de Villiers &amp; Pama (1981) still place Petrus Jacobus (b3c4d7) b. 21 Sept 1788
      under JAN b3c4.</b>&rdquo;</p>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:16px">And in the same thread, from <b>Pieter van Zyl</b>, who had gone and looked
    at the notice:</p>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:12px">I have seen Death Notices or &ldquo;Sterfkennis&rdquo; where
    mistakes were often made with especially the parents. (I also had a look at the specific one that you
    mentioned.) <b>The informant that gives the information to the person who writes the Notice is
    usually a child or even a grandchild.</b> His real dad, Jan, <b>was already dead for more than 40
    years</b> when Petrus Jacobus died. Quite possibly, the informant &ldquo;thought&rdquo; the dad&rsquo;s
    name was G L. Normally the parents are not so important for the executor of the probate, especially
    if they were deceased. <b>I searched for a marriage between a Gerhardus Lodewicus and Magdalena de
    Beer but could not find one.</b> If I remember there was a child of Petrus called Gerhardus Lodewicus
    on his Sterfkennis.</blockquote>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">Ri&euml;l Smit, concurring, added his own case: &ldquo;I have
    seen a mother on a death notice to be actually the wife &mdash; <b>the son filling out the form
    confused his mother with his dad&rsquo;s mother</b>.&rdquo;</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">He remembered right, and the proof is nine lines lower on the same sheet</h3>
  <p><b>Line 3</b> of Notice 678 gives the parents as <b>Gerthardus L&ouml;dewikus Booijsen</b> and
    <b>Magdalena de Beer</b>. <b>Line 12</b> of the same sheet lists eight children, all of age, and the
    fifth of them is:</p>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:12px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.9;text-align:center"><b>JAN GERTHARDUS LODEWIKUS BOOIJSEN</b></p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">The name written in the parents&rsquo; box is the back half of a son&rsquo;s
    name, on the same page, in one clerk&rsquo;s hand. <b>And the half that was dropped is Jan</b> &mdash;
    which is the name the printed genealogy gives for the father, in both its editions. A grandson named
    for his grandfather is the ordinary thing in this family; this archive has spent a whole page
    demonstrating it. <b>The old man&rsquo;s father and the old man&rsquo;s fifth son had the same first
    name, and an informant reaching for one of them arrived carrying too much of the other.</b></p>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><b>Who was doing the remembering.</b> The form is countersigned
    by <b>H. S. Booijsen</b> &mdash; Hendrik Stefanus, the seventh child, whose own name is four lines
    above his signature. He was born about 1828. His grandfather Jan had married in 1770, remarried in
    1793, and was dead before he was ten. <b>He was writing the name of a man he had almost certainly
    never met, about a father who was eighty-eight.</b></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">What this archive now holds, and what it still will not do</h3>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Four things now point one way</h3>
      <p class="cap" style="line-height:1.85">
        <b>1.</b> de Villiers 1893 puts Petrus Jacobus (bapt. 21 Sept 1788) under <b>Jan</b>, b3c4.<br />
        <b>2.</b> de Villiers &amp; Pama <b>1981 still does</b> &mdash; the revision did not move him.<br />
        <b>3.</b> <b>No marriage of a Gerhardus Lodewicus to a Magdalena de Beer can be found</b>, and a
        Cape genealogist went looking.<br />
        <b>4.</b> The disputed name is on the same document, nine lines lower, as a son.</p>
      <p class="cap">And the mother on line 3 &mdash; <b>Magdalena de Beer</b> &mdash; agrees with the book
        exactly. The informant got the woman right and the man wrong, which is what an informant reaching
        for a half-remembered pair of names would do.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And the spine is not being moved on this</h3>
      <p>The pedigree still shows <b>Gerthardus Lodewikus Booysen</b> at the top, marked open, and it
        stays there today. <b>Everything above is secondary.</b> De Villiers is a printed genealogy of
        1893; Pama&rsquo;s revision agreeing with it is one book agreeing with its own earlier edition,
        not two sources agreeing. Against it stands a <b>primary</b> document, a death notice signed by
        a son, and this archive does not let a book overrule a page just because the book is tidier.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px"><b>De Villiers is not a source. He is an index to one.</b>
        His entry for Petrus Jacobus was compiled from the Dutch Reformed baptism register, from the
        entry of <b>21 September 1788</b>. That register entry names the father in a contemporary hand,
        written within days of the birth, by somebody with no reason to be confused about it.
        <b>That single line ends this question outright, and it has gone to the top of the errands
        page.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:16px">This is the second time the forum has done in a day what this
    archive could not do in a fortnight, and both times what it supplied was not a document but
    <b>access to a shelf</b>. The 1981 edition exists in a number of South African houses and in none of
    this project&rsquo;s. <a href={u("/sources/#wikitree")}>Sources &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="kaapsche-2-3">1893 &middot; Volumes two and three of the Cape genealogies &mdash; where the name David Schalk comes from</h2>
  <p class="dek">On 14 September Ri&euml;l Smit posted links to all three parts of de Villiers&rsquo;
    <em>Geslacht-register der oude Kaapsche familien</em> in the WikiTree forum. This archive downloaded
    Part 1, read page 78, and never went back for the other two. They were sitting there the whole time,
    and they answer a question the Namesakes page has been asking since it was written.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:22px">First, a negative, and it is the useful kind</h3>
  <p>Volume 2 has a <b>KOLBE</b> entry, number A483, and it is <b>not this family</b>:</p>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:12px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9"><b>A483.&mdash;KOLBE.</b><br />
      Carel Frederik Kolbe, <b>van Anhalt Dessau</b>, burger te Kaapstad, gehuwd 6 Juni 1762 met Maria
      Magdalena de Wens.<br />
      1 Johannes Marthinus, ged. 6 Februari 1763 &middot; 2 <b>Jacobus</b>, ged. 13 Januari 1765, gehuwd
      6 Juni 1799 met Johanna Magdalena Paulsen &middot; 3 Johanna Elisabeth, ged. 13 Maart 1767
      &middot; 4 Maria Magdalena, ged. 21 Mei 1769 &middot; 5 Marthinus, ged. 12 Augustus 1770 &middot;
      6 Carel Frederik, ged. 11 Augustus 1771 &middot; 7 Salomon, ged. 8 Januari 1773.</p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">A German Kolbe family was at the Cape <b>fifty-eight years before the 1820
    Settler landed</b>, from Anhalt-Dessau, burghers in Cape Town, and Volume 3 confirms the marriage
    from the other side under Paulsen. <b>They are nothing to do with George Augustus Kolbe</b>, whose
    father was a tailor in London and who came out on the settler ships. This archive spends a great
    deal of its time warning itself that a rare-looking surname is a trap; here is the trap, printed, in
    the standard reference, and it is being set down so that nobody &mdash; including this archive
    &mdash; walks into it later.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">Anna Maria van der Merwe, who was a name and is now a family</h3>
  <p>She has been in this archive as the mother on a baptism of 4 January 1807 and almost nothing else.
    Volume 2 carries the van der Merwe genealogy, and she is in it:</p>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:12px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.95">
      (e) 3 <b>Willem</b>, gedoopt 30 September 1764, burger te Stellenbosch, gehuwd 7 November 1784 met
      <b>Hester Maria van der Merwe</b><br />
      &nbsp;&nbsp;(f) 1 Johanna Catharina, gedoopt 8 Maart 1787<br />
      &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2 <b>Anna Maria, gedoopt 5 October 1788, gehuwd met Petrus Jacobus Booijsen</b><br />
      &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;3 <b>David Schalk, gedoopt 10 October 1790</b>, gehuwd met Maria Magdalena van der Merwe<br />
      &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;4 Hendrina Magdalena, gedoopt 10 Juli 1792<br />
      &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;5 Hester Maria, gedoopt 3 November 1793, gehuwd met Michiel van Niekerk<br />
      &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;6 Hendrik Petrus Jacobus, gedoopt 21 Februari 1796</p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Two babies, fourteen days apart</h3>
      <p>Volume 1, page 78, has <b>Petrus Jacobus Booijsen baptised 21 September 1788</b>. Volume 2 has
        <b>Anna Maria van der Merwe baptised 5 October 1788</b>. <b>A fortnight between them</b>, in the
        same colony, and they married each other. This archive has never had a date for her of any kind.</p>
      <p class="cap">The two volumes also confirm the marriage from both directions &mdash; Volume 1
        from his side, Volume 2 from hers. That is one author being consistent with himself, not two
        sources agreeing, and it is worth exactly that much.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And the parents do not agree with the tree</h3>
      <p>This archive has carried, flagged as coming from WikiTree and not from a document, that Anna
        Maria was a daughter of <b>David Schalk van der Merwe and Anna Elizabeth Louw</b>, born 1785.
        De Villiers makes her a daughter of <b>Willem van der Merwe and Hester Maria van der Merwe</b>,
        baptised 1788. <b>Both cannot be right.</b> Neither is being adopted over the other here.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px"><b>But look at what survives the disagreement.</b> On the
        tree&rsquo;s version, David Schalk is her <b>father</b>. On de Villiers&rsquo;s, David Schalk is
        her <b>brother</b>. Two incompatible parentages, and in both of them the name sits one step from
        her. <b>A conclusion that holds under either reading of the evidence is the only kind this
        archive gets to keep.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">The name counted</h3>
  <p>The claim is testable, so it was tested. The three volumes were downloaded as text and the string
    <em>David Schalk</em> counted in each, allowing for the scanner&rsquo;s misreadings.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:12rem">Volume</th><th style="width:8rem">&ldquo;David Schalk&rdquo;</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>Part 1</b> (A&ndash;J, and it holds the whole BOOIJSEN genealogy)</td><td class="m">1</td><td class="cap"><b>And it is our man.</b> &ldquo;David Schalk, gedoopt 4 Januari 1807&rdquo; &mdash; the son of Petrus Jacobus and Anna Maria. The only one in the volume.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Part 2</b></td><td class="m">15</td><td class="cap"><b>Thirteen of the fifteen are inside the van der Merwe section</b>, generation on generation of them &mdash; 1738, 1739, 1761, 1787, 1790, 1791, 1797, 1800, 1804&hellip;</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Part 3</b></td><td class="m">3</td><td class="cap">Scattered, none connected here.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><b>David Schalk is a van der Merwe name.</b> In the entire
    first volume of the Cape genealogies &mdash; which contains every Booijsen de Villiers recorded
    &mdash; it occurs <b>once</b>, and it is the child born the year after a van der Merwe married into
    the line. This archive holds three David Schalks and has built a page around the way names descend
    in this family. <b>It can now say where this one came in, and on what evidence.</b>
    <a href={u("/namesakes/")}>Namesakes &rarr;</a></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">And David Schalk Booijzen married his first cousin</h3>
  <p>This one was not looked for. Volume 3 holds the Viljoens, under their older spelling:</p>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:12px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.95"><b>1,008.&mdash;VILLION, thans VILJOEN.</b><br /><br />
      (e) 12 <b>Hercules Albertus</b>, gedoopt 30 Maart 1777, burger te Graaff-Reinet, <b>gehuwd 26
      Februari 1797 met Magdalena Booijsen</b><br />
      &nbsp;&nbsp;(f) 1 Jan Wilhelmus, ged. 1799 &middot; 2 Magdalena, ged. 1801 &middot; 3 Henning
      Jacobus, ged. 1803 &middot; 4 Anna Cornelia, ged. 1805 &middot; 5 Hercules Albertus, ged. 1807
      &middot; 6 Pieter Nicolaas, ged. 1808 &middot; <b>7 Adriana Christina, gedoopt 19 Augustus 1810</b><br /><br />
      (e) 14 <b>Judith Cornelia Theresia</b>, gedoopt 22 October 1782, <b>gehuwd met Jan Jonathan Booijsen</b></p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">Volume 1 gives the Booijsen side of both marriages, and they are three
    children of one household &mdash; Jan Booijsen and Magdalena de Beer:</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:12px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:4rem">Code</th><th style="width:16rem">Child</th><th>Married</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">d5</td><td><b>Jan Jonathan</b>, ged. 11 Juli 1779, burgher at Graaff-Reinet</td><td><b>28 May 1797</b> &mdash; Judith Cornelia Theresia <b>Viljoen</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">d6</td><td><b>Magdalena</b>, ged. 29 September 1782</td><td><b>26 February 1797</b> &mdash; Hercules Albertus <b>Viljoen</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">d7</td><td><b>Petrus Jacobus</b>, ged. 21 September 1788</td><td>Anna Maria <b>van der Merwe</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><b>A brother and a sister married a sister and a brother,
    three months apart in 1797.</b> Magdalena Booijsen married Hercules Albertus Viljoen in February;
    her brother Jan Jonathan married Hercules Albertus&rsquo;s sister Judith Cornelia Theresia in May.
    Two households became one twice over in a single year.</p>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">Thirty-one years later, <b>David Schalk Booijzen</b> &mdash; son of the
    third of those children &mdash; married <b>Adriana Christina Viljoen</b> at Cradock on 3 November
    1828. Magdalena Booijsen&rsquo;s seventh child is <b>Adriana Christina, baptised 19 August 1810</b>,
    which makes her eighteen in 1828 and makes him <b>her first cousin</b>: his father and her mother
    were brother and sister.</p>
  <p class="cap"><b>What is proved and what is not.</b> De Villiers records Adriana Christina Viljoen&rsquo;s
    baptism and <b>gives her no husband</b>. The marriage date of 3 November 1828 at Cradock is itself
    something this archive holds from WikiTree and has never seen a document for. So the cousin marriage
    rests on a right name, a right age, a right district and a family already doubly tied &mdash; and on
    a date that is not yet a document. <b>It is published as an inference and the weak join is named.</b>
    The Cradock marriage register would close it.</p>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px">One more, for the same page. Jan Jonathan Booijsen and Judith
    Cornelia Theresia Viljoen&rsquo;s sixth child is <b>Judith Cornelia Theresia, baptised 16 August
    1807</b> &mdash; which is <span class="m">b3c4d5e6</span>, the code this archive resolved last week
    to settle a Johannesburg gravestone. She has her mother&rsquo;s three names entire, and her mother
    was a Viljoen. <b>The same mechanism, in the same generation, in the house next door.</b></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="mccabe-1872">1866&ndash;1872 &middot; The widow remarried, and the farm was two hours from Bloemfontein</h2>
  <p class="dek">The Commandant&rsquo;s estate file at Bloemfontein is indexed under the name McCabe, and
    this archive has been saying for days that it did not know who McCabe was. He is in the newspapers,
    with a birth date, an office and a funeral. The same run of papers also settles where the farm was,
    names the first of the twelve children, and gives the Settler an occupation nobody had.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0">The Friend of the Free State, 29 February 1872</h3>
    <p class="m" style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.95">DIED near Modder-river district, Bloemfontein,
      Orange Free State, suddenly, of heart disease, on Friday evening, 23rd February, <b>Francis
      McCABE, Esq., Landdrost of Bloemfontein</b>, aged 54 years, Deeply regretted by a sorrowing widow
      and <b>large family of children and stepchildren</b>, besides many attached friends and
      acquaintances, as well as by the public of this town and district whom he long and faithfully
      served.</p>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:16px">And the obituary beneath it does the rest of the work:</p>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:12px">The late Francis McCABE was the son of <b>Dr. McCABE, a
    Staff-Assistant Surgeon, attached to the British Army on the Cape Frontier</b>, and was born at one
    of the Military Posts, in what is now known as British Kaffraria, on the <b>29th September 1817</b>.
    He <b>married his second wife about ten months since &mdash; the widow of the late Commandant J.G.E.
    KOLBE, of Kafirberg, near this town</b>. He leaves a widow, four sons and two daughters, <b>besides
    a large family of step-children</b>, to mourn their loss.</blockquote>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What that gives Anna Jacoba van der Merwe</h3>
      <p>Married <b>3 April 1843</b>. Widowed <b>3 April 1868</b>, on the anniversary, with twelve
        children. Married Francis McCabe <b>about April 1871</b>. Widowed again <b>23 February 1872</b>.
        <b>Fifteen months.</b> She then lived another twenty-four years and died in 1896, which is why
        the Free State estate index holds her as <span class="m">MCCABE, ANNA JACOBA, nooiensvan VAN DER
        MERWE</span> and why she was so hard to find.</p>
      <p class="cap">The newspaper also counts for us. McCabe left <b>four sons and two daughters</b> of
        his own, and the step-children are &ldquo;a large family&rdquo; &mdash; the twelve. For about
        eleven months there were eighteen children attached to one household.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And how he died</h3>
      <p>He had spent 23 February &mdash; <b>the anniversary of the State&rsquo;s independence</b> &mdash;
        superintending the burghers&rsquo; annual target shooting for the government prize. At 5:15 in
        the afternoon he set off for the Diamond Fields by spring-cart with four horses, Adjutant van
        Rensburg of the Free State Artillery and a servant, meaning to sleep at his own farm
        <b>&ldquo;Uitvlugt&rdquo; on Modder River</b>. At about half past seven, in deceptive moonlight,
        <b>one hour&rsquo;s ride short of the farm of Mr G.F. ENSLIN</b>, the cart went into a sluit
        washed out by the recent rains. He was dead that evening of heart disease.</p>
      <p class="cap"><b>G.F. Enslin is already in this archive</b> &mdash; he is the husband who placed
        the notice of 23 October 1863 saying that Maria Elizabeth Kolbe had died intestate. He is used
        here as a landmark on a road at night, which is how a district knows a man.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px">He was buried in the public cemetery at Bloemfontein
    <b>on the Sunday afternoon at six o&rsquo;clock</b>, the coffin carried from house to hearse and
    hearse to grave by the Commandant of the district, <b>the resident members of the Volksraad, and the
    principal Government officers</b>, in the presence of the State President and upwards of two hundred
    who walked in the procession. His brother <b>Joseph McCabe, the well-known trader and traveller</b>,
    had died in December 1865 in the interior; his brother <b>John died at Bloemfontein in 1860</b>;
    their mother <b>Rose</b>, relict of <b>Patrick McCabe, resident-surgeon</b>, died on 1 August 1858
    aged 72 years 3 months and 8 days. <b>One sister only survived.</b> Four McCabe deaths and a
    surviving sister, all in the Bloemfontein papers, and none of it was looked for.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">The first of the twelve children to be named</h3>
  <p>The Free State criminal court, reported in The Friend in the second half of 1869. Jochemus Jacobus
    Ackerman pleaded guilty to horse stealing at the farm <b>Springfield, ward Upper Modder-river,
    district Bloemfontein</b>, in the beginning of October 1868, and got three years&rsquo; hard labour.
    He had driven off three animals:</p>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:14px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.95">firstly: 1 black mare, with dark brown foal,
      the property of <b>Anna Jacoba van der MERWE, widow of late J.G.E. KOLBE</b>: secondly 1 blue
      schimmel mare, with foal, the property of <b>John KOLBE, jun.</b>: and thirdly, a chestnut riding
      horse, the property of <b>Jephts, a man of color, now residing at Leeuwberg</b>.</p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">Three things at once. The widow is on <b>Springfield, ward Upper Modder
    River</b> six months after her husband&rsquo;s death, which gives the field-cornetcy. <b>John Kolbe,
    junior</b> is holding stock in his own right beside her &mdash; a son, carrying the father&rsquo;s
    name, and the first of the twelve this archive can put a hand on. And <b>Jephts</b>, a man of colour
    living at Leeuwberg, owned a chestnut riding horse and the court wrote his name down beside theirs.
    <b>He is recorded here because he was recorded there.</b> A great many people who lived on these
    farms are in no document at all; he is in one, and this archive is not going to be the second place
    that leaves him out.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">Where the farm was &mdash; and a pin that has to come out</h3>
  <p>An auction advertisement in The Friend of 13 January 1870, placed by W.W. Collins, general agent,
    dated Bloemfontein 30 November 1869, selling the landed property of the insolvent estate of Thos.
    Heatly of Worcester:</p>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:14px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.95">1. The farm, <b>Wildebeestkooi, adjoining the
      farm Leeuberg, the property of the late J.G.E. KOLBE, about two hours ride from the town of
      Bloemfontein</b>; this farm is well adapted for the breeding and depasturing of all kinds of live
      stock, being healthy and well situated.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><b>Two days ago this archive pinned Leeuwberg at
    Fauresmith</b>, on a 1903 land file recording a &ldquo;LEEUWBERG NO 465, DIST. FAURISMITH&rdquo;, and
    published the pin with its own objection attached: Fauresmith is a hundred and thirty kilometres
    from where Kaalspruit was pinned, and the obituary says he died at the one and lies at the other.
    <b>The objection has now been answered, and it answered against the pin.</b> Leeuberg adjoins
    Wildebeestkooi and is about two hours&rsquo; ride from Bloemfontein &mdash; fifteen or twenty
    kilometres, not a hundred and thirty. The Fauresmith farm is a different farm of the same name.
    <b>Withdrawn, and the pin moved.</b> <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Corrections &rarr;</a></p>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">Everything else now agrees. The McCabe obituary calls him <b>&ldquo;of
    Kafirberg, near this town&rdquo;</b>. His own 1864 advertisement signs <b>&ldquo;J.G.E. KOLBE,
    Kafferberg, Kaalspruit&rdquo;</b>. His widow is at Springfield in the ward of the Upper Modder River.
    Kafferberg, Kaalspruit, Leeuberg, Wildebeestkooi and Springfield are one neighbourhood, an easy ride
    north-west of Bloemfontein &mdash; and the soil-conservation district registered as
    <span class="m">KAALSPRUIT &ndash; KAFFERRIVIER (BLOEMFONTEIN)</span>, which was a guess when this
    archive used it, now has three documents standing behind it.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">The Settler kept a school for Bushmen where Colesberg now is</h3>
  <p>The Friend of the Free State, first half of 1869, printed a reminiscence headed <b>&ldquo;FORTY-FIVE
    YEARS AGO&rdquo;, by John MONTGOMERY, Founder of Burghersdorp</b>. He is describing a journey from
    Cradock made about 1824.</p>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:12px">After visiting my friends I continued my journey towards the
    Orange River, <b>by Koolskop, where the father of the late lamented Commandant KOLBE conducted a
    Bushmen mission school, and which is now Colesberg</b>. From there I crossed the Orange River, which
    was almost dry &mdash; the water being in pools only here and there. I then trekked on to
    Philippolis, where old Dam KOK was in command of the Bastards or Griquas.</blockquote>
  <p style="margin-top:14px"><b>This archive had George Augustus Kolbe as a medical practitioner</b>,
    from the 1844 obituary, and as a man who died on a farm in the Colesberg district. Here he is
    twenty years earlier, at a place called <b>Koolskop</b>, running a mission school for Bushmen on the
    ground where the town of Colesberg was laid out in 1830. It explains what the family was doing in
    that corner of the colony at all &mdash; they were there before the town was.</p>
  <p class="cap"><b>What it is worth.</b> Montgomery is writing forty-five years after the fact and
    identifies the man not by name but as the father of a Commandant who had died the year before he
    wrote. That is a recollection, not a register. It is also a specific one, from a man who founded a
    town in the same district and had no reason to invent a school. <b>Recorded as an inference with the
    source printed beside it</b>, not as fact.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">Three smaller things out of the same run</h3>
  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">Foreman of the jury, 1866</h3>
      <p class="cap">A forgery trial at Bloemfontein, before Mr van Soelen &mdash; the same official who
        spoke at Kolbe&rsquo;s graveside two years later. &ldquo;The jury, through their foreman,
        <b>Mr. J.G.B. KOLBE, of Kafferberg</b>, brought in a verdict of Not Guilty.&rdquo; A fourth
        public office, and the initials printed <b>J.G.B.</b> for J.G.E., in the town paper, in his own
        lifetime.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">A grandson, killed by lightning, 1884</h3>
      <p class="cap">&ldquo;The eldest son of Mr. John KOLBE, <b>&lsquo;little Jannie&rsquo;</b>, a
        promising youth ten years old, was struck dead by the electric fluid on his father&rsquo;s farm
        <b>five hours to the northward of Bloemfontein</b>.&rdquo; The Natal Witness index carries the
        same death and names the father <b>George</b>. <b>Two papers, two fathers.</b> If the John of
        1884 is the John Kolbe junior of 1868, this is the Commandant&rsquo;s grandson.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">Kolbe-Booysen, in print</h3>
      <p class="cap">The Name page carries <b>Kolbe-Booysen</b> as a compound the family tree records
        for the wife of <b>David Kolbe Booyzen</b>, married 1921 &mdash; and calls it a tree oddity. The
        Eastern Province Herald prints it twice. <b>It is a household in Port Elizabeth.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:18px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9"><b>16 October 1944 &middot; ENGAGEMENTS</b><br />
      SAWTELLE &ndash; <b>KOLBY-BOOYSEN</b>. &mdash; The engagement has been announced between
      <b>Levina (Finnie)</b>, only daughter of Mr and Mrs <b>D. KOLBE-BOOYSEN</b>, of Port Elizabeth, to
      Raymond Patrick, only son of Mrs D. SAWTELLE, of Cambridge, England.</p>
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9;margin-top:12px"><b>19 April 1945 &middot; TO BE MARRIED</b><br />
      <b>KOLBE-BOOYSEN &ndash; KRUGER</b>. To be married at St Cuthbert&rsquo;s Church, Bingley Street,
      Port Elizabeth, to-day (Thursday), April 19th, at 3.30 p.m., Hilda (S.A.C.S.), only daughter of
      Lieut. and Mrs F. KRUGER, of Heidelburg, Transvaal, to <b>William (S.A.A.F.)</b>, son of Mr and
      Mrs <b>D. KOLBE-BOOYSEN</b>, of Port Elizabeth.</p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:14px"><b>D. is David.</b> David Kolbe Booyzen married Maria Jacoba Smit in 1921
    and the tree recorded her surname as Kolbe-Booysen; the compound had no document behind it. Now it
    has two, six months apart, and with them a town and two children: <b>Levina, called Finnie</b>, the
    only daughter, marrying an Englishman from Cambridge in the last winter of the war, and
    <b>William</b>, of the South African Air Force. <b>Their father hyphenated the two surnames of this
    entire archive and used it as his name.</b></p>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">And the engagement notice spells the headline <b>KOLBY</b> and
    the body <b>KOLBE</b>, four words apart, in one paragraph, in 1944. <a href={u("/name/")}>The Name &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="kwaggafontein-school">1897&ndash;1900 &middot; A farm school in Marico, and the man who countersigned it</h2>
  <p class="dek">The seven Marico compensation claims were read today and gave up nothing but their own
    initials. The same search returned three files nobody had asked for, and those three turned out to
    be part of a bundle of forty-eight: the entire correspondence of a school on a farm, from the
    letter asking for a teacher to the last cheque before the war.</p>

  <p style="margin-top:16px"><b>Start with the negative, because it was the errand.</b> Seven Booyzen
    men claimed compensation as burghers in the district of Marico in 1903 and 1904. All seven detail
    records were pulled. Every one of them reads, in full:</p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:12rem">Reference</th><th>Description, entire</th><th style="width:5rem">Year</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB CJC 906/76</td><td class="m">CLAIMS FOR COMPENSATION BURGHERS. MARICO. JPJ BOOYZEN.</td><td class="m">1903</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB CJC 906/77</td><td class="m">CLAIMS FOR COMPENSATION BURGHERS. MARICO. PJ BOOYZEN.</td><td class="m">1903</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB CJC 907/104</td><td class="m">CLAIMS FOR COMPENSATION BURGHERS. MARICO. GL BOOYZEN.</td><td class="m">1904</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB CJC 907/109</td><td class="m">CLAIMS FOR COMPENSATION BURGHERS. MARICO. JPJ BOOYZEN.</td><td class="m">1904</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB CJC 907/123</td><td class="m">CLAIMS FOR COMPENSATION BURGHERS. MARICO. L BOOYZEN.</td><td class="m">1904</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB CJC 907/125</td><td class="m">CLAIMS FOR COMPENSATION BURGHERS. MARICO. JC BOOYZEN.</td><td class="m">1904</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB CJC 907/126</td><td class="m">CLAIMS FOR COMPENSATION BURGHERS. MARICO. HE BOOYZEN.</td><td class="m">1904</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><b>No remarks field, no farm, no forename, no amount.</b> The
    index line <em>is</em> the whole record. Nothing more about these seven men can be got at a screen,
    from anywhere, and this archive should stop trying. Seven files in one building in Pretoria would
    answer it in an afternoon. That has gone on the errands page as what it is: a door, not a search.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:26px">What came back instead</h3>
  <p>The query was <span class="m">BOOYZEN AND MARICO</span> in the Transvaal depot. Ten documents. Seven
    were the claims. The other three were from <b>source OD</b>, the education files of the South African
    Republic, and all three were about a school.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:16px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9">TAB &middot; OD &middot; OR262/98 &middot; 1898<br />
      H DE VRIES, <b>JGL BOOYZEN</b>, KWAGGAFONTEIN, DISTRIK MARICO, PK KAFFERSKRAAL.<br />
      ZENDEN AANZOEK OM SUBSIDIE. &mdash; <span class="cap">remarks: BIJ OR13059/97</span></p>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:16px">That remark is a thread, and pulling it brought up the whole bundle.
    <span class="m">OR 13059/97</span> is the parent file, and searching the farm name returned
    <b>forty-eight documents</b> running from <b>4 October 1897</b> to <b>April 1900</b>. Read in order
    they are a single continuous story, told in the form of receipts.</p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:9rem">Date</th><th style="width:8rem">Reference</th><th>What it says</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">4 Oct 1897</td><td class="m">OR13059/97</td><td>G.E. ter Haar of Kafferskraal <b>asks for a teacher</b> for the school at Kwaggafontein.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">13 Nov 1897</td><td class="m">OR15382/97</td><td>G.C. Snyman has had <b>no answer</b> and asks for one as soon as possible.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">26 Nov 1897</td><td class="m">OR16045/97</td><td>The school commission <b>has found a teacher</b>, in the person of N. de Vries.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">6 Dec 1897</td><td class="m">OR16200/97</td><td>Telegram: <b>&ldquo;wagon leaves Thursday to fetch teacher De Vries &mdash; is everything in order?&rdquo;</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">Jan 1898</td><td class="m">OR262/98</td><td>De Vries and <b>JGL Booyzen</b> send the application for a subsidy.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">12 Feb 1898</td><td class="m">OR2773/98</td><td>De Vries and <b>JGL Booysen</b> &mdash; a book order.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">23 Feb 1898</td><td class="m">OR2943/98</td><td>Inspector of schools <b>T.C. Stoffberg</b> has forwarded the subsidy application.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">9 Apr 1898</td><td class="m">OR5071/98</td><td>Holidays.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">1898</td><td class="m">OR5429/98</td><td>Stoffberg&rsquo;s <b>inspection report</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">9 May 1898</td><td class="m">OR6919/98</td><td>De Vries and <b>JGL Booysen</b> explain the writing-off of an <b>adverse balance of &pound;61-6-0</b> at the start of the first quarter.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">30 May 1898</td><td class="m">OR8109/98</td><td>The commission&rsquo;s statement on the size, value and fitting-out of the <b>teacher&rsquo;s dwelling</b>, with the field-cornet&rsquo;s valuation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">1 Jul 1898</td><td class="m">OR9812/98</td><td>De Vries and <b>JGL Booyzen</b> send the second-quarter returns, <b>with notice that a certain pupil has left the school</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">27 Aug 1898</td><td class="m">OR13051/98</td><td>J.H. Snyman corrects the department: the teacher has lived in the house <b>since 1 January, not since 4 July</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">15 Sep 1898</td><td class="m">OR13692/98</td><td>De Vries asks <b>why the second quarter&rsquo;s subsidy has still not been paid</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">20 Oct 1898</td><td class="m">OR16104/98</td><td>De Vries and <b>JGL Booijzen</b> send the third-quarter returns, and another pupil has left.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">22 Oct 1898</td><td class="m">OR16356/98</td><td>An account for a subsidy on <b>school furniture</b>, with two receipts and Stoffberg&rsquo;s certificate that the furniture was found suitable.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">15 Dec 1898</td><td class="m">OR19636/98</td><td>De Vries and <b>JGL Booysen</b> send the fourth-quarter returns with a covering letter.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">May 1899</td><td class="m">OR7499/99</td><td><b>JGL Booysen</b>, replying to OR5590/99, states that <b>in his opinion &pound;8-0-0 too little subsidy was paid</b>, and gives his reasons.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">Apr 1900</td><td class="m">OR3259/1900</td><td>De Vries sends his own monthly certificate and asks that <b>the money be sent to the landdrost</b>. The file stops here.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What JGL Booyzen was</h3>
      <p>He is on <b>eight</b> of the forty-eight, and always in the same position: first or second name
        at the head of the letter, beside the teacher, on the documents that involve <b>money and
        returns</b> &mdash; the subsidy application, the book orders, the four quarterly statements, the
        explanation of the deficit, and the argument about the eight pounds. He is on none of the
        documents about holidays, forms, or railway certificates, which the teacher sent alone.</p>
      <p>That is the shape of a <b>school commission officer</b> &mdash; on the evidence of the paper, its
        treasurer. The parallel file at Vaalbank below names a <span class="m">penningmeester</span>
        explicitly and puts him in exactly this slot.</p>
      <p class="cap"><b>He is not the teacher.</b> The teacher is H. de Vries, fetched by wagon from
        somewhere else in December 1897, and the department writes to him about his own salary
        certificates every month. Booyzen is the local man who signs for the school.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And which JGL Booyzen &mdash; not answered</h3>
      <p>This archive holds two men of those initials. <b>Jan Gerthardus Lodewikus Booijsen</b>, fifth of
        the eight children on the 1876 Sterfkennis, born district Graaff-Reinet about 1818, <b>died on
        the farm Vaalbank in the district of Lichtenburg on 25 May 1885</b> &mdash; so he is thirteen
        years dead and cannot be this man. His nephew and namesake <b>Jan Gerhardus Lodewicus Booysen,
        born 4 December 1855</b>, seventh on the 1863 list, is a man of whom this archive has said for
        months that <b>nothing is known of what became of him</b>. In 1898 he would be forty-two.</p>
      <p><b>That is a candidate and not an identification.</b> Marico adjoins Lichtenburg, this family is
        documented in Marico from the 1892 Zeerust marriage, and Jan Gerhardus Lodewicus is the name at
        the top of this line. It is also one of the commonest name-strings in the whole surname. The
        index line gives initials, a farm and a post office, and no more. <b>Recorded open.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:26px">Three spellings of one man, in ten months, by one department</h3>
  <p>This is worth setting down on its own, because this archive has an entire page arguing about the
    letter in the middle of the name. Within a single register series, in a single government office,
    about a single man at a single farm school, the clerks wrote:</p>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:14px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:14px;line-height:2">
      <b>BOOYZEN</b> &nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp; January 1898 &middot; <span class="cap">OR262/98, the subsidy application</span><br />
      <b>BOOYSEN</b> &nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp; 12 February 1898 &middot; <span class="cap">OR2773/98, a book order</span><br />
      <b>BOOYSEN</b> &nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp; 9 May 1898 &middot; <span class="cap">OR6919/98, the deficit</span><br />
      <b>BOOYZEN</b> &nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp; 1 July 1898 &middot; <span class="cap">OR9812/98, second quarter</span><br />
      <b>BOOIJZEN</b> &nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp; 20 October 1898 &middot; <span class="cap">OR16104/98, third quarter</span><br />
      <b>BOOYSEN</b> &nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp; 15 December 1898 &middot; <span class="cap">OR19636/98, fourth quarter</span></p>
  </div>
  <p class="note" style="margin-top:14px">Three spellings, one man, one office, ten months. Nothing in
    this archive has ever shown the point so cleanly. <b>The z is not a branch and the ij is not a
    generation.</b> It is whichever clerk had the pen. <a href={u("/name/")}>The Name &rarr;</a></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:26px">And a second school, on this family&rsquo;s own farm</h3>
  <p>Searching the same series for the surname against <span class="m">ONDERWYZER</span> returned sixteen
    documents, most of them other Booysens running other farm schools across the republic &mdash;
    Wonderboom, Hartsenbergfontein, Kareeboomputten, Zeekoefontein. One of them is not somewhere else.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:16px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9">TAB &middot; OD &middot; OR19199/98 &middot; December 1898<br />
      <b>DS. BOOYSEN</b> (ONDERWYZER JP LOUW) <b>VAALBANK, DISTRICT LICHTENBURG</b>, POSTMERK RIETKUIL.<br />
      ZENDT IN ONGEZEGELD VOLMACHT AAN DEN BOEKHOUDER EN &pound;0-1-6 IN POSTZEGELS.</p>
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9;margin-top:12px">TAB &middot; OD &middot; OR19484/98 &middot; December 1898<br />
      JP LOUW, <b>DS BOOIJSEN</b>, VAALBANK, DISTRIK LICHTENBURG, PK RIETKUIL.<br />
      ZENDEN STATEN VIERDE KWARTAAL 1898 MET VERKLARINGEN 0.19, 0.24 EN 0.25.</p>
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9;margin-top:12px">TAB &middot; OD &middot; OR2037/99 &middot; 6 February 1899<br />
      <b>DS. BOOIJSEN</b>, LOUW (ONDERWIJZER), VAALBANK, DISTRIK LICHTENBURG, PK RIETKUIL.<br />
      VRAAGT INLICHTINGEN OVER HET VERSCHAFFEN VAN <b>LEERMIDDELEN AAN WEEZEN</b>, NAAR AANLEIDING VAN
      EEN GEVAL TEN HUNNEN.</p>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:16px"><b>Vaalbank in the district of Lichtenburg is this archive&rsquo;s own
    ground.</b> Jan Gerthardus Lodewikus Booijsen died on that farm on 25 May 1885, and his daughter
    Magdalena Susanna Maria died on the same farm on 1 May 1920. In between, in December 1898, a Booysen
    on Vaalbank is sending an unstamped power of attorney and eighteen pence in postage stamps to the
    government bookkeeper, co-signing the school&rsquo;s quarterly returns with the teacher J.P. Louw,
    and &mdash; in February 1899 &mdash; <b>writing to ask how school materials can be provided to
    orphans, arising from a case among them</b>.</p>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><b>And the initials will not be read from here.</b>
    <span class="m">DS.</span> may be a man with the forenames <b>David Schalk</b>, which is this family&rsquo;s
    most heavily used name-string and would put a named relative on the farm. It may equally be
    <b>Ds.</b>, the Dutch abbreviation for <em>dominee</em> &mdash; and six weeks earlier, at this very
    school, <span class="m">OR15594/98</span> names <b>&ldquo;JP LOUW DS VAN ROOJEN&rdquo;</b> in exactly the
    same slot, where Ds. van Rooijen is far more likely a minister than a man with those initials.
    <b>One abbreviation, two readings, and this archive cannot choose between them from an index line.</b>
    The file itself would settle it in a sentence. It has gone on the errands page.</p>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px"><b>A correction while doing this.</b> The atlas has carried, since
    it was built, an event under Marico reading &ldquo;1898 &mdash; JGL Booyzen of Kwaggafontein, district
    Marico, <b>in the field-cornet&rsquo;s papers</b>&rdquo;. The papers are the <b>education
    department&rsquo;s</b>, not the field-cornet&rsquo;s. A field-cornet does appear in this bundle, once,
    valuing the teacher&rsquo;s house &mdash; and that is a different document, sent by a different man.
    Corrected in place. <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Corrections &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="piet-retief-1913">1907&ndash;1953 &middot; Piet Retief and Wakkerstroom &mdash; a date for the twin, and a third stone that names children by nickname</h2>
  <p class="dek">Two Kolbe stones at Piet Retief and one at Wakkerstroom, all located yesterday in an
    index line and read today. One of them may put a day on a birth this archive has only ever had as a
    year.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Piet Retief &mdash; G.A. and A.M. Kolbe</h3>
      <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9">&hellip; GEDACHTENIS<br />AAN &hellip; VADER EN MOEDER<br /><b>G.A. KOLBE</b><br />GEB. 23 FEB. 1836<br />OVERL. 7 JAN. 1913<br /><b>A.M. KOLBE</b><br />GEB. 4 MEI 1843<br />OVERL. 21 SEP. 1907<br />VAN HUN KINDERS</p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">The Settler's ninth and tenth children were <b>twins born
        at Burgersdorp in 1836</b> &mdash; Charlotte Felicia and George Augustus &mdash; and this
        archive has never had a day for either. <b>A G.A. Kolbe born 23 February 1836 is the right man
        in the right year, and would date his twin sister too.</b> It is initials and a year, not a name
        and a parent. It would also give the <em>other</em> George Augustus Kolbe &mdash; born
        Burgersdorp 2 October 1861, buried at Wakkerstroom &mdash; a father aged twenty-five, which is
        the shape the eastern Transvaal Kolbes have been missing.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Piet Retief &mdash; P.J. Kolbe</h3>
      <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9"><b>P.J. KOLBE</b><br />GEB. 1_ AUG. 1865<br />OORL. 28 NOV. 1919<br />RUS SAG.</p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">The day of birth will not resolve at any magnification and
        is not guessed. Thirty years younger than the stone beside it; if G.A. is the twin, this is very
        likely his son, and <b>nothing on the stone says so</b>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:30px">Wakkerstroom &mdash; and the third stone in this archive to name children by nickname</h3>
  <blockquote class="panel" style="margin-top:14px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.9;margin:0">IN LIEFDEVOLLE HERINNERING<br />AAN ONS MAMMIE<br /><b>MARIA JOHANNA VAN DER MERWE</b><br />(GEB. KOLBE)<br /><b>26 OKT. 1886 &ndash; 1 OKT. 1953</b><br />Wat hy doen is wel gedaan.<br /><b>MARTZI &middot; P.S. &middot; JUDIE &middot; PIETMAN &middot; CHARL EN SUZAN</b></p>
  </blockquote>
  <p style="margin-top:14px">Her birth is now a <b>date</b>, where this archive had only the year off an
    estate file. And <b>six children are named</b> &mdash; by what they were called, not by what they
    were registered as. She is George Augustus Kolbe's daughter, so these six are
    <b>great-grandchildren of the 1820 Settler</b>, and they are new.</p>
  <p class="note"><b>That is three stones now.</b> The Booyzens at <a href={u("/documents/#silverton-pillar")}>Silverton</a>
    list twelve children on a centre pillar; the Kolbes at <a href={u("/documents/#wakkerstroom-stones")}>Wakkerstroom</a>
    use the same double-stone-and-pillar form; and this one sets six household names under a mother's
    dates. <b>Two families, four hundred miles apart, burying their dead in the same grammar.</b>
    This archive keeps finding children on gravestones that are on no register it can reach &mdash; and
    the reason is simple enough: <b>a register records a birth, and a stone records who was left.</b></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="commandant-kolbe-1868">15 April 1868 &middot; &ldquo;Death of Commandant Kolbe&rdquo; &mdash; the Settler&rsquo;s eldest son, at length</h2>
  <p class="dek">Two lines in this archive: <i>John Gerson Eliezar &ldquo;Jan&rdquo;, 1822, Uitenhage.</i>
    The Grahamstown Journal gives him a marriage, a farm, four public offices, twelve children, a cause
    of death, a deathbed speech about the Basuto boundary &mdash; and a description of his parents
    written by somebody who knew them.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The man</h3>
      <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.9">
        Born <b>7 July 1822</b><br />
        Married <b>3 April 1843</b>, <b>Anna Jacoba van der Merwe</b><br />
        Died <b>3 April 1868</b>, the farm <b>Leeuwberg</b> &mdash; <b>his wedding anniversary</b><br />
        Settled on <b>Kaalspruit</b>, where he lies<br />
        <b>Typhus, or typhoid fever</b>, caught on commando<br />
        &ldquo;He had not completed his 46th year&rdquo;</p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">&ldquo;He returned from commando some four weeks ago,
        suffering illness&hellip; thus adding another to the long list of victims of
        <b>our miserable war</b>.&rdquo; The paper then says the war could have been avoided by good
        management after April 1866 &mdash; an obituary that stops to argue with its own government.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What he held</h3>
      <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.9">
        <b>Commandant of the district</b><br />
        <b>Justice of the Peace</b><br />
        <b>Director of the Bloemfontein Bank</b><br />
        <b>Deacon of the Dutch Reformed Church</b><br />
        and he spoke in the <b>Volksraad</b>, May 1867</p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">He had refused public life for about twelve years after
        <b>the abandonment</b> &mdash; he was among those who protested the British withdrawal and
        applied to <b>Sir George Clerk</b> for compensation for his land so that he might leave with the
        troops. Failing that, &ldquo;he, like many more, had to cast his lot with the Free State&rdquo;.
        He took no office until the end of 1865.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:30px">The household</h3>
  <p><b>A widow and twelve children.</b> Of <b>four daughters</b>, only one was married; <b>the youngest
    was an infant</b>. <b>Two of the sons had been serving with their father in the field</b> and
    &ldquo;have proved themselves to be amongst the bravest of our burghers&rdquo;. Not one of the
    twelve is named, and none of them is in this archive.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:30px">The deathbed, which is the part worth keeping</h3>
  <blockquote class="panel" style="font-family:var(--serif,Georgia,serif);font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.85">
    <p style="margin:0">&ldquo;On his death-bed, and while he still retained his full senses, he
      declared to the Doctor, to Mr. SALZMANN, and to others that were at his bed-side, that
      <b>this State had taken too much land from the Basutos</b>, and that he had felt so all along, and
      had declared it as his opinion in the Volksraad, in May 1867, at the time of the making of the
      &lsquo;corn peace&rsquo;; and now, on the bed from which he never expected to rise again, he
      wished by message to warn the Raad, and to beg of them to <b>rescind their resolution, annul their
      protest, and to treat with the Governor for an equitable settlement of the boundary line</b>.&rdquo;</p>
  </blockquote>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">He was interred the Monday following; Mr. P. Grobler, Elder of
    the Church, conducted the service in the minister's absence, and the President of the Free State
    delivered an address.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:30px">And it describes his parents</h3>
  <p class="note"><b>&ldquo;His parents came out with the settlers of 1820; his father was of German
    extraction, if not birth, but resided and married in London. His mother &mdash; a thorough-going and
    fine old English lady &mdash; it will be remembered died in Burghersdorp about three months
    since.&rdquo;</b></p>
  <p>That is <b>George Augustus Kolbe and Margaret Downing</b>, described in 1868 by somebody who knew
    them, in a newspaper that had no genealogical interest whatever. <i>German extraction, if not
    birth</i> is exactly right for a man born in Westminster to a W&uuml;rttemberg father &mdash; and
    this archive worked that out from a Soho baptism, a London Gazette notice and a census, none of
    which this writer had. It also confirms from a fourth direction that
    <b>the father became a missionary to the Griquas at Philippolis, and that most of the family grew
    up there</b> &mdash; which is what the children's baptism places already said.</p>

  <p class="note"><b>One date does not fit, and it is left not fitting.</b> This archive has
    <b>Margaret Downing dying in 1867</b>, on the strength of her death notice. This obituary, printed
    15 April 1868, says she died <b>at Burgersdorp about three months since</b> &mdash; which is
    January 1868. A death notice is a form with a date on it and a newspaper&rsquo;s
    &ldquo;about three months&rdquo; is a journalist rounding, so the notice should win; but this
    archive has already been caught once by an estate filed thirteen years after the death it records,
    and will not simply wave this away. <b>Her place of death is new either way: Burgersdorp.</b></p>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px"><b>And one small thing, from the same search.</b> Grahamstown
    Journal, 9 February 1872, quoting the Colesberg <i>Advertiser</i> under the heading
    <b>&ldquo;Terrible Warning&rdquo;</b>: an action before the Resident Magistrate between
    <b>Mr. Geo. KOLBE, plaintiff, and Mr. T. NELSON, watchmaker, defendant</b> &mdash; Kolbe
    &ldquo;sought to recover a watch which had been entrusted to defendant for repair, but which,
    despite repeated promises, the latter neglected to repair or to deliver&rdquo;. A Kolbe of
    Colesberg, in court, over a watch.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="werdmuller-1874">1874&ndash;1909 &middot; The Werdmullers &mdash; a nameless baptism, four newspaper notices, and a Victoria Cross</h2>
  <p class="dek">The Settler's fourth daughter married a Werdmuller and vanished from this archive.
    Ten newspaper hits, found in the same collection as her father's obituary, put four generations back
    on the page &mdash; with one link in the middle that is not proved and is marked as such.</p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:11rem">Date</th><th>What was printed</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">10 Apr 1857<br /><span class="cap">Burgersdorp doopregister</span></td>
            <td>A child born <b>14 February 1857</b>, baptised at Burgersdorp, mother <b>Augusta Louisa Kolbe</b>, father <b>Coenraad Johannes Otto Werdmuller</b>. <b>The Name column of the index is empty</b>, which is why this archive had never seen it. <a href={u("/documents/#burgersdorp-swept")}>The sweep &rarr;</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">7 Jul 1874<br /><span class="cap">Eastern Province Herald</span></td>
            <td>&ldquo;MARRIED at Burghersdorp <b>by Special Licence</b>, on Tuesday the 7th July 1874, by the Rev. William Cromar, <b>Coenraad Johannes Otto Werdmuller D&rsquo;ELGG</b> to Miss <b>Elizabeth Johanna Catharine VORSTER</b>, both of Burghersdorp.&rdquo; The <b>von Elgg</b> this archive has only ever had as a bracket after a name, printed as <i>D&rsquo;Elgg</i>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">13 Feb 1884<br /><span class="cap">Queenstown Free Press</span></td>
            <td>&ldquo;MARRIED &mdash; at Wesley Church, Queenstown &hellip; <b>Charles Andrew WERDMULLER, of Steynsburg. Second son of the late C.J.O. WERDMULLER, of Burghersdorp</b>, to <b>Charlotte Anne CALLAGHAN</b>, eldest daughter of Hugh Callaghan, Esq., of Ashby Manor, Queenstown.&rdquo; <b>C.J.O. is dead by February 1884.</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">17 Feb 1891<br /><span class="cap">Queenstown Free Press</span></td>
            <td>&ldquo;DIED &mdash; At <b>Hoopstad, Orange Free State</b> &hellip; at the age of <b>32 years, after a long illness</b>, <b>Charlotte Anne WERDMULLER</b> &hellip; leaving a husband and <b>two little girls</b> to mourn their loss.&rdquo; An obituary paragraph adds that she had reached Hoopstad only months earlier, in indifferent health, with an organic disorder of the heart.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">31 Jul 1909<br /><span class="cap">South Africa, a weekly journal</span></td>
            <td>&ldquo;MARTINEAU &mdash; WERDMULLER &mdash; On July 31, at East London, <b>Horace Robert Martineau, V.C.</b>, of Durban, to <b>Amy Werdmuller, second daughter of Charles Werdmuller, J.P. of Hoopstad, O.R.C.</b>&rdquo;</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p><b>Read in order they make a family.</b> Charles Andrew, of Steynsburg in 1884, is
      <b>Charles Werdmuller, J.P. of Hoopstad</b> by 1909 &mdash; the same town his wife died in. The
      <b>two little girls</b> left in 1891 are the reason the 1909 notice can call Amy a
      <b>second daughter</b>. And a child of Charles Andrew's father was baptised at Burgersdorp in
      April 1857 to a mother named <b>Augusta Louisa Kolbe</b>.</p>

    <p class="note"><b>The link that is not proved, and this archive will not pretend otherwise.</b>
      Nothing read here names Charles Andrew's mother. His father remarried &mdash; or a man of exactly
      the same name married &mdash; at Burgersdorp in 1874, to Elizabeth Johanna Catharine Vorster. If
      Charles Andrew is the child born 14 February 1857 he was twenty-seven when he married and
      <b>Augusta Louisa Kolbe is his mother</b>; if he belongs to the later marriage he is no relation
      to this family at all, and the 1874 date makes that impossible for a man marrying in 1884 as a
      second son. <b>The dates favour Kolbe strongly and the documents do not say it.</b> Set down as a
      chain with a gap in it.</p>

    <p>If the chain holds, <b>Amy Werdmuller is a great-granddaughter of the 1820 Settler</b>, and in
      1909 she married a holder of the <b>Victoria Cross</b>. This archive records the two letters
      exactly as the journal printed them and has <b>not</b> gone looking for which action he won it
      for; that is somebody else's page, and the temptation to borrow it is exactly the temptation this
      archive is built to resist.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="burgersdorp-swept">1853&ndash;1859 &middot; The Burgersdorp register swept image by image &mdash; and why no search could have found this</h2>
  <p class="dek">The two missing Barry children have been the standing errand of this project. The index
    could not produce them. Two hundred and sixty-seven register images later, one of the two is found
    &mdash; and the reason she was invisible turns out to be worth more than she is.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0">The entry</h3>
    <p class="m" style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.9">Image group <b>008039088</b>, image <b>427</b><br />
      Name: <b>Catherine Mary Sophia</b> &middot; Born <b>10 Jul 1855</b><br />
      Parents: <b>John Barry</b> &middot; <b>Margaret Louisa Kolbe</b><br />
      Baptism <b>22 Jul 1855</b>, Burgersdorp</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p><b>Her sister has the same name.</b> On 25 December 1859, at Dordrecht, the same parents baptised
      another <b>Catharina Mary Sophia</b>, born 4 August 1859 &mdash; the woman who married into the
      Booyzens and whose name reached Australia. Two daughters, one name, birthdays four years apart in
      two different towns. <b>A baptism is not done twice</b>, so the ordinary reading is the ordinary
      Cape practice: the first child died and the name was given again. This archive has no burial for
      her and dates the death only as <b>before August 1859</b>.</p>
    <p>It also says something about the name. This archive has already shown that
      <b>Catherine Maria Sophia is a Kolbe name</b>, off the godmother at the 1848 Graaff-Reinet font,
      Catharine Sophia Maria Kolber. <b>It was thought important enough to use, lose, and use again.</b>
      <a href={u("/namesakes/")}>Namesakes &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><b>Why no search would ever have found her &mdash; and this
    is the finding that outlasts her.</b> The FamilySearch film viewer carries an <b>index panel under
    each image</b>, listing every entry on that page: name, birth date, both parents, event, place. On
    image 427 the row reads <span class="m">&nbsp;| 10 Jul 1855 | John Barry | Margaret Louisa Kolbe |
    Baptism |</span> &mdash; <b>the Name column is empty.</b> The child is indexed with no name at all.
    Her forenames are written plainly on the register page and simply were not keyed. <b>No search by
    surname, forename or spelling variant can return a record that has neither</b>, which is why nine
    separate attempts here &mdash; Barry, Berry, Barrie, Bary, Barrey, Barri, Bari, and the mother under
    Kolbe, Kolber and Colbe &mdash; all came back with the same seven children. <b>The parents are
    indexed. The child is not.</b> Searching by the parent is the only thing that reaches her, and a
    parent search only works if you already know to run it on a page you cannot see.</p>

  <p style="margin-top:16px">The same sweep caught a second nameless row, on image 513: a child born
    <b>14 February 1857</b>, baptised 10 April 1857 at Burgersdorp, to <b>Augusta Louisa Kolbe</b> and
    <b>Coenraad Johannes Otto Werdmuller</b>. She is the Settler's fourth daughter, born at
    Graaff-Reinet in 1829, and <b>this is the first of her children this archive has ever seen</b>.
    Two nameless entries in 267 images, and both of them belong to this family.</p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:20px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:12rem">What was swept</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Images <b>338&ndash;604</b></td><td class="cap">1853 through to 1859, when the family had moved to Dordrecht. About 2,400 indexed entries read.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>1854</b></td><td class="cap">Clean. No Barry.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>1855</b></td><td class="cap"><b>Catherine Mary Sophia, 10 July.</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>1856</b></td><td class="cap">Clean.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>1857</b></td><td class="cap">No Barry &mdash; but the Werdmuller child, on image 513.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>1858</b></td><td class="cap">Clean.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>The eleventh child</b></td><td class="cap"><b>STILL MISSING.</b> Not at Burgersdorp between 1853 and 1859. The ground left to sweep is 1851&ndash;1852, the Dordrecht register after 1863, and the possibility that the child was born and died between two of the known baptisms and was never entered at all.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Blank pages</td><td class="cap">About thirty images in the range returned no index rows. Some are genuinely blank; this archive cannot say all of them are, and says so.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="kolbe-obituary-1844">19 December 1844 &middot; The Grahamstown Journal buries the Settler &mdash; and names a London square and eight sons</h2>
  <p class="dek">Printed eighteen days after he died, four hundred miles from the farm, and never read
    here until today. It is the fullest contemporary account of George Augustus Kolbe that exists, and
    almost every line of it says something this archive did not have.</p>

  <blockquote class="panel" style="margin-top:18px;font-family:var(--serif,Georgia,serif);font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.85">
    <p style="margin:0"><b>DIED</b> at his Farm, <b>Wirtemberg</b>, near the Orange River, December 1st,
      <b>the Rev. George Augustus KOLBE, aged 44 years</b>, a man possessed of a most amiable temper,
      and of more than ordinary abilities. He was <b>the last remaining of eight sons of John Godloff
      KOLBE Esq. of Brunswick-square, London</b>, has left a <b>Widow and ten Children</b> to lament
      their loss, and to whom he has been a most affectionate Husband and Father. The Inhabitants of
      the District of Colesberg, and the surrounding Districts, will sustain a heavy loss,
      <b>he having been for many years engaged in rendering them medical advice and assistance</b>
      whenever called upon. His very long affliction for nearly a year has been borne with exemplary
      patience and resignation. <b>His disease was an affection on the lungs</b>, which brought on
      extreme weakness and debility. He died in peace, in sure and certain hope of a glorious
      immortality, through the merits of his Redeemer, Jesus Christ.</p>
    <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">Grahamstown Journal, Thursday 19 December 1844, transcribed
      in the eGGSA newspaper extracts.</p>
  </blockquote>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Eight sons</h3>
      <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.8">This archive knew John Gottlob
        Kolbe of Conduit Street through a census, a Gazette notice, a burial and a death index, and knew
        of <b>one son</b> &mdash; the one who sailed. The obituary says George Augustus was <b>the last
        remaining of eight</b>.</p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">Seven brothers, all dead before December 1844, and not one
        of them is in this archive. The three women in the 1841 Conduit Street household &mdash;
        Marianne, Charlotte and Amelia &mdash; are sisters or daughters; the sons are simply absent,
        because dead men do not appear in a census.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Brunswick Square, not Conduit Street</h3>
      <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.8">The 1841 census has John
        Gottlob Kolbe at <b>Conduit Street</b>, Mayfair, and he died at number 34 in 1845. The obituary
        &mdash; written from the son's side, and printed while the father was still alive &mdash; places
        him at <b>Brunswick-square</b>, in Bloomsbury, a mile and a half away.</p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">Both can be true: the family may have been at Brunswick
        Square when George Augustus sailed in 1819 and at Conduit Street twenty years later. <b>This
        archive has already had to move this man once</b>, off the family tree's Lamb's Conduit Street
        and onto Conduit Street proper, and it is not going to move him again on one line of print.
        Recorded as a second address and not as a correction.
        <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Corrections &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">A doctor, in everything but the word</h3>
      <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.8">His death notice calls him an
        <b>Agriculturist</b>. The trades this archive had were <b>Reverend Missionary, Chemist, Tailor</b>.
        The obituary says he had <b>for many years rendered medical advice and assistance to the
        inhabitants of the District of Colesberg and the surrounding districts</b>, whenever called
        upon.</p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">That is the difference between a trade listed on a form and
        what a man actually did for thirty years. It also explains the <b>chemist</b>: on that frontier,
        in the 1830s, the chemist <em>was</em> the doctor.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What he died of, and what does not fit</h3>
      <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.8">Ill for <b>nearly a year</b>,
        of <b>an affection on the lungs</b> &mdash; consumption, in the language of the time.</p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px"><b>Two things in the notice are wrong and are printed here
        anyway.</b> <b>Aged 44</b>: he was born 7 December 1802 and died 1 December 1844, five days
        short of forty-two. And <b>ten children</b>: this archive counts twelve born, one of whom died
        an infant, which leaves eleven. A death notice in a newspaper is written by the grieving and set
        by a compositor four hundred miles away. <b>It is the best kind of source and it is not a
        register.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="naairs-three">1937&ndash;1963 &middot; BARRY, MOUNTJOY and DASCHNER on NAAIRS &mdash; the first time they have been asked</h2>
  <p class="dek">The national archives index has been used here for Booyzens and for Kolbes. The other
    three surnames in this family had never been put to it. Two of the three are small enough to read
    entire; the third is not, and that is a finding of its own.</p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:10rem">Surname</th><th style="width:6rem">Documents</th><th>What that means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>DASCHNER</b></td><td class="m">12</td><td class="cap">The entire surname, in every South African repository. Two of the twelve are this archive's own.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>MOUNTJOY</b></td><td class="m">136</td><td class="cap">Readable in a sitting. Thirty-four are estates or death notices.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>BARRY</b></td><td class="m">5,022</td><td class="cap"><b>Not workable as a surname search.</b> Barry is also a forename, a company name and a place. It has to be paired with a second keyword.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>BARRY <b>AND</b> BURGERSDORP</td><td class="m">1</td><td class="cap">A municipality file. Nothing.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>BARRY <b>AND</b> INDWE</td><td class="m">0</td><td class="cap">Nothing at all.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>BARRY <b>AND</b> BARKLY</td><td class="m">2</td><td class="cap"><b>And both of them matter.</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">A farm owner and a bywoner, both called R.P.K. Barry</h3>
      <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.8">SAB &middot; VWR &middot; volume 2 &middot; B645/8/38 &middot; 1937<br /><br />
        AANSOEK DEUR PLAASEIENAAR<br />BARKLY-OOS.<br /><b>RPK BARRY.</b><br />BYWONER<br /><b>RPK BARRY</b> (SEUN).</p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">An application by a farm owner at Barkly East. The owner is
        <b>R.P.K. Barry</b>. The bywoner on the place is <b>R.P.K. Barry, his son</b>. Two men of those
        initials on one farm, one holding the land and one working it as a tenant, and a state file
        between them. <b>Bywoner</b> is not a labourer and not an owner: it is the word South Africa
        used in the 1930s for a white tenant on another man's ground, and <span class="m">VWR</span> is
        the Department of Social Welfare. This archive has one Richard Pieter Kolbe Barry, born 1899,
        and <b>cannot say which of the two he is.</b> The file is not read.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Henry James <em>Kolbe</em> Barry</h3>
      <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.8">NAB &middot; MSCE &middot; 2183/1958<br /><br />
        <b>BARRY, HENRY JAMES KOLBE.</b><br />BORN IN BARKLY EAST, CAPE PROVINCE.<br />UNMARRIED. DECEASED ESTATE.<br />1958&ndash;1959</p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px"><b>The whole argument of this archive, in one man's name.</b>
        Henry James is the Barry forename followed here since the Graaff-Reinet font of 1848. Kolbe is
        the surname this project has spent itself proving is the source of half the family's given
        names. Here they are together, in a man born in this archive's own town and dying
        <b>unmarried</b> in Natal in 1958 &mdash; so the names stop with him. The third Barry found
        carrying Kolbe as a forename. <a href={u("/namesakes/")}>Namesakes &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><b>And two men got their dates from two sources on the same
    afternoon.</b> <b>William Herman Daschner</b>, who was a name on his father's 1942 notice and
    nothing else, is <span class="m">MHG 2703/63</span> in the estate index and
    &ldquo;DASCHNER Willie 1915&ndash;1963&rdquo; on a West Park stone. The index gives the registered
    name and the year; the stone gives the household name and the same year. <b>Fritz Johannes
    Mountjoy</b>, baptised at Barkly East in 1894, lies in Barkly East main cemetery, 1893&ndash;1973.
    Neither was found by looking for him. Both fell out of sweeping a surname.</p>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px"><b>A method note, because it cost an hour.</b> NAAIRS treats
    two keywords as <b>OR</b> unless the operator between them is sent explicitly. BARRY with
    BURGERSDORP returned <b>6,245</b> documents &mdash; more than BARRY alone &mdash; and looked for a
    moment like a rich seam. With <span class="m">AND</span> sent, it returns <b>one</b>. A search that
    returns more than its own first term is not a search.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="wakkerstroom-stones">1901&ndash;1954 &middot; The Wakkerstroom stones &mdash; two men of one name, and a child returned to the right cemetery</h2>
  <p class="dek">The eGGSA Mpumalanga collection holds thirteen Kolbe stones, and ten of them are in one
    small cemetery in the eastern Transvaal. Read in a morning, they settle a question this archive had
    opened and closed twice the same day, and take back a word it should not have used.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">George Augustus Kolbe, 1863&ndash;1954</h3>
      <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9">VADER<br /><b>GEORGE AUGUSTUS</b><br />GEB. 29 JULIE 1863<br />OORL. 28 SEPT. 1954<br /><span style="opacity:.8">Hy het die goeie stryd gestry / Hy het die wedloop voleindig / Hy het die geloof behou. II Timotheus 4:7</span><br /><span style="opacity:.8">In tere herinnering van sy dankbare kinders, kleinkinders en nageslag</span></p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px"><b>29 July 1863 is the Colesberg font date to the day.</b>
        The baptism of 11 October 1863 gives him born 29 July, son of Frederik Fortunatus Kolbe and
        Elizabeth Johanna du Toit. Ninety-one years later a stone four hundred miles north cuts the same
        date. <b>That is the whole northward argument confirmed by a source that has nothing to do with
        the register.</b> His half of a double stone; his wife has the other, and a pillar between them
        reads <b class="m">VREDE &middot; KOLBE</b> &mdash; the same form as the Booyzen stone at
        Silverton. <a href={u("/documents/#silverton-pillar")}>The pillar at Silverton &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Martha Sophia, born Klopper, 1867&ndash;1950</h3>
      <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9">MOEDER<br /><b>MARTHA SOPHIA</b><br />(GEB. KLOPPER)<br />GEB. 27 MEI 1867<br />OORL. 14 OKT. 1950<br /><span style="opacity:.8">Ons dank God elke maal as ons aan haar dink</span></p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">The birth is new; the death matches the civil register to
        the day. She was <b>seventeen</b> when she married him. Her daughter Maria Johanna, born at
        Wakkerstroom in 1886 and dead there in 1953, lies in the same ground under a van der Merwe
        stone.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">George Augustus Kolbe, 1861&ndash;1934 &mdash; the other one</h3>
      <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9">IN LIEFVOLLE HERINNERING AAN ONS OUERS<br /><b>GEORGE AUGUSTUS KOLBE</b><br />GEB. 2 OKTOBER 1861<br />OORL. 8 NOVEMBER 1934<br />EN<br /><b>SUSANNA MARGARETHA KOLBE</b><br />GEB. 26 OKTOBER 1861<br />OORL. 28 JULIE 1940<br />HULLE RUS</p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px"><b>Two men of one name in one cemetery</b>, born two years
        apart and buried twenty apart. He was born at <b>Burgersdorp</b> &mdash; where the 1820 Settler
        is buried, and where the Settler's twin son George Augustus was born in 1836. That descent is
        not documented here and is not claimed. He died at Standerton and was brought back to
        Wakkerstroom.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Martha Angeline Kolbe, sixty days old</h3>
      <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9">TER GEDACHTENIS VAN<br />ONS GELIEFDE KLEINE<br /><b>MARTHA ANGELINE KOLBE</b><br />GEBOREN 9 OCT. 1901<br />OVERLEDEN 8 DEC. 1901<br />HEBR. 6<br /><span style="opacity:.8">&mdash; and cut across the base: NEWCASTLE</span></p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px"><b>This morning this archive put her at Volksrust, and said
        Volksrust held a camp.</b> Both halves are wrong and both are withdrawn. The stone is in
        Wakkerstroom. The index said &ldquo;Volksrust, Eastvaal District Council&rdquo;, which is a
        modern <em>district municipality</em>, and Wakkerstroom lies inside it &mdash; an administrative
        area read as a place of burial. And with the town goes the camp: a family stone in a town
        churchyard, in Dutch, cut by a monumental mason twenty-five miles away in Natal, is not a camp
        burial. <a href={u("/corrections/")}>The correction &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><b>And the prisoner of war is decided, in the other
    direction.</b> At breakfast this archive gave the 1902 POW record &mdash; <span class="m">WO
    108/368</span>, age 40, residence Wakkerstroom &mdash; to the man born in 1863, on the strength of
    the residence. By lunch it had withdrawn that, because he was thirty-nine. The stones close it: the
    <em>other</em> George Augustus Kolbe of Wakkerstroom was born <b>2 October 1861</b>, and was
    therefore exactly <b>forty from October 1901 to October 1902</b>. <b>The prisoner is the elder
    man.</b> Published, withdrawn and reversed inside one day, and all three are on the corrections
    page, because a reader is owed the whole of that and not just the answer.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="middelburg-stones">1930&ndash;1951 &middot; Four Booyzen stones in one Cape cemetery, read</h2>
  <p class="dek">Middelburg main cemetery, in the eastern Cape, holds four stones spelt with a
    <b>z</b>. Three of them are one household. The fourth is a warning.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Gert Jacobus Booyzen</h3>
      <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9">IN DANKBARE HULDE<br />AAN<br />ONS GELIEFDE VADER EN OUPA<br /><b>GERT JACOBUS BOOYZEN</b><br />GEB. 19 JAN. 1870<br />OORL. 2_ AUG. 1951<br />SY GENADE WAS VIR MY GENOEG</p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px"><b>19 January 1870 is exactly what this archive already
        had</b>, and this is the first source for it that is not the death notice it came from. The
        death was a bare year; it is now a month. <b>The day is a 2 and then a digit this archive
        cannot read</b>, and it is not going to guess one. <i>Vader en oupa</i> &mdash; he was a
        grandfather, which nothing here had said. And the last line is in the first person:
        <i>his grace was enough for <b>me</b></i>, cut twenty-one years after his wife died.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Isabella Fredericka, born Coetsee</h3>
      <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9">TER GEDAGTENIS<br />AAN<br /><b>ISABELLA FREDERICKA BOOYZEN</b><br />(GEB. COETSEE)<br />GEB. 15 APRIL 1874<br />OORL. 31 MAART 1930<br />HEMELSE RUS</p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px"><b>Her birth is now a date.</b> This archive had
        &ldquo;c. May 1874&rdquo;, worked back from an age on a notice; the stone gives
        <b>15 April 1874</b>. The death matches to the day, and is the third independent source saying
        <b>1930</b> against the 1943 this archive once published &mdash; 1943 being only the year her
        husband finally filed. <a href={u("/corrections/")}>The correction &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Pieter J. Booyzen &mdash; and one word that carries the whole stone</h3>
      <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9">HIER RUS<br /><b>MY</b> GELIEFDE SEUN<br /><b>PIETER J. BOOYZEN</b><br />GEB. 29 DES. 1904<br />OORL. 7 JUNIE 1937<br />NA DIE KRUIS DIE KROON</p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">Both dates confirm this archive exactly; he was thirty-two.
        The reading is in the second line. <b><i>My</i> geliefde seun, not <i>ons</i></b> &mdash; one
        surviving parent, not two. His mother had been dead <b>seven years</b>. So the stone was cut by
        his father, who lay down beside them both fourteen years after that. <b>The grammar of the
        inscriptions gives the order the household went in.</b></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Catharina Johanna Helena, born Beyleveldt &mdash; the warning</h3>
      <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9">HIER RUS<br /><b>CATHARINA JOHANNA HELENA BOOYZEN</b><br />(GEB. BEYLEVELDT)<br />GEB. 7 JANUARIE 1855<br />OORL. 16 MAART 1937</p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">A fourth Booyzen stone in the same ground, and
        <b>no connection to the other three that this archive can find</b>. The Cape civil register
        carries her under a chain of surnames that is its own biography &mdash;
        <b class="m">BOOYZEN BYLEVELD LANGENBOG BOOYZEN</b> &mdash; and dates her birth 1857 where the
        stone says 1855. Her probate names her husband <b>Johannes Hendrik</b>. She died
        <b>twelve weeks before</b> Pieter J. went into the same cemetery. Same surname, same town, same
        year: <b>exactly the shape that gets two families welded into one</b>, which is why she is
        written down here and not quietly left out.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><b>What four stones in one cemetery are worth.</b> Between
    them they moved a birth from a guessed month to a date, a death from a year to a month, and
    confirmed two dates this archive already had from sources that had nothing to do with the ones it
    got them from. None of that is a new person. <b>It is the difference between a record and a
    corroborated record</b>, and it took one search and five photographs.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="silverton-pillar">1940 &middot; The pillar at Silverton &mdash; twelve household names, matched to eleven legal ones</h2>
  <p class="dek">The centre pillar of the double stone at Silverton lists the children of Stefanus
    Francois Booyzen and Beatrix Magdalena Catharina Boshoff by the names they were called at home.
    His 1940 estate lists eleven children by the names they were registered under. The two lists have
    never been set side by side, and doing it identifies a twelfth child.</p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:9rem">On the stone</th><th style="width:22rem">On the 1940 estate</th><th>How the match is made</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m"><b>OUBOET</b></td><td>David Schalk Booyzen</td><td class="cap">Not a name: <i>ouboet</i> is Afrikaans for eldest brother. Matched by <b>position</b> &mdash; he heads the estate list.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><b>OUSIE</b></td><td>Susanna Fredrika Aletta Booyzen</td><td class="cap">Not a name either: <i>ousie</i>, eldest sister. Matched by <b>position</b> &mdash; and she is the only daughter left over once the other three are matched by name.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><b>BESSIE</b></td><td>Elizabeth Magrieta Booyzen</td><td class="cap">Bessie &larr; Elizabeth</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><b>BETIE</b></td><td>Beatrix Magdalena Catharina Booyzen</td><td class="cap">Betie &larr; Beatrix &mdash; her mother's name entire</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><b>HENNA</b></td><td>Johanna Catharina Booyzen</td><td class="cap">Henna &larr; Johanna. The weakest of the nine: <i>Henna</i> can also stand for Hendrina, and there is no Hendrina on the estate.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><b>EVERT</b></td><td>Ewert Philippus Booyzen</td><td class="cap">The same name, spelt as the family said it</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><b>&#10013; BABA</b></td><td><b>Nobody</b></td><td class="cap"><b>A TWELFTH CHILD.</b> Cut with a birth star and a cross and no name at all &mdash; <i>baba</i> is simply <i>baby</i>. Not on the 1940 estate because the child was already dead when it was written.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><b>FANIE</b></td><td>Stephanus Francois Booyzen the younger</td><td class="cap">Fanie &larr; Stefanus. Standard, and unambiguous.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><b>WILLIE</b></td><td>Willem Hendrik Booyzen</td><td class="cap">Willie &larr; Willem</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><b>PIETIE</b></td><td>Petrus Jacobus Booyzen</td><td class="cap">Pietie &larr; Petrus</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><b>JOHAN</b></td><td>Johan Abraham Frederik Booyzen</td><td class="cap">The same name</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><b>HENNIE</b></td><td>Hendrik Rudolph Philippus Booyzen</td><td class="cap">Hennie &larr; Hendrik</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p><b>Eleven names on the estate, twelve on the stone, and every one of the eleven is claimed
      exactly once.</b> That is what makes the twelfth real. Nine of the matches are ordinary Afrikaans
      diminutives; two &mdash; <i>ouboet</i> and <i>ousie</i> &mdash; are not names but places in the
      family, and they fall to the two children the diminutives leave over. Nothing is left unassigned
      except <b>&#10013;&nbsp;BABA</b>.</p>

    <p class="note"><b>This archive has met this shape before.</b> Joseph Heinrich Daschner's 1942
      death notice named eleven children; the Brixton burial register turned up a twelfth,
      <b>George Downing Daschner</b>, three months and five days old, buried in 1925 and therefore not
      on a notice written seventeen years later.
      <a href={u("/register/")}>The register &rarr;</a> A death notice lists the living. A gravestone,
      cut by the family, lists the children. <b>That is the difference between the two documents, and
      it is worth more than either of them alone.</b></p>

    <p class="cap"><b>What is not claimed.</b> No birth date, no sex, no order of birth beyond the
      stone's own order, and no name &mdash; the child is entered here as <b>Baba Booyzen</b> because
      that is the only word cut for it. The matches above are readings of nicknames, not documents, and
      the household page marks every one of them as inferred rather than stated.
      The mother's own name is on the stone as <b>Betie</b>'s: the family reused it inside one
      generation.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="four-negatives">What was searched today and not found</h2>
  <p class="dek">Four questions worked properly and answered <em>no</em>, or <em>not yet</em>. They are
    set down at the same length as the finds, because a family history that publishes only its successes
    is asking to be believed on trust.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Elizabetha van der Merwe&rsquo;s marriage</h3>
      <p>The second wife of Petrus Jacobus Booysen (1788&ndash;1876), named on her son&rsquo;s 1908 death
        notice. If the marriage could be dated it would <b>split the eight children of the 1876
        Sterfkennis between two mothers</b> instead of leaving all eight under a printed warning.
        <b>Searched</b>: Booysen &times; van der Merwe marriages across all South African collections;
        Elizabeth van der Merwe with a Booysen spouse; the Cape registers 1820&ndash;1835.
        <b>Nothing.</b> Every Booysen&ndash;van der Merwe marriage the indexes carry is twentieth
        century. The eight stay under the warning.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The four remaining 1876 children</h3>
      <p><b>David Schalk</b>, <b>Magdalena Johanna</b>, <b>Anna Eliezabet</b>, <b>Eliezabet Cornelia</b>
        and <b>Anna Maria Booijsen</b> &mdash; five of the eight on the 1876 Sterfkennis with no document
        of their own. <b>Still none.</b> The Transvaal probate index carries nobody of those names who
        can be tied to this family, and the Cape index carries too many. The three that <em>were</em>
        settled today &mdash; Petrus Jacobus, Jan Gerthardus Lodewikus and Hendrik Stefanus &mdash; were
        all settled by somebody else&rsquo;s death notice, not their own.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Ockert Johannes Stephanus Booysen&rsquo;s estate</h3>
      <p>He died at Klerksdorp on 16 January 1921 and <b>has no estate file of his own</b> in the indexed
        Transvaal probate records. Six index hits under four spellings, every one of them him as
        somebody else&rsquo;s relative. His death is a civil registration, which names no parents, and
        the films are not ordered by estate number so there is nothing to browse along.
        <a href="#diederik-1935">What was found instead &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The three untraced daughters</h3>
      <p><b>Anna Johanna Maria</b>, <b>Elesabettha Maria Cornelya</b> and <b>Magdalena Johanna
        Booijsen</b>, all of age on their mother&rsquo;s 1891 notice and on nothing else. Two of the
        three are probably dead before 1908, because their father&rsquo;s notice names four children
        where their mother&rsquo;s named seven. And this archive <b>cannot show</b> that the
        <b>Anna Catharina Grobelaar</b> of 1908 is the <b>Anna Johanna Maria</b> of 1891: the forenames
        do not agree, and guessing that they do is the exact mistake this site spent today undoing.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="two-hard-ones">Two questions worked hard and not closed</h2>
  <p class="dek">Both of these were predicted to fail before they were started. One did. The other moved
    further than expected, and neither is resolved &mdash; which is set out here at the same length as
    the finds, because that is the only way the finds are worth anything.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">1 &middot; Whose son was Stephanus Francois Booyzen?</h3>
  <p>He sits on this site as a son of <b>Petrus Jacobus Booysen</b> and his second wife
    <b>Johanna Margaritha Pretorius</b>, placed there <b>on his birth date alone</b> because his own 1940
    Sterfkennis names neither parent &mdash; his widow wrote <em>oorlede</em> for both. Then the 1900
    death notice of Elizabetha Margaretha Marais named a <b>Stephanus François Booijzen</b> as a son of
    <b>DAVID SCHALK BOOIJZEN</b>, and the dispute opened.</p>

  <figure style="margin:18px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/huwelijk-1905-mosselbaai.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="The Mossel Bay duplicate marriage register sheet for 5 September 1905, one entry to the page." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>DUPLICAAT ORIGINEEL REGISTER &middot; Huwelijk bevestigd te
      Mosselbaai.</strong> Eight columns &mdash; and none of them asks where anybody was born, which is
      the whole difficulty.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure style="margin:18px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1905-woonplaats-pretoria.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Entry 1318 of the Mossel Bay marriage register, 5 September 1905: Stefanus Francois Booyzen, 30, jonkman, metselaar, woonplaats Pretoria; Beatrix Magdalena Catharina Boshoff, 16, jongdochter, of Mosselbaai, parents consenting." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Entry <strong>1318</strong>, <em>Duplicaat Origineel Register</em>,
      Mosselbaai, <strong>5 September 1905</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What the register gives</h3>
      <p><b>Stefanus Francois Booyzen</b>, <b>30</b>, <b>JONKMAN</b> &mdash; a bachelor &mdash;
        <b>METSELAAR</b>, a mason, <b>woonplaats tijdens het huwelijk: PRETORIA</b>. He married
        <b>Beatrix Magdalena Catharina Boshoff</b>, <b>16</b>, <em>jongdochter</em> of Mosselbaai, with
        her <b>ouders</b> consenting, in the N.G. Kerk by <b>John Murray</b>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What it does not give</h3>
      <p><b>No parents. No birthplace.</b> This register has a column for <em>woonplaats tijdens het
        huwelijk</em> and none for <em>waar geboren</em> &mdash; unlike the Zeerust register of 1892,
        which gave this archive Hendrik Stefanus&rsquo;s Graaff-Reinet. <b>The question is not settled and
        this document cannot settle it.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">
    <b>But it tilts.</b> In <b>September 1905</b> Stephanus Francois was a <b>bachelor of thirty living
    at PRETORIA</b>. The 1900 notice calls David Schalk&rsquo;s son Stephanus François an
    <b>unmarried major</b>; and <b>eleven weeks later</b>, on 27 November 1905, <b>David Schalk Booijzen
    of Goede Hoop, Pretoria</b> married again in that same city.
    <br /><br />
    Two Booyzens in Pretoria in one autumn, one of them exactly the bachelor son the 1900 notice
    describes. <b>Against</b> that: the existing placement rests on a birth year and nothing else, and
    both men are masons, which discriminates nothing &mdash; Willem Hermanus was a mason at Indwe too,
    and he is either this man&rsquo;s half-brother or his uncle depending on which reading wins.
    <br /><br />
    <b>Still marked Serious.</b> It moves, it does not close, and this archive is not going to decide a
    father on a city. <a href={u("/disputed/")}>Disputed &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:38px">2 &middot; The Oliviers of Cradock</h3>
  <p>Anna Catharina Johanna Olivier&rsquo;s parents came into this archive yesterday as two names on a
    baptism. The problem stated then was that <b>at least four men of nearly that name were baptising
    children in that church in the 1820s</b>, and separating them is the only way her line goes higher.
    <b>They are not separated.</b> But this couple now has a footprint of its own.</p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m" style="width:10rem">Date</th><th style="width:18rem">Event</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m"><b>8 Dec 1816</b></td><td><b>They marry</b></td><td><b>GRAAFF-REINET</b> &mdash; index only, no image</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">25 Jul 1820</td><td>Gert Cornelis Olivier born</td><td>baptised 2 Oct 1820, Cradock</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">15 May 1823</td><td>Aletta Catharina Olivier born</td><td>baptised 20 Oct 1823, Cradock</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">10 Sep 1824</td><td><b>Anna Johanna Olivier born</b></td><td>baptised 4 Oct 1824, Cradock</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">Feb 1826</td><td>Ockert Johannes Stephanus the younger born</td><td>baptised 3 Apr 1826</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">
    <b>They married at GRAAFF-REINET.</b> That is the town where Petrus Jacobus Booysen was baptised in
    1788, where his son was born in 1812, where Jan Gerardus Lodewicus was born in 1818 and where Hendrik
    Stefanus was born about 1829. <b>The Oliviers and the Booysens were in the same small Karoo town at
    the same time</b>, moved to Cradock, and their children married each other at Potchefstroom in 1850,
    six hundred miles north.
    <br /><br />
    <b>What is still not done:</b> four children is not a family, an index entry with no image is not a
    marriage register, and this archive still cannot tell this Ockert Johannes Stephanus Olivier from the
    Ockert Johannis Fredrik Lodewyk, the Okker Petrus Jacobus and the Ockert Johannes Frederik who were
    all baptising at Cradock in the same decade. <b>Her line still stops with her parents.</b>
  </div>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:16px">Marriage: South Africa, Civil Marriage Records, 1801&ndash;1974
    &middot; Mosselbaai certificate <span class="m">1318</span>, FamilySearch
    <span class="m">1:1:6ZH5-CQZW</span>. Oliviers: Dutch Reformed Church Registers (Cape Town Archives)
    &middot; <span class="m">1:1:62V5-DRCK</span> (marriage, image unavailable),
    <span class="m">1:1:6LXQ-ZMNM</span>, <span class="m">1:1:6KRL-Q4H7</span>,
    <span class="m">1:1:6KRN-K37Y</span>, <span class="m">1:1:6LX3-J5WM</span>.
    Read 13 September 2026.</p>
</section>











<Rule name="awl" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="barry-1915">1915 &middot; The death notice, read entire at last</h2>
  <p class="dek">Estate <span class="m">925</span>, Grahamstown, stamped 18 April 1915. This archive has
    shown the top of this page for weeks and quoted four lines from it. The children's list is further
    down, and it contradicts this site in two places.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:20px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1915-john-barry-full.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="The full 1915 death notice for John Barry, estate 925, showing the list of eight children." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Dated Barkly East, <strong>29 March 1915</strong>. Signed
        <strong>James H. Barry &mdash; "Son (second)"</strong>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td style="width:6.5rem">1. Name</td><td><b>John Barry</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>2. Birthplace</td><td>At sea (exact whereabouts unknown), British</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3. Parents</td><td><b>John Barry</b> (address unknown)<br /><b>Barry, born Neagle</b> (Christian names and address unknown)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4. Age</td><td><b>83 (about)</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>5. Occupation</td><td><b>Carpenter and builder</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>6. Residence</td><td><b>Manorowen, Barkly East District</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>7a. Wife</td><td><b>Margaret Wilhelmina Elizabeth Barry, born Kolbe</b> &mdash; <b>died 2nd August 1912</b>, in community</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7c. Married at</td><td><b>Burghersdorp (probably)</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>8. Died</td><td>3rd August 1903, <b>on farm Manorowen</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>11&ndash;14</td><td>movables <b>yes</b> &middot; immovables <b>no</b> &middot; over &pound;300 <b>yes</b> &middot; will <b>no</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figure style="margin:18px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1915-kolbe-died-1912.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question seven: Margaret Wilhelmina Elizabeth Barry born Kolbe, died 2nd August 1912, in community." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>"died 2nd August 1912."</strong> This archive has carried her death
      as <em>1912</em> and nothing more.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 style="margin-top:30px">Eight children, where the registration said eleven</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1915-eight-children.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question ten of the 1915 notice, listing eight children of John Barry." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Question ten, entire.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:24rem">As typed</th><th>What this archive has</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>John Augustus Barry</b></td><td>A son of the same name as his father's &mdash; and the reason this site has been calling the father "John Augustus Barry I". Both his own documents call him simply <b>John Barry</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>James Henry Barry</b></td><td>The second son. He signed this form, and died at Barkly East on 23 June 1939.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>George Augustus Barry</b></td><td class="m"><b>New.</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Henry Nicholas Barry</b></td><td>Hendrik Nicholaas &mdash; who married Anna Johanna Maria Schoeman and signed his own daughter's death registration in 1912.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Frederick Fortunatus Barry</b></td><td>Carries the Kolbe name.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Richard Peter Barry</b></td><td>Present at his father's death in 1903, and the name on the Cape Rebel roll.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Margaret Mary Bedford, born Barry</b>, married to Tom Bedford</td><td>Margaretha Louisa Maria, m. Thomas Bedford 1875.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Kathleen Mary Boyson, born Barry</b>, married in community of property to <b>William Boyson</b></td><td>The only Booyzen marriage on the list, and it is spelled <b>Boyson</b> by an English clerk.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <b>Two corrections come straight off this page.</b>
    <br /><br />
    <b>Dorothea Regina Barry and Ruben Jacobus Josuah Barry are not on it.</b> This archive has carried
    both as children of John Barry, "named on the 1915 death notice". They are not named on it. What the
    registers do hold is a <b>Dorothea Regina</b> baptised at Slang River in 1892 and a
    <b>Ruben Jacobus Hosea</b> baptised at Dordrecht in 1888 &mdash; both children of
    <a href={u("/who/hendrik-nicholaas-barry/")}>Henry Nicholas Barry</a> and Anna Johanna Maria
    Schoeman, so <b>grandchildren, not children</b>. <a href={u("/corrections/")}>What was taken back
    &rarr;</a>
    <br /><br />
    <b>And eight is not eleven.</b> The 1903 registration, made by Richard Barry twelve years earlier,
    answers the printed question "the total number of children deceased has had" with
    <b>eleven</b>. This list has eight. <b>Three children died between 1903 and 1915, or before</b>, and
    this archive cannot yet name one of them. <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <p>One more thing worth noticing. The form asks where the marriage took place and the answer is
      <b>"Burghersdorp (probably)"</b>. A son of the house, writing twelve years after his father's death
      and three after his mother's, does not know where his parents were married and says so in
      brackets. It sits beside line 3, where he gives his grandmother's surname as <b>Neagle</b> and
      admits he has neither her Christian names nor an address.</p>
    <blockquote><b>This is the same failure this archive has been tracking since 1840</b>, in a family
      that wrote in English and filed with a solicitor. Six death notices here fail to name a parent
      properly; two of them are John Barry's and his wife's.</blockquote>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="pow-1901">1900&ndash;1901 &middot; Six Kolbes and three David Schalk Booysens, each with a farm against his name</h2>
  <p class="dek">The War Office kept a <b>list of Boer prisoners of war, numbers 1 to 32,561</b> &mdash;
    <span class="m">WO 108/368</span> at The National Archives. It gives a name, an age, a nationality
    and, for most of them, <b>the farm and district the man was taken from</b>. That last column is what
    makes it a genealogical source rather than a casualty list.</p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:15rem">Name as indexed</th><th class="m">Age</th><th class="m">b.</th><th>Farm and district</th><th class="m">Held at</th><th class="m">Folio</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>George Augustus Kolbe</b> &mdash; <span class="cap">of Burgersdorp, b. 2 Oct 1861</span></td><td class="m">40</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td><td><b>Wakkerstroom</b>, Wakkerstroom &mdash; <em>Transvaaler</em></td><td class="m">Natal</td><td class="m">709</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>George Augustus Kolbe</b></td><td class="m">32</td><td class="m">1869</td><td>Sterkfontein, <b>Bethulie</b> &mdash; <em>Free Stater</em></td><td class="m">Natal</td><td class="m">581</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Andries Frederick Kolbe</b></td><td class="m">45</td><td class="m">1856</td><td><b>LEEUWBERG</b>, Bloemfontein</td><td class="m">Cape Town &amp; Natal</td><td class="m">431</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Williem Johannes Kolbe</td><td class="m">54</td><td class="m">1847</td><td>Panfontein, Bloemfontein &mdash; <em>Free Stater</em></td><td class="m">Natal</td><td class="m">603</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ian Henry Kolbe</td><td class="m">51</td><td class="m">1849</td><td>Linberg, Bloemfontein</td><td class="m">Cape Town</td><td class="m">294</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pietrus Emelius Strucker Kolbe</td><td class="m">24</td><td class="m">1876</td><td>Bossiespan, <b>Fauresmith</b></td><td class="m">Cape Town</td><td class="m">381</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">Leeuwberg, again</h3>
      <p>This archive wrote <b>Leeuwberg</b> into the record a week ago, from an entirely different
        document &mdash; <b>John Kolbe junior and the Jephts of Leeuwberg</b>. Here it is again, in a
        War Office list, as the farm a Kolbe was taken from in 1901.</p>
      <p class="cap">Not proof of a link. But the same farm carrying the same surname forty years apart
        is not nothing.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">The Settler&rsquo;s own forenames, twice</h3>
      <p><b>Two men called George Augustus Kolbe</b> were taken prisoner in the same war &mdash; one at
        <b>Wakkerstroom</b>, one at <b>Bethulie</b>.</p>
      <p><b>The Wakkerstroom one was already identified on this site</b>, from the gravestones, before
        this search was run: <b>George Augustus Kolbe of Burgersdorp, born 2 October 1861</b>, who was
        forty from October 1901 to October 1902 where the other Wakkerstroom man of that name, born
        29 July 1863, was thirty-nine all year. <b>The folio number is the only thing this sweep added
        to him.</b> <a href={u("/documents/#wakkerstroom-stones")}>The stones &rarr;</a></p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px">The Bethulie man is new and unattached. No descent is
        claimed for him.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">Five of six in one corner of the Free State</h3>
      <p>Leeuwberg, Panfontein and Linberg near <b>Bloemfontein</b>, Bossiespan in <b>Fauresmith</b>,
        Sterkfontein in <b>Bethulie</b>. That is the country the Settler preached in &mdash; across the
        Orange River from Colesberg, where he farmed Wirtemberg.</p>
      <p class="cap">And where the Commandant&rsquo;s farm turned out to be, <b>two hours from
        Bloemfontein</b>, when the Fauresmith pin was withdrawn earlier this month.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">And three David Schalk Booysens</h3>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m">Age</th><th class="m">b.</th><th>Farm and district</th><th class="m">Folio</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">53</td><td class="m">c.1848</td><td>Pretoria, Boksburg</td><td class="m">442</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">41</td><td class="m">c.1860</td><td><b>VAALBANK</b>, Lichtenburg</td><td class="m">491</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">33</td><td class="m">c.1868</td><td><b>Kapsteinkop, CRADOCK</b> &mdash; <em>British Subject</em></td><td class="m">575</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:8px">Two of those three places are this family&rsquo;s own ground, and
    one is the <b>Vaalbank</b> whose farm school this archive documented a week ago. <b>It is a lead and
    not a finding.</b> This archive has already shown that David Schalk is a van der Merwe name before
    it is a Booysen one, and there are <b>fifty-four Booysens and twenty Booyens</b> in the same list.
    A <b>Petrus Jacobus Booysen, aged 27, of Pretoria and Pietersberg</b>, is among them &mdash; the
    right name, the wrong generation, and recorded here so that he is not later mistaken for the
    Petrus Jacobus this archive holds.</p>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><b>One thing about the index that will cost somebody a day.</b>
    It renders <b>Jan as &ldquo;Ian&rdquo;</b>, systematically &mdash; Ian Henry Kolbe, Ian Gert Booysen,
    Ian Lodewyk Booysen, Ian Jonathan Booysen, Ian Adriaan Booyens. <b>A search for <em>Jan</em> in this
    set returns nothing.</b> It also writes Willem as <em>Williem</em> and Petrus as <em>Pietrus</em>.
    Searched on the surname and read down; that is the only way it works.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="barry-ruswarp">1841 and 1851 &middot; The Yorkshire John Barry is real, and he is a shipowner at Whitby</h2>
  <p class="dek"><a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open question 6</a> has stood since it was written:
    one profile welds <b>a Yorkshire farmer of Bilsdale, christened 1796 and buried at St Hilda&rsquo;s
    on 3 January 1853</b>, to a John Barry who died at de Bruins Poort in the Cape on
    <b>8 January 1853, five days later</b>. The burial registers have already been searched and hold no
    Barry at Bilsdale at all. <b>The censuses do better than a negative: they produce the man.</b></p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:18px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">1841 &middot; HO107/1265, book 11, folio 38, page 4</h3>
      <p><b>JOHN BARRY, head, aged 45, born Yorkshire.</b> Address <b>SHIP YARD, RUSWARP, WHITBY</b>.
        Schedule 1116. In the house with him: <b>Jno, Mary, Ann and Catharine Barry</b>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">1851 &middot; HO107/2374, folio 563, page 12, line 6</h3>
      <p><b>JOHN BARRY, head, married, aged 55, born WHITBY.</b> Occupation <b>SHIP OWNER</b>. Address
        <b>SHIP YARDS, RUSWARP, WHITBY</b>. Schedule 46. With <b>Mary</b> (b. 1809),
        <b>Catherine</b> (b. 1832) and <b>Elizabeth</b> (b. 1836).</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><b>He is not a farmer, he is not of Bilsdale, and he is alive
    in 1851.</b> Ruswarp is a township on the Esk a mile above Whitby harbour, thirty miles from
    Bilsdale across the moor. <b>The occupation is <em>Ship Owner</em> and the street is
    <em>Ship Yards</em>.</b></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">And there is nobody else he could be</h3>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m" style="width:7rem">Census</th><th class="m">Barrys in the North Riding</th><th>Where they were</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">1841</td><td class="m"><b>50</b></td>
          <td>Whitby 22, Scarborough 13, Brompton 10, Allerston 2, Lythe 2, Fylingdales 1 &mdash;
            registration districts <b>Whitby 25, Scarborough 23, Pickering 2</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">1851</td><td class="m"><b>52</b></td>
          <td>Ruswarp 8, Whitby 7, Sneaton 7, Brompton 10, Easingwold 4, Fylingdales 3, Hinderwell 2,
            Great Smeaton 2, Lythe 1, Eston 1, Filey 1 &mdash; districts <b>Whitby 26, Scarborough 17,
            Easingwold 4, Guisborough 3, Northallerton 2</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:8px"><b>Not one of the hundred and two is in the Helmsley
    registration district</b>, which is the district Bilsdale sits in. And across both censuses there is
    <b>exactly one John Barry born in 1796</b> in the whole North Riding: the shipowner at Ruswarp, in
    1841 at Ship Yard and in 1851 at Ship Yards, the same house ten years apart.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:22px">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0">What question 6 becomes</h3>
    <p><b>The English half of that profile has been taken apart and rebuilt.</b> There is a real John
      Barry born in the North Riding in 1796. He is a <b>Whitby shipowner</b>, not a Bilsdale farmer of
      Holly Bower; there is <b>no Barry in 5,934 indexed Bilsdale burials</b>; and he was still keeping
      house at Ruswarp in the spring of 1851. <b>Whether he is the man the tree meant or a man the tree
      found and dressed in somebody else&rsquo;s clothes, he cannot also have been buried at
      St Hilda&rsquo;s.</b></p>
    <p class="cap">What is still open, and it is a smaller question than it was: whether the Whitby
      shipowner has any connection at all to the John Barry who died at de Bruins Poort. Nothing here
      says he does. <b>The five-day coincidence that built the profile is now a coincidence with nothing
      on the English side of it.</b></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="kolbe-gazette">1824&ndash;1845 &middot; Five notices in the London Gazette, and a house coming apart in one year</h2>
  <p class="dek">This archive already had one of these &mdash; the 1836 dissolution &mdash; and had
    treated it as a lucky find. <b>There are five.</b> Together they give a tailoring business from
    Church Street to Conduit Street across twenty-one years, and they date the move that the rate books
    could only bracket.</p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m" style="width:9rem">Date</th><th class="m" style="width:9rem">Issue &middot; page</th><th>What it says</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="m">12 May 1824</td><td class="m">18027 &middot; 796</td>
          <td><em>&ldquo;the Partnership heretofore subsisting between the undersigned, <b>John Gotlob
            KOLBE and John Henry KOLBE, of Church-Street, Soho, in the County of Middlesex,
            TAILORS</b>, was this day dissolved&hellip;&rdquo;</em></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="m">9 Oct 1824</td><td class="m">18069 &middot; 1668</td>
          <td><b>INSOLVENT DEBTORS.</b> Petitions to be heard at Portugal-Street, Lincoln&rsquo;s-Inn
            Fields, on Friday 5 November 1824. <em>&ldquo;<b>John Henry KOLBE (sued as Henry Kolbe),
            formerly of No. 22, Norfolk-Street, Middlesex-Hospital, and late of No. 4,
            Mansfield-Place</b>&hellip;&rdquo;</em></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="m">16 Nov 1824</td><td class="m">18080 &middot; 1900</td>
          <td>The same man in the insolvency lists a second time, the name printed
            <em>&ldquo;Kolbe, John Henry&rdquo;</em>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="m">30 Sep 1836</td><td class="m">19424 &middot; 1702</td>
          <td><em>&ldquo;<b>John Gbttlob KOLBE and David Fletcher, of Conduit-Street, Bond-Street</b>, in
            the County of Middlesex, <b>Tailors</b>&hellip;&rdquo;</em> <span class="cap">(already held
            here)</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="m">21 Mar 1845</td><td class="m">20455 &middot; 930</td>
          <td><em>&ldquo;the Copartnership heretofore subsisting between the undersigned, <b>John Gotlieb
            KOLBE and Richard Davies, of Conduit-street, Bond-street, Tailors</b>, has been dissolved by
            mutual consent.&rdquo;</em> <b>Eight months before he died.</b></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">1824, which was the worst year they had</h3>
      <p><b>12 May</b> &mdash; father and son dissolve the partnership at Church Street.<br />
        <b>15 August</b> &mdash; <b>Mrs Mary Kolbe, the second wife, dies aged 36</b>, and is buried in
        the German Lutheran register.<br />
        <b>9 October and 16 November</b> &mdash; <b>John Henry is in the insolvent debtors&rsquo;
        lists</b>, out of Soho and living at Mansfield Place.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px">He was twenty-four. He died eight years later, aged
        thirty-two. <b>Five years before all this he was eighteen and losing a six-pound coat outside a
        Westminster public house.</b> <a href={u("/documents/#kolbe-old-bailey")}>The trial &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And the move that the rate books could only bracket</h3>
      <p>The Westminster rate books have him in <b>Church Street South to 1831</b>. The Gazette has him
        in <b>Conduit Street by September 1836</b>. Between those two dates the family left Soho for
        Mayfair, and he was working with a partner at each end &mdash; <b>David Fletcher to 1836,
        Richard Davies to March 1845</b>.</p>
      <p class="cap">Which makes the obituary&rsquo;s &ldquo;<em>Esq.</em> of Brunswick-square&rdquo;
        harder still. <b>He was signing himself a tailor in the Gazette eight months before he
        died.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><b>All five are free.</b> The London Gazette&rsquo;s own
    archive at <span class="m">thegazette.co.uk</span> is searchable back to 1665 and returns the OCR
    text in an Atom feed &mdash; <span class="m">/all-notices/notice/data.feed?text=&hellip;</span> with
    a publication-date range. <b>This archive found the 1836 notice months ago and never ran the same
    search again with the dates widened.</b> The other four had been sitting there the whole time.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="kolbe-old-bailey">9 January 1819 &middot; A brother of the Settler says where his father lives</h2>
  <p class="dek">This archive spent a fortnight arguing about a London address from obituaries, wills and
    tax books. <b>The son of the house answered it out loud, in a courtroom, in January 1819</b>, and
    somebody wrote it down.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:18px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9"><b>The Proceedings of the Old Bailey &middot;
      Session of 13 January 1819 &middot; trial t18190113-149</b><br />
      <b>309. RICHARD KERR</b> was indicted for stealing, on the 9th of January, <b>one coat, value
      6<i>l.</i></b>, the property of <b>John Henry Kolbe</b>.</p>
  </div>

  <blockquote style="margin-top:14px"><b>JOHN HENRY KOLBE.</b> <b>My father is a tailor, and lives in
    Church-street, Soho.</b> On the 9th of January, about eleven o&rsquo;clock at night, I stopped at the
    Star and Crown public-house, Westminster, and left my coat over my horse&rsquo;s head &mdash; I had a
    chaise standing at the door; I saw the prisoner go out, and suspected him &mdash; I went out, and
    missed my coat.</blockquote>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:8px">Henry Betts, a constable, had taken Kerr in charge for another
    offence; two other witnesses did not appear. <b>NOT GUILTY.</b> Second Middlesex Jury, before
    Mr Recorder.</p>

  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">Who is speaking</h3>
      <p><b>John Henry Kolbe, baptised at Westminster on 29 July 1800</b> &mdash; one of the eight sons,
        and eighteen years old on the night he lost his coat. He died on 13 April 1832, aged
        thirty-two, and was buried in the German Lutheran register.</p>
      <p class="cap">His younger brother <b>George Augustus sailed for Algoa Bay the following year</b>.
        This is the closest thing this archive has to a voice from the house he left.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">What he settles</h3>
      <p>The rate books put his father at <b>Church Street South, St Anne&rsquo;s Soho</b> in 1811, 1819,
        1828 and 1831. <b>The son says the same street, in 1819, under oath, to a court that had no
        interest in where he lived.</b></p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px">Two sources with nothing whatever to do with each other
        &mdash; a parish tax list and a criminal indictment &mdash; agreeing on an address the family
        tree had wrong twice.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">And what it shows of them</h3>
      <p>A coat worth <b>six pounds</b> &mdash; several months&rsquo; wages for a labourer, and exactly
        what a master tailor&rsquo;s son would be wearing. A <b>chaise and a horse</b> at eleven at night
        outside a Westminster public house. <b>&ldquo;My father is a tailor&rdquo;</b>, said plainly,
        twenty-five years before an obituary preferred <em>Esq.</em></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><b>How it was found, which is the part worth repeating.</b>
    Not by searching the Old Bailey. It came out of a <b>surname sweep of FindMyPast for Kolbe in
    London</b>, in a list of 154 rows that was mostly rate books, where two entries said
    <em>Old Bailey Court Records</em> and were nearly scrolled past. The trial text itself is free,
    at <span class="m">oldbaileyonline.org</span>, and always was.
    <a href={u("/documents/#church-street-south")}>The rate books &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="eight-sons">1792&ndash;1819 &middot; Five of the eight sons, named at last</h2>
  <p class="dek">The obituary of <b>George Augustus Kolbe</b>, December 1844, calls him
    <b>&ldquo;the last remaining of eight sons of John Godloff Kolbe Esq. of Brunswick-square&rdquo;</b>.
    Seven brothers died before him and this archive had never found a single one. The route was always
    obvious &mdash; the London parish registers &mdash; and was never open.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:18px">
    <p><b>The control first.</b> George Augustus himself: baptised at Westminster on
      <b>2 February 1803</b>, father <b>John Gottlow Kolbe</b>, mother <b>Mary</b>. This archive already
      had that date from another route entirely. <b>The register agrees, so the register can be trusted
      for the rest of them.</b></p>
  </div>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m">Baptised</th><th style="width:14rem">Name</th><th class="m"></th><th style="width:12rem">Father written as</th><th class="m">Batch</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">9 Jan 1792</td><td>Juliet Rosinia</td><td class="m">F</td><td class="m">John</td><td class="m">C72301-3</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">11 Jun 1794</td><td>Mary Ann</td><td class="m">F</td><td class="m">John</td><td class="m">C32052-8</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">29 Dec 1795</td><td>Elizabeth</td><td class="m">F</td><td class="m">John</td><td class="m">C15051-2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">17 Jan 1798</td><td><b>Charles</b></td><td class="m">M</td><td class="m">John</td><td class="m">C15051-2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">29 Jul 1800</td><td><b>John Henry</b></td><td class="m">M</td><td class="m">John</td><td class="m">I02559-1</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">2 Feb 1803</td><td><b>George Augustus</b></td><td class="m">M</td><td class="m">John Gottlow</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">12 Mar 1805</td><td><b>Frederick Charles</b></td><td class="m">M</td><td class="m">John Gottleb</td><td class="m">C15051-2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">3 Feb 1807</td><td><b>Charles Samuel</b></td><td class="m">M</td><td class="m">John Gottleb</td><td class="m">C15051-2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">18 Aug 1813</td><td>Frederica Carolina</td><td class="m">F</td><td class="m">John Gottleb</td><td class="m">C06236-1</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">4 Jul 1819</td><td>Jane Day</td><td class="m">F</td><td class="m">John Gottlob</td><td class="m">C06236-1</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:8px">All ten at Westminster. The mother is <b>Mary</b> in every single
    entry &mdash; which turned out to be two women, not one. <a href={u("/documents/#lutheran-seven")}>The
    burials say why &rarr;</a></p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What this gives</h3>
      <p><b>Five of the eight sons, named:</b> Charles 1798, John Henry 1800, George Augustus 1803,
        Frederick Charles 1805, Charles Samuel 1807. Three brothers are still missing, and the gaps in
        the run &mdash; <b>1808 to 1812</b> and <b>1814 to 1818</b> &mdash; are where they must sit.</p>
      <p class="cap">And a detail that reads as tragedy once the burials are put beside it: there is a
        <b>Charles</b> in 1798 and a <b>Charles Samuel</b> in 1807. Families reused a name when the first
        child carrying it had died.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">One that was thrown out</h3>
      <p><b>Frederick Kolbe, baptised at Westminster on 16 December 1816.</b> Right surname, right
        parish, right decade, and the run has a gap exactly there. He is <b>not a brother</b>: his father
        is <b>George</b>, his mother <b>Elizabeth</b>, and his register batch is C72301-7 rather than the
        family&rsquo;s.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px">He was one click from being made a son. The thing that
        stopped it was reading the parents&rsquo; names on the transcript instead of the name, place and
        year in the result list. <b>The same check threw out Henry Kolbe, baptised at Southwark on
        2 February 1800 &mdash; father Henry, mother Susannah.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:30px">Then the same search in Westminster&rsquo;s own registers, which the
    FamilySearch index is only a summary of</h3>
  <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px">The table above comes from <b>England Births &amp; Baptisms</b>,
    which is FamilySearch&rsquo;s index and gives the place as &ldquo;Westminster&rdquo; and nothing
    finer. <b>Westminster Archives&rsquo; own baptism register set names the parish &mdash; and holds a
    child the other index does not.</b></p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m" style="width:9rem">Baptised</th><th style="width:13rem">Name</th><th>Parish</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">9 Jan 1792</td><td>Juliet Rosinia</td><td>St James, Piccadilly</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">11 Jun 1794</td><td>Mary Ann</td><td>St James, Piccadilly</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">29 Dec 1795</td><td>Elizabeth</td><td><b>St Anne, Soho</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">17 Jan 1798</td><td>Charles</td><td><b>St Anne, Soho</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">29 Jul 1800</td><td>John Henry</td><td>St James</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">2 Feb 1803</td><td>George Augustus</td><td><b>St Anne, Soho</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">12 Mar 1805</td><td>Frederick Charles</td><td><b>St Anne, Soho</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">3 Feb 1807</td><td>Charles Samuel</td><td><b>St Anne, Soho</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">4 Jul 1810</td><td><b>LOUISA MARIA &mdash; born 13 May 1810</b></td><td><b>St Anne, Soho</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">18 Aug 1813</td><td>Frederica Carolina</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">4 Jul 1819</td><td>Jane Day</td><td><b>St Anne, Soho</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:8px"><b>An eleventh child, and the only one with a birth date as well
    as a baptism date.</b> Louisa Maria, born 13 May 1810, baptised 4 July, father John Gottlob, mother
    Mary. She sits in the 1808&ndash;1812 gap, and she is not in the FamilySearch index at all.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And three of them married</h3>
      <p><b>Elizabeth Kolbe married JOHN MILNE in 1817 at St Clement Danes</b>, both of that parish. She
        was twenty-one.</p>
      <p><b>John Henry Kolbe married MATILDA FORD on 26 April 1819 at St Margaret, Westminster</b>, both
        of that parish. <b>He was eighteen, and three months earlier he had been in the Old Bailey losing
        a six-pound coat.</b> <a href={u("/documents/#kolbe-old-bailey")}>The trial &rarr;</a></p>
      <p><b>Jane Day Kolbe married in 1853 in the City of London</b> &mdash; GRO index, 1853 Q3, vol AM,
        1170, p.33. The youngest of the eleven, and she lived to thirty-four at least.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">A grandchild, probably</h3>
      <p><b>MARY ANN MARIA FORD KOLBE</b> married at Whitechapel in the first quarter of 1847 &mdash;
        GRO index vol AZ, 840, p.16.</p>
      <p><b>&ldquo;Ford&rdquo; is Matilda&rsquo;s maiden name</b>, carried as a forename in the way
        families did carry them, and there is no other Ford anywhere near this family. <b>She is very
        probably John Henry and Matilda&rsquo;s daughter</b>, born after the 1819 marriage and married
        fifteen years after her father died.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px">That is an inference from a forename and it is written down
        as one. <b>The certificate would settle it, and it has not been ordered.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="lutheran-seven">1807&ndash;1832 &middot; Seven Kolbe burials in a German Lutheran register, and two wives called Mary</h2>
  <p class="dek">The 1844 obituary of George Augustus Kolbe calls him
    <b>&ldquo;the last remaining of eight sons of John Godloff Kolbe Esq.&rdquo;</b> Seven brothers died
    before December 1844 and this archive had never found one of them. It had guessed where to look
    &mdash; the German Lutheran church of the Savoy, TNA series <span class="m">RG 4</span> &mdash; and
    had never been able to get at the registers. <b>They are on FindMyPast, and they hold seven Kolbes.</b></p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m">Died</th><th class="m">Buried</th><th style="width:16rem">Name as written</th><th class="m">Age</th><th class="m">Image</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">6 Dec 1807</td><td class="m">11 Dec 1807</td><td><b>Chs Saml Kolbe</b></td><td class="m">1y 10d</td><td class="m">RG 4/4631 f.47<br />RG 4/4630 f.44</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">8 Mar 1809</td><td class="m">14 Mar 1809</td><td><b>Frederick Kolbe</b></td><td class="m">4y 6w</td><td class="m">RG 4/4631 f.49</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">1 Jul 1814</td><td class="m">3 Jul 1814</td><td>Fredericka Caroline Kolbe</td><td class="m">11 months</td><td class="m">RG 4/4631 f.61</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">2 Aug 1814</td><td class="m">5 Aug 1814</td><td><b>Mary Kolbe</b></td><td class="m">46</td><td class="m">RG 4/4631 f.62</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">15 Aug 1824</td><td class="m">&mdash;</td><td><b>Mrs Mary Kolbe</b></td><td class="m">36</td><td class="m">RG 4/4631 f.81</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">13 Apr 1832</td><td class="m">22 Apr 1832</td><td><b>John Henry Kolbe</b></td><td class="m">32</td><td class="m">RG 4/4631 f.100</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:8px">Six rows, seven records: the 1807 child is entered twice, once in
    RG 4/4631 and once in RG 4/4630, with the same death date and the same age to the day. That is a
    register and its duplicate, not two children. <b>RG 4/4630 was not on this archive&rsquo;s list of Savoy
    volumes.</b></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">Matched against the baptisms, which were found the same afternoon</h3>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:14px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">Three brothers, dead and dated</h3>
      <p><b>Charles Samuel</b>, baptised 3 February 1807 &mdash; dead at <b>one year and ten days</b>.
        <b>Frederick Charles</b>, baptised 12 March 1805 &mdash; dead at <b>four years and six weeks</b>.
        <b>John Henry</b>, baptised 29 July 1800 &mdash; dead at <b>thirty-two</b>, on 13 April 1832,
        twelve years before the obituary that counted him.</p>
      <p class="cap">And a sister with them: Frederica Carolina, baptised 18 August 1813, buried at
        eleven months.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">Two wives, and both of them Mary</h3>
      <p>The archive had one Mary and could not make her fit. <b>There are two.</b> The first died
        <b>2 August 1814 aged 46</b> &mdash; born about 1768, and buried a month after her
        eleven-month-old daughter. The second died <b>15 August 1824 aged 36</b> &mdash; born about
        1788, and she is the Mary named as mother at the 1819 baptism of Jane Day Kolbe.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px">Which is why a single Mary would not work: she would have
        been bearing children from 1792 to 1819, and the woman who died in 1814 could not have been the
        mother in 1819.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><b>What is still missing.</b> Three of the eight sons remain
    unnamed. The German Lutheran <b>baptism</b> registers &mdash; <span class="m">RG 4/4627</span> and
    <span class="m">RG 4/4629</span> &mdash; are not on FindMyPast under any dataset name this archive
    could find, only the burials. The gaps in the Westminster baptism run, <b>1808&ndash;1812</b> and
    <b>1814&ndash;1818</b>, are where the other three sit.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="church-street-south">1800&ndash;1845 &middot; Not Brunswick Square: a tailor rated in Soho for thirty-one years</h2>
  <p class="dek">An 1844 obituary puts the family at <b>Brunswick-square</b>. His own 1842 will and 1845
    death put him in <b>Conduit Street</b>, off Bond Street. Those are different parts of London and this
    archive could not choose between them. It has been waiting for a trade directory. What decided it was
    a <b>rate book</b> &mdash; the list of who paid the parish rates, house by house, year after year.</p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:18px">
    <p><b>One hundred and twenty-one entries under twenty-four spellings of one forename.</b>
      Westminster Rate Books 1634&ndash;1900 index him as Gothel, Gotleb, Gotleib, Gotlieb, Gotliel,
      Gotlob, Gotlobe, Goltief, Gottieb, Gottiel, Gottlieb, Gottlob, Gutlieb, Kottieb, Gilliet and
      &mdash; once &mdash; <em>Jos Gottiel</em>. <b>A name search for &ldquo;Gottlieb&rdquo; finds one
      entry in twenty-four.</b> The run is only visible because the surname was searched and the
      forenames read.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m">Year</th><th style="width:15rem">Written as</th><th>Address</th><th>Parish</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">1800</td><td>John Kolbe</td><td><b>Broad Street North</b>, folio 41</td><td>St James, Piccadilly</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">1811</td><td>John G Kolbe</td><td><b>Church Street South</b></td><td>St Anne, Soho</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">1819</td><td>John Gottlob Kolbe</td><td>Church Street South</td><td>St Anne, Soho</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">1828</td><td>John Gotlieb Kolbe</td><td>Church Street South</td><td>St Anne, Soho</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">1829</td><td>John Gottlieb Kolbe <span class="cap">(land tax)</span></td><td>Church Street</td><td>Westminster, St Anne, Soho</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">1831</td><td>John Gottieb Kolbe</td><td>Church St South</td><td>St Anne, Soho</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The 1800 entry settles an older question too</h3>
      <p>This archive holds a line from a printed list of <b>234 master tailors</b> who met over the
        Combination Act in December 1799: <em>&ldquo;Kolbe, John, 18 Broad-street, Soho&rdquo;</em>. It
        had no way to test it. <b>The rate book for 1800 puts a John Kolbe in Broad Street North.</b>
        Two sources, neither aware of the other, one street.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And what it does to Brunswick Square</h3>
      <p><b>Brunswick Square is in Bloomsbury.</b> It is not in Westminster and would not appear in any
        of these books, so they cannot disprove it. What they can do is close the gap: he is rated in
        Soho from 1800 to 1831, he is in the <b>1841 census at St George Hanover Square</b> with Amelia
        A, Charlotte and Marianne Kolbe, and he <b>dies in St George Hanover Square in 1845</b>.
        <b>There is no window left for a Bloomsbury house.</b> Conduit Street is in St George Hanover
        Square. The obituary is the odd one out.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:16px"><b>What a rate book is, and why it beat the directory.</b> A
    trade directory lists the men who paid to be listed. A rate book lists everyone the parish could get
    money out of, every year, by street. This archive spent a fortnight trying to get at a London
    directory of the 1820s past Google Books&rsquo; quota and HathiTrust&rsquo;s bot check. <b>The answer
    was in a tax record, which nobody thinks of as a genealogical source because it has no names of
    relatives in it.</b> It has something better: an address, repeated thirty-one times.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="fmp-negatives">September 2026 &middot; Four things FindMyPast does not have, and the controls that prove it</h2>
  <p class="dek">A month&rsquo;s full subscription was opened on 16 September. Four of the questions put
    to it came back empty. <b>A nil return is worthless until a control shows the search works</b>, so
    each one below is published with the count that makes it mean something.
    <a href={u("/method/#controlled-negative")}>The method &rarr;</a></p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0">First, the trap &mdash; and it is the kind that answers wrongly rather than emptily</h3>
    <p>FindMyPast indexes the <b>21st Light Dragoons</b> under <b>four different regiment strings</b>,
      and they hold different War Office series.</p>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:12px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:18rem">Indexed as</th><th class="m">records</th><th>series</th><th class="m">years</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="m">21st Dragoons</td><td class="m">342</td><td class="m">WO 22, WO 23, WO 121</td><td class="m">1801&ndash;1886</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m"><b>21st Regt Of Light Dragoons</b></td><td class="m">128</td><td class="m"><b>WO 97 only</b></td><td class="m">1793&ndash;1813</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">21st Light Dragoons</td><td class="m">66</td><td class="m">WO 22, WO 23, WO 76</td><td class="m">1801&ndash;1877</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">21st Hussars</td><td class="m">574</td><td class="m">WO 22, 23, 76, 97, 121, 131</td><td class="m">1801&ndash;1919</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:12px">Searching the obvious string alone would have produced a
      negative against an index holding <b>no WO 97 at all</b> &mdash; and WO 97, the Chelsea discharge
      papers, is the series that would give James Mountjoy his parish and county of birth.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">1 &middot; James Mountjoy is not there</h3>
      <p><b>All four strings swept in full: 1,110 records.</b> No Mountjoy, Montjoy, Monjoy, Mountjoye or
        Montjoie in any of them.</p>
      <p><b>The controls.</b> WO 23/136, the 1806&ndash;07 volume, holds <b>18 men of the regiment</b>,
        Mark Allen to Jno Warnsnop. WO 23/147, 1817&ndash;26, holds <b>12</b>. The WO 97 run holds
        <b>128</b>, 1793 to 1813. The alphabetical run through the Ms goes Martin, McClay, McCracken,
        McCue, McGregor, McNary, Merrick, Morgan, Morris, Morrison &mdash; and in the other sweep,
        Moses straight to Napier.</p>
      <p class="cap"><b>Eighteen pre-1860 Mountjoys do exist in the set</b> and every one is 36th, 44th
        or 60th Foot.</p>
      <p><b>And the WO 97 half of it is as strong as a negative can be made.</b> That string resolves to
        <b>&ldquo;WO 97 &mdash; Chelsea Pensioners British Army Service Records 1760&ndash;1913&rdquo;</b>,
        which is the exact series this archive named as the answer to question 12. Its 128 men of the
        regiment are indexed with <b>birth parish, birth town, birth county, birth country, age at
        attestation and date of attestation</b>. They attested between <b>1793 and 1813</b>, and the
        distribution peaks precisely where James Mountjoy&rsquo;s would fall &mdash; <b>35 in 1800,
        25 in 1799, 10 in 1801, 9 in 1805</b>. They were born in England (95), Ireland (26), Scotland
        (2), Wales (2), and one each in Switzerland, Germany and unspecified Great Britain.</p>
      <p class="cap">So the sentence this archive has been waiting to write is available for a hundred and
        twenty-eight other men and not for him. <b>WO 97 before 1855 holds only soldiers discharged to a
        pension.</b> A man who left the ranks at the Cape without one, and stayed to make saddles, is
        exactly the man it would not hold.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">2 &middot; Margaret Downing has no London baptism</h3>
      <p>Her Burgersdorp gravestone says <b>born London, 23 February 1803</b>.</p>
      <p><b>The controls.</b> London baptisms 1801&ndash;1807 under the surname Downing: <b>28</b> &mdash;
        Louisa three times, Eliza three times, Susanna Delafield, Frances, Robert, Eliza Eleanor,
        Jacobus, Joannes, George Philip Thomas, Dennis, Hugh Robt., Joseph, Louisa Towgood twice,
        Thomas, William, Samuel and the rest. <b>No Margaret in 1803.</b> Westminster&rsquo;s own
        registers 1798&ndash;1810: <b>14</b> Downing baptisms, and again no Margaret. The set holds
        <b>239</b> Downings in Westminster overall.</p>
      <p class="cap">A Margaret Downing does turn up in the Roman Catholic banns of <b>St Patrick&rsquo;s,
        Soho Square</b>, two streets from the Kolbes&rsquo; own door &mdash; but the date is
        <b>22 September 1861</b> and the groom is Henry Shea. Not her, and written down so that she does
        not become her later.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">3 &middot; The Yorkshire half of question 6 does not exist</h3>
      <p>One profile welds a farmer of <b>Bilsdale</b>, christened 1796 and buried at <b>St Hilda&rsquo;s
        on 3 January 1853</b>, to a John Barry who died at de Bruins Poort in the Cape on
        <b>8 January 1853</b> &mdash; five days later.</p>
      <p><b>The controls.</b> FindMyPast&rsquo;s Yorkshire Burials includes Bilsdale, St Hilda, from the
        Ryedale Family History Society transcripts, with burials at 1849, 1851, 1857, 1859 and on
        through the 1860s &mdash; <b>January 1853 sits inside an indexed run</b>.</p>
      <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:10px">
        <table><tbody>
          <tr><td>Burials recorded at Bilsdale</td><td class="m"><b>5,934</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Barrys among them</td><td class="m"><b>0</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Barry baptisms at Bilsdale</td><td class="m"><b>0</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Barry burials elsewhere in Yorkshire</td><td class="m">771</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Barry burials anywhere in Yorkshire in 1853</td><td class="m">1 &mdash; Sheffield, no forename</td></tr>
          <tr><td>John Barry burials in Yorkshire, all years</td><td class="m">66 &mdash; 1837, then 1856</td></tr>
        </tbody></table>
      </div>
      <p class="cap"><b>A man buried at St Hilda&rsquo;s on 3 January 1853 is not in an index of 5,934
        Bilsdale burials</b>, while 771 Barrys are buried elsewhere in the same county. The surname is
        not invisible to the index. That profile has nothing under its English half.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">4 &middot; No Cork baptism for a Barry born to a Neagle</h3>
      <p>John Augustus Barry&rsquo;s 1903 death registration, made by a son who was in the room, answers
        <em>Irishman born in Ireland</em> and gives his mother only as <b>&ldquo;born Neagle&rdquo;</b>.
        The family says County Cork. He was 84 years 2 months, so born about November 1818.</p>
      <p><b>The controls.</b> Irish Roman Catholic baptisms in Cork <b>for 1818 alone: 12,273</b>. John
        Barry baptisms in Cork, 1816 to 1820: <b>71</b>. John Barry baptisms in all Ireland with a
        mother surnamed <b>Nagle: four</b> &mdash; 1811, 1845, 1860, 1876. <b>None between 1816 and
        1820.</b> Under <em>Neagle</em> or <em>Nagel</em>: none at all.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px">The honest limit of this one: it tests the Roman Catholic
        registers. He may not have been Catholic, and the Church of Ireland registers for Cork largely
        burnt in the Four Courts in 1922.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><b>And a citation of this archive&rsquo;s own that does not
    resolve.</b> This site has been carrying <span class="m">R_177176049779</span> for Petrus Jacobus
    Booysen&rsquo;s 1812 birth &mdash; a FindMyPast transcript reference recorded once, from a search run
    without a subscription, and never seen. Opened twice on a live full subscription, it returns
    <b>a 500 error from FindMyPast&rsquo;s own transcript page</b>. <b>It does not open.</b> A reference
    cited and unread is exactly what this archive corrects other people for.
    <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Corrections &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

